[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 7, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel: labeled `a`.\n\nTitle: `OCellus: Training a virtual cell foundation model`.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Left dashed box: `Training from multi-modal single-cell data`.\n- Input modalities shown with icons and labels:\n  - `scRNA-seq`\n  - `scPerturb-seq`\n  - `Spatial transcriptomics`\n- These modalities are grouped by a brace and fed into an `OCellus Transformer Encoder`.\n- Output from the encoder is labeled `Multi-modal Cell Embedding`, shown as two vertical embedding/vector-like blocks.\n\nRight dashed box: `OCellus pretraining and alignment (based on Qwen LLM)`.\n- The multi-modal cell embedding flows into `Data integration`.\n- Data integration connects to a `Qwen LLM model`.\n- The Qwen LLM model connects to a `Tiny LLM`.\n- The Tiny LLM connects to the final `Aligned OCellus Base Model`, represented by an atom-like icon.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell RNA sequencing data.\n- Perturbation single-cell sequencing data.\n- Spatial transcriptomics data.\n- Cell embeddings derived from multi-modal single-cell data.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Multi-modal biological inputs are encoded by the OCellus Transformer Encoder.\n- Encoded outputs become multi-modal cell embeddings.\n- Embeddings are integrated with a Qwen LLM-based pretraining/alignment pipeline.\n- A Tiny LLM is used before producing the aligned OCellus base model.\n\nFindings/claim shown:\n- The figure schematizes the training workflow for OCellus, a virtual cell foundation model integrating multi-modal single-cell data with an LLM-based alignment pipeline."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 7, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic scientific figure with visible panels labeled **b** and **c**.\n\nPanel **b** shows a central model architecture titled **“Central training hub and plug-in modular integration (Emulating self-supervised learning)”**. Inputs on the left are biological data sources: **Cell Data**, **Spatial Transcriptome Data**, and a **Gene Knockout database**. These feed into an **Encoder**, which connects to a central **Qwen LLM backbone**, then to a **Predictor**. Around the LLM backbone are plug-in/interface modules labeled **GNN+PPI**, **Agent-driven knowledge recall**, **Plug-ins / EvenClock for temporal data**, and **LoRA fine-tuning**. Outputs on the right include **Cell Types**, **Perturbation Predictions**, **Spatial Maps**, and **NL Explanations**.\n\nPanel **c** is titled **“Four-stage OCellus Training Pipeline”** and lists four sequential stages: **1 Pretrain** with “Pre-training base model,” **2 Merge** with “Multi-model integration,” **3 Multi-task** with “Instruction fine-tuning for all rules,” and **4 GNN-Agent** with “Specialized graph & knowledge driven reasoning.”\n\nThe figure represents a multimodal biological AI/LLM framework integrating cell data, spatial transcriptomics, gene knockout resources, graph/PPI information, temporal plug-ins, knowledge recall, and LoRA fine-tuning to produce biological predictions, maps, classifications, and natural language explanations."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 7, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic scientific workflow for “OCellus” applied to single-cell and spatial omics data.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Left panel shows input modalities feeding into an “OCellus Transformer Encoder”:\n  - `scRNA-seq` represented by colored cell icons.\n  - `scPerturb-seq` represented by a perturbation/gene-expression style grid.\n  - `Spatial Transcriptomics` represented by a tissue image.\n- The encoder outputs:\n  - `multi-modal cell embedding`\n  - `multi-model data-specific embedding`\n\nMiddle panel shows downstream task modules or prediction targets:\n- `Cell State`\n- `Drug Sensitivity`\n- `Perturbation`\n- `Spatial Neighbor`\n- `Spatial Deconvolution`\n- `Spatial Cell-Cell Communication`\n- `Spatial Imputation`\n\nOutputs illustrated on the right side of the middle panel include:\n- `Predicted spatial structure`\n- `Predicted perturbation profiles`\n- `Gene map`\n\nRight panel depicts a user interaction with OCellus:\n- User asks: “In this embryo brain slice, where would TP53 knockout have the strongest effect?”\n- OCellus returns a text response describing spatially heterogeneous predicted effects.\n- The visible finding states that ventricular zone progenitors show the strongest effect, with listed gene changes including `MDM2 -2.5 log2FC`, `CDKN1A -2.1`, `SERPINE1 +1.8`, while cortical plate neurons are largely resistant.\n- The response also mentions compensatory `ATM-CHEK2-RAD51` activation in the ventricular zone, suggesting region-specific DNA damage checkpoint recruitment.\n\nBiological/source objects visible:\n- Single cells, perturbation assay data, spatial transcriptomics tissue image, embryo brain slice context, predicted spatial maps, cell-neighborhood/network diagrams, and gene-expression/perturbation profiles.\n\nModel/interface elements:\n- Central transformer encoder labeled `OCellus Transformer Encoder`.\n- OCellus icon used for prediction and chat-style response.\n- A natural-language query interface connecting user questions to spatial perturbation predictions."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 10, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific data-composition figure titled **“OCellus Training Data Composition.”**\n\nVisible panels:\n\n- **Panel a: “27 multi-task training samples”**\n  - Horizontal bar chart on a log-scaled x-axis labeled **“Training samples (log scale)”**.\n  - Bars are grouped by task type with a legend:\n    - **Single-cell** in blue\n    - **Spatial** in magenta\n  - Visible task labels include spatial_neighborhood, spatial_density, spatial_domain, gene_function_string, spatial_celltype, developmental_stage, spatial_celltype, spatial_composition, spatial_celltype_genes, perturbation, spatial_tissue_region, cross_species_celltype, DeepMap_n_cell_line, grn_general, spatial_cellchat, Gene2disease, cell_Gene2disease, cell_state_transition, perturbation_ranking, Gene2phenotype, GL_synthetic, condition_generation, GL_phenotype, drug_response, Cellmarker, and GL_disease.\n  - The largest visible sample counts are approximately **591.3K**, **240.0K**, **160.0K**, and **140.0K**.\n\n- **Panel b: “4-stage training pipeline sample budget”**\n  - Pie chart showing total training sample budget of about **2.58M samples**.\n  - Visible segments:\n    - **Stage 3: Multi-task, 27 tasks, 1.91M samples**, shown as **73.8%**\n    - **Stage 1: Pretraining, 7 tasks, 0.68M samples**, shown as **26.2%**\n  - The chart implies a staged training pipeline, though only Stage 1 and Stage 3 are visibly represented/labeled.\n\n- **Panel c: “Training data sources (SToCorpus-88M + salusSTS + perturbation)”**\n  - Donut chart summarizing training data sources, centered text indicating approximately **~108M cells**.\n  - Legend lists biological data sources and proportions:\n    - **cellxgene human/mouse SC: 33%**\n    - **HuBMAP Visium: 15%**\n    - **salusSTS human subcellular: 17%**\n    - **10x Visium: 12%**\n    - **Other ST: 1%**\n    - **Stereo-seq: 9%**\n    - **MERFISH: 5%**\n    - **Integrated ST: 3%**\n    - **Perturb-Seq: 4%**\n    - **Sci-Plex: 2%**\n    - **CRISPR screen: 1%**\n    - **ST MuSiCSeq: 1%**\n\nBiological source objects visible/referenced:\n- Single-cell transcriptomic data\n- Spatial transcriptomics data\n- Human and mouse cells\n- Visium, MERFISH, Stereo-seq, Perturb-seq, Sci-Plex, CRISPR screen datasets\n- Tasks involving genes, diseases, phenotypes, cell states, cell types, tissues, perturbations, drug response, and gene regulatory networks\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure describes training sample construction and composition for a model/training pipeline.\n- It summarizes task-level sample counts, staged training budget allocation, and source-dataset composition.\n- No neural architecture diagram or explicit model input/output interface is shown.\n\nMain finding conveyed:\n- The training corpus combines large-scale single-cell and spatial biology sources, totaling roughly **108M cells**, with a multi-task training stage dominating the sample budget at about **73.8%** of **2.58M** training samples."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 13, "caption": "Figure 2 . Multi-task benchmark and encoder quality. (a) Multi-task heatmap across 22 task types and 3 7 model configurations of the same Qwen3.5-9B backbone: base model, OCellus-Pretrain (after Stage 2 8 merging), and the full OCellus model -all evaluated under identical protocols on held-out test sets. The 9 color scale encodes the task-appropriate primary metric: balanced accuracy for short-answer classification 10 tasks, gene-set recall for ranking/generation tasks, and proportion-vector cosine similarity for spatial 11 deconvolution. Tasks are grouped by category on the x-axis: single-cell core (6), single-cell extended (2), 12 single-cell cross-domain (4), spatial core (5), and spatial extended (5). The full OCellus model achieves 13", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific results figure comparing representation/model performance across single-cell and spatial transcriptomics tasks.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Top panel: A heatmap grid with three model rows:\n  - `OCellus`\n  - `OCellus-Pretrain`\n  - `Qwen-3.5-9B base`\n- Heatmap column groups:\n  - `SC core`\n  - `SC extended`\n  - `SC cross-domain`\n  - `Spatial core`\n  - `Spatial extended`\n- The heatmap reports values on a 0.00 to 1.00 scale using a yellow-to-red color map.\n- Caption beneath heatmap: `Primary metric (Balanced accuracy / Recall / Cosine)`\n\nVisible task labels include:\n- SC core: `perturbation`, `cell_gmppi`, `cell_phenotype`, `gene2disease`, `t-cellmetabase`, `CellMix`\n- SC extended: `virus_response`, `perturbation_rank`\n- SC cross-domain: `cross_species_celltype`, `development_stage`, `gene_knock-stripe`, `cell_state_transfer`\n- Spatial core: `spatial_celltype`, `spatial_neighborhood`, `spatial_tissue_region`, `spatial_celltype`, `spatial_lamination`\n- Spatial extended: `spatial_vz`, `spatial_domain`, `spatial_density`, `spatial_colocalization`, `spatial_comp_gene`\n\nBottom panel:\n- Three grouped bar charts:\n  - `KNN accuracy (k=5)`\n  - `Linear probe accuracy`\n  - `NMI (clustering)`\n- X-axis biological datasets/tissues:\n  - `pancreas`\n  - `lung`\n  - `pbmc`\n  - `atora`\n  - `Average`\n- Y-axis ranges from 0.00 to 1.00.\n- Bars compare multiple embedding or foundation models, including labels such as `OCellus`, `CellHermes`, `pca`, `scgpt`, `scFoundation`, `nicheformer`, `scVI`, `GenePT`, `scimilarity`, `scBERT`, `CellPLM`, `UCE`, `GeneCompass`, and `geneformer`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell datasets/tissues: pancreas, lung, PBMC, and atora.\n- Spatial transcriptomics-related tasks involving spatial cell type, tissue region, neighborhood, lamination, domain, density, and colocalization.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- `OCellus` appears to achieve high scores across many SC and spatial benchmark tasks in the heatmap.\n- `OCellus-Pretrain` and `Qwen-3.5-9B base` generally show lower and more variable heatmap scores.\n- In the bottom bar charts, `OCellus` is among the highest-performing methods for KNN and linear probe accuracy across datasets.\n- NMI clustering scores are lower overall than classification/probe accuracies and vary more strongly by dataset and model."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 16, "caption": "Figure 3 . Pancreas cell embeddings -UMAP deep dive. (a) Six foundation models compared on the 4 same 16,382 cells from the pancreas benchmark (14 cell types): OCellus, PCA, scFoundation, scGPT, 5 Geneformer, scVI (identical random seed). Linear-probe accuracy is reported under each model name. 6", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing UMAP embeddings of single-cell data, likely pancreatic cell types, with cell-type annotations and gene-expression overlays.\n\nPanel details:\n- **Panel a**: Six small UMAP plots comparing different embedding or model representations:\n  - **OCellus**\n  - **PCA**\n  - **scFoundation**\n  - **scGPT**\n  - **Geneformer**\n  - **scVI**\n  Each plot is colored by **Cell type**, with a legend listing: acinar, activated_stellate, alpha, beta, delta, ductal, endothelial, epsilon, gamma, macrophage, mast, quiescent_stellate, schwann, and t_cell. Axes are labeled **UMAP 1** and **UMAP 2**.\n\n- **Panel b**: A larger UMAP embedding with labeled clusters for the same biological cell types, including acinar, ductal, alpha, beta, delta, epsilon, gamma, macrophage, mast, t_cell, endothelial, schwann, activated_stellate, and quiescent_stellate.\n\n- **Panels c-f**: Gene-expression UMAP overlays using color intensity labeled as **log counts**:\n  - **c**: **INS (beta cells)**\n  - **d**: **GCG (alpha cells)**\n  - **e**: **SST (delta cells)**\n  - **f**: **PPY (gamma cells)**\n\nVisible biological source objects:\n- Single cells grouped into pancreatic/endocrine and associated cell-type clusters.\n- Marker genes shown for endocrine cell identities: INS, GCG, SST, and PPY.\n\nVisible transformations/model interfaces:\n- Dimensionality reduction to UMAP space.\n- Comparison of embeddings from PCA and multiple single-cell foundation or representation models: OCellus, scFoundation, scGPT, Geneformer, and scVI.\n- Gene-expression values projected onto the UMAP coordinates as log-count intensity maps.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Different models produce different degrees of cell-type separation and cluster structure.\n- The large annotated UMAP separates multiple pancreatic cell types.\n- Marker-gene overlays localize INS, GCG, SST, and PPY expression to distinct regions corresponding to beta, alpha, delta, and gamma cell populations."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 20, "caption": "Figure 4 . EvenClock spatial encoding and full-slice celltype prediction. (a) EvenClock algorithmic", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **a-d** about **EvenClock**, a clock-like spatial/text encoding scheme applied to a biological tissue/spatial transcriptomics example.\n\nPanel **a** shows a schematic circular “EvenClock” layout. It has concentric rings labeled **sec**, **min**, and **hr**, with a center labeled **center cell**. The outer clock face is divided into sectors with clock-like positions labeled **12, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10**. A scale annotation indicates **“1× median_nn (sec ring radius)”**. To the right is an **“EvenClock text encoding”** box listing sector prefixes and top genes, examples including:\n- **12-sec | TUBA1B, TMSB4X, STMN1**\n- **12-min | GAPDH, ACTB, RPLP0**\n- **2-sec | KRT1, COL1A1, COL3A1**\n- **4-sec | MYH9, ACTA2, TAGLN**\n- **6-hr | ALB, APOA1, HP**\n- **8-min | CD3D, TRAC, CD8A**\n\nA note states that each sector contributes top-N genes as a text prefix.\n\nPanel **b** shows a colored tissue/spatial map labeled **Ground truth**, with cell or spot classes including **Spinal Cord, Brain, Cartilage Primordium, Lung, Liver, Heart, Dorsal Root Ganglion, Others**.\n\nPanel **c** shows a similar tissue/spatial map labeled **OCellus prediction**, with many predicted anatomical or cell-type categories shown in a long legend.\n\nPanel **d** shows a correctness map comparing predictions to ground truth, with legend entries **Correct**, **Others**, and **Wrong**. Most points appear marked correct, with scattered wrong predictions.\n\nOverall, the figure presents a spatial neighborhood encoding method using clock-sector gene summaries, then compares model predictions against ground-truth biological tissue labels in an embryonic/anatomical spatial dataset."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 23, "caption": "Figure 5 . Perturbation prediction: OCellus-GNN benchmark, response network, pathway heatmap, and 7 response manifold. (a) Pearson correlation on top-20 differentially expressed genes per perturbation: 8 OCellus-GNN 0.945 (n=457 test perturbations, Replogle 2022 K562), GEARS 0.84 (reproduced), scGPT 9 0.80 and CPA 0.78 (literature values from scPerturBench). (b) Direction accuracy: OCellus-GNN 98.4%", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure labeled **a-j** comparing perturbation-response prediction methods.\n\nVisible content:\n- **a**: Bar plot titled **Pearson correlation**, comparing methods including **CPA**, **GEARS**, **OCellus**, **scGEN**, and **MOFT**. OCellus has the highest visible Pearson value around **0.94**, GEARS around **0.84**.\n- **b**: Bar plot titled **Direction accuracy**, same method comparison. OCellus is highest at about **99.6%**; GEARS is about **54.6%**.\n- **c**: Scatter plot titled **Predicted vs true**, with true log2FC on x-axis and predicted log2FC on y-axis. Two method annotations are visible: **OCellus r = 0.95** and **GEARS r = 0.84**.\n- **d**: Line plot titled **GNN training (Replogle 2022)** showing score over epochs. It includes curves/markers for **Direction accuracy** and **Pearson**, with an orange dashed reference line labeled **GEARS 0.84**.\n- **e**: Network diagram titled **TP53 KO: response network**. Central node appears to be **TP53**, connected to genes including **BCL2, BAX, BBC3, MYC, RB1, PTEN, CCND1, CDKN1A, RPS8, MDM2, ATM, CHEK2, FAS, GADD45B, SERPINE1, PMAIP1**. Node colors encode **log2FC**.\n- **f**: Heatmap titled **True log2FC by pathway**, rows include perturbations such as **TP53_KO, PTEN_KO, MYC_KO, BRCA1_KO**. Columns are grouped by pathways including **Apoptosis**, **Cell cycle arrest**, **DNA repair**, **JAK signaling**, and **SASP**.\n- **g**: Heatmap titled **OCellus log2FC by pathway**, matching the same pathway-grouped layout as panel f, showing model-predicted log2FC values.\n- **h**: t-SNE scatter titled **True log2FC**, colored by log2FC.\n- **i**: t-SNE scatter titled **OCellus predicted log2FC**, colored by predicted log2FC.\n- **j**: t-SNE scatter titled **GEARS predicted log2FC**, colored by predicted log2FC.\n\nBiological source objects visible:\n- Gene knockouts/perturbations including **TP53_KO, PTEN_KO, MYC_KO, BRCA1_KO**.\n- Response genes/pathway markers associated with apoptosis, cell-cycle arrest, DNA repair, JAK signaling, and SASP.\n- Dataset/training reference visible as **Replogle 2022**.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Perturbation response prediction expressed as **log2 fold-change/log2FC**.\n- Evaluation by **Pearson correlation**, **direction accuracy**, predicted-vs-true scatter, pathway heatmaps, response network visualization, and t-SNE embedding.\n- Models compared include **OCellus**, **GEARS**, **CPA**, **scGEN**, and **MOFT**.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- **OCellus** appears to outperform the compared methods in Pearson correlation and direction accuracy and better matches true pathway-level and embedding-level log2FC patterns than **GEARS**."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 28, "caption": "Figure 6 . Interpretable perturbation prediction: TP53 and BRCA1 knockout case studies, and the TP53 PPI response network. (a) TP53-knockout prediction case study. The model produces quantitative log2 fold-change values for the top response genes (MDM2, CDKN1A, SERPINE1) under LoRA-enabled mode; the explainer role (LoRA-disabled) then provides a natural-language interpretation linking the predicted downregulation of MDM2 and CDKN1A -direct transcriptional targets of TP53 -and the compensatory upregulation of SERPINE1 to TP53's known role as a tumor suppressor regulating cell -cycle arrest, apoptosis, and senescence-associated secretory activation. (b) BRCA1-knockout prediction case study. The model predicts upregulation of MDM2, RAD51, and FANCD2; the explainer interprets RAD51 and FANCD2 upregulation as compensatory activation of homologous-recombination repair mediated by the BRCA2-PALB2 axis, which functions independently of BRCA1. (c) Protein-interaction response network centered on TP53 (STRING v12, confidence ≥ 0.70): dark-blue node marks the knocked-out gene (TP53), grey nodes are direct PPI neighbors, and green nodes are predicted response genes identified across the TP53 and BRCA1 case studies. The network visualization shows that the response genes are not randomly distributed but cluster around direct PPI neighbors of TP53, consistent with the perturbation signal propagating through physical protein contacts.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel biological/scientific figure with panels labeled **a**, **b**, and **c**.\n\nPanel **a** shows a schematic of **gene knockout** leading to a **TP53 KO prediction**. A DNA/gene knockout icon points to a matrix-style prediction output. The matrix has response genes labeled **MDM2**, **CDKN1A**, and **SERPINE1** on the rows, and predictor/context genes labeled **MDM2** and **CDKN1A** on the columns. A color bar indicates **log2FC**. A callout states that an **interpretative model provides context-specific response gene prediction**.\n\nPanel **b** shows a similar gene knockout schematic leading to a **BRCA1 KO prediction**. The output is a bar chart with response genes **RAD51** and **FANCD2**, both labeled “up,” with a vertical **log2FC** axis. A callout says the prediction matches a **differential context explanation**.\n\nPanel **c** shows a protein-protein interaction/network diagram centered on the knockout gene **TP53**, colored teal. Surrounding nodes are **PPI neighbors** in gray and **response genes** in green. The legend defines teal as **Knockout gene**, gray as **PPI neighbors**, and green as **Response genes**, described as identified across panels a and b and other context-specific predictors. Visible network labels include **TP53**, **MDM2**, **RAD51**, **RAD52**, **FANCD2**, **TGF-BR**, **RPAR1**, **CLFX**, **COB3**, **CARO**, **RN1S1**, **RPNG**, **PURG9**, **SEEB1**, **MPRS1**, **HME3**, **TWRV2**, **CCT2**, and others.\n\nOverall, the figure illustrates an interpretable model for predicting gene-expression responses after knockout perturbations, using context-specific predictor-response relationships and mapping response genes onto a PPI network around **TP53**."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 29, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with three labeled panels: **a**, **b**, and **c**.\n\nPanel **a** shows a 2D embedding/scatter plot labeled **“Mouse (1000) vs Human (1000)”**. Points are colored by **Species**, with a legend indicating **Mouse** and **Human**. The plot compares mouse and human cells in an embedding space.\n\nPanel **b** shows the same or similar 2D cell embedding labeled **“Ground truth cell type”**. Points are colored by known cell-type annotations. The legend lists biological cell types including **Astro**, **Endo**, **Epi**, **ExN**, **Immune**, **IN**, **Micro**, **NPC/Glia**, **Oligo**, and **Peri**.\n\nPanel **c** shows a corresponding embedding labeled **“Ocellus prediction (87.0% correct)”**. Points are colored by predicted cell type using the **Ocellus** model/interface, with the same cell-type categories as panel b.\n\nBiological source objects are single cells from **mouse** and **human**, visualized in a reduced-dimensional embedding. The transformation shown is likely dimensionality reduction of cell profiles into 2D coordinates, followed by annotation either by species, ground-truth cell type, or model-predicted cell type. The visible finding is that Ocellus cell-type predictions broadly match the ground-truth cell-type structure, with **87.0% correct** reported in the panel title."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 32, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains two labeled panels, `a` and `b`, describing an “OCellus-Agent” system and an ablation-style performance result.\n\nPanel `a` is a schematic architecture diagram. It shows an interface on the left with a user icon, `Gradio Web UI`, and `report` output. The main system is organized into three layers:\n\n- `Layer 3 Coordinator`: includes `Planner LoRA`, a `Router rule-based`, and a `3-layer Verifier`, with a `retry` loop.\n- `Layer 2 Experts`: includes expert modules labeled `Data Loader`, `Annotator`, `Perturbation`, `Spatial`, and `Explainer`, with small processor-chip icons indicating model or computational components. The `Explainer` is marked `base`.\n- `Layer 1 Tools`: grouped into `data`, `OCellus`, `Visualization`, and `External Tools`. Visible tool icons/labels include data access, preprocessing, time series, stats analysis, configuration/dev tools, integration, assay data, pathway, tissue, workflow, trends, annotated, reaction, view, interactive, comparison, API client, integration troubleshooting, global, collaboration, local tools, API management, and data source.\n\nBiological/source objects shown include DNA/helix imagery for `Perturbation` and a tissue/cell-pattern icon for `Spatial`, suggesting biological perturbation and spatial biology analysis modules. The diagram shows arrows from the coordinator/router to expert modules and from experts down to tools, indicating routing, tool use, verification, and retry-based workflow control.\n\nPanel `b` is a horizontal bar chart comparing pipeline accuracy across ablations, labeled `Pipeline accuracy (Verifier-L3 ACCEPT, 80 trials)`. Reported results are:\n\n- `No LoRA switching`: 42.5% `(n=34/80)`\n- `Flat ReAct loop`: 51.3% `(n=41/80)`\n- `No Verifier layers`: 51.3% `(n=41/80)`\n- `No Layer-3 Critic`: 58.8% `(n=47/80)`\n- `OCellus-Agent (full)`: 75.0% `(n=60/80)`\n\nThe visible finding is that the full `OCellus-Agent` configuration has the highest reported pipeline accuracy among the compared variants."]}]

