./data/input_representation_taxonomy_2026-07-11/runs/classification_dense_coverage/workers4_record_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_docling_json_20260711_053919/debug/dense_provenance.json:29813:      "text": "7 Supplementary\n7.2 Limitations\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\nPanel E is a bar plot for \u201cSilmitasertib + IFN\u03b2 (Novel Hit - C2S-predicted)\u201d measuring \u201cHLA ABC MFI\u201d across silmitasertib concentrations in nM, comparing DMSO only versus DMSO + 2 U/ml IFN\u03b2. The main visible finding is that silmitasertib with IFN\u03b2 strongly increases HLA ABC MFI relative to DMSO-only conditions.\nFigure 8: C2S allows for conversion from expression information into cell sentence format with minimal information loss. Using a linear model fitted between rank and original expression, cell sentences can be converted back to expression accurately.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel scatter/density-style plot comparing gene expression against gene log-rank for two datasets or tissues: **PBMC** and **Heart**. Each panel has x-axis **Gene Log-Rank** and y-axis **Normalized Expression**. Blue point/line density forms a descending trend, with a red fitted regression line overlaid.\n\nVisible annotations:\n- PBMC: **R\u00b2 = 0.866**, **Slope = -0.601**, **Intercept = 2.395**\n- Heart: **R\u00b2 = 0.824**, **Slope = -0.550**, **Intercept = 2.237**\n\nMain visible finding: normalized expression decreases approximately linearly as gene log-rank increases in both PBMC and Heart, with strong negative correlations.\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\nFigure 9: FLOPs usage by C2S-Scale 2B versus other foundation models and simple model baselines.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nBar plot comparing total FLOPs used by different models on a logarithmic y-axis labeled \u201cTotal FLOPs.\u201d The title is \u201cTotal FLOPs Used By Different models.\u201d The x-axis lists models: C2S-Scale-27B, C2S-Scale-2B, C2S-Scale-1B, C2S-Scale-410M, scFoundation, scGPT, Geneformer, scVI, Linear Classifier, CellTypist, and PCA.\n\nEach bar has a numeric FLOP label: C2S-Scale-27B is highest at 5.2e22, followed by C2S-Scale-2B at 3.2e21. Other large models are around 1e19 to 1e20, including scFoundation at 1.2e20, C2S-Scale-1B at 8.0e19, scGPT at 7.3e19, Geneformer at 4.2e19, and C2S-Scale-410M at 2.4e19. Classical or smaller methods are much lower: scVI 6.7e15, Linear Classifier 4.1e12, CellTypist 8.6e11, and PCA 1.4e10.\n\nMain visible finding: C2S-Scale-27B uses orders of magnitude more total FLOPs than the other compared models, while PCA, CellTypist, and Linear Classifier use the least.",
./analysis/codex_screening_run_artifacts_20260706/inputs/dedup_update_2026-06-10/new_records_screening_ready.json:5023:      "abstract": "Abstract\n                \n                  Motivation\n                  Frozen DNA encoders are often used as genomic feature extractors, but downstream fine-tuning does not show what information is already linearly accessible in their unchanged embeddings. We introduce MINA (Model Interrogation of Nucleotide Architectures), a lightweight probing benchmark for testing whether frozen DNA embeddings can recover (i) a 5-way protein-family label for each gene and (ii) the 1,536-dimensional GenePT embedding of each gene’s natural-language summary. We compare recoverability between canonical coding sequence and TSS-centred genomic contexts.\n                \n                \n                  Results\n                  \n                    In 3,244 human protein-coding genes from five families, frozen encoders recovered the family-annotation target most clearly from coding sequence. NT-v2 with meanD pooling reached macro-F1 0.828 /\n                    κ\n                    0.821, compared with 0.672 /\n                    κ\n                    0.702 for a CDS 4-mer baseline. Alignment to GenePT natural-language descriptions was weaker. Replacing CDS with 196,608 bp TSS-centred windows substantially reduced performance across all four encoders, indicating that the recoverable signal is primarily coding-sequence family signal rather than generic gene-function signal from arbitrary genomic context.\n                  \n                \n                \n                  Availability and implementation\n                  \n                    Source code:\n                    https://github.com/Austin-Senna/dna_to_text\n                    ; Python ≥3.11.\n                  \n                \n                \n                  Contact\n                  \n                    asw2215@columbia.edu\n                  \n                \n                \n                  Supplementary information\n                  Supplementary tables, figures, and reproducibility details are included at the end of this preprint.",
