soning evaluation. The reasoning judge is invoked only when the answer is at least partially correct and both pre-gates pass. This avoids providing a training signal for 'how well the model reasons about incorrect conclusions,' as such a signal would encourage plausible but wrong available under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning reasoning chains.\nWhen invoked, the judge evaluates the CoT chain across five weighted dimensions:\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nThe two most heavily weighted dimensions are Independence ( d ind ), which measures whether conclusions derive from gene evidence rather than restating the query, and Counterfactual Robustness ( d rob ), which tests whether reasoning holds under different tissue or disease contexts. These two dimensions are the most diagnostic of genuine biological reasoning versus superficial pattern matching.\nThree additional dimensions complete the rubric: Causal Sufficiency, Granularity Discipline, and Consistency (full definitions and weights are provided in Appendix A.4).\nAsixth dimension, Label Semantic Match , is logged for monitoring but excluded from the reward to avoid double-counting with the answer judge.\nTogether, the ontology-aware answer judge and the gated reasoning rubric cover semantic correctness and reasoning structure, but neither inspects the factual content of intermediate claims, which motivates the layer developed next.\n\n3 Method\n3.8 Fact-Aware Reinforcement Learning\nThe ontology-grounded reward described above ensures prediction correctness, but a correct answer produced through flawed reasoning (such as citing fabricated gene markers or hallucinating pathway associations) represents a distinct failure mode that the answer judge cannot detect.\nHallucination-accuracy trade-off. Standard hallucination-penalized RLVR using a count-based ratio R = R task + α · R hallu suffers from two failure modes: (i) redundancy-induced dilution , where the policy outputs many correct but uninformative sentences to inflate the denominator and suppress the apparent hallucination rate; and (ii) penalty-induced conservatism , where a large α drives the policy toward terse outputs that sacrifice the multi-step reasoning required for biological inference.\nAn effective fact reward must therefore weight claims by their informational importance to defeat dilution, while modulating its influence based on the current task competence of the model to avoid premature conservatism.\nInspired by Baichuan-M3 [49], RVQ-Alpha employs a saliency-weighted, dynamically gated factverification reward adapted for single-cell biological reasoning.\nSaliency-weighted fact reward. Each reasoning chain is decomposed into independently verifiable atomic claims, which are evaluated against domain-specific knowledge bases covering cell-type markers, pathway memberships, and disease-gene associations, and subsequently classified as SUPPORTED, REFUTED, or UNCERTAIN.\nEach representative claim c ∗ k is assigned a saliency weight that measures the maximum cosine similarity between its embedding and the embeddings of the response sentences. The weighted fact reward captures this variation in claim importance:\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nThe denominator accumulates weighted information content rather than the raw claim count. Consequently, appending low-salience sentences barely increases it, whereas high-saliency refuted claims incur disproportionately large penalties, focusing the signal on core biological assertions.\nDetails regarding the claim extraction procedure, knowledge base inventory, and verification caching are provided in Appendix A.4.\nDynamic multi-objective aggregation. Afixed coupling between R task and R fact is counterproductive early in training, when the model has not yet learned to generate substantive claims worth verifying. RVQ-Alpha applies a soft gating coefficient:\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nwhere σ is the logistic sigmoid function, κ controls the transition steepness, µ is the inflection point, and ∆ represents a temperature scale.\nThe final composite reward combines task performance with dynamically gated factual accuracy:\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nThis formulation partitions the training process into three regimes:\n- a Protection Zone ( R task < τ min, λ ≈ 0) where the task reward dominates;\n- a Transition Zone ( τ min ≤ R task ≤ τ max) where factual constraints increase nonlinearly;\nand a Constraint Zone ( R task > τ max, λ ≈ 1) where full hallucination suppression is engaged.\nTogether with the ontology-grounded task reward, this implements a two-layer verification curriculum: the model must first demonstrate semantic correctness before factual-accuracy constraints engage. The empirical effects of this progression are examined in Section 4.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.1 Base Model\nRVQ-Alpha uses a single base LLM across all training stages and task families: Qwen3-4B-Thinking2507 [51] (4.0,B parameters, V text = 151,941), extended with Nc · C = 8 × 32 = 256 RVQ single-cell vocabulary slots, five structural delimiter tokens, and the top-500 gene name tokens (tokenizer details in §3.2).\nThe Thinking variant was selected because our RLVR objective explicitly rewards the reasoning trace (§3.7), aligning the native reasoning behavior of the model with the downstream training objective.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.2 Training Protocol\nWe train RVQ-Alpha in three sequential stages: continued pretraining (CPT), supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR). The pipeline begins from the single backbone introduced above; each subsequent stage initializes from the merged checkpoint produced by the unified checkpoint-selection procedure described at the end of this subsection. Training uses sixteen NVIDIA H20 96,GB GPUs in total: four GPUs each for CPT and SFT, and eight GPUs for each RLVR run, with an additional eight GPUs hosting the LLM judge. Table 4 summarizes the per-stage configuration.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nTable 4: Training-protocol summary. Stage-wise training settings for the three-stage pipeline. Full hyperparameter grids, the checkpoint-merging configuration, and prompt templates are deferred to the appendix.\n\nBase checkpoint, CPT = Qwen3-4B + 256 RVQ to- kens. Base checkpoint, SFT = Merged CPT checkpoint. Base checkpoint, RLVR (per run) = Merged SFT checkpoint. Training data, CPT = ≈ 225,000 records, six- source mixture. Training data, SFT = ≈ 121,000 records, five- source mixture. Training data, RLVR (per run) = 28-200 records per task. Split granularity, CPT = Donor-level; 8 held-out donors. Split granularity, SFT = Same as CPT. Split granularity, RLVR (per run) = Same donor pool. Sequence length, CPT = 16,384 packed. Sequence length, SFT = 16,384 packed (prompt cap 4,096). Sequence length, RLVR (per run) = 4,096 prompt + 16,384 re- sponse. Effective batch, CPT = 2 seq / 32,768 tok. Effective batch, SFT = 2 seq / 32,768 tok. Effective batch, RLVR (per run) = 16 prompts × 8 rollouts = 128 trajectories. Optimizer / peak LR, CPT = Fused AdamW / 5 × 10 - 5. Optimizer / peak LR, SFT = Fused AdamW / 1 × 10 - 6. Optimizer / peak LR, RLVR (per run) = AdamW / 1 × 10 - 6. LR schedule, CPT = Cosine / 10% warmup. LR schedule, SFT = Cosine / 20% warmup. LR schedule, RLVR (per run) = Cosine / 10 warmup steps (0.5%). Total updates, CPT = 13,000. Total updates, SFT = ≈ 7,000-15,000 (2 epochs). Total updates, RLVR (per run) = 2,000 RLVR optimizer updates. Compute, CPT = 4 × H20 96,GB. Compute, SFT = 4 × H20 96,GB. Compute, RLVR (per run) = 8 train + 8 judge H20 96,GB. Stage output, CPT = Merged CPT checkpoint. Stage output, SFT = Merged SFT checkpoint. Stage output, RLVR (per run) = Merged RLVR check- point\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.2 Training Protocol\nContinued pretraining. CPT extends the base model on a six-source mixture of ≈ 225,000 records combined in natural proportions (with no per-source weighting). The evaluation is held out at the donor level on eight donors (A013, D094-D096, D099, D101-D103) that are disjoint from the training set by construction.\nEach 16,384-token packed sequence is sharded across GPUs using fully sharded data parallelism with a (data-parallel, context-parallel, tensor-parallel) degree configuration of ( 2, 2, 1 ) .\nOptimization uses AdamW at a peak learning rate of 5 × 10 -5 with a 10% cosine warmup and a weight decay of 0.1; computation uses BF16 mixed precision with FP32 accumulation for gradient reduction and FP32 optimizer states.\nTraining covers 13,000 optimizer updates at an effective global batch size of 2 sequences (32,768 tokens per step); the resulting ≈ 4.26 × 10 8 training tokens correspond to approximately one to two effective epochs over the corpus.\nSupervised fine-tuning. SFT initializes from the merged CPT checkpoint and refines it on ≈ 121,000 chat-template conversations with chain-of-thought reasoning drawn from five sources, inheriting the CPT donor-held-out split.\nThe loss is computed exclusively on assistant spans under the Qwen3 chat template.\nSFT is deliberately light-touch: we decrease the peak learning rate by two orders of magnitude to 1 × 10 -6 and raise the warmup ratio from 0.1 to 0.2 to smooth the transition from the peak learning rate of CPT.\nParallelism, precision, sequence length, and effective batch size match those of CPT; the run covers two epochs at 32,768 tokens per step.\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nRLVR. RLVR initializes from the merged SFT checkpoint and covers three per-task runs: the homogeneous tissue task, the homogeneous disease-state task, and the heterogeneous cell-type task.\nWe adopt GSPO [46] on top of the GRPO advantage estimator [47], with the full objective deferred to §3.4.\nBecause the geometric-mean likelihood ratio of GSPO concentrates with response length, we tighten the clip bounds to 3 × 10 -4 /4 × 10 -4 (compared to the PPO default of 0.2) and disable all KL and entropy regularization.\nThe reward combines rule-based format and repetition checks with an LLM judge (Qwen3-30B-A3B, hosted on a separate inference server); the reward formula, judge rubric, and weights are specified in §3.7.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.2 Training Protocol\nEach step generates 128 trajectories (16 prompts × 8 rollouts) with a temperature of 1.0; training runs for 2,000 steps on eight GPUs with AdamW at a learning rate of 1 × 10 -6 (Table 4).\nUnified Checkpoint Protocol. Each stage selects its output using the same protocol: saving periodically (every 1,000 steps for CPT and SFT, and every five RLVR optimizer updates for RLVR), scoring every saved checkpoint on a fixed held-out benchmark, and averaging the top-5 checkpoints by score with uniform weights (linear weight averaging in parameter space). The resulting merged checkpoint serves as the stage output from which the subsequent stage initializes and which is evaluated in this paper. The ranking metrics differ by stage: CPT and SFT use held-out single-cell LLM-judge accuracy, while RLVR uses task-specific LLM-judge accuracy on the per-task validation set.\nAll reported results use a single seed (seed 42). The checkpoint ranking uses stochastic one-sample decoding ( T = 0.6, topp = 0.95, topk = 20); individual scores therefore carry sampling noise that the top-5 merge attenuates. Donor disjointness between training and evaluation is strictly enforced for the homogeneous cell-type task and maintained as an operating convention for the remaining population tasks. The mixed-precision configuration (BF16 compute, FP32 gradient accumulation, FP32 AdamW moments) is summarized in the appendix. Full hyperparameter grids, the checkpointmerging configuration, the RLVR training hyperparameters, and prompt templates are deferred to the supplementary material.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.3 Datasets\nCorpus identity. We draw pretraining and supervised fine-tuning data from the ScBank repository of donor-level human scRNA-seq profiles.\nOur working corpus is a 55-file indexed donor-level subset comprising 31,481,661 cells from approximately 45 source study families (the provenance index is released with the repository).\nThe indexed corpus spans 101 standardized tissues , 103 disease states , and 859 standardized cell-type labels (Table 5).\nThese counts describe the harmonized metadata space of the corpus and exceed the effective class counts of any individual benchmark task (§4.4).\nOf the 55 dataset IDs, 53 are traceable to upstream ScBank source records; the remaining two are documented in our corpus index without a corresponding catalog match.\nMetadata harmonization. Tissue, disease, and cell-type annotations were canonicalized through fieldalias normalization and dictionary-based value mapping before any downstream task construction.\nBecause metadata completeness varies across files (for instance, the donor identifier and tissue are present in all 55 files, whereas disease, cell type, sex, and age coverage is partial, as shown in Table 5), RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nTable 5: Corpus summary: 55-file indexed subset of ScBank. Label counts reflect the harmonized metadata space of the indexed corpus, not the effective class counts of any downstream benchmark (cf. Table 6). †\n\nIndexed files, Value = 55. Total cells, Value = 31,481,661. Source study families, Value = ≈ 45 distinct upstream studies. Standardized tissues, Value = 101. Standardized disease states, Value = 103. Standardized cell types, Value = 859. Catalog traceability, Value = 53/55 IDs linked to upstream catalog entries\n† Core metadata fields (donor ID, tissue) are present in all 55 files; disease and cell type in 54; sex in 50; age in 42.\nwe characterize the corpus by its harmonized label space rather than by a uniformly populated metadata schema.\nQCand preprocessing. The corpus supports metadata-level claims regarding label harmonization and retention, but it does not provide a fully reconstructable expression-matrix preprocessing pipeline, as standard per-cell quality control fields (such as gene count, total count, and mitochondrial fraction) are only sparsely populated across the processed files.\nAccordingly, we report only the metadata-supported harmonization here; the expression-to-token pipeline (binning, RVQ token assignment) is detailed in §3.2.\nTraining variants. Both the SFT and RL stages operate on task-specific JSONL instances derived from the indexed corpus.\nSingle-cell cell-type classification uses one of three training pools: a filtered pool of 98,873 cells (the default for the main text) and two larger unfiltered pools of 286,452 and 2,867,679 cells, which are used only where explicitly noted.\nUnless otherwise stated, the main-text results use the 98,873-sample filtered pool (Table 6).\nThe population task-pool counts in Table 6 refer to construction-pool sizes; the per-task subsampling for training is described alongside each task.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.4 Benchmarks\nDownstream evaluation is organized around two capability clusters: single-cell annotation at the individual-cell level, and population-level analysis over cohorts of 3-48 cells. Within each cluster, task targets are decomposed orthogonally from the population-construction mechanism: the supervision axis represents the label family (cell type, tissue, or disease), whereas the terms homogeneous and heterogeneous refer to how the internal composition of a population is assembled.\nSingle-cell benchmark. The single-cell benchmark evaluates cell-type generalization on eight heldout ScBank datasets (A013, D094-D096, D099, D101-D103) totaling 11,914 test samples across 12 tissues, five diseases, and 106 cell types (Table 6).\nAninth held-out ID (D097) is reserved for RVQ representation-boundary audits (§4.5) and does not appear in any main-text evaluation.\nPer-dataset accuracy and LCAD results on this eight-dataset pool are reported in §5.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.4 Benchmarks\nTable 6: Benchmark task mapping. Training-pool counts are construction-pool sizes (§4.3). The default cell-type training pool is the 98,873-sample filtered variant; the 286,452-sample and 2,867,679-sample unfiltered pools are used only when explicitly stated. Label counts are the effective class counts per task and differ from the corpus-level counts in Table 5. '-' marks a task without a released evaluation artifact.\n\nSingle-cell, Task = Cell-type ID. Single-cell, Input = 1 cell. Single-cell, Training pool = 98,873 (filtered). Single-cell, Eval set = 11,914 / 8 files. Single-cell, Labels = 106. Pop. homogeneous, Task = Cell type. Pop. homogeneous, Input = 3-30 cells 3-15 cells. Pop. homogeneous, Training pool = 4,624 (pool) 45,421. Pop. homogeneous, Eval set = - 2,000. Pop. homogeneous, Labels = 28. Pop. homogeneous, Task = Tissue. Pop. homogeneous, Input = . Pop. homogeneous, Training pool = (pool). Pop. homogeneous, Eval set = (sampled). Pop. homogeneous, Labels = 10. Pop. homogeneous, Task = Disease. Pop. homogeneous, Input = 1-40 cells. Pop. homogeneous, Training pool = 49,427 (pool). Pop. homogeneous, Eval set = 2,000 (sampled). Pop. homogeneous, Labels = 5. Pop. heterogeneous, Task = Cell type. Pop. heterogeneous, Input = 16-48 cells, 2-8 types. Pop. heterogeneous, Training pool = 225 (pool). Pop. heterogeneous, Eval set = -. Pop. heterogeneous, Labels = 68. Pop. heterogeneous, Task = Tissue. Pop. heterogeneous, Input = 10-25 cells, 2-6 tissues. Pop. heterogeneous, Training pool = 100 (pool). Pop. heterogeneous, Eval set = -. Pop. heterogeneous, Labels = 2. Pop. heterogeneous, Task = Disease. Pop. heterogeneous, Input = Not included in this benchmark.. Pop. heterogeneous, Training pool = Not included in this benchmark.. Pop. heterogeneous, Eval set = . Pop. heterogeneous, Labels = \nWe refer to this setting as held-out-dataset evaluation . It demonstrates generalization to datasets not seen during training, although the released benchmark does not independently establish a benchmark-wide leave-one-tissue-out or donor-level out-of-distribution protocol (see Table 7).\nPopulation benchmarks. Population samples are constructed along two orthogonal axes.\nA homogeneous population shares a single supervision label; a heterogeneous population intentionally mixes labels, and the model must recover the composition within the population.\nThree homogeneous tasks are released (cell type, 3-30 cells; tissue, 3-15; disease, 1-40) alongside two heterogeneous tasks (cell type, 16-48 cells from 2-8 types; tissue, 10-25 cells from 2-6 tissues). A heterogeneous disease task is not included in the present benchmark.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.4 Benchmarks\nConstruction uses a per-sample processing identifier on all homogeneous tasks and on the heterogeneous cell-type task to prevent cross-sample mixing; the heterogeneous tissue task intentionally relaxes this key to maximize cross-tissue diversity.\nAll counts in Table 6 represent released pool sizes, not final published training splits.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.5 Evaluation Protocol\nLLM-as-judge for post-training evaluation. All post-training numbers in this paper are produced by an offline evaluation judge that is implemented and run separately from the training-time reward judge (§3.7) and the training-time monitoring judge. These layers share neither a scoring scheme nor aggregation logic. Following Zheng et al. [33], the offline judge is DeepSeek-chat (DeepSeek-AI, dense) accessed through the DeepSeek cloud API. For each prediction, it emits a task-aware semantic relation label together with a continuous semantic score in [ 0, 1 ] ; this scalar is then thresholded to form a binary accuracy. For the single-cell cell-type task, we adopt a conservative operating point of τ = 0.8. Under this threshold, exact matches and strong ontology-consistent refinements are counted as correct, whereas broader lineage-adjacent or merely partially related predictions are not credited as fine-grained biological identification. For population tasks, the offline judge is applied at the per-cell level with task-specific prompt families (cell type, tissue, disease state), and per-cell scores are aggregated into sample-level summaries at a grain that depends on the reported metric (per-cellthresholded accuracy vs. per-sample mean-score correctness; see the Population evaluation paragraph below). Thresholds are task-dependent: we follow the operating points of τ = 0.75 for cell-type and tissue-type populations and the stricter τ = 0.85 for the disease-state population, consistent with the per-task figure captions in App. A.1-App. A.2.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nThe offline evaluation judge and the training-time reward judge are operationally separate judge stacks, implemented in distinct codepaths with different endpoints and prompt configurations, and served by different model families (reward judge: Qwen3-30B-A3B, Alibaba, MoE; offline evaluation judge: DeepSeek-chat, DeepSeek-AI, dense). Consequently, this separation spans multiple model-, serving-, and prompt-level axes rather than reusing the deployed judge stack from the RL phase. The intent is to reduce the risk of self-preference bias [52] in the reported metrics, not to establish formal statistical independence between the two judge stacks. 3\nCell Ontology-Aware Metrics. Beyond binary accuracy, we compute three additional metrics.\nFirst, LCAD (Lowest Common Ancestor Distance) [53, 10, 54]: a severity-of-error metric that measures the hierarchical distance between predicted and ground-truth cell types on the Cell Ontology [50, 55] is-a directed acyclic graph (DAG). Formally,\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nwhere ct and cp represent the ground-truth and predicted cell-type labels mapped to Cell Ontology identifiers ( CL:NNNNNNN ), LCA ( ct , cp ) is their lowest common ancestor in the is-a DAG, and d ( · , · ) is the unweighted shortest-path length in the number of is-a edges.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.5 Evaluation Protocol\nThis metric was formally introduced by Shi et al. [53] for hierarchical out-of-distribution evaluation and adapted to single-cell foundation model benchmarking by scFM-Bench [10]; the graph-distanceon-ontology family dates to Wu & Palmer (1994) and Pesquita et al. [54].\nName resolution proceeds through a five-tier deterministic cascade before any LLM-derived signal is consulted: (i) exact string match, (ii) direct Cell Ontology lookup via the OBO Foundry release of the Cell Ontology ( cl.obo ; 2025-11-03; 3,373 CL terms), (iii) human-curated synonym mapping, (iv) rule-based string normalization, and (v) semantic-relation estimation from the offline evaluation judge (capped at 6.0; see §4.7 for the full cascade and per-dataset resolution statistics).\nSecond, Non-leaf accuracy : this evaluates predictions that are valid sub-types of the ground-truth label.\nThird, Macro-F1 and Weighted-F1 : these metrics evaluate performance across imbalanced cell-type distributions.\nPopulation evaluation. Population evaluation is organized by task family rather than under a single monolithic rubric. The offline evaluation pipeline covers four task families: (i) homogeneous celltype populations, (ii) heterogeneous cell-type populations, (iii) homogeneous tissue populations, and (iv) homogeneous disease-state populations. Each is scored with its own semantic-relation vocabulary (cell-type, tissue, or disease-state categories) rather than a shared joint tissue, + ,cell-type rubric.\nFor every family, the offline judge first parses the response into per-cell predictions, scores each cell under the task-specific rubric, and then aggregates the per-cell scores into sample-level summaries. Task-dependent thresholds ( τ = 0.75 for cell-type and tissue populations, τ = 0.85 for disease-state) are applied at the reporting grain of each table or figure rather than as a single global cutoff.\nBoth per-cell and per-sample statistics are retained: per-cell thresholded accuracy is reported at the cell-type grouping in App. A.2, while pass@ k (§5) is computed based on per-sample correctness (a sample is correct when its mean per-cell score clears the task threshold).\n3 Atraining-time monitoring judge also runs during RL to log progress metrics; it uses the same Qwen3-30B-A3B model as the reward judge and does not contribute to the policy gradient. Reward-side multi-sample aggregation (three judge samples per answer, with the mean used for the reward signal, the median used for the hard gate, and conservative tie-breaking over ontology categories) is a property of the training-time reward system and is described in App. A.4; it is not part of the offline evaluation protocol used to compute the accuracy numbers reported in the main text.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nBecause population evaluation sets are sampled per task (Table 6) rather than pooled, we report accuracy by task; the Joint (all correct) row in Table 9 acts as a locally defined summary row over the three per-task reports rather than a protocol-wide population metric.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.5 Evaluation Protocol\nTerminology for held-out evaluation. Throughout this paper, held-out evaluation refers to evaluation on the eight held-out ScBank datasets listed above, not to a benchmark-wide tissue holdout or a donor-level closure.\nThe cell-type label space is shared between training and held-out datasets, as is standard in cell-type generalization studies.\nWe reserve the term zero-shot for baselines (e.g., LangCell) whose encoder was trained without any task-specific labels. In contrast, RVQ-Alpha is supervised and RL-trained, and its held-out numbers measure dataset-level generalization rather than label-space novelty.\nSplit and leakage control. We audit data separation at three levels (Table 7).\nRVQ representation.\nAll nine held-out dataset IDs (the eight evaluation datasets plus D097) are absent from the RVQ pretraining set; after fitting, evaluation cells are encoded through the frozen tokenizer without updating RVQ parameters.\nSupervised training.\nTraining, validation, and test partitions are produced by independent construction procedures, and population samples are grouped by a per-sample processing identifier on all homogeneous tasks and on the heterogeneous cell-type task.\nEvaluation scope.\nWe claim held-out-dataset evaluation and a clean RVQ representation boundary, but not strict donorlevel separation across the whole benchmark, a leave-one-tissue-out protocol, or a fully verified donor-level out-of-distribution benchmark: the released benchmark does not independently establish those stronger properties.\nTable 7: Split and leakage control. Nine held-out dataset IDs (eight evaluation files plus D097) define the representation-boundary scope; all main-text evaluation uses the eight-file subset only.\n\nRVQ represen- tation, Mechanism = Held-out IDs excluded from the RVQ pretraining set; frozen tokenizer at evaluation time.. RVQ represen- tation, Verified = ID-level membership check.. RVQ represen- tation, Scope limitation = Upstream donor closure of RVQ training data is not verified.. Supervised training, Mechanism = Independent construction procedures for train/- val/test partitions; per- sample processing iden- tifier groups population samples.. Supervised training, Verified = Dataset-level separa- tion.. Supervised training, Scope limitation = No unified donor-level split definition across all tasks.. Evaluation pool, Mechanism = Eight held-out datasets (11,914 samples).. Evaluation pool, Verified = Dataset-level holdout.. Evaluation pool, Scope limitation = Does not establish leave- one-tissue-out or donor- level OOD.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nReproducibility. We release the benchmark-generation code, the sampled evaluation sets, and the reference configurations.\nTask-level pools can be regenerated from this code, although a self-contained benchmark distribution (with frozen donor lists and split definitions) is not included in this release and is deferred to future work.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.6 Baselines\nRVQ-Alpha emits free-form natural-language cell-type labels end-to-end, graded by an LLM judge (§4.5). The primary numerical comparison is established through a two-layer rule. Protocol compatibility requires that a candidate produce a categorical label through its native, published inference protocol so that the same LLM judge can grade it without constructing a new benchmark bridge. Capacity fairness in the primary comparison additionally restricts that set to systems whose parameter scale is close enough to our 4B backbone for an accuracy gap to be interpretable as a method gap rather than a capacity gap. Three baselines satisfy both layers:\n1. LangCell [24]: a Geneformer + PubMedBERT dual-encoder that performs zero-shot cell-type annotation via nearest-prototype scoring over cell-type names.\n2. Cell-o1 [30]: a Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct model trained with SFT and GRPO that emits natural-language labels with reasoning chains.\n3. C2S-Scale-Gemma2-2B [20]: a Gemma-2-2B model fine-tuned on cell sentences that emits a label string under the same text-generation protocol.\nOther candidates anticipated by the single-cell community fall outside the primary comparison for diverse reasons rather than a single criterion. scGPT [5] and Geneformer [6] require an external protocol bridge before our judge can grade them; CellFM [7] requires task-specific fine-tuning for cell-type annotation; and C2S-Scale-Gemma2-27B [20] is protocol-compatible but, at 6.8 × the size of our backbone, falls outside the capacity-fairness layer and is therefore reported separately in §5.2 as a capacity-scaled reference rather than as a primary comparison point. App. A.6 documents the per-system rationale.\n\n4 Experimental Setup\n4.7 LCAD Computation\nComputing LCAD (Eq. 24) requires mapping free-text predictions to Cell Ontology identifiers. We use the deterministic five-tier cascade summarized in §4.5; the only LLM-derived signal enters at Tier 4 and is bounded by a static lookup table whose maximum value is 6.0, so any reported LCAD > 6 originates from the ontology graph rather than the fallback. Across the eight evaluation datasets, the ontology graph (Tiers 1-3) resolves 49.6% of LCAD computations, with per-dataset coverage ranging from 22.6% on the lowest-coverage corpus to 84.1% on the highest, and leaves no unresolved predictions. The per-dataset breakdown and the full cascade specification appear in App. A.7.\n\n5 Results\n5.1 Single-Cell Level Tasks\nTable 8 reports cell type annotation accuracy across training stages. Feature-Injection SFT establishes a strong baseline, and RLVR training yields consistent improvements across all categories.\nOOD generalization. The 12.1 percentage point improvement on OOD immune cells (70.7% → 82.8%, Table 8) demonstrates that RLVR training enhances out-of-distribution generalization. We attribute this to the fact verification reward, which forces the model to ground its predictions in actual expression patterns rather than memorizing RVQ-to-label mappings.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nTable 8: Single-cell annotation accuracy (%) across training stages (Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507 base, pass@8). OOD = out-of-distribution tissues/cell types not seen during training. Both Post-SFT and Post-RLVR use the same pass@8 protocol, so the ∆ column isolates the contribution of RLVR.\n\nIn-distribution (common), Post-SFT = 85.3. In-distribution (common), Post-RLVR = 89.1. In-distribution (common), ∆ = +3.8. OODimmune datasets, Post-SFT = 70.7. OODimmune datasets, Post-RLVR = 82.8. OODimmune datasets, ∆ = +12.1. Rare cell types (neuroblastoma), Post-SFT = 36.1. Rare cell types (neuroblastoma), Post-RLVR = 65.0. Rare cell types (neuroblastoma), ∆ = +28.9. Average across 8 tissues, Post-SFT = 72.4. Average across 8 tissues, Post-RLVR = 83.6. Average across 8 tissues, ∆ = +11.2\nControlled comparison and per-dataset losses. Aggregate accuracy obscures per-dataset variance. Across the eight evaluation datasets in Figure 5, RVQ-Alpha achieves the highest accuracy on four (A013, D095, D096, D102) but underperforms on the other four: Cell-o1 outperforms RVQ-Alpha on D094 (37.4%), and C2S-Gemma2-2B leads on D099, D101, and D103 (with the accuracy on D103 reaching 96.0%, outperforming RVQ-Alpha by a wide margin). We note a potential issue of training-data overlap for C2S-Gemma2-2B on D103, as the training corpus of this model is not fully documented [20] (see §7). In terms of LCAD (Figure 6), C2S-Gemma2-2B is closer to the ground truth on D096, D099, D102, and D103 (where its LCAD is 0.39). We report these negative results transparently to avoid selection bias, and we discuss the corresponding limitations in §7.\n\n5 Results\n5.1 Single-Cell Level Tasks\nRare cell type recognition. On rare neuroblastoma samples, accuracy nearly doubles from 36.1% to 65.0% (+28.9 pp, Table 8). LCAD analysis (Eq. 24) confirms that even incorrect predictions after RLVR are biologically closer to the ground truth: on the neuroblastoma subset, the mean LCAD drops from 5.2 (post-SFT) to 2.8 (post-RLVR), indicating that the model has learned meaningful hierarchical structure from the Cell Ontology.\n\n5 Results\n5.2 Cross-System Comparison and Methodological Scope\nPrimary comparison set. Figures 5-6 compare RVQ-Alpha against the three baselines selected by the two-stage criteria of §4.6 on the full 8-dataset OOD split: LangCell [24], Cell-o1 [30], and C2S-Gemma22B [20]. Each model generates free-form natural-language cell-type labels end-to-end (satisfying the protocol-compatibility criterion) and operates at a parameter scale comparable to our 4B backbone, ensuring a fair method-level comparison (satisfying the capacity-fairness criterion). RVQ-Alpha outperforms baselines on four of the eight datasets but underperforms on the remaining four (Table 16, App. A.8); we report all per-dataset numbers transparently.\nCellTok: the closest contemporary system. CellTok [31] is the most relevant missing comparison: both RVQ-Alpha and CellTok employ a design combining a discrete tokenizer and a Qwen-family LLM, and both operate end-to-end on cell-type labels. We do not include a numerical CellTok comparison because the official CellTok model checkpoints are unavailable at the time of writing. The CellTok preprint was posted in October 2025; the project repository 4 indicates that the model checkpoints and datasets are forthcoming. Three architectural differences between RVQ-Alpha and CellTok are documented in §3.2 (i.e., residual quantization across eight codebooks versus a single shared codebook, 10 versus 34 tokens per cell, and RLVR with fact verification versus SFT only); these distinctions do not depend on running CellTok. We will incorporate a direct comparison with CellTok once the weights become publicly available.\n[4 https://github.com/sunnsset/CellTok (accessed April 2026).](https://github.com/sunnsset/CellTok)\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n\n5 Results\n5.2 Cross-System Comparison and Methodological Scope\nFigure 5: Per-dataset cell type accuracy across models. Bar-chart comparison of RVQ-Alpha (built on a Qwen3-4B backbone with the chosen ( Nc = 8, C = 32 ) RVQ tokenizer and GSPO reinforcement learning) against LangCell, Cell-o1, and C2S-Gemma2-2B on 8 evaluation datasets. Sampling budget: RVQ-Alpha uses pass@8 (8 stochastic decodings, best-of-8 selected by LLM judge); baselines use pass@1 (single-sample predictions from released checkpoints). This asymmetry favors RVQ-Alpha; see §5.2 for discussion. RVQ-Alpha wins on A013 (49.4%), D095 (80.9%), D096 (54.4%), and D102 (47.8%); Cell-o1 wins on D094 (37.4%); C2S-Gemma2-2B wins on D099 (64.3%), D101 (34.7%), and D103 (96.0%; see §7 for a training-data overlap caveat). LangCell is the weakest model on every dataset, peaking at 27.2% on D096. Baseline selection criteria are described in §4.6.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a grouped bar chart comparing model accuracy across datasets.\n\nVisible content:\n- Single panel bar plot.\n- X-axis label: `Datasets`.\n- Y-axis label: `Accuracy (%)`.\n- Dataset labels on x-axis: `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D099`, `D101`, `D102`, `D103`.\n- Legend at top with four model names:\n  - `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC-8_CS-32-GSPO`\n  - `LangCell`\n  - `Cell-O1`\n  - `C2S-Gemma2-2B`\n- Bars are color-coded by model.\n- Red star markers appear above the highest-performing bar for each dataset.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- No biological objects, microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecular structures, or experimental specimens are visually shown.\n- The figure appears to summarize biological or cell-related benchmark results only through dataset/model names, but the plot itself contains no visible biological source imagery.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No workflow diagram, model architecture, embeddings, attention maps, or transformation pipeline is shown.\n- The only model-related information is comparative performance by named models.\n\n5 Results\n5.2 Cross-System Comparison and Methodological Scope\nFindings visible from the chart:\n- `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC-8_CS-32-GSPO` is highest on several datasets, including `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D101`, and `D102`.\n- `C2S-Gemma2-2B` is highest on `D099` and `D103`.\n- Approximate highest values shown include about `85.0` for `D095` with Qwen, `64.3` for `D099` with C2S-Gemma2-2B, and `96.8` for `D103` with C2S-Gemma2-2B.\n- Accuracy varies substantially across datasets and models, with some models near zero or low single digits on certain datasets.\nBaselines outside the primary comparison set. The remaining baseline candidates fail to meet at least one of the two criteria in §4.6 for distinct reasons; we discuss them separately. Protocol mismatch. scGPT [5] and Geneformer [6] produce cell embeddings rather than labels; evaluating them under our protocol requires attaching a nearest-prototype classification head with an external text encoder, which introduces confounding factors absent from the native-protocol baselines. We include LangCell, which natively pairs a Geneformer cell encoder with a PubMedBERT text encoder, as the representative of this encoder-only family. Task adaptation. CellFM [7] requires fine-tuning its 80M variant on target labels for cell-type annotation. Capacity mismatch. C2S-Scale-Gemma2-27B [20] satisfies the protocol criterion, as it produces labels under the same end-to-end protocol as C2S-Gemma2-2B. However, with 27B parameters, it is 6.8 × larger than the 4.0B backbone of RVQ-Alpha, placing it outside the capacity-fairness criterion of the primary comparison. We already evaluate the parameter-matched counterpart, C2S-Gemma2-2B. For reference, C2S-Scale-Gemma2-27B achieves 95.43% accuracy on multi-tissue immune cell-type annotation [20]; this result should be interpreted in the context of the parameter difference.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n5 Results\n5.2 Cross-System Comparison and Methodological Scope\nFigure 6: Per-dataset Lowest Common Ancestor Distance (LCAD; Eq. 24) across models. Lower is better: LCAD measures the unweighted edge distance between predicted and ground-truth cell types along is-a relations in the Cell Ontology DAG [53, 10]. Sampling budgets are identical to Figure 5: RVQ-Alpha at pass@8, baselines at pass@1. RVQ-Alpha achieves the lowest mean LCAD on A013 (2.40) and D095 (2.22); C2S-Gemma2-2B leads on four datasets (D096, D099, D102, and notably D103 with LCAD = 0.39). LangCell's bar heights span 2.79-7.53 across datasets, consistent with the cross-lineage nature of its errors. See Table 15 for the per-dataset ontology-vs-fallback resolution breakdown.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a grouped bar chart comparing `LCAD Mean` across datasets labeled `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D099`, `D101`, `D102`, and `D103`.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: `Datasets`\n- Y-axis: `LCAD Mean`\n- Legend/model labels:\n  - `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC=8_CS=32-GSPO`\n  - `LangCell`\n  - `Cell-O1`\n  - `C2S-Gemma2-2B`\n- Each dataset has four colored bars corresponding to the four methods/models.\n- Red star markers appear above some bars, indicating highlighted or selected values, but the figure does not define their meaning.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, cells, tissues, model architecture diagrams, or transformation workflows are visible. The figure appears to report quantitative model performance or metric comparison across datasets.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- `LangCell` has the highest bars for several datasets, including `A013`, `D095`, `D096`, `D102`, and `D103`.\n- `Cell-O1` is highest on `D099`.\n- `C2S-Gemma2-2B` is highest on `D094` and `D101`.\n- The chart compares model-level `LCAD Mean` values rather than showing raw biological imagery.\n\n5 Results\n5.3 Population-Level Tasks\nTable 9 summarizes population-level task accuracy across training stages.\nUnder the task-specific offline evaluation protocol (§4.5), the Joint (all correct) row is a local summary of the three per-task reports rather than a single cross-task metric. The 4.6 × per-task improvement on cell type classification (16.8% → 78.0%) is our strongest per-task result, demonstrating that RLVR is particularly effective for multi-cell reasoning tasks that require integrating evidence across many RVQ token sequences within a single response. Furthermore, Figure 7 shows that inference-time scaling (pass@ k ) yields monotonic accuracy gains across all three population tasks. We compute pass@ k based on per-sample correctness, where a sample is considered correct if its mean per-cell score exceeds the task threshold. Disease state prediction benefits the most (an increase of 12.8 pp from k = 1 to k = 16). The performance gap between tasks narrows at higher values of k , suggesting that inference-time scaling is particularly effective for challenging tasks where the model has learned the correct reasoning patterns but applies them inconsistently. Per-cell-type accuracy distributions across all three tasks are detailed in Appendix A.2.\nPer-cell reasoning. For heterogeneous populations, the reward function decomposes into per-cell reasoning judgments, which are aggregated under a tolerance threshold for missing or underdeveloped RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\nTable 9: Population-level task accuracy (%) (Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507 base; §4). Population samples contain 3-48 cells each, requiring multi-cell reasoning. Per-task rows use the task-specific offline evaluation protocol described in §4.5 (cell-type and tissue: τ = 0.75; disease-state: τ = 0.85). The Joint (all correct) row is a locally defined summary over the three per-task reports, not a protocol-wide joint tissue + cell-type metric.\n\nCell Type Classification, Post-SFT = 16.8. Cell Type Classification, Post-RLVR = 78.0. Cell Type Classification, Improvement = 4.6 ×. Disease State (COVID-19), Post-SFT = 33.1. Disease State (COVID-19), Post-RLVR = 66.2. Disease State (COVID-19), Improvement = 2.0 ×. Tissue Type Identification, Post-SFT = 42.5. Tissue Type Identification, Post-RLVR = 71.3. Tissue Type Identification, Improvement = 1.7 ×. Joint (all correct), Post-SFT = 8.2. Joint (all correct), Post-RLVR = 52.1. Joint (all correct), Improvement = 6.4 ×\n\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\n5 Results\n5.3 Population-Level Tasks\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel line chart showing LLM judge accuracy versus number of generations `k`.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: `Number of Generations (k)` with values `1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16`.\n- Y-axis: `LLM Judge Accuracy`, scaled from about `60%` to `95%`.\n- Legend at top with three series:\n  - `Cell Type` in blue\n  - `Disease State` in red\n  - `Tissue Type` in teal\n\nFindings shown:\n- Accuracy increases as the number of generations increases for all three categories.\n- `Cell Type` starts around `84%` at `k=1` and reaches about `88%` at `k=16`.\n- `Disease State` starts around `68%` and rises to about `81%`.\n- `Tissue Type` starts around `79%` and reaches about `90%`.\n- At `k=16`, highlighted diamond markers indicate final values, with `Tissue Type` highest, `Cell Type` slightly lower, and `Disease State` lowest.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- The plotted categories refer to biological/biomedical metadata labels: cell type, disease state, and tissue type.\n- No actual biological images, cells, tissue microscopy, or molecular structures are visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to summarize an LLM judging or classification/evaluation process over multiple generated outputs.\n- No model architecture, interface diagram, or data transformation pipeline is shown.\n\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\nFigure 7: Pass@ k accuracy across population-level tasks. LLM judge accuracy improves with the number of sampled generations k . Disease state (red) benefits most (+12.8 pp), and the inter-task gap narrows at higher k . Final ranking at k = 16: Tissue Type (90.0%) > Cell Type (88.0%) > Disease State (80.8%).\ncells (Appendix A.4). This mechanism forces the model to produce genuine multi-cell analyses rather than resorting to pattern-matching shortcuts that exploit a single salient cell.\n\n5 Results\n5.4 Ablation Studies\nTable 10 summarizes the contribution of each RVQ-Alpha component. We isolate one component at a time, retrain Stage 3, and re-evaluate the model on the held-out single-cell and OOD splits. The Evidence-First constraint and Fact Verification module contribute the largest gains, while the iterated reward-hacking defenses prevent degenerate output behaviors that would otherwise dominate training.\nEffect of Evidence-First constraint. Removing the Evidence-First requirement (allowing direct label prediction without gene evidence) reduces single-cell accuracy by 8.3 pp on average and increases RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning Notes (qualitative failure modes). (i) Without the specificity-penalty gate , the model collapses to enumerating many gene markers without committing to a cell-type call. (ii) Without the prompt-imitation detector , 12% of generations contain near-verbatim system-prompt fragments; enabling the detector reduces this to < 1%. Both behaviours are eliminated by the dual hard gates A ′ and A ′′ (Appendix A.4).\nTable 10: Ablation of RVQ-Alpha components. Each row removes one component from the full system; ∆ is reported relative to the full reference. Single-cell Acc is the mean accuracy across the eight single-cell datasets; OOD Acc is accuracy on out-of-distribution immune datasets; Hallu. Rate is the LLM-judge-detected rate of unsupported biological claims; LCAD > 5 Rate is the fraction of predictions whose LCAD distance (Eq. 24) exceeds 5 is-a edges. Two further qualitative failure modes that motivated hard gates A ′ and A ′′ are reported in the Notes below; full reward-design history is in Appendix A.4.\n\nFull RVQ-Alpha (reference), Single-cell Acc = 83.6%. Full RVQ-Alpha (reference), OODAcc = 82.8%. Full RVQ-Alpha (reference), Hallu. Rate = 4.2%. Full RVQ-Alpha (reference), LCAD > 5 Rate = 1.00 ×. - Evidence-First constraint, Single-cell Acc = - 8.3pp. - Evidence-First constraint, OODAcc = -. - Evidence-First constraint, Hallu. Rate = 23.7%. - Evidence-First constraint, LCAD > 5 Rate = -. - Fact Verification, Single-cell Acc = -. - Fact Verification, OODAcc = - 6.5pp. - Fact Verification, Hallu. Rate = -. - Fact Verification, LCAD > 5 Rate = 1.41 ×\nthe hallucination rate from 4.2% to 23.7% (Table 10, row 2). This confirms that explicit gene-level grounding is essential for the LLM-native tokenization scheme.\nEffect of Fact Verification. Disabling the Fact Verification module while keeping other reward components reduces OOD accuracy by 6.5pp and increases biologically implausible predictions (LCAD > 5 edges; Eq. 24) by 41% (Table 10, row 3). This demonstrates that expression-grounded verification is the primary driver for generalization to unseen distributions.\n\n5 Results\n5.4 Ablation Studies\nEffect of defense mechanisms. The marker-stuffing defense resolves a reward-hacking pattern where the model over-specifies predictions with excessive gene markers; enabling it restores answer quality while maintaining a stable reward signal (Table 10, Notes (i)). The template-regurgitation detector reduces system-prompt copying from 12% to < 1% of responses (Table 10, Notes (ii)). Both defenses are implemented through the dual hard gates A ′ and A ′′ described in Appendix A.4.\n\n6 Analysis\n6.1 RVQ Compression Quality\nTable 11 compares six configurations of the RVQ tokenizer on held-out cells, parameterized by the codebook count and the codebook size, denoted throughout this section as ( Nc , C ) . The chosen configuration, ( 8, 32 ) , achieves the best score on 7 of the 9 metrics: it minimizes MSE (0.0738) and MMD(0.0298), maximizes per-cell Pearson correlation (0.704) and ACS (0.708), and yields the highest scores across all three clustering metrics (ARI 0.247, AMI 0.557, NMI 0.589). It remains competitive on the other two metrics, ranking second in codebook usage (52.31%, trailing ( 32, 32 ) at 55.18%) and third in per-gene Pearson correlation (0.116).\nTwo configurations illustrate the failure modes avoided by ( 8, 32 ) . Wide-and-shallow codebooks ( 16, 512 ) underutilize capacity: only 9.81% of the 8,192 entries are actively used, leaving over 90% dormant. Narrow-and-deep sequences ( 128, 8 ) , conversely, suffer uniform degradation across all reconstruction metrics (PCC c drops to 0.652, ACS drops to 0.661, MMD rises to 0.0388) without providing any clustering benefits. Thus, ( 8, 32 ) combines optimal reconstruction and clustering quality with a minimal footprint on the LLM side (only 256 added vocabulary entries and 8 tokens per cell), making it the standard configuration for the remainder of RVQ-Alpha.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n\n6 Analysis 6.1 RVQ Compression Quality Table 11: RVQtokenizer configuration ablation. Evaluation of six ( Nc , C ) variants on held-out cells, where Nc is the codebook count and C is the codebook size. Usage indicates the percentage of actively used codebook entries; PCCc/PCCg denote per-cell/per-gene Pearson correlation; ACS is the average cosine similarity; MMD is the maximum mean discrepancy; ARI/AMI/NMI are clustering metrics based on held-out cell-type labels. Bold text marks the best score per column; ⋆ marks the configuration adopted by RVQ-Alpha, which attains the best score on 7 of the 9 metrics. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, Usage% = 52.31. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, MSE ↓ = 0.0738. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, PCC c ↑ = 0.704. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, PCC g ↑ = 0.116. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, ACS ↑ = 0.708. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, MMD ↓ = 0.0298. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, ARI ↑ = 0.247. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, AMI ↑ = 0.557. ( 8, 32 ) ⋆, NMI ↑ = 0.589. ( 8, 256 ), Usage% = 24.02. ( 8, 256 ), MSE ↓ = 0.0775. ( 8, 256 ), PCC c ↑ = 0.690. ( 8, 256 ), PCC g ↑ = 0.113. ( 8, 256 ), ACS ↑ = 0.692. ( 8, 256 ), MMD ↓ = 0.0332. ( 8, 256 ), ARI ↑ = 0.245. ( 8, 256 ), AMI ↑ = 0.552. ( 8, 256 ), NMI ↑ = 0.584. ( 16, 64 ), Usage% = 43.46. ( 16, 64 ), MSE ↓ = 0.0771. ( 16, 64 ), PCC c ↑ = 0.694. ( 16, 64 ), PCC g ↑ = 0.115. ( 16, 64 ), ACS ↑ = 0.698. ( 16, 64 ), MMD ↓ = 0.0327. ( 16, 64 ), ARI ↑ = 0.220. ( 16, 64 ), AMI ↑ = 0.543. ( 16, 64 ), NMI ↑ = 0.577. ( 16, 512 ), Usage% = 9.81. ( 16, 512 ), MSE ↓ = 0.0796. ( 16, 512 ), PCC c ↑ = 0.697. ( 16, 512 ), PCC g ↑ = 0.117. ( 16, 512 ), ACS ↑ = 0.702. ( 16, 512 ), MMD ↓ = 0.0368. ( 16, 512 ), ARI ↑ = 0.217. ( 16, 512 ), AMI ↑ = 0.521. ( 16, 512 ), NMI ↑ = 0.556. ( 32, 32 ), Usage% = 55.18. ( 32, 32 ), MSE ↓ = 0.0759. ( 32, 32 ), PCC c ↑ = 0.695. ( 32, 32 ), PCC g ↑ = 0.118. ( 32, 32 ), ACS ↑ = 0.698.\n\n( 32, 32 ), MMD ↓ = 0.0309. (\n\n6 Analysis\n6.1 RVQ Compression Quality\n 32, 32 ), ARI ↑ = 0.214. ( 32, 32 ), AMI ↑ = 0.541. ( 32, 32 ), NMI ↑ = 0.575. ( 128, 8 ), Usage% = 50.39. ( 128, 8 ), MSE ↓ = 0.0801. ( 128, 8 ), PCC c ↑ = 0.652. ( 128, 8 ), PCC g ↑ = 0.105. ( 128, 8 ), ACS ↑ = 0.661. ( 128, 8 ), MMD ↓ = 0.0388. ( 128, 8 ), ARI ↑ = 0.220. ( 128, 8 ), AMI ↑ = 0.535. ( 128, 8 ), NMI ↑ = 0.569\n\n6 Analysis\n6.2 Semantic Understanding of RVQ Tokens\nEvidence for the hierarchy hypothesis. Section 3.2 hypothesizes that the variance-ordered decomposition produced by residual quantization exhibits a coarse-to-fine correspondence with the hierarchical organization of cellular identity. Figure 8 supports this hypothesis: a directed-graph analysis over 100K class-balanced cells shows that earlier codebooks (CB-0-CB-2) predominantly partition cells at broader identity levels (major cell groups, representative subtypes, shared cell states), whereas later codebooks (CB-3-CB-4) refine within-lineage variation into functional programs and pathway markers. We note that this serves as evidence rather than formal proof; the decomposition allows, but does not guarantee, such an alignment, as residual codebooks are trained symmetrically.\nSemantic structure of predictions. To assess whether the LLM develops a biological understanding of RVQtokens rather than merely memorizing mappings, we examine the semantic confusion structure of its predictions alongside the corresponding embedding-space organization in Appendix A.1 (Figures 10 and 11). The confusion matrix shows that most off-diagonal mass concentrates between ontologically adjacent cell types rather than across unrelated lineages, confirming that RVQ tokenization preserves the local semantic structure of gene expression. The companion t-SNE visualization shows that biologically related cell types cluster together in the RVQ embedding space of the LLM. Neuronal cells form a distinct cluster separated from immune and epithelial lineages, while immune subtypes (T cells, NK, Myeloid) exhibit substantial overlap, which is consistent with their shared ontological proximity. Based on these complementary views, we hypothesize three properties regarding the underlying interaction between RVQ and reasoning: (1) attention over RVQ positions tracks biologically relevant gene modules; (2) attention shifts across codebook positions depending on the biological property the model evaluates (lineage versus functional state); and (3) codebook combinations unseen during training continue to yield biologically coherent predictions. We state these explicitly as hypotheses, leaving direct evidence from attention patterns to future work. Companion confusion matrices for tissue type and disease state, along with an alluvial diagram of misclassification flows, are provided in the same appendix (Figures 12-14).\n\n6 Analysis\n6.3 Reward System Dynamics\nThe piecewise gated reward (Eq. 19) yields stable training dynamics, with the positive path ( s + ans > 0) and the negative path remaining well-separated throughout training. The two hard gates, A ′ and A ′′ , eliminate three reward-hacking modes that emerged during training (marker stuffing, template regurgitation, and multi-pattern over-specification), which are documented in Table 10.\nFigure 9a illustrates the joint progression of response length and accuracy during RLVR training.\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (w\n=== END ===\n\n=== NODE PATH:  (TaxonomyCodedDocument) ===\nInstance identifiers to fill (1 instances):\n[\n  {\n    \"title\": \"RVQ-Alpha\"\n  }\n]\n\n=== SCHEMA (fill all fields per instance) ===\n{\n  \"$defs\": {\n    \"TaxonomyCodedRoute\": {\n      \"description\": \"One route coded against taxonomy v1 while retaining lossless evidence.\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"route_label\": {\n          \"title\": \"Route Label\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"model_name\": {\n          \"title\": \"Model Name\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"lifecycle_phase\": {\n          \"enum\": [\n            \"pretraining\",\n            \"fine_tuning\",\n            \"inference\",\n            \"evaluation\",\n            \"unclear\"\n          ],\n          \"title\": \"Lifecycle Phase\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"task_or_configuration_verbatim\": {\n          \"title\": \"Task Or Configuration Verbatim\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"source_object_verbatim\": {\n          \"title\": \"Source Object Verbatim\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"source_object_normalized\": {\n          \"title\": \"Source Object Normalized\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"source_modality_normalized\": {\n          \"title\": \"Source Modality Normalized\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"transformation_chain_verbatim\": {\n          \"items\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"title\": \"Transformation Chain Verbatim\",\n          \"type\": \"array\"\n        },\n        \"transformation_chain_normalized\": {\n          \"items\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"title\": \"Transformation Chain Normalized\",\n          \"type\": \"array\"\n        },\n        