./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/debug/trace_data.json:62:            "text_content": "(Appendix A.1) to select 1,000,000 cells from 7,331 donors, spanning 783 cell types, 347 tissue types, and 128 disease conditions (Appendix B).\n\nFor each cell, we generate structured text descriptions by combining metadata annotations with functional context. Cell type information is enriched using OBO Cell Ontology (Smith et al., 2007) definitions, while biological processes are captured through pathway activity analysis with pySCENIC (Aibar et al., 2017) applied to 34 curated MSigDB Hallmark pathways (Liberzon et al., 2011) (Appendix A.2, Figure 8). For each cell, we identify the two most enriched pathways and translate them into human-readable descriptions of active biological processes using the corresponding MSigDB (Liberzon et al., 2011) definitions. An example of such a description can be found in Appendix A.3.\n\nThe resulting descriptions provide interpretable summaries of both cellular identity and functional state, bridging high-dimensional expression profiles with structured biological knowledge. To ensure robust evaluation, we perform donor-level data splitting (80/10/10) to prevent information leakage between training and test sets.\n\n#### 4.1.2 BASELINES\n\nFor our Cell2Text models, we extract classification labels from the generated descriptions using regular expressions to enable fair comparison with traditional classification approaches. We compare our approach against standard supervised learning methods that directly optimize for classification accuracy. All hyperparameters and training details for the baseline models are provided in Appendix D.\n\nDisease, Cell Type, and Tissue Classification . First, we compare against a single linear layer that is trained on top of the corresponding output embedding of the special CLS token from the frozen Geneformer (Geneformer+Head). We also compare against a gradient-boosting model (LightGBM) trained on Geneformer embeddings to perform multi-class classification (LGBM).\n\nPathway Classification . Similarly, we use ... [truncated, total 3586 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/debug/trace_data.json:132:            "text_content": "Figure 8: Overview of the distribution of pathway categories in the dataset.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel bar chart showing counts by category.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: `Categories`\n- Y-axis label: `Count`\n- Bars are sorted in descending order from left to right.\n- Category labels are rotated diagonally and appear to be Hallmark gene set names from MSigDB.\n- The tallest labeled categories include:\n  - `HALLMARK_UV_RESPONSE_DN` with count `302,664`\n  - `HALLMARK_APICAL_JUNCTION` with count `205,134`\n  - `HALLMARK_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION` with count `167,957`\n  - `HALLMARK_ANDROGEN_RESPONSE` with count `151,116`\n  - `HALLMARK_INTERFERON_ALPHA_RESPONSE` with count `147,365`\n  - `HALLMARK_HEDGEHOG_SIGNALING` with count `130,456`\n  - `HALLMARK_CHOLESTEROL_HOMEOSTASIS` with count `122,403`\n  - `HALLMARK_INTERFERON_GAMMA_RESPONSE` with count `115,216`\n  - `HALLMARK_TNFA_SIGNALING_VIA_NFKB` with count `107,099`\n- Additional visible categories include pathways such as `MYC_TARGETS_V2`, `PI3K_AKT_MTOR_SIGNALING`, `MYOGENESIS`, `KRAS_SIGNALING`, `APOPTOSIS`, `EPITHELIAL_MESENCHYMAL_TRANSITION`, `COAGULATION`, `HYPOXIA`, `MITOTIC_SPINDLE`, `HEME_METABOLISM`, `ADIPOGENESIS`, `PEROXISOME`, `OXIDATIVE_PHOSPHORYLATION`, and `KRAS_SIGNALING_DN`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Gene-set/pathway categories, specifically Hallmark biological processes/signatures.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to visualize aggregated count values per Hallmark category.\n- No model architecture, microscopy, molecular structures, or computational workflow interface is shown.\n\nFinding:\n- `HALLMARK_UV_RESPONSE_DN` has the highest count, followed by `HALLMARK_APICAL_JUNCTION` and `HALLMARK_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION`; counts decrease steadily across the remaining Hallmark categories.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/debug/trace_data.json:137:            "text_content": "Figure 8 shows a skewed distribution, with hallmark TNFα via NF κ B (302,664) and hallmark UV response DN (205,134) leading, likely due to inflammation and stress studies, boosted by brain and blood tissue dominance (Figure 6). Pathways like hallmark interferonα response (167,957) and hallmark allograft rejection (151,116) follow, reflecting immune biases from lymphoid samples. Rare pathways like hallmark spermatogenesis (3,109) are underrepresented, possibly due to tissue specificity.\n\n## C CELL TYPE SIMILARITY DISTRIBUTION\n\nThe similarity scores, computed using Personalized PageRank on the Cell Ontology graph, exhibit a characteristic distribution that validates their utility. As shown in Figure 9, the distribution of similarity scores is highly skewed, with the vast majority of cell type pairs having a similarity value close to zero, reflecting the sparse and hierarchical nature of the ontology where most cell types are distantly related.\n\nQuantitatively, the similarity scores range from 0 to 1, with a mean of 0.049 and median of 0.016, confirming the heavy-tailed nature of the distribution. The low median relative to the mean (0.016 vs 0.049) indicates strong right skewness. Notably, 95% of cell type pairs have similarity scores below 0.215, while only the top 1% of pairs achieve similarities above 0.438, demonstrating that truly related cell types are rare and easily distinguished from the majority of unrelated pairs.\n\nThe cumulative distribution function (CDF) in Figure 10 further illustrates this property, with the sharp rise in the curve at low similarity values confirming that a large fraction of pairwise similarities are small. This heavy-tailed nature of the distribution is crucial, as it demonstrates the metric's ability to effectively discriminate between the few closely related cell types and the many unrelated ones, which is essential for our nuanced evaluation of model predictions.\n\nStatistical analysis confirms the heavy-tailed nature of our similar... [truncated, total 2772 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/debug/trace_data.json:192:            "text_content": "RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 11: RVQtokenizer configuration ablation. Evaluation of six ( Nc , C ) variants on held-out cells, where Nc is the codebook count and C is the codebook size. Usage indicates the percentage of actively used codebook entries; PCCc/PCCg denote per-cell/per-gene Pearson correlation; ACS is the average cosine similarity; MMD is the maximum mean discrepancy; ARI/AMI/NMI are clustering metrics based on held-out cell-type labels. Bold text marks the best score per column; ⋆ marks the configuration adopted by RVQ-Alpha, which attains the best score on 7 of the 9 metrics.\n\n| ( N c , C )   |   Usage% |   MSE ↓ |   PCC c ↑ |   PCC g ↑ |   ACS ↑ |   MMD ↓ |   ARI ↑ |   AMI ↑ |   NMI ↑ |\n|---------------|----------|---------|-----------|-----------|---------|---------|---------|---------|---------|\n| ( 8, 32 ) ⋆   |    52.31 |  0.0738 |     0.704 |     0.116 |   0.708 |  0.0298 |   0.247 |   0.557 |   0.589 |\n| ( 8, 256 )    |    24.02 |  0.0775 |     0.690 |     0.113 |   0.692 |  0.0332 |   0.245 |   0.552 |   0.584 |\n| ( 16, 64 )    |    43.46 |  0.0771 |     0.694 |     0.115 |   0.698 |  0.0327 |   0.220 |   0.543 |   0.577 |\n| ( 16, 512 )   |     9.81 |  0.0796 |     0.697 |     0.117 |   0.702 |  0.0368 |   0.217 |   0.521 |   0.556 |\n| ( 32, 32 )    |    55.18 |  0.0759 |     0.695 |     0.118 |   0.698 |  0.0309 |   0.214 |   0.541 |   0.575 |\n| ( 128, 8 )    |    50.39 |  0.0801 |     0.652 |     0.105 |   0.661 |  0.0388 |   0.220 |   0.535 |   0.569 |\n\n### 6.2 Semantic Understanding of RVQ Tokens\n\nEvidence for the hierarchy hypothesis. Section 3.2 hypothesizes that the variance-ordered decomposition produced by residual quantization exhibits a coarse-to-fine correspondence with the hierarchical organization of cellular identity. Figure 8 supports this hypothesis: a directed-graph analysis over 100K class-balanced cells shows t... [truncated, total 4612 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/debug/trace_data.json:1107:                  "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 8",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/debug/dense_provenance.json:1219:      "text": "4 EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS\n4.1 EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n4.1.1 DATASET CONSTRUCTION\nWe construct a large-scale multimodal dataset that pairs single-cell gene expression profiles with natural language descriptions to enable cross-modal learning between genomic data and biological knowledge. The dataset is derived from the CELLxGENE Census (Program et al., 2024), using a principled sampling strategy\n(Appendix A.1) to select 1,000,000 cells from 7,331 donors, spanning 783 cell types, 347 tissue types, and 128 disease conditions (Appendix B).\nFor each cell, we generate structured text descriptions by combining metadata annotations with functional context. Cell type information is enriched using OBO Cell Ontology (Smith et al., 2007) definitions, while biological processes are captured through pathway activity analysis with pySCENIC (Aibar et al., 2017) applied to 34 curated MSigDB Hallmark pathways (Liberzon et al., 2011) (Appendix A.2, Figure 8). For each cell, we identify the two most enriched pathways and translate them into human-readable descriptions of active biological processes using the corresponding MSigDB (Liberzon et al., 2011) definitions. An example of such a description can be found in Appendix A.3.\nThe resulting descriptions provide interpretable summaries of both cellular identity and functional state, bridging high-dimensional expression profiles with structured biological knowledge. To ensure robust evaluation, we perform donor-level data splitting (80/10/10) to prevent information leakage between training and test sets.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/debug/dense_provenance.json:3734:      "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 8: Overview of the distribution of pathway categories in the dataset.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel bar chart showing counts by category.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: `Categories`\n- Y-axis label: `Count`\n- Bars are sorted in descending order from left to right.\n- Category labels are rotated diagonally and appear to be Hallmark gene set names from MSigDB.\n- The tallest labeled categories include:\n  - `HALLMARK_UV_RESPONSE_DN` with count `302,664`\n  - `HALLMARK_APICAL_JUNCTION` with count `205,134`\n  - `HALLMARK_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION` with count `167,957`\n  - `HALLMARK_ANDROGEN_RESPONSE` with count `151,116`\n  - `HALLMARK_INTERFERON_ALPHA_RESPONSE` with count `147,365`\n  - `HALLMARK_HEDGEHOG_SIGNALING` with count `130,456`\n  - `HALLMARK_CHOLESTEROL_HOMEOSTASIS` with count `122,403`\n  - `HALLMARK_INTERFERON_GAMMA_RESPONSE` with count `115,216`\n  - `HALLMARK_TNFA_SIGNALING_VIA_NFKB` with count `107,099`\n- Additional visible categories include pathways such as `MYC_TARGETS_V2`, `PI3K_AKT_MTOR_SIGNALING`, `MYOGENESIS`, `KRAS_SIGNALING`, `APOPTOSIS`, `EPITHELIAL_MESENCHYMAL_TRANSITION`, `COAGULATION`, `HYPOXIA`, `MITOTIC_SPINDLE`, `HEME_METABOLISM`, `ADIPOGENESIS`, `PEROXISOME`, `OXIDATIVE_PHOSPHORYLATION`, and `KRAS_SIGNALING_DN`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Gene-set/pathway categories, specifically Hallmark biological processes/signatures.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to visualize aggregated count values per Hallmark category.\n- No model architecture, microscopy, molecular structures, or computational workflow interface is shown.\n\nFinding:\n- `HALLMARK_UV_RESPONSE_DN` has the highest count, followed by `HALLMARK_APICAL_JUNCTION` and `HALLMARK_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION`; counts decrease steadily across the remaining Hallmark categories.\nFigure 8 shows a skewed distribution, with hallmark TNF\u03b1 via NF \u03ba B (302,664) and hallmark UV response DN (205,134) leading, likely due to inflammation and stress studies, boosted by brain and blood tissue dominance (Figure 6). Pathways like hallmark interferon\u03b1 response (167,957) and hallmark allograft rejection (151,116) follow, reflecting immune biases from lymphoid samples. Rare pathways like hallmark spermatogenesis (3,109) are underrepresented, possibly due to tissue specificity.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.json:1989:      "orig": "We further evaluated the potential of BFT in cell-level embedding representation. As shown in Extended Data Figure 6b, we directly obtained cell embeddings by weighted aggregation of singlecell expression data and gene embeddings. In Figure 2d, we evaluated the heterogeneity of cell embeddings using phenotypic labels and cell type labels respectively, and found that BFT-based embeddings perform the best. This result also reveals an interesting phenomenon: BFT-based embeddings outperform scGPT, a single-cell foundation model pre-trained with large-scale singlecell datasets. This indicates that BFT indeed has the ability to align LLMs with biological knowledge. In Extended Data Figure 8, we used UMAP to compare the PCA embeddings of raw data and BFT-based embeddings. We found that BFT-based embeddings eliminate batch effects across patients and distinguishes clusters of different cell types.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.json:1990:      "text": "We further evaluated the potential of BFT in cell-level embedding representation. As shown in Extended Data Figure 6b, we directly obtained cell embeddings by weighted aggregation of singlecell expression data and gene embeddings. In Figure 2d, we evaluated the heterogeneity of cell embeddings using phenotypic labels and cell type labels respectively, and found that BFT-based embeddings perform the best. This result also reveals an interesting phenomenon: BFT-based embeddings outperform scGPT, a single-cell foundation model pre-trained with large-scale singlecell datasets. This indicates that BFT indeed has the ability to align LLMs with biological knowledge. In Extended Data Figure 8, we used UMAP to compare the PCA embeddings of raw data and BFT-based embeddings. We found that BFT-based embeddings eliminate batch effects across patients and distinguishes clusters of different cell types."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.json:5040:      "orig": "Extended Data Figure 8: UMAP visualization of cell-level embeddings. a : PCA embeddings of the raw data, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively. b : Cell embeddings derived from LLM-BFT, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.json:5041:      "text": "Extended Data Figure 8: UMAP visualization of cell-level embeddings. a : PCA embeddings of the raw data, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively. b : Cell embeddings derived from LLM-BFT, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.dclg:438:    We further evaluated the potential of BFT in cell-level embedding representation. As shown in Extended Data Figure 6b, we directly obtained cell embeddings by weighted aggregation of singlecell expression data and gene embeddings. In Figure 2d, we evaluated the heterogeneity of cell embeddings using phenotypic labels and cell type labels respectively, and found that BFT-based embeddings perform the best. This result also reveals an interesting phenomenon: BFT-based embeddings outperform scGPT, a single-cell foundation model pre-trained with large-scale singlecell datasets. This indicates that BFT indeed has the ability to align LLMs with biological knowledge. In Extended Data Figure 8, we used UMAP to compare the PCA embeddings of raw data and BFT-based embeddings. We found that BFT-based embeddings eliminate batch effects across patients and distinguishes clusters of different cell types.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.dclg:1338:      Extended Data Figure 8: UMAP visualization of cell-level embeddings. a : PCA embeddings of the raw data, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively. b : Cell embeddings derived from LLM-BFT, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.md:132:We further evaluated the potential of BFT in cell-level embedding representation. As shown in Extended Data Figure 6b, we directly obtained cell embeddings by weighted aggregation of singlecell expression data and gene embeddings. In Figure 2d, we evaluated the heterogeneity of cell embeddings using phenotypic labels and cell type labels respectively, and found that BFT-based embeddings perform the best. This result also reveals an interesting phenomenon: BFT-based embeddings outperform scGPT, a single-cell foundation model pre-trained with large-scale singlecell datasets. This indicates that BFT indeed has the ability to align LLMs with biological knowledge. In Extended Data Figure 8, we used UMAP to compare the PCA embeddings of raw data and BFT-based embeddings. We found that BFT-based embeddings eliminate batch effects across patients and distinguishes clusters of different cell types.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003008_6a0122710a41/full_2026_07_06_rec_003008_6a0122710a41_docling_json_20260812_200717/docling/document.md:373:Extended Data Figure 8: UMAP visualization of cell-level embeddings. a : PCA embeddings of the raw data, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively. b : Cell embeddings derived from LLM-BFT, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively.
