./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/debug/dense_provenance.json:8890:      "text": "6 Analysis\n6.2 Semantic Understanding of RVQ Tokens\nEvidence for the hierarchy hypothesis. Section 3.2 hypothesizes that the variance-ordered decomposition produced by residual quantization exhibits a coarse-to-fine correspondence with the hierarchical organization of cellular identity. Figure 8 supports this hypothesis: a directed-graph analysis over 100K class-balanced cells shows that earlier codebooks (CB-0-CB-2) predominantly partition cells at broader identity levels (major cell groups, representative subtypes, shared cell states), whereas later codebooks (CB-3-CB-4) refine within-lineage variation into functional programs and pathway markers. We note that this serves as evidence rather than formal proof; the decomposition allows, but does not guarantee, such an alignment, as residual codebooks are trained symmetrically.\nSemantic structure of predictions. To assess whether the LLM develops a biological understanding of RVQtokens rather than merely memorizing mappings, we examine the semantic confusion structure of its predictions alongside the corresponding embedding-space organization in Appendix A.1 (Figures 10 and 11). The confusion matrix shows that most off-diagonal mass concentrates between ontologically adjacent cell types rather than across unrelated lineages, confirming that RVQ tokenization preserves the local semantic structure of gene expression. The companion t-SNE visualization shows that biologically related cell types cluster together in the RVQ embedding space of the LLM. Neuronal cells form a distinct cluster separated from immune and epithelial lineages, while immune subtypes (T cells, NK, Myeloid) exhibit substantial overlap, which is consistent with their shared ontological proximity. Based on these complementary views, we hypothesize three properties regarding the underlying interaction between RVQ and reasoning: (1) attention over RVQ positions tracks biologically relevant gene modules; (2) attention shifts across codebook positions depending on the biological property the model evaluates (lineage versus functional state); and (3) codebook combinations unseen during training continue to yield biologically coherent predictions. We state these explicitly as hypotheses, leaving direct evidence from attention patterns to future work. Companion confusion matrices for tissue type and disease state, along with an alluvial diagram of misclassification flows, are provided in the same appendix (Figures 12-14).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/debug/dense_provenance.json:9123:      "text": "6 Analysis\n6.3 Reward System Dynamics\nFigure 8: RVQ codebook hierarchy across cell lineages. Directed graph over 100K class-balanced cells encoded by the chosen ( Nc = 8, C = 32 ) RVQ tokenizer, showing how successive codebook levels decompose cell identity from coarse lineage to fine-grained pathway activity. Five columns are labeled: CB-0: Major Cell Group (Immune, Epithelial, Stromal Mesenchymal, Endothelial, Neural Glial, Parenchymal Other), CB-1: Representative Subtype (17 nodes, e.g. T/NK, B/Plasma, Myeloid, Fibroblast, Capillary, Neuron), CB-2: Shared Cell State (Proliferating, Quiescent, Activated Inflammatory, Stressed, Differentiating Transitional, Senescent Damage-associated), CB-3: Functional Program (Cell Cycle, Proteostasis, OXPHOS Metabolism, Immune Inflammatory Response, ECM Adhesion, Secretory Effector), and CB-4: Pathway Activity (IFN/NF\u03ba B, TGF\u03b2 /EMT, WNT/Notch, mTOR/MYC, Hypoxia/P53). The structure transitions from a strict hierarchy (black edges, CB-0 \u2192 CB-1) to a many-to-many combinatorial mapping (blue edges, CB-1 \u2192 CB-2 \u2192 CB-3; orange edges, CB-3 \u2192 CB-4), demonstrating that earlier codebooks capture coarse lineage identity while later codebooks encode cross-cutting cell states, functional programs, and pathway markers.\nResponse length initially rises during exploration, peaks at \u223c 9,500 tokens around step 250, and then contracts to \u223c 7,000 tokens as accuracy continues to improve. This demonstrates an RL-induced compression of the reasoning chains.\nTo validate that the dimensions of reasoning quality improve concurrently with task accuracy, Figure 9b plots all six dimensions against the accuracy of the LLM judge over the training steps. All dimensions exhibit strong positive correlations ( r = 0.60-0.92), confirming that the RLVR reward system drives genuine improvements in reasoning quality alongside task performance.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling_graph/provenance.json:1227:      "text": "4 EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS\n4.1 EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n4.1.1 DATASET CONSTRUCTION\nWe construct a large-scale multimodal dataset that pairs single-cell gene expression profiles with natural language descriptions to enable cross-modal learning between genomic data and biological knowledge. The dataset is derived from the CELLxGENE Census (Program et al., 2024), using a principled sampling strategy\n(Appendix A.1) to select 1,000,000 cells from 7,331 donors, spanning 783 cell types, 347 tissue types, and 128 disease conditions (Appendix B).\nFor each cell, we generate structured text descriptions by combining metadata annotations with functional context. Cell type information is enriched using OBO Cell Ontology (Smith et al., 2007) definitions, while biological processes are captured through pathway activity analysis with pySCENIC (Aibar et al., 2017) applied to 34 curated MSigDB Hallmark pathways (Liberzon et al., 2011) (Appendix A.2, Figure 8). For each cell, we identify the two most enriched pathways and translate them into human-readable descriptions of active biological processes using the corresponding MSigDB (Liberzon et al., 2011) definitions. An example of such a description can be found in Appendix A.3.\nThe resulting descriptions provide interpretable summaries of both cellular identity and functional state, bridging high-dimensional expression profiles with structured biological knowledge. To ensure robust evaluation, we perform donor-level data splitting (80/10/10) to prevent information leakage between training and test sets.",
