./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/docling_graph/graph.html:1820:        "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 8",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/chunks.json:1214:    "text": "4 EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS\n4.1 EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n4.1.1 DATASET CONSTRUCTION\nWe construct a large-scale multimodal dataset that pairs single-cell gene expression profiles with natural language descriptions to enable cross-modal learning between genomic data and biological knowledge. The dataset is derived from the CELLxGENE Census (Program et al., 2024), using a principled sampling strategy\n(Appendix A.1) to select 1,000,000 cells from 7,331 donors, spanning 783 cell types, 347 tissue types, and 128 disease conditions (Appendix B).\nFor each cell, we generate structured text descriptions by combining metadata annotations with functional context. Cell type information is enriched using OBO Cell Ontology (Smith et al., 2007) definitions, while biological processes are captured through pathway activity analysis with pySCENIC (Aibar et al., 2017) applied to 34 curated MSigDB Hallmark pathways (Liberzon et al., 2011) (Appendix A.2, Figure 8). For each cell, we identify the two most enriched pathways and translate them into human-readable descriptions of active biological processes using the corresponding MSigDB (Liberzon et al., 2011) definitions. An example of such a description can be found in Appendix A.3.\nThe resulting descriptions provide interpretable summaries of both cellular identity and functional state, bridging high-dimensional expression profiles with structured biological knowledge. To ensure robust evaluation, we perform donor-level data splitting (80/10/10) to prevent information leakage between training and test sets.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/chunks.json:3729:    "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 8: Overview of the distribution of pathway categories in the dataset.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel bar chart showing counts by category.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: `Categories`\n- Y-axis label: `Count`\n- Bars are sorted in descending order from left to right.\n- Category labels are rotated diagonally and appear to be Hallmark gene set names from MSigDB.\n- The tallest labeled categories include:\n  - `HALLMARK_UV_RESPONSE_DN` with count `302,664`\n  - `HALLMARK_APICAL_JUNCTION` with count `205,134`\n  - `HALLMARK_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION` with count `167,957`\n  - `HALLMARK_ANDROGEN_RESPONSE` with count `151,116`\n  - `HALLMARK_INTERFERON_ALPHA_RESPONSE` with count `147,365`\n  - `HALLMARK_HEDGEHOG_SIGNALING` with count `130,456`\n  - `HALLMARK_CHOLESTEROL_HOMEOSTASIS` with count `122,403`\n  - `HALLMARK_INTERFERON_GAMMA_RESPONSE` with count `115,216`\n  - `HALLMARK_TNFA_SIGNALING_VIA_NFKB` with count `107,099`\n- Additional visible categories include pathways such as `MYC_TARGETS_V2`, `PI3K_AKT_MTOR_SIGNALING`, `MYOGENESIS`, `KRAS_SIGNALING`, `APOPTOSIS`, `EPITHELIAL_MESENCHYMAL_TRANSITION`, `COAGULATION`, `HYPOXIA`, `MITOTIC_SPINDLE`, `HEME_METABOLISM`, `ADIPOGENESIS`, `PEROXISOME`, `OXIDATIVE_PHOSPHORYLATION`, and `KRAS_SIGNALING_DN`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Gene-set/pathway categories, specifically Hallmark biological processes/signatures.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to visualize aggregated count values per Hallmark category.\n- No model architecture, microscopy, molecular structures, or computational workflow interface is shown.\n\nFinding:\n- `HALLMARK_UV_RESPONSE_DN` has the highest count, followed by `HALLMARK_APICAL_JUNCTION` and `HALLMARK_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION`; counts decrease steadily across the remaining Hallmark categories.\nFigure 8 shows a skewed distribution, with hallmark TNFα via NF κ B (302,664) and hallmark UV response DN (205,134) leading, likely due to inflammation and stress studies, boosted by brain and blood tissue dominance (Figure 6). Pathways like hallmark interferonα response (167,957) and hallmark allograft rejection (151,116) follow, reflecting immune biases from lymphoid samples. Rare pathways like hallmark spermatogenesis (3,109) are underrepresented, possibly due to tissue specificity.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/docling_graph/provenance.json:8898:      "text": "6 Analysis\n6.2 Semantic Understanding of RVQ Tokens\nEvidence for the hierarchy hypothesis. Section 3.2 hypothesizes that the variance-ordered decomposition produced by residual quantization exhibits a coarse-to-fine correspondence with the hierarchical organization of cellular identity. Figure 8 supports this hypothesis: a directed-graph analysis over 100K class-balanced cells shows that earlier codebooks (CB-0-CB-2) predominantly partition cells at broader identity levels (major cell groups, representative subtypes, shared cell states), whereas later codebooks (CB-3-CB-4) refine within-lineage variation into functional programs and pathway markers. We note that this serves as evidence rather than formal proof; the decomposition allows, but does not guarantee, such an alignment, as residual codebooks are trained symmetrically.\nSemantic structure of predictions. To assess whether the LLM develops a biological understanding of RVQtokens rather than merely memorizing mappings, we examine the semantic confusion structure of its predictions alongside the corresponding embedding-space organization in Appendix A.1 (Figures 10 and 11). The confusion matrix shows that most off-diagonal mass concentrates between ontologically adjacent cell types rather than across unrelated lineages, confirming that RVQ tokenization preserves the local semantic structure of gene expression. The companion t-SNE visualization shows that biologically related cell types cluster together in the RVQ embedding space of the LLM. Neuronal cells form a distinct cluster separated from immune and epithelial lineages, while immune subtypes (T cells, NK, Myeloid) exhibit substantial overlap, which is consistent with their shared ontological proximity. Based on these complementary views, we hypothesize three properties regarding the underlying interaction between RVQ and reasoning: (1) attention over RVQ positions tracks biologically relevant gene modules; (2) attention shifts across codebook positions depending on the biological property the model evaluates (lineage versus functional state); and (3) codebook combinations unseen during training continue to yield biologically coherent predictions. We state these explicitly as hypotheses, leaving direct evidence from attention patterns to future work. Companion confusion matrices for tissue type and disease state, along with an alluvial diagram of misclassification flows, are provided in the same appendix (Figures 12-14).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/docling_graph/provenance.json:9131:      "text": "6 Analysis\n6.3 Reward System Dynamics\nFigure 8: RVQ codebook hierarchy across cell lineages. Directed graph over 100K class-balanced cells encoded by the chosen ( Nc = 8, C = 32 ) RVQ tokenizer, showing how successive codebook levels decompose cell identity from coarse lineage to fine-grained pathway activity. Five columns are labeled: CB-0: Major Cell Group (Immune, Epithelial, Stromal Mesenchymal, Endothelial, Neural Glial, Parenchymal Other), CB-1: Representative Subtype (17 nodes, e.g. T/NK, B/Plasma, Myeloid, Fibroblast, Capillary, Neuron), CB-2: Shared Cell State (Proliferating, Quiescent, Activated Inflammatory, Stressed, Differentiating Transitional, Senescent Damage-associated), CB-3: Functional Program (Cell Cycle, Proteostasis, OXPHOS Metabolism, Immune Inflammatory Response, ECM Adhesion, Secretory Effector), and CB-4: Pathway Activity (IFN/NFκ B, TGFβ /EMT, WNT/Notch, mTOR/MYC, Hypoxia/P53). The structure transitions from a strict hierarchy (black edges, CB-0 → CB-1) to a many-to-many combinatorial mapping (blue edges, CB-1 → CB-2 → CB-3; orange edges, CB-3 → CB-4), demonstrating that earlier codebooks capture coarse lineage identity while later codebooks encode cross-cutting cell states, functional programs, and pathway markers.\nResponse length initially rises during exploration, peaks at ∼ 9,500 tokens around step 250, and then contracts to ∼ 7,000 tokens as accuracy continues to improve. This demonstrates an RL-induced compression of the reasoning chains.\nTo validate that the dimensions of reasoning quality improve concurrently with task accuracy, Figure 9b plots all six dimensions against the accuracy of the LLM judge over the training steps. All dimensions exhibit strong positive correlations ( r = 0.60-0.92), confirming that the RLVR reward system drives genuine improvements in reasoning quality alongside task performance.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_183921/docling/document.json:48150:      "orig": "Figure 8: C2S allows for conversion from expression information into cell sentence format with minimal information loss. Using a linear model fitted between rank and original expression, cell sentences can be converted back to expression accurately.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_183921/docling/document.json:48151:      "text": "Figure 8: C2S allows for conversion from expression information into cell sentence format with minimal information loss. Using a linear model fitted between rank and original expression, cell sentences can be converted back to expression accurately."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/docling_graph/nodes.csv:27:"TaxonomyCodedRoute_fec1b8ea132bc9bf","TaxonomyCodedRoute","entity","TaxonomyCodedRoute","6.2","RVQ-Alpha","evaluation","directed-graph analysis over 100K class-balanced cells","100K class-balanced cells","class-balanced cells","single-cell transcriptomics","[]","[]","earlier codebooks (CB-0-CB-2)","discrete_biological_symbol_stream","learned_quantized_id_or_codebook_token","the variance-ordered decomposition produced by residual quantization","tokenizer_sequence","semantic_annotation","actual_model_input","earlier codebooks (CB-0-CB-2) predominantly partition cells at broader identity levels","6.2 Semantic Understanding of RVQ Tokens","Figure 8","text_plus_figure","","{""document_id"": ""8347d3e0743ed61166ac3f5bf8bf4c41"", ""match"": ""verbatim"", ""chunks"": [4, 51, 59, 97], ""pages"": [3, 4, 30, 31, 33, 48], ""refs"": [""#/texts/61"", ""#/texts/63"", ""#/texts/64"", ""#/texts/641"", ""#/texts/642"", ""#/texts/643"", ""#/texts/646"", ""#/tables/8""], ""spans"": [{""chunk"": 4, ""start"": 2332, ""end"": 2335}, {""chunk"": 51, ""start"": 2263, ""end"": 2266}, {""chunk"": 59, ""start"": 11, ""end"": 14}, {""chunk"": 97, ""start"": 2233, ""end"": 2236}]}","","","",""
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/docling/chunks.json:8885:    "text": "6 Analysis\n6.2 Semantic Understanding of RVQ Tokens\nEvidence for the hierarchy hypothesis. Section 3.2 hypothesizes that the variance-ordered decomposition produced by residual quantization exhibits a coarse-to-fine correspondence with the hierarchical organization of cellular identity. Figure 8 supports this hypothesis: a directed-graph analysis over 100K class-balanced cells shows that earlier codebooks (CB-0-CB-2) predominantly partition cells at broader identity levels (major cell groups, representative subtypes, shared cell states), whereas later codebooks (CB-3-CB-4) refine within-lineage variation into functional programs and pathway markers. We note that this serves as evidence rather than formal proof; the decomposition allows, but does not guarantee, such an alignment, as residual codebooks are trained symmetrically.\nSemantic structure of predictions. To assess whether the LLM develops a biological understanding of RVQtokens rather than merely memorizing mappings, we examine the semantic confusion structure of its predictions alongside the corresponding embedding-space organization in Appendix A.1 (Figures 10 and 11). The confusion matrix shows that most off-diagonal mass concentrates between ontologically adjacent cell types rather than across unrelated lineages, confirming that RVQ tokenization preserves the local semantic structure of gene expression. The companion t-SNE visualization shows that biologically related cell types cluster together in the RVQ embedding space of the LLM. Neuronal cells form a distinct cluster separated from immune and epithelial lineages, while immune subtypes (T cells, NK, Myeloid) exhibit substantial overlap, which is consistent with their shared ontological proximity. Based on these complementary views, we hypothesize three properties regarding the underlying interaction between RVQ and reasoning: (1) attention over RVQ positions tracks biologically relevant gene modules; (2) attention shifts across codebook positions depending on the biological property the model evaluates (lineage versus functional state); and (3) codebook combinations unseen during training continue to yield biologically coherent predictions. We state these explicitly as hypotheses, leaving direct evidence from attention patterns to future work. Companion confusion matrices for tissue type and disease state, along with an alluvial diagram of misclassification flows, are provided in the same appendix (Figures 12-14).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_04/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121_8f4709456aba/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000121_8f4709456aba_docling_json_20260812_214234/docling/chunks.json:9118:    "text": "6 Analysis\n6.3 Reward System Dynamics\nFigure 8: RVQ codebook hierarchy across cell lineages. Directed graph over 100K class-balanced cells encoded by the chosen ( Nc = 8, C = 32 ) RVQ tokenizer, showing how successive codebook levels decompose cell identity from coarse lineage to fine-grained pathway activity. Five columns are labeled: CB-0: Major Cell Group (Immune, Epithelial, Stromal Mesenchymal, Endothelial, Neural Glial, Parenchymal Other), CB-1: Representative Subtype (17 nodes, e.g. T/NK, B/Plasma, Myeloid, Fibroblast, Capillary, Neuron), CB-2: Shared Cell State (Proliferating, Quiescent, Activated Inflammatory, Stressed, Differentiating Transitional, Senescent Damage-associated), CB-3: Functional Program (Cell Cycle, Proteostasis, OXPHOS Metabolism, Immune Inflammatory Response, ECM Adhesion, Secretory Effector), and CB-4: Pathway Activity (IFN/NFκ B, TGFβ /EMT, WNT/Notch, mTOR/MYC, Hypoxia/P53). The structure transitions from a strict hierarchy (black edges, CB-0 → CB-1) to a many-to-many combinatorial mapping (blue edges, CB-1 → CB-2 → CB-3; orange edges, CB-3 → CB-4), demonstrating that earlier codebooks capture coarse lineage identity while later codebooks encode cross-cutting cell states, functional programs, and pathway markers.\nResponse length initially rises during exploration, peaks at ∼ 9,500 tokens around step 250, and then contracts to ∼ 7,000 tokens as accuracy continues to improve. This demonstrates an RL-induced compression of the reasoning chains.\nTo validate that the dimensions of reasoning quality improve concurrently with task accuracy, Figure 9b plots all six dimensions against the accuracy of the LLM judge over the training steps. All dimensions exhibit strong positive correlations ( r = 0.60-0.92), confirming that the RLVR reward system drives genuine improvements in reasoning quality alongside task performance.\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:6127:          "text": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological machine-learning figure about token/sequence embeddings and taxonomy/functional classification, likely from a genomics or metagenomics model analysis.\n\nVisible panels:\n\nA. Token embedding  \nFit-SNE scatter plot labeled “Token embedding” with axes “Fit-SNE 1” and “Fit-SNE 2.” Points are colored by token type: genus name, gene name, nucleotide sequence. Dashed outlines highlight clusters. Right-side token examples include gene-name tokens such as “ORF9b,” “Gp18,” “PM19L,” gene-function phrases such as “long-chain mannosyl-transferase adenyl-nucleotide,” and nucleotide strings such as “AAAAA,” “AGCAG,” “AGCCG,” “TTGTG,” “TAGTG,” “TCGTG.”\n\nB. Taxonomic token embedding  \nFit-SNE scatter plot with points colored for Bacteria, Virus, and Fungi. Right-side examples list viral taxonomy groups: “Riboviria” with Orbirivus, Coltivirus, Fijivirus; “Duplodnaviria” with Bc431virus, Obolenskvirus, Homburgvirus; and “Monodnaviria” with Deltapapillomavirus, Lambdapapillomavirus, Iotapapillomavirus. Dashed outlines mark separated viral token clusters.\n\nC. Feature representation  \nHeatmap for “OmniNA-1.7B” showing layers EB, T1 through T24 and columns for tasks/features including sequence conservation, taxonomy, bacteria, fungi, SARS-CoV-2, DNA element, base function, virus. Color scale indicates ARI from negative to positive, with stars marking best ARI.\n\nD. Sequence conservation  \nFit-SNE density/contour plot labeled “Sequence conservation,” Layer 2. Two classes are shown: High in orange and Low in blue, with overlapping but differently concentrated contours.\n\nE. Taxonomy classification  \nFit-SNE contour plot labeled “Taxonomy classification,” Layer 3. Three separated biological source groups are shown: Bacteria, Virus, and Fungi.\n\nF. Bacteria and archaea classification  \nFit-SNE scatter plot labeled “Bacteria and archaea classification,” Layer 2. Multiple colored clusters represent bacterial and archaeal classes/groups, though the legend is not shown in this panel.\n\nG. Phylogenetic/taxonomic tree  \nCircular tree with central labels including Archaea and Bacteria. Branch regions are colored and annotated with groups such as Campylobacterota, Thermoproteota, Bacillota, Blue-green algae, Planctobacteria, Mollicutaeota, Euryarchaeota Garrity and Holt, Thermoplasmatota, Nitrososphaerota, and Crenarchaeota.\n\nH. SARS-CoV-2 phylogeny  \nFit-SNE scatter/trajectory plot labeled “SARS-CoV-2 phylogeny,” Layer 8. Shows clade-related colored point distributions and contour-like trajectories. “Wuhan-Hu-1/2019” is labeled in red. Numeric annotations appear along curves, including negative values on the left and positive values on the right.\n\nI. SARS-CoV-2 variants  \nViolin/box-style plot of Pearson correlation for SARS-CoV-2 variant clades. X-axis lists Clade 19, Clade 20, Clade 21, Clade 22, Clade 23; y-axis is Pearson correlation. Significance markers are shown between groups.\n\nJ. Viral functional protein  \nFit-SNE scatter plot labeled “Viral functional protein,” Layer 2. Points are colored by coding sequence/protein class. Legend includes Coat protein, Viral capsid, Matrix protein, Viral RNA polymerase, Nucleocapsid protein, Virulence factor, Envelope protein, Hemagglutinin, Neuraminidase, and Spike protein.\n\nOverall finding shown visually: learned token and sequence representations separate nucleotide, gene, taxonomy, conservation, viral protein function, SARS-CoV-2 clades, and broad microbial taxonomic categories across specific model layers."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:6166:          "text": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological machine-learning figure about token/sequence embeddings and taxonomy/functional classification, likely from a genomics or metagenomics model analysis.\n\nVisible panels:\n\nA. Token embedding  \nFit-SNE scatter plot labeled “Token embedding” with axes “Fit-SNE 1” and “Fit-SNE 2.” Points are colored by token type: genus name, gene name, nucleotide sequence. Dashed outlines highlight clusters. Right-side token examples include gene-name tokens such as “ORF9b,” “Gp18,” “PM19L,” gene-function phrases such as “long-chain mannosyl-transferase adenyl-nucleotide,” and nucleotide strings such as “AAAAA,” “AGCAG,” “AGCCG,” “TTGTG,” “TAGTG,” “TCGTG.”\n\nB. Taxonomic token embedding  \nFit-SNE scatter plot with points colored for Bacteria, Virus, and Fungi. Right-side examples list viral taxonomy groups: “Riboviria” with Orbirivus, Coltivirus, Fijivirus; “Duplodnaviria” with Bc431virus, Obolenskvirus, Homburgvirus; and “Monodnaviria” with Deltapapillomavirus, Lambdapapillomavirus, Iotapapillomavirus. Dashed outlines mark separated viral token clusters.\n\nC. Feature representation  \nHeatmap for “OmniNA-1.7B” showing layers EB, T1 through T24 and columns for tasks/features including sequence conservation, taxonomy, bacteria, fungi, SARS-CoV-2, DNA element, base function, virus. Color scale indicates ARI from negative to positive, with stars marking best ARI.\n\nD. Sequence conservation  \nFit-SNE density/contour plot labeled “Sequence conservation,” Layer 2. Two classes are shown: High in orange and Low in blue, with overlapping but differently concentrated contours.\n\nE. Taxonomy classification  \nFit-SNE contour plot labeled “Taxonomy classification,” Layer 3. Three separated biological source groups are shown: Bacteria, Virus, and Fungi.\n\nF. Bacteria and archaea classification  \nFit-SNE scatter plot labeled “Bacteria and archaea classification,” Layer 2. Multiple colored clusters represent bacterial and archaeal classes/groups, though the legend is not shown in this panel.\n\nG. Phylogenetic/taxonomic tree  \nCircular tree with central labels including Archaea and Bacteria. Branch regions are colored and annotated with groups such as Campylobacterota, Thermoproteota, Bacillota, Blue-green algae, Planctobacteria, Mollicutaeota, Euryarchaeota Garrity and Holt, Thermoplasmatota, Nitrososphaerota, and Crenarchaeota.\n\nH. SARS-CoV-2 phylogeny  \nFit-SNE scatter/trajectory plot labeled “SARS-CoV-2 phylogeny,” Layer 8. Shows clade-related colored point distributions and contour-like trajectories. “Wuhan-Hu-1/2019” is labeled in red. Numeric annotations appear along curves, including negative values on the left and positive values on the right.\n\nI. SARS-CoV-2 variants  \nViolin/box-style plot of Pearson correlation for SARS-CoV-2 variant clades. X-axis lists Clade 19, Clade 20, Clade 21, Clade 22, Clade 23; y-axis is Pearson correlation. Significance markers are shown between groups.\n\nJ. Viral functional protein  \nFit-SNE scatter plot labeled “Viral functional protein,” Layer 2. Points are colored by coding sequence/protein class. Legend includes Coat protein, Viral capsid, Matrix protein, Viral RNA polymerase, Nucleocapsid protein, Virulence factor, Envelope protein, Hemagglutinin, Neuraminidase, and Spike protein.\n\nOverall finding shown visually: learned token and sequence representations separate nucleotide, gene, taxonomy, conservation, viral protein function, SARS-CoV-2 clades, and broad microbial taxonomic categories across specific model layers.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad/full_2026_07_06_rec_001838_bb6ae63f17ad_docling_json_20260812_200246/docling/document.md:141:For each cell, we generate structured text descriptions by combining metadata annotations with functional context. Cell type information is enriched using OBO Cell Ontology (Smith et al., 2007) definitions, while biological processes are captured through pathway activity analysis with pySCENIC (Aibar et al., 2017) applied to 34 curated MSigDB Hallmark pathways (Liberzon et al., 2011) (Appendix A.2, Figure 8). For each cell, we identify the two most enriched pathways and translate them into human-readable descriptions of active biological processes using the corresponding MSigDB (Liberzon et al., 2011) definitions. An example of such a description can be found in Appendix A.3.
