data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/13_crops/models/model_e0f70ec41e97_512bd31d3b54/adjudicator/prompt.txt:12:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 | Overview of framework. a . We curated a diverse collection of light microscopy datasets.  The  left  panel  summarizes  dataset  diversity  across  microscopy  types,  cell types,  perturbation  conditions  and  species.  The  right  panel  presents  a  hierarchical organization  of  the  datasets,  grouped  into  pre-training  and  evaluation  sets,  with  the number  of  images  annotated  at  each  level  of  the  hierarchy.  Microscopy  types  are", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with three labeled panels: `a. Dataset characterization`, `b. Model architecture`, and `c. Downstream analysis based on learned image embedding`.\n\nPanel `a` summarizes dataset characterization. A circular chart shows microscopy/data categories including `BF`, `DIC`, `Conf`, `WF`, `SR`, `QPI`, `PC`, `Cell type (100+)`, `Perturbation (300+)`, and species labels including `Human`, `Mouse`, `Yeast`, and `Bacteria`. The center contains example microscopy cell images. A right-side hierarchy organizes data by `Granularity`, `Scale`, `Attribute`, and `Number`, mapping `Single-cell`, `Full-field`, `Subcellular`, `Cell-level`, and `Population` to attributes such as `Protein localization`, `Cell identity/state`, and `Perturbation response`. Counts are shown for `Pre-training` and `Evaluation`, including values such as `72,832`, `2,622,150`, `40,000`, `156,669`, `95,125`, and `117,628`.\n\nPanel `b` shows a model architecture for single-cell image-text learning. Inputs include a generated `Caption` with keywords such as `Species`, `Source`, `Microscopy`, `Cell type`, and `Biological attribute`, plus a `Single-cell image`. The caption is passed through a `Tokenizer` into `Text tokens`. The image is passed through an `Image encoder` into `Image tokens`. A `Q-Former` module connects text/image tokens to image-text alignment, represented by a matrix comparing `Image feature` entries `I1 ... Im` with `Text feature` entries `T1 ... Tn`. A second shared-weight `Q-Former` receives masked image tokens, followed by an `Image decoder`, performing `Mask tokens reconstruction`.\n\nPanel `c` illustrates downstream analysis using learned image embeddings. `Evaluation images` are input into `scMIR`, producing a learned image embedding matrix. The embeddings are used for `Cell classification`, `Morphological inference`, `Cell clustering`, and `Batch correction`, shown as schematic point clusters and transformed groupings.\n\nVisible biological source objects include single-cell microscopy images and schematic cells. The figure describes transformations from microscopy images and captions into tokenized text/image representations, learned embeddings, reconstructed image tokens, and downstream biological analysis outputs."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 | Classification and clustering performance, visualization of scMIR embeddings. a . Radar plot of F1-score performance (scaled ×100) across 17 classification tasks from 13 light microscopy  datasets. scMIR  is compared  with  task-oriented models, CytoImageNet,  EfficientNetB0,  and  Microsnoop. b .  Quantitative  assessment  of unsupervised  cross-dataset  alignment  using  normalized  mutual  information  (NMI), purity score (PS), and adjusted Rand index (ARI) computed from joint embeddings (metrics scaled ×100). c . t-SNE visualization of scMIR embeddings. Left, embeddings colored  by  dataset  identity  demonstrate  compact  dataset-specific  clustering  despite experimental  heterogeneity.  Right,  the  same  embeddings  colored  by  dataset-level biological attributes reveal coherent higher-level biological organization across studies and microscopy modalities. Source data are provided as a Source Data file.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about scMIR performance and embeddings.\n\nPanel a: “Classification performance”\nA radar/spider chart comparing classification performance across multiple microscopy image datasets. Dataset labels around the chart include BBB-C048, BBB-C014, CNMC, Raabin, DIBaS, CoNSeP, LiveCell, FCD, AINU, COOS7-5, COOS7-4, COOS7-3, COOS7-2, COOS7-1, CYCLOPs, CM4AI, and AIRFIHA. Methods shown in the legend are Task-oriented, CytolImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR. The red scMIR curve generally forms the outer envelope, indicating stronger classification performance across most datasets.\n\nPanel b: “Clustering performance”\nA grouped bar chart comparing CytolImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR on clustering metrics NMI, PS, and ARI. scMIR has the highest visible values: NMI 70.4, PS 56.5, and ARI 36.7. Other methods are lower, with approximate values shown above bars, e.g. NMI 63.4, 66.3, 68.4; PS 47.2, 48.7, 52.0; ARI 29.5, 29.4, 33.8.\n\nPanel c: “Visualization of scMIR embeddings”\nTwo t-SNE scatter plots of scMIR embeddings.\n\nLeft plot: “Visualization of dataset”\nPoints are colored by dataset/source. The legend includes AINU, AIRFIHA, BBBC014, BBBC048, CM4AI, CNMC, COOS7, CYCLOPs, CoNSeP, DIBaS, FCD, LIVECell, and Raabin. The plot shows separated and partially overlapping clusters in t-SNE space.\n\nRight plot: “Visualization of dataset attribute”\nThe same embedding space colored by biological or dataset attribute categories: Bacteria, Cancer, Immune, Immune Cancer, Somatic Stem, and Subcellular. An arrow labels a cluster as “CNMC,” and another annotation indicates “CD4/CD8.” The visible finding is that scMIR embeddings group samples by both dataset identity and broader biological/image attributes.\n\nBiological source objects visible or implied by labels include microscopy-derived cell, tissue, and biomedical image datasets, including bacteria, cancer, immune-cell, stem-cell, and subcellular image sources. The main transformation shown is embedding visualization using t-SNE. The model/interface being evaluated is scMIR against baseline image representation models."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Fig. 3 | Perturbation phenotypic profiling of scMIR embeddings. a . Schematic overview of phenotypic inference from perturbation image embeddings. Chemical perturbations", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about image-based profiling of chemical perturbations.\n\nPanel a shows a workflow: a multi-well plate is imaged by microscopy, producing cellular fluorescence images. These images are passed through a “Feature extractor” model/interface to generate “Image embeddings.” Embeddings from wells of chemical perturbation are then compared, with dashed circles indicating wells associated with the same chemical perturbation.\n\nPanel b is titled “Performance comparison on BBBC021” and contains three scatter plots comparing methods by Mean Average Precision (mAP) on the x-axis and Folds of Enrichment on the y-axis. The subplots are:\n- “Treatment level-MoAs”\n- “Well level-Compounds”\n- “Well level-MoAs”\n\nMethods in the legend are CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR. scMIR appears as the red point and has the highest performance in the shown plots.\n\nPanel c is titled “Treatment representations clustering” for BBBC021. It shows a UMAP scatter plot with axes UMAP1 and UMAP2. Points represent control wells, well-level profiles, and treatment-level profiles. Labeled biological/chemical mechanism clusters include Epithelial, Protein synthesis, Actin disruptors, DNA damage, Kinase inhibitors, and Microtubule destabilizers.\n\nPanel d is titled “Heatmap visualization of of AZ-A perturbation representations” for BBBC021. It shows a heatmap with DMSO and multiple dose/replicate-like row labels on the left, and a color scale from about -3 to +3 on the right. The heatmap visualizes learned perturbation representation values or embedding features across conditions."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 9, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single heatmap panel showing many rows of features/samples against labeled columns of biological taxa. The columns include an initial `DNSO` label followed by microbial genus/species-style labels such as `A2 (4)`, `anisomycin`, `desmococcus`, `emetic`, `equitabilis`, `episodica`, `flavumaurantiacum`, `minorygin`, `nocobacta`, `taylor`, and `viridimicis` variants with replicate/group numbers in parentheses.\n\nVisible elements:\n- One rectangular clustered-style heatmap, though no dendrograms are visible.\n- X-axis labels are rotated vertically and densely packed.\n- No visible y-axis labels.\n- A vertical color bar at right ranges approximately from `-4` in blue through `0` white to `4` red.\n- Color encoding appears to represent normalized abundance/expression/intensity values, with red indicating higher values and blue indicating lower values.\n- The heatmap contains block-like patterns of red and blue across groups, suggesting differential signal across taxa/conditions.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Likely microbial taxa or biological sample groups, based on genus/species-like labels.\n- No cells, tissues, organisms, microscopy images, or molecular structures are directly visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data appear transformed into a scaled matrix or z-score-like heatmap due to the centered diverging color scale.\n- No model architecture, workflow diagram, or interface is shown.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- Several column groups show localized enrichment or depletion patterns.\n- Strong red regions are visible near labels including `equitabilis`, `taylor`, and some `nocobacta`/related columns.\n- Strong blue regions appear in multiple blocks, including early `A2/AZ` columns and some mid-to-late taxa groups.\n- Exact quantitative findings cannot be determined beyond the color-scale patterns because row labels and caption are absent."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Fig.  4 | a .  Schematic  illustration  of  hierarchical  feature  aggregation  on  the  Human Protein Atlas  (HPA)  dataset.  Single-cell  image  embeddings  are  first  extracted  from individual microscopy images and then aggregated to obtain higher-level representations at multiple biological scales, including cell line-level, protein localization-level, and protein-level features. b . Similarity matrix of protein localization groups computed from scMIR-derived protein-level features, with expertdefined  hierarchical  organelle  annotations  indicated  (red  and  yellow). c .  Cell-line similarity  matrices  derived  from  bulk  mRNA  expression  (left)  and  morphology-", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure about scMIR embeddings from HPA single-cell images.\n\nPanel a: “Feature aggregation on HPA images”\n- Shows multiple single-cell fluorescence microscopy images as biological source objects.\n- Pipeline diagram: single-cell images pass through a “Feature extractor,” producing feature vectors.\n- Features are combined by “Feature aggregation.”\n- Outputs are three embedding types: “Cell line embeddings,” “Protein localization embeddings,” and “Protein embeddings.”\n- Model/interface elements are schematic blocks and arrows, not code or UI.\n\nPanel b: “scMIR protein localization embedding similarity heatmap”\n- Heatmap of protein localization embedding similarity.\n- Rows/columns are subcellular localization labels including nucleoli rim, nucleoli, nucleoli fibrillar center, vesicles, peroxisomes, lysosomes, endosomes, lipid droplets, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, cytoplasmic bodies, cytosol, centriolar satellite, centrosome, microtubule ends, microtubules, intermediate filaments, actin filaments, focal adhesion sites, cell junctions, plasma membrane, mitochondria, nuclear membrane, nucleoplasm, nuclear bodies, and nuclear speckles.\n- Red outlined regions annotate localization groups: Nucleoli, Endomembrane system, Cytosol, Cytoskeleton, Plasma membrane, Mitochondria, and Nucleus.\n- Darker blue blocks indicate higher similarity within some localization groups.\n\nPanel c: “Cell line similarity heatmaps based on mRNA and scMIR embeddings”\n- Two side-by-side heatmaps compare cell line similarity.\n- Left heatmap labeled “mRNA.”\n- Right heatmap labeled “scMIR embeddings.”\n- Cell lines listed include NB4, SK-MEL-30, MCF-7, Hep-G2, A-549, OE19, HaCaT, SuSa, PC-3, HeLa, CACO-2, EFO-21, HDLM-2, U-251MG, A-431, SiHa, GAMG, U2OS, BJ, hTCEpi, RPTEC/TERT1, HUVEC/TERT2, HCN-M2, ASC52telo, hTERT-RPE1, AF22, RT-4, RH30, HEK293, REH, HAP1, SH-SY5Y, THP-1, JURKAT, HEL, and K-562.\n- Color bar shows cosine similarity from -1.00 to 1.00.\n\nPanel d: “Mantel statistic comparison”\n- Bar chart comparing Mantel statistic for “Cell line” and “Protein localization.”\n- Methods shown by color: CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR.\n- scMIR has the highest visible values: 0.29 for Cell line and 0.31 for Protein localization.\n- Other labeled values: Cell line 0.22, 0.20, 0.24; Protein localization 0.14, 0.16, 0.17.\n\nPanel e: “Comparison of protein interaction prediction performance”\n- Bar chart comparing methods: CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR.\n- Metrics: Spearman, Kendall, and TopBottomSep@10%.\n- scMIR has the highest visible performance on all metrics: Spearman 4.69, Kendall 3.14, TopBottomSep@10% 2.70.\n- Other visible values: Spearman 0.96, 1.14, 2.58; Kendall 0.64, 0.77, 1.72; TopBottomSep@10% 0.36, 0.44, 0.66.\n\nOverall finding shown: scMIR embeddings derived from HPA single-cell images preserve biologically meaningful subcellular localization and cell-line similarity structure and outperform the comparison image embedding methods in the plotted protein interaction prediction metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 13, "caption": "Fig. 5 | Batch effect of image embeddings. UMAP visualizations of image-level features extracted  by  scMIR  and  representative  pretrained  baselines  on  the  COOS7  [53], cpg0000 [1] and BBBC021 [58] datasets. Features are colored by plate IDs. IMAD quantifies  the  consistency  of  UMAP  patterns  across  batches,  with  higher  values indicating improved batch-effect mitigation. UMAP: Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection; IMAD: inverse median absolute deviation.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure comparing UMAP embeddings across datasets and feature/model methods.\n\nVisible structure:\n- A 3 row x 4 column grid of scatter/UMAP plots.\n- Rows are labeled vertically by dataset:\n  - `CODS7`\n  - `cpg0000`\n  - `BBBC021`\n- Columns compare model or feature sources:\n  - `CytoImageNet`\n  - `EfficientNetB0`\n  - `Microsnoop`\n  - `scMIR`\n- Each panel title reports an `IMAD` score.\n  - For `CODS7`: CytoImageNet `IMAD=0.75`, EfficientNetB0 `IMAD=1.03`, Microsnoop `IMAD=1.15`, scMIR `IMAD=1.47`\n  - For `cpg0000`: CytoImageNet `IMAD=0.415`, EfficientNetB0 `IMAD=0.352`, Microsnoop `IMAD=0.252`, scMIR `IMAD=0.421`\n  - For `BBBC021`: CytoImageNet `IMAD=0.493`, EfficientNetB0 `IMAD=0.518`, Microsnoop `IMAD=0.369`, scMIR `IMAD=0.608`\n\nAxes and visual encodings:\n- Each panel has axes labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`.\n- Points are colored by `Plates IDs`, with a legend of multiple colored dots at the bottom.\n- Marginal density curves appear above and to the right of each UMAP scatter plot, showing distributions along UMAP1 and UMAP2 for the colored groups.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The figure appears to analyze microscopy or cell-image-derived datasets, including `CytoImageNet`, `cpg0000`, and `BBBC021`.\n- The plotted objects are embedded biological image samples or cell/microscopy-derived feature representations, colored by plate identity.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Feature representations from different models or methods are projected into 2D using UMAP.\n- Compared representations include CytoImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR.\n- IMAD is used as a quantitative comparison metric for each embedding.\n\nVisible findings:\n- `scMIR` has the highest reported IMAD in the `CODS7` and `BBBC021` rows.\n- `Microsnoop` has the lowest reported IMAD in the `cpg0000` and `BBBC021` rows.\n- The embeddings show varying degrees of clustering and plate-associated separation across methods and datasets."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 38, "caption": "Fig. S1 | t-SNE visualization of CytoImageNet embeddings. Embeddings colored by dataset identity.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single t-SNE scatter plot titled “Visualization of dataset.” The x-axis is labeled “t-SNE 1” and the y-axis is labeled “t-SNE 2.” Points are colored by dataset, with a legend titled “Dataset” listing AINU, AIRFIHA, BBBC014, BBBC048, CM4AI, CNMC, COOS-7, CYCLOPs, CoNSeP, DIBaS, FCD, LIVECell, and Raabin.\n\nThe figure visualizes embedded samples as colored point clusters. Several datasets form distinct separated clusters, while some appear as multiple subclusters. No biological source objects are directly shown; the plot likely represents transformed image/sample features projected into two dimensions using t-SNE. No model architecture or interface is visible. The main visible finding is dataset-level clustering/separation in the embedding space."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 39, "caption": "Fig. S2 | t-SNE visualization of CytoImageNet embeddings. Embeddings colored by dataset-level biological attributes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a 2D t-SNE scatter plot titled “Visualization of dataset attribute.” It has one visible panel with axes labeled “t-SNE 1” and “t-SNE 2.”\n\nThe points are colored by dataset attribute, with a legend on the right labeled “Dataset attribute.” Legend categories include:\n\n- Bacteria: blue\n- Cancer: green\n- Immune: purple\n- Immune Cancer: pink\n- Somatic Stem: yellow-green\n- Subcellular: cyan\n\nThe plot shows multiple separated clusters in t-SNE space. Subcellular samples form a large cyan cluster in the lower center/right and another cluster near the center-left. Cancer samples appear as many green clusters distributed across the upper and left regions. Immune samples form purple clusters, including a large upper-center cluster and smaller clusters on the right and lower-left. Immune Cancer appears as a compact pink cluster in the lower-left. Somatic Stem appears as a small yellow-green cluster near the top. Bacteria appears as a small blue cluster near the central upper-left area.\n\nNo biological source objects are visually shown directly; the figure represents embedded dataset samples. The transformation shown is dimensionality reduction by t-SNE from higher-dimensional data into two plotted coordinates. No model architecture or interface is depicted."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 40, "caption": "Fig. S3 | t-SNE visualization of EfficientNetB0 embeddings. Embeddings colored by dataset identity.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel scatter plot titled “Visualization of dataset” showing a t-SNE embedding.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Axes labeled `t-SNE 1` and `t-SNE 2`.\n- Points are colored by dataset identity.\n- Legend title: `Dataset`.\n- Legend entries: `AINU`, `AIRFIHA`, `BBBC014`, `BBBC048`, `CM4AI`, `CNMC`, `COOS-7`, `CYCLOPs`, `CoNSeP`, `DIBaS`, `FCD`, `LIVECell`, `Raabin`.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The plot appears to compare image-derived samples from multiple biomedical or microscopy datasets, but the image itself does not show raw cells, tissues, bacteria, or microscopy panels directly.\n- No individual biological structures are visibly identifiable in this extracted figure beyond point clusters.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- Dimensionality reduction using t-SNE is shown, mapping dataset samples into two dimensions.\n- Color encodes dataset membership.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Samples form dataset-specific clusters with varying separation.\n- Several datasets occupy distinct regions of the embedding.\n- `CYCLOPs` forms a large broad cluster in the lower-right region.\n- `LIVECell` appears as multiple separated cyan clusters in the lower-left region.\n- Other datasets form compact or moderately dispersed clusters across the plot."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 41, "caption": "Fig. S4 | t-SNE visualization of EfficientNetB0 embeddings. Embeddings colored by dataset-level biological attributes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel t-SNE scatter plot titled “Visualization of dataset attribute.” The x-axis is labeled “t-SNE 1” and the y-axis is labeled “t-SNE 2.” Points are colored by dataset attribute, with a legend on the right.\n\nVisible legend categories:\n- Bacteria: blue\n- Cancer: green\n- Immune: purple\n- Immune Cancer: pink\n- Somatic Stem: yellow-green\n- Subcellular: cyan\n\nThe figure visualizes high-dimensional data embedded into two t-SNE dimensions, showing clustered groups by dataset/source attribute. The largest visible clusters appear to be cyan “Subcellular,” green “Cancer,” and purple “Immune.” Smaller clusters include blue “Bacteria,” pink “Immune Cancer,” and yellow-green “Somatic Stem.”\n\nNo biological structures, microscopy images, molecular diagrams, model architectures, or experimental transformations are shown beyond the t-SNE dimensionality reduction scatter visualization."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 42, "caption": "Fig.  S5  |  t-SNE  visualization  of  Microsnoop  embeddings.  Embeddings  colored  by dataset identity.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel t-SNE scatter plot titled “Visualization of dataset.” The x-axis is labeled “t-SNE 1” and the y-axis is labeled “t-SNE 2.” Points are colored by dataset, with a legend on the right titled “Dataset.”\n\nVisible dataset labels in the legend:\nAINU, AIRFIHA, BBBC014, BBBC048, CM4AI, CNMC, COOS-7, CYCLoPs, CoNSeP, DIBaS, FCD, LIVECell, Raabin.\n\nThe figure shows clustered embeddings from multiple biological imaging datasets. Each dataset forms one or more colored point clusters in the 2D t-SNE projection. A large CYCLoPs cluster occupies much of the right side, LIVECell appears as several teal clusters near the upper region, CM4AI appears as a red cluster near the bottom, and COOS-7 appears as multiple brown clusters in the lower-right region.\n\nTransformation shown: dimensionality reduction/embedding visualization using t-SNE.\n\nNo model architecture, image panels of biological objects, microscopy examples, or interface components are visible in this extracted image."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 43, "caption": "Fig.  S6  |  t-SNE  visualization  of  Microsnoop  embeddings.  Embeddings  colored  by dataset-level biological attributes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a 2D t-SNE scatter plot titled “Visualization of dataset attribute.”\n\nVisible elements:\n- Single panel scatter plot.\n- X-axis label: “t-SNE 1”.\n- Y-axis label: “t-SNE 2”.\n- Legend title: “Dataset attribute”.\n- Legend categories:\n  - Bacteria, blue\n  - Cancer, green\n  - Immune, purple\n  - Immune Cancer, pink\n  - Somatic Stem, yellow\n  - Subcellular, cyan\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The plotted points represent samples or embeddings grouped by dataset attribute.\n- Biological dataset categories include bacteria, cancer, immune, immune cancer, somatic stem, and subcellular.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The visualization uses t-SNE dimensionality reduction to project high-dimensional data into two dimensions.\n- No model architecture or explicit model interface is shown.\n\nFindings visible in the figure:\n- “Subcellular” samples form a large cyan cluster on the right side, with several smaller cyan clusters lower-right and center-right.\n- “Cancer” samples appear as multiple green clusters spread across the top and lower regions.\n- “Immune” samples form several purple clusters mainly on the left and central-left regions.\n- “Immune Cancer” appears as a compact pink cluster near the lower center-left.\n- “Somatic Stem” appears as a small yellow cluster near the lower-left.\n- “Bacteria” appears as small blue clusters near the lower center-left."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 44, "caption": "Fig. S7  | Heatmap  visualization  of different perturbation representations at a concentration of 0.003 on BBBC021.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a heatmap-style scientific figure, likely showing normalized values across experimental conditions or treatments.\n\nVisible content:\n- Single heatmap panel with many horizontal rows and 13 labeled columns.\n- Column labels along the x-axis:\n  - `DMSO`\n  - `AZ-C(1)`, `AZ-C(2)`, `AZ-C(3)`\n  - `camptothecin(1)`, `camptothecin(2)`, `camptothecin(3)`\n  - `mitoxantrone(1)`, `mitoxantrone(2)`, `mitoxantrone(3)`\n  - `vincristine(1)`, `vincristine(2)`, `vincristine(3)`\n- A vertical color scale at right ranges approximately from `-2` to `3`.\n  - Blue indicates lower values.\n  - White indicates near-zero values.\n  - Red indicates higher values.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The rows likely represent measured biological features such as genes, proteins, transcripts, or other molecular readouts, but row labels are not visible, so the exact object type cannot be determined from the image alone.\n- The columns represent control and drug-treatment conditions with three apparent replicates for most treatments.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Values appear transformed or standardized, likely z-scores or normalized expression/intensity values, inferred from the diverging blue-white-red scale centered near zero.\n- No explicit model architecture, computational interface, or workflow diagram is shown.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Replicates within each treatment group show broadly similar vertical color patterns.\n- `vincristine` columns show strong red signal in upper regions and stronger blue signal in lower-middle regions.\n- `camptothecin` and `mitoxantrone` groups show related but distinct red/blue banding patterns.\n- `DMSO` and `AZ-C` columns differ from the drug-treated groups in several row blocks.\n- The figure suggests treatment-specific changes across many measured features."]}, {"figure_index": 14, "page_no": 45, "caption": "Fig. S8  | Heatmap  visualization  of different perturbation representations at a concentration of 0.03 on BBBC021.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel heatmap showing a matrix of normalized values, with a diverging blue-white-red color scale at right ranging roughly from -3 to +3.\n\nVisible labels:\n- Columns are treatment/sample groups with replicates:\n  - DMSO\n  - AZ-C(1), AZ-C(2), AZ-C(3)\n  - AZ138(1), AZ138(2), AZ138(3)\n  - camptothecin(1), camptothecin(2), camptothecin(3)\n  - colchicine(1), colchicine(2), colchicine(3)\n  - docetaxel(1), docetaxel(2), docetaxel(3)\n  - vincristine(1), vincristine(2), vincristine(3)\n- Row labels are not visible.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The image appears to represent high-dimensional biological measurements across drug-treated samples, likely genes/proteins/features by sample condition, but the exact measured entity is not shown.\n\nTransformations/visual encoding:\n- Values are displayed as a heatmap with red indicating higher positive values, blue indicating lower negative values, and white/near-white near zero.\n- Replicate columns are grouped by compound/treatment.\n- No clustering dendrograms, statistical annotations, or panel letters are visible.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Docetaxel replicates show strong contrasting bands, including prominent red and blue regions.\n- Camptothecin and colchicine groups show broad regions of blue-to-red shifts across many rows.\n- Vincristine replicates show strong red bands in the mid-lower region and blue bands near the bottom.\n- AZ-C and AZ138 groups show milder structured variation compared with docetaxel/vincristine."]}, {"figure_index": 15, "page_no": 46, "caption": "Fig. S9  | Heatmap  visualization  of different perturbation representations at a concentration of 1.0 on BBBC021.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis appears to be a single-panel heatmap, likely from a transcriptomic or gene-expression analysis.\n\nVisible elements:\n- One rectangular heatmap panel with many rows and columns.\n- Rows are unlabeled or too small to read, likely representing genes, transcripts, features, or samples.\n- Columns are labeled with biological/sample names including entries such as `DMSO`, multiple `AZ...` labels, and drug/compound-like labels including `epothilone`, `emetine`, `anisomycin`, `didemnin`, `laromustine`, `mitomycin`, `nocodazole`, `taxol`, and `vincristine`.\n- A vertical color scale at the right ranges approximately from `-4` to `4`.\n- Color encoding uses blue-white-red, where blue indicates lower/negative values, white near zero, and red higher/positive values.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- The figure likely represents molecular measurements across experimental treatment conditions or compounds.\n- The exact organism, cell type, assay, and feature identity are not visible in the image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Values appear normalized or standardized, likely z-scores or centered/scaled measurements, based on the symmetric colorbar from negative to positive values.\n- No explicit model architecture, prediction interface, clustering dendrogram, or statistical annotations are visible.\n\nFindings visible:\n- Distinct column-wise blocks show treatment-specific patterns of increased and decreased signal.\n- Several treatments show strong red or blue bands, suggesting differential molecular responses across conditions.\n- No precise biological conclusion can be assigned from the image alone because row labels and caption/context are absent."]}, {"figure_index": 16, "page_no": 47, "caption": "Fig. S10 | Heatmap visualization of of AZ-C perturbation representations.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel heatmap showing many row-wise features across treatment/condition columns. The x-axis labels include `DMSO` and concentration-like groups: `0.001(1)`, `0.001(2)`, `0.001(3)`, `0.003(1-3)`, `0.01(1-3)`, `0.03(1-3)`, `0.1(1-3)`, `0.3(1-3)`, and `1.0(1-3)`, suggesting triplicate measurements per dose. No row labels are visible.\n\nThe color scale at right ranges approximately from `-3` to `+3`, with blue indicating lower values, white near zero, and red indicating higher values, consistent with standardized or normalized abundance/expression values. Biological source objects are not identifiable from the image alone; the rows could represent molecular features such as genes, proteins, metabolites, or other measured variables, but this is not specified.\n\nVisible finding: most values are near neutral with scattered red/blue bands, while the `1.0(1)` column shows several strong vertical blocks of positive and negative signal, indicating a pronounced response or outlier-like pattern at that condition compared with lower concentrations."]}, {"figure_index": 17, "page_no": 48, "caption": "Fig. S11 | Heatmap visualization of of AZ-C perturbation representations.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel heatmap, likely showing a matrix of normalized quantitative values.\n\nVisible elements:\n- One heatmap panel with many horizontal rows and 22 vertical columns.\n- A vertical color bar at right ranging approximately from `-3` to `3`, with blue indicating lower values, white near zero, and red indicating higher values.\n- X-axis labels are rotated vertically and include:\n  - `DMSO`\n  - `0.003(1)`, `0.003(2)`, `0.003(3)`\n  - `0.01(1)`, `0.01(2)`, `0.01(3)`\n  - `0.03(1)`, `0.03(2)`, `0.03(3)`\n  - `0.1(1)`, `0.1(2)`, `0.1(3)`\n  - `0.3(1)`, `0.3(2)`, `0.3(3)`\n  - `1.0(1)`, `1.0(2)`, `1.0(3)`\n  - `3.0(1)`, `3.0(2)`, `3.0(3)`\n- No visible title, y-axis labels, legend title, panel letters, or organism/cell/tissue labels.\n- No visible microscopy, anatomical structures, molecular diagrams, model architecture, or workflow schematic.\n\nLikely interpretation from visible labels only:\n- Columns appear to represent DMSO control and multiple dose/concentration groups with three replicates each.\n- Rows represent many measured features, such as genes, proteins, metabolites, or other biological variables, but the specific biological source objects are not shown.\n- The displayed transformation appears to be a centered/scaled value such as z-score or standardized abundance, inferred from the symmetric red-white-blue color scale around zero.\n- The figure shows heterogeneous up- and down-regulated row patterns across treatment conditions, with some dose/replicate-specific vertical bands of stronger red or blue signal."]}, {"figure_index": 18, "page_no": 49, "caption": "Fig. S12 | Performance comparison on cpg0000.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle scatter plot titled “Well level-Compounds.” The x-axis is labeled “Mean Average Precision (mAP)” with ticks from about 0.1 to 0.4. The y-axis is labeled “Folds of Enrichment” with ticks around 15, 20, 30, and 40.\n\nVisible data: four colored circular markers with black outlines:\n- Blue point near mAP 0.13, enrichment 15\n- Orange point near mAP 0.16, enrichment 19\n- Green point near mAP 0.22, enrichment 24\n- Red point near mAP 0.36, enrichment 39\n\nNo visible panel letters, legend, biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, transformations, or model architecture/interface diagrams are shown. The figure suggests a positive relationship between mAP and folds of enrichment across four compound-level/well-level conditions, with the red point performing highest on both metrics."]}]
