data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/13_crops/models/model_e0f70ec41e97_512bd31d3b54/adjudicator/stderr.log:34:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 | Overview of framework. a . We curated a diverse collection of light microscopy datasets.  The  left  panel  summarizes  dataset  diversity  across  microscopy  types,  cell types,  perturbation  conditions  and  species.  The  right  panel  presents  a  hierarchical organization  of  the  datasets,  grouped  into  pre-training  and  evaluation  sets,  with  the number  of  images  annotated  at  each  level  of  the  hierarchy.  Microscopy  types  are", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with three labeled panels: `a. Dataset characterization`, `b. Model architecture`, and `c. Downstream analysis based on learned image embedding`.\n\nPanel `a` summarizes dataset characterization. A circular chart shows microscopy/data categories including `BF`, `DIC`, `Conf`, `WF`, `SR`, `QPI`, `PC`, `Cell type (100+)`, `Perturbation (300+)`, and species labels including `Human`, `Mouse`, `Yeast`, and `Bacteria`. The center contains example microscopy cell images. A right-side hierarchy organizes data by `Granularity`, `Scale`, `Attribute`, and `Number`, mapping `Single-cell`, `Full-field`, `Subcellular`, `Cell-level`, and `Population` to attributes such as `Protein localization`, `Cell identity/state`, and `Perturbation response`. Counts are shown for `Pre-training` and `Evaluation`, including values such as `72,832`, `2,622,150`, `40,000`, `156,669`, `95,125`, and `117,628`.\n\nPanel `b` shows a model architecture for single-cell image-text learning. Inputs include a generated `Caption` with keywords such as `Species`, `Source`, `Microscopy`, `Cell type`, and `Biological attribute`, plus a `Single-cell image`. The caption is passed through a `Tokenizer` into `Text tokens`. The image is passed through an `Image encoder` into `Image tokens`. A `Q-Former` module connects text/image tokens to image-text alignment, represented by a matrix comparing `Image feature` entries `I1 ... Im` with `Text feature` entries `T1 ... Tn`. A second shared-weight `Q-Former` receives masked image tokens, followed by an `Image decoder`, performing `Mask tokens reconstruction`.\n\nPanel `c` illustrates downstream analysis using learned image embeddings. `Evaluation images` are input into `scMIR`, producing a learned image embedding matrix. The embeddings are used for `Cell classification`, `Morphological inference`, `Cell clustering`, and `Batch correction`, shown as schematic point clusters and transformed groupings.\n\nVisible biological source objects include single-cell microscopy images and schematic cells. The figure describes transformations from microscopy images and captions into tokenized text/image representations, learned embeddings, reconstructed image tokens, and downstream biological analysis outputs."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 | Classification and clustering performance, visualization of scMIR embeddings. a . Radar plot of F1-score performance (scaled ×100) across 17 classification tasks from 13 light microscopy  datasets. scMIR  is compared  with  task-oriented models, CytoImageNet,  EfficientNetB0,  and  Microsnoop. b .  Quantitative  assessment  of unsupervised  cross-dataset  alignment  using  normalized  mutual  information  (NMI), purity score (PS), and adjusted Rand index (ARI) computed from joint embeddings (metrics scaled ×100). c . t-SNE visualization of scMIR embeddings. Left, embeddings colored  by  dataset  identity  demonstrate  compact  dataset-specific  clustering  despite experimental  heterogeneity.  Right,  the  same  embeddings  colored  by  dataset-level biological attributes reveal coherent higher-level biological organization across studies and microscopy modalities. Source data are provided as a Source Data file.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about scMIR performance and embeddings.\n\nPanel a: “Classification performance”\nA radar/spider chart comparing classification performance across multiple microscopy image datasets. Dataset labels around the chart include BBB-C048, BBB-C014, CNMC, Raabin, DIBaS, CoNSeP, LiveCell, FCD, AINU, COOS7-5, COOS7-4, COOS7-3, COOS7-2, COOS7-1, CYCLOPs, CM4AI, and AIRFIHA. Methods shown in the legend are Task-oriented, CytolImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR. The red scMIR curve generally forms the outer envelope, indicating stronger classification performance across most datasets.\n\nPanel b: “Clustering performance”\nA grouped bar chart comparing CytolImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR on clustering metrics NMI, PS, and ARI. scMIR has the highest visible values: NMI 70.4, PS 56.5, and ARI 36.7. Other methods are lower, with approximate values shown above bars, e.g. NMI 63.4, 66.3, 68.4; PS 47.2, 48.7, 52.0; ARI 29.5, 29.4, 33.8.\n\nPanel c: “Visualization of scMIR embeddings”\nTwo t-SNE scatter plots of scMIR embeddings.\n\nLeft plot: “Visualization of dataset”\nPoints are colored by dataset/source. The legend includes AINU, AIRFIHA, BBBC014, BBBC048, CM4AI, CNMC, COOS7, CYCLOPs, CoNSeP, DIBaS, FCD, LIVECell, and Raabin. The plot shows separated and partially overlapping clusters in t-SNE space.\n\nRight plot: “Visualization of dataset attribute”\nThe same embedding space colored by biological or dataset attribute categories: Bacteria, Cancer, Immune, Immune Cancer, Somatic Stem, and Subcellular. An arrow labels a cluster as “CNMC,” and another annotation indicates “CD4/CD8.” The visible finding is that scMIR embeddings group samples by both dataset identity and broader biological/image attributes.\n\nBiological source objects visible or implied by labels include microscopy-derived cell, tissue, and biomedical image datasets, including bacteria, cancer, immune-cell, stem-cell, and subcellular image sources. The main transformation shown is embedding visualization using t-SNE. The model/interface being evaluated is scMIR against baseline image representation models."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Fig. 3 | Perturbation phenotypic profiling of scMIR embeddings. a . Schematic overview of phenotypic inference from perturbation image embeddings. Chemical perturbations", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about image-based profiling of chemical perturbations.\n\nPanel a shows a workflow: a multi-well plate is imaged by microscopy, producing cellular fluorescence images. These images are passed through a “Feature extractor” model/interface to generate “Image embeddings.” Embeddings from wells of chemical perturbation are then compared, with dashed circles indicating wells associated with the same chemical perturbation.\n\nPanel b is titled “Performance comparison on BBBC021” and contains three scatter plots comparing methods by Mean Average Precision (mAP) on the x-axis and Folds of Enrichment on the y-axis. The subplots are:\n- “Treatment level-MoAs”\n- “Well level-Compounds”\n- “Well level-MoAs”\n\nMethods in the legend are CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR. scMIR appears as the red point and has the highest performance in the shown plots.\n\nPanel c is titled “Treatment representations clustering” for BBBC021. It shows a UMAP scatter plot with axes UMAP1 and UMAP2. Points represent control wells, well-level profiles, and treatment-level profiles. Labeled biological/chemical mechanism clusters include Epithelial, Protein synthesis, Actin disruptors, DNA damage, Kinase inhibitors, and Microtubule destabilizers.\n\nPanel d is titled “Heatmap visualization of of AZ-A perturbation representations” for BBBC021. It shows a heatmap with DMSO and multiple dose/replicate-like row labels on the left, and a color scale from about -3 to +3 on the right. The heatmap visualizes learned perturbation representation values or embedding features across conditions."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 9, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single heatmap panel showing many rows of features/samples against labeled columns of biological taxa. The columns include an initial `DNSO` label followed by microbial genus/species-style labels such as `A2 (4)`, `anisomycin`, `desm
