PT-5` generally rank highly across accuracy, F1 score, and MCC.\n- Specificity is near 1.0 for most models, but `BigGPT` has extremely low specificity.\n- `BigGPT` shows high recall but poor specificity and negative or near-zero MCC.\n- `GPT-4o` appears low on recall and MCC compared with most other models."]}, {"figure_index": 24, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel bar chart showing **Specificity** on the y-axis from 0.0 to 1.0. The x-axis lists model names, rotated diagonally: **XGBoost-R**, **GPT-3**, **GPT-3.5**, **DeepSeek-V3**, **DeepSeek-R1**, **GPT-4**, **Claude 3.5**, **Claude 3.7 Sonnet**, **Claude 3 Opus**, **Sonnet 4**, and **BioGPT**.\n\nMost models have high specificity near ~0.9-1.0, while **BioGPT** has much lower specificity, around ~0.1. Bars are individually colored. No biological source objects, image transformations, microscopy, anatomical structures, or model interface screenshots are visible; this is a quantitative performance comparison plot."]}, {"figure_index": 25, "page_no": 24, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 4 | Comparative evaluation of logical reasoning capabilities between XunZi-R and GPT-4o. a -x Radar plots showing reasoning performance across 24 MeSH disease categories, excluding neoplasms and nervous system diseases. Outer green bars indicate the recall of XunZi-R for each individual disease subtype within a category.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with 24 circular radar-style plots labeled **a–x**. Each panel corresponds to a medical or biological disease category:\n\na. Bacterial infections and mycoses  \nb. Psychological phenomena and processes  \nc. Digestive system diseases  \nd. Stomatognathic diseases  \ne. Mental disorders  \nf. Respiratory tract diseases  \ng. Otorhinolaryngologic diseases  \nh. Musculoskeletal diseases  \ni. Eye diseases  \nj. Urologic and male genital diseases  \nk. Cardiovascular diseases  \nl. Hemic and lymphatic diseases  \nm. Congenital abnormality  \nn. Skin diseases  \no. Metabolic diseases  \np. Endocrine system diseases  \nq. Immune system diseases  \nr. Disorders of environmental origin  \ns. Animal diseases  \nt. Pathology  \nu. Occupational diseases  \nv. Chemically induced disorders  \nw. Wounds and injuries  \nx. Behavior and behavior mechanisms\n\nEach panel contains an outer green circular histogram/ring with many radial ticks or bars and an inner radar/spider plot with overlaid colored polygon traces. The visible legend identifies two model/interface traces: **XunZi-R** in blue and **GPT-4o** in orange. The plots appear to compare model performance or scores across subcategories within each disease/biomedical class, using radial axes with visible numeric markings such as 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.\n\nBiological/medical source objects represented are disease and disorder categories rather than microscopy objects or anatomical images. The figure visually reports comparative findings across biomedical taxonomy groups: XunZi-R and GPT-4o have similar radar profiles in many categories, with category-specific differences visible in the inner polygons. No exact quantitative values can be read reliably from the extracted image alone."]}, {"figure_index": 26, "page_no": 25, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 5 | See next page for caption.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle visible panel labeled **a**. It is a bar chart showing **F1 score** on the y-axis, ranging from 0.0 to 1.0, comparing multiple language/model systems on the x-axis.\n\nVisible x-axis labels:\n- XunZi-R\n- Claude 3.7 Sonnet\n- Claude Sonnet 4\n- Claude Sonnet 4.5\n- DeepSeek-R1\n- DeepSeek-V3\n- GPT-4o\n- GPT-5\n- o3\n- GPT-4\n\nThe tallest bar is **XunZi-R**, around **0.90 F1 score**. Other models cluster around approximately **0.64-0.69**, with **GPT-4o** and **GPT-4** among the higher non-XunZi-R values.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, tissue samples, transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 27, "page_no": 27, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel stacked bar chart comparing counts of molecules across diseases and omics types.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Y-axis: “Num. of molecules”, ranging up to slightly above 50,000.\n- X-axis: disease categories, mostly cancers followed by neurodegenerative diseases.\n- Legend title: “Omics type”.\n- Color encoding:\n  - Blue: Transcriptomics\n  - Green: Proteomics\n  - Orange: Phosphoproteomics\n- Group labels beneath the x-axis:\n  - “Cancer”\n  - “Neurodegenerative diseases”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Disease entities including melanoma, colon cancer, breast cancer, rectal cancer, glioblastoma, esophageal cancer, acute myeloid leukemia, lung adenocarcinoma, thyroid cancer, ovarian cancer, pancreatic cancer, liver cancer, bladder cancer, thymoma, sarcoma, stomach cancer, testicular cancer, kidney papillary cell carcinoma, lung squamous cell carcinoma, Alzheimer’s disease, Huntington’s disease, Lewy body dementia, Parkinson’s disease, and progressive supranuclear palsy.\n- Molecular measurement categories from omics datasets: transcriptomics, proteomics, and phosphoproteomics.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- No microscopy, imaging transformation, computational model architecture, or model interface is shown.\n- The data are transformed into stacked categorical bars, where total bar height represents the combined number of molecules across omics types for each disease.\n\nFindings visible from the chart:\n- Alzheimer’s disease has the highest total molecule count, exceeding 50,000, with large transcriptomics and proteomics contributions and a smaller phosphoproteomics segment.\n- Most cancer categories have total molecule counts roughly between 20,000 and 40,000.\n- Neurodegenerative diseases vary substantially, with Alzheimer’s disease highest and Parkinson’s disease lower than Alzheimer’s and progressive supranuclear palsy.\n- Transcriptomics and proteomics are present for all shown diseases; phosphoproteomics appears as an additional orange segment for many but not all categories."]}, {"figure_index": 28, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a bar chart labeled panel “a” with the title “Pan-cancer-AUPR”. The y-axis is labeled “AUPR” and ranges from 0.0 to about 0.8+. The x-axis compares six methods/models: XunZi, XunZi-R, XunZi-M, DNN, GPT-4o, and SVM.\n\nVisible values above bars:\n- XunZi: 0.799\n- XunZi-R: 0.736\n- XunZi-M: 0.716\n- DNN: 0.599\n- GPT-4o: 0.576\n- SVM: 0.550\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, or workflow transformations are visible in this extracted panel. The figure reports comparative model performance on a pan-cancer task using AUPR, with XunZi showing the highest AUPR among the displayed methods."]}, {"figure_index": 29, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel `d` shows a bar chart titled `Pan-cancer-AUPR`. The y-axis is labeled `AUPR`, ranging roughly from 0.0 to 0.8+, and the x-axis is labeled `CTD testing`.\n\nVisible model/method labels and values:\n- `XunZi-R`: 0.791\n- `XunZi`: 0.601\n- `XunZi-M`: 0.477\n- `GPT-4o`: 0.475\n- `DNN`: 0.416\n- `SVM`: 0.407\n\nThe figure compares predictive performance across methods on CTD testing using AUPR. No biological source objects, microscopy, anatomical structures, molecular diagrams, transformations, or model interface schematics are visible in this extracted panel. The main finding shown is that `XunZi-R` has the highest AUPR, followed by `XunZi`, while `SVM` is lowest among the displayed methods."]}, {"figure_index": 30, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle visible panel labeled **g**. It is a bar chart titled **“Neurodegenerative diseases-AUPR”**.\n\nVisible axes and labels:\n- Y-axis: **AUPR**, ranging approximately from 0.0 to 0.7.\n- X-axis categories: **Xunzi**, **Xunzi-M**, **Xunzi-R**, **GPT-4o**, **SVM**, **DNN**.\n\nDisplayed values:\n- Xunzi: **0.624**\n- Xunzi-M: **0.606**\n- Xunzi-R: **0.515**\n- GPT-4o: **0.424**\n- SVM: **0.326**\n- DNN: **0.303**\n\nBiological/source objects: The figure refers to **neurodegenerative diseases**, but no biological images, tissues, cells, genes, proteins, or datasets are visibly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: The plot compares predictive/model methods by AUPR; visible methods include Xunzi variants, GPT-4o, SVM, and DNN. No architecture, workflow, or model interface is shown.\n\nFinding: **Xunzi has the highest AUPR**, followed by **Xunzi-M** and **Xunzi-R**; **DNN** is lowest among the shown methods."]}, {"figure_index": 31, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle visible panel labeled **j**. It is a bar chart titled **“Neurodegenerative diseases-AUPR”** showing **AUPR** performance on **CTD testing**.\n\nVisible labels:\n- Y-axis: **AUPR**, ranging approximately 0.0 to 0.8\n- X-axis categories: **Xunzi**, **Xunzi-M**, **Xunzi-R**, **GPT-4o**, **DNN**, **SVM**\n- Bar values:\n  - Xunzi: **0.737**\n  - Xunzi-M: **0.713**\n  - Xunzi-R: **0.547**\n  - GPT-4o: **0.469**\n  - DNN: **0.444**\n  - SVM: **0.426**\n\nBiological/source context visible in the figure: **neurodegenerative diseases** and **CTD testing**. No biological objects, molecular structures, microscopy images, or experimental transformations are shown. The figure compares model/interface performance using AUPR, with **Xunzi** highest and **SVM** lowest among the displayed methods."]}, {"figure_index": 32, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel `b` shows a two-set Venn diagram comparing pan-cancer gene/disease associations from `DisGeNET` and `CTD`.\n\nVisible labels and values:\n- Left circle: `DisGeNET`\n- Right circle: `CTD`\n- Shared overlap: `3100 (59%)`\n- DisGeNET-only region: `1499 (28%)`\n- CTD-only region: `685 (13%)`\n- Bottom label: `Pan-cancer`\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The figure compares pan-cancer entries or associations from two biomedical databases, DisGeNET and CTD.\n- No specific genes, diseases, tissues, cell types, or organisms are shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data are transformed into an overlap/uniqueness summary using a Venn diagram.\n- No model architecture, algorithm interface, microscopy, sequencing workflow, or computational pipeline is visible.\n\nFinding:\n- Most pan-cancer entries are shared between DisGeNET and CTD: 3100 entries, representing 59%.\n- DisGeNET contributes 1499 unique entries, 28%.\n- CTD contributes 685 unique entries, 13%."]}, {"figure_index": 33, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a scientific plot panel labeled **“e”** with the title **“Pan-cancer-ROC”**. It is a ROC curve plot with:\n\n- **X-axis:** False positive rate\n- **Y-axis:** True positive rate\n- Multiple colored ROC curves comparing models:\n  - **XunZi (AUC=0.65)**\n  - **XunZi-M (AUC=0.62)**\n  - **XunZi-R (AUC=0.68)**\n  - **GPT-4o (AUC=0.57)**\n  - **DNN (AUC=0.51)**\n  - **SVM (AUC=0.55)**\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, molecular diagrams, or model architecture/interface schematics are visible. The panel presents a quantitative performance comparison for pan-cancer classification or prediction, with **XunZi-R showing the highest AUC among the listed models** and **DNN near chance-level performance**."]}, {"figure_index": 34, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-set Venn diagram comparing **DisGeNET** and **CTD**. The left circle is labeled **DisGeNET**, the right circle **CTD**. The overlap contains **276 (38%)**, indicating shared items between the two resources. The DisGeNET-only region contains **307 (43%)**, and the CTD-only region contains **138 (19%)**.\n\nVisible elements:\n- One Venn diagram panel\n- Labels: **DisGeNET**, **CTD**\n- Counts and percentages: **307 (43%)**, **276 (38%)**, **138 (19%)**\n- No visible biological source objects, molecular structures, microscopy images, model architecture, or transformation workflow\n- Finding shown: partial overlap between DisGeNET and CTD with the largest unique portion in DisGeNET and a smaller unique portion in CTD."]}, {"figure_index": 35, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a ROC curve plot panel labeled **“k”**. It is titled **“Neurodegenerative diseases-ROC”** and compares classifier performance on **DisGeNET testing**.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: **False positive rate**\n- Y-axis: **True positive rate**\n- Diagonal gray dashed reference line indicating random performance\n- Multiple ROC curves for models/methods:\n  - **XunZi** with **AUC = 0.81**\n  - **XunZi-M** with **AUC = 0.80**\n  - **XunZi-R** with **AUC = 0.73**\n  - **GPT-4o** with **AUC = 0.69**\n  - **DNN** with **AUC = 0.60**\n  - **SVM** with **AUC = 0.71**\n\nBiological/domain context:\n- The task concerns **neurodegenerative diseases**\n- Evaluation dataset/source shown: **DisGeNET testing**\n\nFindings:\n- **XunZi** performs best among the shown methods, with the highest AUC of **0.81**\n- **XunZi-M** is close behind with **AUC = 0.80**\n- **DNN** has the weakest displayed performance with **AUC = 0.60**"]}, {"figure_index": 36, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nExtracted panel labeled **c** showing a **Pan-cancer ROC** plot for **CTD testing**.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Single ROC curve panel with x-axis **False positive rate** and y-axis **True positive rate**.\n- Multiple model curves with legend:\n  - **XunZi (AUC=0.73)**, dashed green\n  - **XunZi-M (AUC=0.60)**, orange\n  - **XunZi-R (AUC=0.81)**, blue\n  - **GPT-4o (AUC=0.56)**, purple\n  - **DNN (AUC=0.50)**, green\n  - **SVM (AUC=0.52)**, gray\n- Findings visible from the plot: **XunZi-R** has the highest reported AUC (**0.81**), followed by **XunZi** (**0.73**). GPT-4o, DNN, and SVM are near chance-level performance, while XunZi-M is intermediate.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- No biological images, cells, tissues, molecules, or experimental source objects are visible.\n- The figure is a model-performance evaluation plot for pan-cancer CTD testing.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- ROC analysis comparing classifier/model outputs.\n- No visible model architecture, transformation pipeline, or interface schematic."]}, {"figure_index": 37, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle visible panel labeled “f”, showing a bar chart titled “Pan-cancer-AUPR”. The y-axis is labeled “AUPR” with values from 0.0 to about 0.7. The x-axis compares six methods/models:\n\n- XunZi-R: 0.642\n- XunZi: 0.623\n- XunZi-M: 0.584\n- GPT-4o: 0.560\n- DNN: 0.419\n- SVM: 0.386\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy, tissue, cells, genes, proteins, or molecular structures are visually shown. The figure presents a quantitative model-performance comparison, likely for a pan-cancer prediction task using AUPR as the metric. Main finding: XunZi-R has the highest AUPR, followed by XunZi and XunZi-M; GPT-4o is lower than the XunZi variants but higher than DNN and SVM."]}, {"figure_index": 38, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel ROC curve plot titled “Neurodegenerative diseases-ROC” with x-axis “False positive rate” and y-axis “True positive rate.” The panel is labeled “CTD testing” below the plot.\n\nVisible model/condition curves and legend:\n- XunZi, AUC = 0.84\n- XunZi-M, AUC = 0.82\n- XunZi-R, AUC = 0.76\n- GPT-4o, AUC = 0.61\n- DNN, AUC = 0.64\n- SVM, AUC = 0.60\n- Gray dashed diagonal baseline representing chance-level performance\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, tissues, cells, organisms, or molecular structures are shown. The figure is a model-performance comparison for neurodegenerative disease classification or prediction on CTD testing data. The main visible finding is that XunZi has the highest ROC-AUC, followed closely by XunZi-M and XunZi-R, while GPT-4o, DNN, and SVM perform closer to the chance baseline."]}, {"figure_index": 39, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single panel labeled “I” showing a bar chart titled “Neurodegenerative diseases-AUPR.” The y-axis is labeled “AUPR” with values from 0.0 to about 0.7. The x-axis compares six methods: XunZi, XunZi-M, XunZi-R, GPT-4o, SVM, and DNN. The bottom label reads “DisGeNET testing.”\n\nVisible results:\n- XunZi: 0.639\n- XunZi-M: 0.630\n- XunZi-R: 0.510\n- GPT-4o: 0.499\n- SVM: 0.451\n- DNN: 0.340\n\nThe figure reports model performance for neurodegenerative disease prediction/evaluation using AUPR on DisGeNET testing data. No biological source objects, microscopy, molecular structures, or transformation diagrams are shown; this is a quantitative model-comparison plot."]}, {"figure_index": 40, "page_no": 30, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 8 | ROC performance of XunZi across different cancer types. a -o ROC curves and AUC values from fivefold cross-validation showing the performance of XunZi in identifying disease-relevant regulators across 15 cancer types.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing ROC curves for a model labeled “XunZi” across 15 cancer types. Panels are arranged in a 5 × 3 grid and labeled a–o.\n\nVisible panels:\n\n- a: Adrenal gland tumors, XunZi AUC = 0.92\n- b: Bladder cancer, AUC = 0.83\n- c: Breast cancer, AUC = 0.68\n- d: Cervical cancer, AUC = 0.79\n- e: Cholangiocarcinoma, AUC = 0.82\n- f: Colorectal cancer, AUC = 0.85\n- g: Esophago gastric adenocarcinoma, AUC = 0.79\n- h: Head-and-Neck squamous-cell carcinoma, AUC = 0.85\n- i: Hepatocellular carcinoma, AUC = 0.66\n- j: Kidney cancer, AUC = 0.78\n- k: Ovarian cancer, AUC = 0.83\n- l: Pancreatic cancer, AUC = 0.83\n- m: Prostate cancer, AUC = 0.76\n- n: Testicular germ-cell tumor, AUC = 0.92\n- o: Thyroid carcinoma, AUC = 0.85\n\nEach panel plots sensitivity (`Sn`) on the y-axis versus `1-Sp` on the x-axis, with an orange ROC curve and a blue dashed diagonal reference line. The biological source objects are cancer or tumor categories; no cell images, tissue micrographs, molecular structures, or anatomical specimens are directly shown. The transformation represented is model performance evaluation summarized as ROC curves and AUC values. The model interface visible in the legend is “XunZi.”\n\nMain finding visible from the figure: XunZi shows above-random diagnostic/classification performance across all listed cancer types, with strongest AUCs shown for adrenal gland tumors and testicular germ-cell tumor, both 0.92, and weakest AUCs shown for hepatocellular carcinoma, 0.66, and breast cancer, 0.68."]}, {"figure_index": 41, "page_no": 31, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 9 | ROC performance of XunZi across different cancer types (continued from Extended Data Fig. 8). a -i ROC curves and AUC values from fivefold cross-validation showing the performance of XunZi in identifying disease-relevant regulators across 9 cancer types.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing ROC curves for a model or method labeled “XunZi” across nine cancer types.\n\nVisible panels:\n- a: Uterine cancer, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.96\n- b: Acute myeloid leukemia, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.84\n- c: Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.84\n- d: Glioma, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.95\n- e: Mesothelioma, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.95\n- f: Sarcoma, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.91\n- g: Skin cutaneous melanoma, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.92\n- h: Thymoma, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.86\n- i: Uveal melanoma, ROC curve, XunZi AUC = 0.98\n\nAxes and plotted elements:\n- Each panel plots sensitivity, labeled `Sn`, on the y-axis.\n- Each panel plots `1-Sp` on the x-axis, corresponding to one minus specificity.\n- Orange step curves represent the XunZi ROC performance.\n- A blue dashed diagonal line represents chance-level classification.\n- Legends report the AUC value for each cancer type.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cancer disease categories are the visible biological/clinical sources: uterine cancer, acute myeloid leukemia, diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, glioma, mesothelioma, sarcoma, skin cutaneous melanoma, thymoma, and uveal melanoma.\n- No cellular images, tissues, microscopy, molecular structures, or omics feature names are visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure presents model evaluation outputs as ROC curves.\n- The visible model/interface name is “XunZi.”\n- No architecture diagram, feature transformation, input modality, training pipeline, or data preprocessing details are shown.\n\nFindings:\n- XunZi shows high discrimination across all displayed cancer types, with AUC values ranging from 0.84 to 0.98.\n- The strongest visible AUC is for uveal melanoma, AUC = 0.98.\n- The lowest visible AUCs are acute myeloid leukemia and diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, both AUC = 0.84."]}, {"figure_index": 42, "page_no": 32, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 10 | ROC performance of XunZi across different neurodegenerative diseases. a -d ROC curves and AUC values from fivefold cross-validation showing the performance of XunZi in predicting functional gene relevance in Alzheimer's disease, Huntington's disease, Lewy body-related disease, and progressive supranuclear palsy.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific plot showing ROC performance curves for a model labeled “XunZi” across neurodegenerative disease classifications.\n\nVisible panels:\n- **a**: “Alzheimer’s disease” ROC curve, orange line, legend “XunZi (AUC=0.80)”.\n- **b**: “Huntington’s diseases” ROC curve, orange step-like line, legend “XunZi (AUC=0.85)”.\n- **c**: “Lewy body related disease” ROC curve, orange step-like line, legend “XunZi (AUC=0.78)”.\n- **d**: “Progressive supranuclear palsy” ROC curve, orange step-like line, legend “XunZi (AUC=0.90)”.\n\nAxes and labels:\n- x-axis in all panels: `1-Sp` likely false positive rate / 1-specificity.\n- y-axis in all panels: `Sn` likely sensitivity.\n- Blue dashed diagonal reference line in each panel, representing chance-level performance.\n- Orange curve represents model performance.\n\nBiological / disease objects:\n- Alzheimer’s disease\n- Huntington’s disease\n- Lewy body related disease\n- Progressive supranuclear palsy\n\nModel interface / method shown:\n- ROC curve evaluation of the “XunZi” model.\n- Reported AUC values quantify classification performance.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Best visible AUC: Progressive supranuclear palsy, AUC 0.90.\n- Huntington’s disease: AUC 0.85.\n- Alzheimer’s disease: AUC 0.80.\n- Lewy body related disease: AUC 0.78.\n- All orange ROC curves lie above the diagonal chance line, indicating better-than-random classification performance."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_a62125696693_e71435b6733d/specificity_selector/stderr.log:46:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 1, "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of this work. Cell-o1 achieves state-of-the-art on the CellPuzzles task.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific schematic and results figure about the “CellPuzzles” task and a Cell-o1 training strategy.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: “The CellPuzzles Task”\n  - Subpanel “(1) Previous LLM-based Annotation (Cell by Cell; No reasoning)”\n  - Input example lists marker genes: “MALAT1, RPS27, RPL10, RPL13, RPL41, EEF1A1, …”\n  - Output: “This is a CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell.”\n  - Subpanel “(2) CellPuzzles (Batch-level; Reasoning required)”\n  - Input asks to jointly assign one unique cell type to each cell in a batch using expressed genes and donor context, and provide reasoning plus final answer.\n  - Output shows reasoning text and a small cell-to-cell assignment diagram with labels including:\n    - CD4-positive, alpha-beta T Cell\n    - CD8-positive, alpha-beta T Cell\n    - Capillary Endothelial Cell\n    - Plasma Cell\n    - Respiratory Basal Cell\n\n- Middle panel: “Cell-o1 Training Strategy”\n  - Subpanel “(1) Reasoning Distillation”\n  - Shows example reasoning boxes labeled “Reasoning 1” and “Reasoning 2” with corresponding “Answer 1” and “Answer 2” diagrams.\n  - Reasoning 1 states cell 9 shows strong immunoglobulin expression and is marked “Reject.”\n  - Reasoning 2 states there are 10 cells and 10 candidate types, so each type must match exactly one cell, marked “Accept.”\n  - Subpanel “(2) Reinforcement Learning”\n  - Flow diagram includes:\n    - Input Question\n    - Policy Model\n    - Reference Model\n    - Reward Function\n    - Advantage\n    - Group Computation\n    - Reward\n    - Update\n    - KL\n    - Cold Start\n\n- Right panel: “Results”\n  - Subpanel “(1) Cell-level Accuracy” shown as a radial/polar bar chart.\n  - Values visible include 0.42, 0.58, 0.65, 0.68, 0.26, 0.26, 0.14, 0.09.\n  - Cell-o1 is highlighted with “(+5.65%).”\n  - Subpanel “(2) Batch-level Accuracy” shown as another radial/polar bar chart.\n  - Values visible include 0.03, 0.14, 0.19, 0.33, and multiple 0.00/0.01 labels.\n  - Cell-o1 is highlighted with “(+73.05%).”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell gene-expression examples and cell type annotations.\n- Cell types shown include T cells, endothelial cells, plasma cells, and respiratory basal cells.\n- Marker genes shown include MALAT1, RPS27, RPL10, RPL13, RPL41, EEF1A1, VIM, ADAMDEC1, CCL, and IGHA1/JCHAIN.\n\nModel interfaces and transformations:\n- The figure contrasts prior cell-by-cell LLM annotation with batch-level reasoning over multiple cells.\n- It depicts reasoning distillation where candidate reasoning-answer pairs are accepted or rejected.\n- It depicts reinforcement learning using a policy model, reference model, reward function, KL regularization, group computation, advantages, and rewards.\n\nFindings:\n- Cell-o1 appears to outperform comparison models in both cell-level and batch-level accuracy.\n- Reported improvement labels are +5.65% for cell-level accuracy and +73.05% for batch-level accuracy.\n- Legend compares Llama3.1-8B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama3.3-70B-Instruct, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o, o3-mini, o1, and Cell-o1."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Figure 2: CellPuzzles formulates cell type annotation as a batch-level reasoning task that integrates gene expression and contextual metadata, inspired by how experts annotate cells in practice.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic comparison of two cell type annotation workflows with four main panel regions arranged in two rows.\n\nTop row: A human expert task workflow. The left “Input” panel shows clustered single-cell data with colored clusters labeled Cluster 2, Cluster 3, Cluster 4, and Cluster N, each associated with marker gene lists such as “SFTPC, SFTPA1, SFTPB, NAPSA, ...”, “C1QA, APOE, MARCO, LYZ, ...”, and “IGHM, MZB1, JCHAIN, XBP1, ...”. The center panel labeled “Human Expert Task” shows an expert assigning a cell type label to each cluster using representative marker genes, contextual metadata, biological knowledge, and reference sources. The right “Output” panel lists assigned cell types including Pulmonary Alveolar Type 2 (AT2) Cells, CD8+ Cytotoxic T Cells, Lung Pericyte, Alveolar Macrophages, and Plasma Cells.\n\nBottom row: A “CellPuzzles Task” workflow. The left “Input” panel shows individual cells from clusters, with representative sampled cells labeled [Cell 1], [Cell 2], [Cell 3], [Cell 4], and [Cell N], each paired with gene lists such as “S100A9, TMSB10, RPL37, ...”, “MALAT1, FTL, B2M, ...”, “MALAT1, FTL, AKR1B10, ...”, “B2M, MT2A, TMSB4X, ...”, and “IGLC3, IGLC2, IGHM, ...”. Arrows indicate selected representative cells from clusters. The center panel labeled “CellPuzzles Task” shows a model/interface labeled “Cell-o1” receiving N cells, contextual metadata, and N candidate cell types, then reasoning to determine the optimal label assignment and provide reasoning traces. The right “Output” panel shows colored cell-to-label assignment lines connecting cells to candidate labels including CD4-positive, alpha-beta T Cell; CD8-positive, alpha-beta T Cell; Smooth Muscle Cell; Lung Macrophage; Non-classical Monocyte; Capillary Endothelial Cell; Plasma Cell; and Respiratory Basal Cell.\n\nBiological source objects include single-cell clusters, individual cells, marker genes, and immune/lung-related cell type labels. The figure depicts a transformation from marker gene or cell-level input data to annotated biological cell type outputs, comparing expert manual annotation with an automated CellPuzzles/Cell-o1 reasoning task."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Figure 3: Data distribution of CellPuzzles . (A) Age group distribution with male-female breakdown. (B) Sample distribution across a wide range of disease conditions. (C) Number of unique cell types observed across different tissues. For clarity, only the top 20 entries are shown in (B) and (C).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific chart with three labeled panels:\n\n(A) Age Group Distribution with Sex Breakdown  \nHorizontal bar/dot plot showing sample count by age group: 0-9 through 80-89 years. Beige horizontal bars represent total age-group sample counts, with circular markers split by sex: blue for Male and pink for Female. The largest visible age group is 60-69, followed by smaller counts in other age bins.\n\n(B) Sample Distribution across Diseases  \nHorizontal bar chart showing sample counts for disease or tissue-condition categories. Visible labels include lung adenocarcinoma, chronic obstructive condition, squamous cell lung cancer, Normal, interstitial lung disease, pulmonary fibrosis, non-small cell lung cancer, Crohn disease, lung large cell carcinoma, non-specific interstitial, chronic rhinosinusitis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, lymphangioleiomyomatosis, pleomorphic carcinoma, B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, pulmonary sarcoidosis, pneumonia, common variable immunodeficiency, acute myeloid leukemia, and type 1 diabetes mellitus. Lung adenocarcinoma has the largest count.\n\n(C) Number of Unique Cell Types per Tissue  \nHorizontal bar chart showing unique cell type counts by tissue. Visible tissues include ileum, lung, blood, duodenum, colon, bone marrow, nose, sigmoid colon, vermiform appendix, descending colon, transverse colon, lymph node, brain, caecum, jejunum, kidney, pleural effusion, liver, rectum, and adrenal tissue. Ileum and lung show the highest visible unique cell type counts.\n\nBiological source objects: human tissues, diseases/clinical conditions, age groups, sex categories, and cell-type diversity counts.  \nTransformations/interfaces: categorical metadata summarized into horizontal bar plots and sex-stratified dot/bar visualization. No microscopy, molecular structure, model architecture, or computational model interface is shown.  \nMain finding shown: the dataset/sample composition is uneven across age, disease, and tissue categories, with particularly high counts for lung adenocarcinoma in disease distribution and high unique cell-type counts for ileum/lung tissues."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Figure 4: Fine-grained analysis of model performance. (A) Cell-level and batch-level accuracy across varying numbers of cells per batch. (B) Distribution of per-batch cell-level accuracy across models.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot labeled `(A)` and `(B)`.\n\nPanel `(A)` contains two radar/spider plots comparing model accuracy by the number of cells per batch. The left radar plot is titled `Cell-level Acc by Number of Cells per Batch`; the right radar plot is titled `Batch-level Acc by Number of Cells per Batch`. Axes are labeled with batch cell counts `8`, `9`, `10`, `11`, `12`, `13`, `14`, and `15`. A legend lists multiple models or methods: `Llama3.1-8B-Instruct`, `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`, `Llama3.3-70B-Instruct`, `GPT-4o-mini`, `GPT-4o`, `o3-mini`, `o1`, and `Cell-o1`.\n\nPanel `(B)` is a violin plot titled `Per-batch Cell-level Acc Distribution`. The y-axis is labeled `Per-batch Cell-level Accuracy`, ranging from approximately `0.0` to `1.0`. The x-axis lists the same model names, rotated diagonally. The plot compares distributions of per-batch cell-level accuracy across models.\n\nNo biological source objects are directly visible in the image; it shows quantitative performance summaries rather than microscopy, cells, tissues, molecular diagrams, or biological specimens. The visible transformation is aggregation of model evaluation results into radar plots and violin distributions. The model interface is a comparison among named language/model systems, including Llama, Qwen, GPT, o-series models, and `Cell-o1`.\n\nMain visible finding: `Cell-o1` and `o1` show the highest per-batch cell-level accuracy distributions in panel `(B)`, while smaller or earlier models such as `Llama3.1-8B-Instruct` appear much lower. Panel `(A)` similarly shows larger radar coverage for stronger models, especially `Cell-o1`, `o1`, and `o3-mini` on cell-level accuracy, with generally lower and more variable batch-level accuracy."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Figure 5: Comparing training and inference dynamics across different configurations.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot with four labeled conditions: **(A)**, **(B)**, **(C)**, and **(D)**. Each condition contains two stacked line charts.\n\nVisible content:\n- Top row plots **Training Reward** in red on the left y-axis and **Response Length** in blue on the right y-axis, across training steps or iterations on the x-axis.\n- Bottom row plots **Test Performance** in red on the left y-axis and **Response Length** in blue on the right y-axis.\n- Each panel includes a small legend-like text box specifying:\n  - **Base: w/ SFT**\n  - **RL: GRPO** or **RL: PPO**\n  - **Reward: Batch Reward** or **Reward: Cell & Batch Reward**\n\nPanel details:\n- **(A)**: Base with SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward increases gradually; response length fluctuates around a high range. Test performance rises gradually.\n- **(B)**: Base with no SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward remains near zero; response length increases sharply late in training. Test performance stays near zero.\n- **(C)**: Base with SFT, RL method PPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward is noisy and variable; response length decreases over time. Test performance remains relatively flat around ~0.18–0.20.\n- **(D)**: Base with SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Cell & Batch Reward. Training reward increases to the highest visible level among panels; response length stays moderately high. Test performance increases overall with fluctuations.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, cells, tissues, or molecular diagrams are visible. The figure appears to report machine learning or reinforcement learning training outcomes, comparing model/interface conditions involving SFT, GRPO, PPO, reward design, training reward, test performance, and generated response length."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 6: Analysis of model reasoning behavior. (A) Step-by-step reasoning traces generated by each model for the same task. (B) Expert evaluation of reasoning quality comparing Cell-o1 with baseline models. (C) Proportion of traces exhibiting self-reflection and curriculum reasoning behaviors.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure comparing cell type assignment reasoning across language-model or agent systems.\n\nPanel (A) shows qualitative examples of cell type analysis and final assignments. It compares outputs from `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`, `GPT-4o`, `o1`, and `Cell-o1`. Each model interface lists predicted labels for `Cell 1` through `Cell 10`, with red and blue line annotations indicating `Right Prediction` and `Wrong Prediction`. Error categories are labeled across the top: `A. Incorrect Reasoning`, `B. No Reasoning`, `C. Plausible but Incorrect`, `D. Forced Assignment Error`, `E. Correct Reasoning, Wrong Conclusion`.\n\nThe biological source objects are single cells or cell clusters represented as numbered cells, with cell-type labels such as germinal center B cell, CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell, plasma cell, intestine goblet cell, T-helper 1 cell, capillary endothelial cell, vein endothelial cell, IgA plasma cell, activated CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell, and memory B cell. Text boxes describe cell-by-cell marker-gene reasoning, including genes such as `DEFA`, `SPP1`, `COL6`, `CCL5`, `IL1`, and `S100A4`.\n\nThe figure includes model/agent interfaces labeled `Self-Reflection Reasoning` and `Curriculum Reasoning`, shown with small icon graphics next to `Cell-o1`. Text callouts describe reasoning corrections and final assignments, including examples where cells are identified as plasma cells, CD4-positive alpha-beta T cells, and activated CD4-positive alpha-beta T cells.\n\nPanel (B) is a horizontal stacked bar chart comparing performance between `o1`, `GPT-4o`, and `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`. The legend shows `Model wins`, `Tie`, and `Cell-o1 wins`. Values indicate Cell-o1 wins most often for GPT-4o and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct comparisons, while o1 has a more balanced result.\n\nPanel (C) contains two bar charts: `Self-Reflection Reasoning` and `Curriculum Reasoning`. The x-axis compares `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`, `GPT-4o`, `o1`, and `Cell-o1`; the y-axis is `Proportion (%)`. Cell-o1 has the highest proportions in both charts, with visible values around `57%` for self-reflection reasoning and `77%` for curriculum reasoning."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 17, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow for single-cell gene expression data and annotation.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left panel labeled “A Batch of Cells from a Donor,” showing a syringe/sample collection icon and multiple colored circular cells.\n- Middle panel labeled “Donor Information,” with a small clinical metadata table:\n  - Tissue: Lung\n  - Disease: Lung Adenocarcinoma\n  - Age: 68\n  - Sex: Male\n  - Smoking Status: Non-smoker\n- Middle-right panel labeled “Gene Expression Profiles,” showing a heatmap-like matrix with rows for Cell 1, Cell 2, Cell 3, … Cell N and columns Gene 1 through Gene N.\n- Right panel labeled “Contextual Metadata,” containing a natural-language prompt: “The cell is from a male at the 68-year-old stage, originating from the lung. The patient has been diagnosed with Lung adenocarcinoma.”\n- Far-right panel labeled “Top Expressed Genes,” listing top genes per cell:\n  - Cell 1: S100A9, TMSB10, RPL37, RPL18A…\n  - Cell 2: MALAT1, FTL, B2M, CD74…\n  - Cell N: IGLC3, IGLC2, IGHM, MT-CO3,…\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Donor-derived cells, apparently single cells from lung tissue.\n- Clinical context indicates lung adenocarcinoma from a 68-year-old male non-smoker.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- Raw donor cell batch plus donor metadata are paired with gene expression profiles.\n- Metadata is converted into contextual natural-language text.\n- Expression profiles are summarized as top expressed genes per cell.\n\nFinding/message:\n- The figure illustrates integration of single-cell gene expression data with donor clinical metadata to produce cell-level contextual descriptions and top expressed gene lists."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small illustrative icon or logo, resembling a stylized target with an arrow/dart. It does not contain scientific figure elements such as panels, axes, labels, biological specimens, microscopy, model diagrams, transformations, or reported findings."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small cartoon-style ladybug or insect icon. It does not contain scientific panels, labels, biological microscopy/source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nSmall decorative icon showing a shield with medical-style symbols and a red/blue motif. It does not contain scientific figure panels, labels, biological specimens, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a tiny cropped icon or decorative graphic. It does not show discernible scientific figure panels, labels, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, microscopy, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small schematic/icon-like graphic, possibly a signpost or simple UI symbol. It does not show a scientific figure, experimental panels, biological source objects, model interfaces, transformations, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small stylized microscope icon/emoji-like graphic. It does not show scientific data, figure panels, labels, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 14, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nSmall decorative icon/illustration showing stylized lab or science-themed objects, including a flask-like shape and small geometric marks. It is not a scientific figure: no data panels, labels, axes, biological source objects, transformations, model interfaces, or findings are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 15, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nSmall stylized illustration/icon of a brain with two pink hemispheres and dark outlines. No visible scientific panels, labels, axes, scale bars, biological specimen imaging, model interface, transformations, or reported findings."]}, {"figure_index": 16, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small cartoon-style fruit icon, likely a strawberry, with simple colored shapes and no scientific panels, labels, biological microscopy/assay content, model interface, transformations, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 17, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small dec