ormations, or interpretable experimental findings."]}, {"figure_index": 23, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small cartoon-style icon: a clipboard or chart board with a red trend line, blue circular points, and a magnifying glass. It does not show scientific data panels, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, or specific findings."]}, {"figure_index": 24, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small icon or logo-like graphic, not a scientific figure. It contains stylized shapes resembling a vertical rocket or marker with red/yellow elements and gray/black outlines. There are no visible figure panels, labels, biological objects, model interfaces, plots, microscopy images, or scientific findings."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_d3b03caa65b1_43456f6aed10/sufficiency_selector/stderr.log:46:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 1, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small app/logo-style icon with a circular badge and simple colored shapes. It does not show scientific figure panels, labels, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, microscopy, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 3, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **a-g** describing **XunZi**, an AI biologist framework combining reasoning and multimodal biological data fusion.\n\nPanel **a** shows the overall architecture:\n- **XunZi-R reasoning module**:\n  - Inputs include **literature**, **gene and disease knowledge**, **Gene Ontology**, and **pathway annotations**.\n  - These feed into **biological knowledge continual pretraining** of a **Mistral 7B** model, producing a **vertical LLM for biology**.\n  - Additional sources include **public databases** and **literature review**, producing a **360K curated gene-disease mechanism dataset**.\n  - A logical reasoning interface is shown with prompt text: “Is gene XX functional in disease YY?” and response fields for **mechanism summary**, **impacted genes**, and **impacted pathways**.\n- **XunZi-M multimodal data fusion**:\n  - Inputs include **protein interactions**, **biological processes**, **knowledge graph**, and **613 TB multi-omics**.\n  - These are transformed through a stacked **fusion** module.\n  - The fused representation feeds a **multimodal foundation model**, **graph convolutional network (GCN)**, and **disease-specific fine-tuning**.\n- Outputs from XunZi-R and XunZi-M feed into **XunZi AI biologist**, which ranks gene-disease-output associations and includes **feedback** and **validation** by human users/clinicians.\n\nPanel **b** is a horizontal bar chart titled **Top 6 disease classes**, showing counts of gene-disease associations, with the largest category **Neoplasms**, followed by **Congenital abnormality**, **Digestive system diseases**, **Nervous system diseases**, **Cardiovascular disease**, and **Urologic and male genital diseases**.\n\nPanel **c** compares classification performance using confusion-matrix-style heatmaps:\n- **XunZi-R**: positive class 90.2%, negative class 94.0%, with smaller off-diagonal errors.\n- **GPT-4o**: positive class 54.1%, negative class 93.5%, with a much larger positive-to-negative error of 45.9%.\n\nPanel **d** shows two circular/radar-style performance plots for **Neoplasms** and **Nervous system diseases**, comparing **XunZi-R** and **GPT-4o**. XunZi-R is shown with a larger blue performance contour than the orange GPT-4o contour.\n\nPanel **e** shows multi-omics dataset scale for **Pan-cancer** and **Neurodegenerative diseases**, comparing **sample count** and **data size (TB)**. Visible labels include **14,380 samples / 579.3 TB** for pan-cancer and **9,897 samples / 33.9 TB** for neurodegenerative diseases.\n\nPanel **f** is an ROC-like curve for **Pan-cancer**, plotting **Sn** versus **1-Sp**, comparing models including **XunZi**, **XunZi-R**, **XunZi-M**, **GPT-4o**, **DNN**, and **SVM**. XunZi has the highest reported AUC, **0.85**.\n\nPanel **g** is a similar ROC-like curve for **Neurodegenerative diseases**, with the same model types. XunZi again performs best, with reported AUC **0.80**."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 4, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure with labeled panels `a-o`, combining model performance plots, gene/protein embedding maps, wet-lab validation assays, western blots, animal experiment schematics, and LLM-style query output.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- `a`: ROC curves for `NSCLC`, plotting sensitivity `Sn` versus `1-Sp`. Methods compared include `XunZi`, `XunZi-R`, `XunZi-M`, `GPT-4o`, `DNN`, and `SVM`, with AUC values shown in the legend. `XunZi` has the highest visible AUC, `0.86`.\n\n- `b`: t-SNE scatter plot with axes `t-SNE 1` and `t-SNE 2`. Points are labeled as `Reported`, `Predicted`, and `Others`. Gene labels include `EGFR` and `MYO1B`.\n\n- `c`: Bar chart for `NSCLC: A549` showing `Cell viability (versus NC)` after perturbing multiple genes. X-axis includes genes such as `MYO1B`, `NAV3`, `FLCN`, `WDR33`, `EIF2AK4`, `PIK3R4`, `BRCC3`, `ASXL1`, `EPO`, `GTF2H`, `HIPK2`, `GFPT1`, `MATR3`, `UCHL5`, `MARK3`, `NUP155`, `NR2C1`, `PGAM5`, `MAP2K5`, and `PPM1A`. Significance annotations are shown above selected bars.\n\n- `d`: Interface-like question-answer panel. A user asks whether `MYO1B` is involved in lung cancer functionally. The response attributed to `XunZi` states that `MYO1B` may influence lung cancer progression through cytoskeletal dynamics, cell migration, invasion, and pathways including PI3K/AKT and MAPK/ERK. It lists impacted genes including `AKT1`, `ERK`, `PIK3CA`, and `PTEN`.\n\n- `e`: Western blot panel comparing `Ctrl`, `siMYO1B-1`, and `siMYO1B-2`. Protein rows include `p-Erk`, `Erk`, `p-AKT`, `AKT`, `MYO1B`, and `Tubulin`, with molecular weights shown at left.\n\n- `f`: Quantification bar chart of `MYO1B/tubulin levels`, comparing `Ctrl`, `siMYO1B-1`, and `siMYO1B-2`.\n\n- `g`: Quantification bar chart of `p-Erk/Erk levels`, comparing `Ctrl`, `siMYO1B-1`, and `siMYO1B-2`.\n\n- `h`: Quantification bar chart of `p-AKT/AKT levels`, comparing `Ctrl`, `siMYO1B-1`, and `siMYO1B-2`.\n\n- `i`: Mouse experimental schematic. Day 1 treatment with `20 mg kg^-1 MPTP or saline, i.p., 4x`, followed by behavioral studies on `Days 19-22`.\n\n- `j`: Mouse experimental schematic. Day 1 `PFFs or PBS stereotaxic injection`, followed by behavioral studies on `Days 91-94`.\n\n- `k`: ROC curves for `PD-associated genes`, plotting `Sn` versus `1-Sp`. Methods include `XunZi`, `XunZi-R`, `XunZi-M`, `GPT-4o`, `DNN`, and `SVM`; `XunZi` has the highest visible AUC, `0.88`.\n\n- `l`: UMAP scatter plot with axes `UMAP 1` and `UMAP 2`, showing `Reported`, `Predicted`, and `Others`. A marker legend indicates `Kinase`. Labels include `LRRK2` and `PINK1`.\n\n- `m`: ROC curves for `PD-associated kinases`, again comparing `XunZi`, `XunZi-R`, `XunZi-M`, `GPT-4o`, `DNN`, and `SVM`. `XunZi` has the highest visible AUC, `0.92`.\n\n- `n`: t-SNE scatter plot with `Reported`, `Predicted`, and `Others` points. Labeled Parkinson’s disease-related kinase genes include `CHEK2`, `PINK1`, `STK13`, `IRAK4`, `DAPK2`, `LRRK2`, and `GRK5`.\n\n- `o`: Bar chart for `N2a` cells showing `Cell viability` across controls and genes including `mDpp+`, `Lrrk2`, `Cdk5`, `Pbk`, `Grk1`, `Chk2`, `Map2k2`, `Raf1`, `Trib3`, `Trib1`, `Irak4`, `Dapk2`, `Grk5`, `Ptk`, `Dyrk3`, `Map3k7`, `Stk33`, `Tssk6`, `Cdk2`, `Tnik`, `Stk32c`, and `Chk1`, with significance annotations.\n\nBiological source objects visible: NSCLC/A549 lung cancer cells, N2a neuronal cells, mouse Parkinson’s disease model schematics, genes/proteins/kinases including `MYO1B`, `EGFR`, `LRRK2`, `PINK1`, AKT/ERK signaling proteins, and PD-associated kinases.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces shown: ROC evaluation, t-SNE and UMAP embedding projections, predicted versus reported gene/kinase annotations, siRNA knockdown validation, western blot quantification, cell viability assays, and an LLM-like biomedical question-answer interface for `XunZi`.\n\nMain visible finding: `XunZi` outperforms comparator models in ROC analyses for NSCLC genes, PD-associated genes, and PD-associated kinases, and predicted candidates such as `MYO1B` and PD-related kinases are followed by experimental validation panels showing altered signaling or cell viability."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 6, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure labeled **a–m** about **CHEK2/Chk2 in Parkinson’s disease models**.\n\nPanel details:\n- **a**: Schematic comparison of model responses. A user asks whether **CHEK2** is functionally involved in Parkinson’s disease. **GPT-4o** answers “No,” while **XunZi** answers “Yes,” describing CHEK2 as linked to genome stability, DNA repair, oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, and listing impacted genes/pathways.\n- **b**: Western blot images from **SN** tissue comparing **saline** vs **MPTP** groups. Proteins shown: **Stk33, p-Irak4, Irak4, p-Chk2, Chk2, Tubulin**.\n- **c**: Bar plot quantifying **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels**, showing increased phosphorylation in **MPTP** vs saline.\n- **d**: Western blot images from **SN** comparing **PBS** vs **α-Syn PFFs** groups, with the same protein markers as panel b.\n- **e**: Bar plot quantifying **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels**, increased in **α-Syn PFFs** vs PBS.\n- **f**: Mouse experimental timeline: stereotaxic **AAV injection** on day 1, **MPTP or saline intraperitoneal injections** around day 28, behavioral studies on days 46–48, and immunoblotting/IHC on day 49.\n- **g**: Pole test bar plot. Groups include **AAV-control + saline**, **AAV-control + MPTP**, and **AAV-Chk2 KD + MPTP**. MPTP increases time to turn; Chk2 knockdown reduces impairment.\n- **h**: Rotarod test bar plot. MPTP reduces latency to fall; **AAV-Chk2 KD** improves performance relative to MPTP control.\n- **i**: Western blot from **SN** across AAV-control saline, AAV-control MPTP, and **AAV-Chk2 KD MPTP** groups. Proteins shown: **p-Chk2, Chk2, TH, Tubulin**.\n- **j**: Quantification of **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels**, increased by MPTP and reduced by **Chk2 knockdown**.\n- **k**: Quantification of **TH/tubulin levels**, decreased by MPTP and partially restored by **Chk2 knockdown**.\n- **l**: Immunohistochemistry images of dopaminergic neuron marker staining, comparing **AAV-vector + saline**, **AAV-vector + MPTP**, and **AAV-Chk2 KD + MPTP**.\n- **m**: Bar plot of **number of TH-positive neurons**, reduced in MPTP and increased with **AAV-Chk2 KD**.\n\nVisible biological source objects and assays:\n- Mouse Parkinson’s disease models using **MPTP** and **α-Syn PFFs**.\n- Brain region labeled **SN**, likely substantia nigra.\n- Molecular assays: western blotting, immunoblotting, immunohistochemistry.\n- Behavioral assays: pole test and rotarod test.\n- Viral manipulation: **AAV-mediated Chk2 knockdown**.\n\nVisible finding:\n- **Chk2 phosphorylation increases** in Parkinson’s disease models.\n- **AAV-Chk2 knockdown** reduces Chk2 activation, improves motor behavior, restores **TH** levels, and preserves **TH-positive neurons** relative to MPTP controls."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 8, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical research figure labeled **a-o**.\n\nVisible content:\n- **a, b:** Bar/scatter plots for behavioral assays in mouse treatment groups. Panel a is **Pole test**, y-axis “Time to turn (s)”. Panel b is **Rotarod test**, y-axis “Latency to fall (s)”. Groups include **Sham**, **Sham + 2 µg CCT**, **MPTP**, **MPTP + DNL-201**, **MPTP + 0.2 µg CCT**, and **MPTP + 2 µg CCT**. Statistical brackets with p-values are shown.\n- **c:** Western blot panel from **SN** tissue, with lanes for Sham, MPTP, MPTP + 0.2 µg CCT, and MPTP + 2 µg CCT. Blots include **p-Lrrk2, Lrrk2, p-p53, p53, TH, p-Chk2, Chk2, p-Chk1, Chk1, Tubulin**.\n- **d, e:** Quantification bar plots for **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels** and **p-Lrrk2/Lrrk2 levels** across Sham, MPTP, and CCT-treated MPTP groups.\n- **f:** Immunohistochemistry microscopy images of dopaminergic neuron staining in SN-like tissue regions. Groups include Sham, Sham + 2 µg CCT, MPTP, MPTP + DNL-201, MPTP + 0.2 µg CCT, and MPTP + 2 µg CCT. Insets/boxed regions and scale bars are visible.\n- **g:** Bar plot quantifying **No. of TH-positive neurons** for the same MPTP/CCT/DNL-201 groups.\n- **h, i:** Behavioral assay bar/scatter plots for an **α-syn PFFs** model. Panel h is **Pole test**, panel i is **Rotarod test**. Groups include **Sham**, **α-syn PFFs**, and **α-syn PFFs + 2 µg CCT**.\n- **j:** Western blot panel from **SN** for α-syn PFFs model, with blots labeled **p-p53, p53, p-α-Syn, α-Syn, TH, p-Chk2, Chk2, p-Chk1, Chk1, Tubulin**.\n- **k, l:** Quantification bar plots for **p-α-syn/α-syn levels** and **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels** in Sham, α-syn PFFs, and α-syn PFFs + 2 µg CCT.\n- **m:** Immunohistochemistry microscopy images for Sham, α-syn PFFs, and α-syn PFFs + 2 µg CCT, with boxed inset regions and scale bars.\n- **n:** Bar plot quantifying **No. of TH-positive neurons** in Sham, α-syn PFFs, and α-syn PFFs + 2 µg CCT.\n- **o:** Protein/gene interaction network diagram with nodes labeled as genes/kinases, including central highlighted kinase-like nodes such as **GRK5, IRAK4, CHK2, DAPK2, STK33**, and orange nodes including **PINK1** and **LRRK2**. Functional regions are labeled **Dopaminergic neuron function**, **DNA damage/cell cycle**, **Apoptosis/cell death**, **Mitochondrial dysfunction**, **Oxidative stress**, and **Autophagy/proteostasis**. Legend indicates **Kinase**, **Gene**, **PPI**, and **XunZi** edges.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Mouse or animal model **substantia nigra/SN** tissue.\n- Dopaminergic neurons marked by **TH-positive** staining.\n- Parkinsonian disease-related models involving **MPTP** and **α-synuclein preformed fibrils (α-syn PFFs)**.\n- Protein phosphorylation and expression targets including **LRRK2, p53, Chk2, Chk1, α-synuclein, TH**.\n\nMain visible findings:\n- MPTP and α-syn PFFs groups show impaired behavioral performance and reduced TH-positive neurons.\n- CCT treatment appears associated with improved behavioral metrics, restored TH-positive neuron counts, and reduced phosphorylation ratios such as **p-Lrrk2/Lrrk2**, **p-Chk2/Chk2**, and **p-α-syn/α-syn** in plotted comparisons.\n- Network panel connects Parkinson’s-related genes/kinases to pathways involving mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress, autophagy/proteostasis, dopaminergic neuron function, DNA damage/cell cycle, and apoptosis/cell death."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 20, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small black-and-white icon or pictogram of a person wearing a graduation cap next to a presentation board with a star/spark symbol. It does not contain scientific panels, biological objects, experimental data, model architecture, labels, plots, microscopy, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 20, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nA small black-and-white icon showing a person wearing a graduation cap next to a small presentation/board symbol. It does not contain scientific panels, data plots, biological objects, model interfaces, experimental transformations, labels, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 20, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nSmall UI-style icon showing a green check mark inside a square box with a beige/gray border. No scientific panels, labels, biological objects, transformations, model interfaces, or experimental findings are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 20, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small UI-style icon: a green check mark inside a gray circular outline on a light background. It does not contain scientific panels, labels, biological objects, model interfaces, transformations, plots, or experimental findings."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 20, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small UI-style icon: a green check mark inside a light square with a dark border. It does not contain scientific figure panels, labels, biological objects, model interfaces, transformations, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 20, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small checkmark icon inside a circular outline, likely a UI/status symbol. It contains no scientific panels, labels, biological objects, e