tions of the 26 MeSH disease categories”\n- X-axis label: “Disease category”\n- Y-axis label: “Num. of gene-disease associations”\n- Y-axis uses a logarithmic scale with ticks at approximately 1, 10, 100, 1000, and 10000.\n- Bars are ordered from highest to lowest count and colored with a red-to-blue gradient.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- Disease categories from MeSH.\n- Gene-disease associations, likely counts of associations between genes and diseases grouped by category.\n\nDisease categories shown include:\n- Neoplasms\n- Congenital abnormality\n- Digestive system diseases\n- Nervous system diseases\n- Cardiovascular diseases\n- Urologic and male genital diseases\n- Skin disease\n- Mental disorders\n- Metabolic diseases\n- Endocrine system diseases\n- Pathology\n- Immune system diseases\n- Musculoskeletal diseases\n- Respiratory tract diseases\n- Hemic and lymphatic diseases\n- Eye diseases\n- Bacterial infections and mycoses\n- Chemically induced disorders\n- Stomatognathic diseases\n- Otorhinolaryngologic diseases\n- Behavior and behavior mechanisms\n- Animal diseases\n- Wounds and injuries\n- Occupational diseases\n- Disorders of environmental origin\n- Psychological phenomena and processes\n\nVisible finding:\n- Neoplasms have the highest number of gene-disease associations, above 10,000.\n- Most major disease categories have hundreds to several thousand associations.\n- “Disorders of environmental origin” and “Psychological phenomena and processes” have the fewest associations, near the bottom of the logarithmic scale.\n\nNo multi-panel layout, biological microscopy images, molecular structures, workflow transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 18, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar-chart figure comparing model performance metrics.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: `Accuracy`\n- Top middle: `Recall`\n- Top right: `Specificity`\n- Bottom left: `F1 Score`\n- Bottom middle: `MCC`\n\nLabels/models shown along x-axes include:\n`Xunzi-R`, `o3`, `GPT-5`, `DeepSeek-R1`, `DeepSeek-V3`, `GPT-4.1`, `Claude Sonnet 4`, `Claude 3.7 Sonnet`, `Claude Sonnet 4.5`, `GPT-4o`, and `BioGPT`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. The figure contains only quantitative evaluation plots; no microscopy, organisms, tissues, cells, genes, proteins, pathways, or other biological objects are shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No visible image transformations or model interface diagrams.\n- The figure appears to summarize benchmark/classification performance across language models using standard metrics.\n\nFindings visible from the plots:\n- `Xunzi-R` appears highest or near-highest across Accuracy, Recall, F1 Score, and MCC.\n- Most models have high Specificity, close to 1.0, except `BioGPT`, which is very low in Specificity.\n- `BioGPT` has high Recall but poor Specificity and negative MCC.\n- Several Claude/GPT models show lower Recall and MCC than the leading models."]}, {"figure_index": 19, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel bar chart showing **Accuracy** on the y-axis, ranging from 0.0 to 1.0. The x-axis contains rotated model names, including **X-LMM-AR**, **GPT-5**, **GPT-4o**, **DeepSeek V3**, **DeepSeek R1**, **GPT-4.1**, **Claude Sonnet 4**, **Claude Sonnet 4.5**, **GPT-4.5**, and **BioGPT**.\n\nVisible finding: most models achieve roughly **0.6-0.85 accuracy**, with **X-LMM-AR** and GPT models near the highest values, while **BioGPT** is substantially lower at about **0.43**.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy panels, molecular structures, transformations, or model-interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 20, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel bar chart comparing model performance by `F1 Score`.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Y-axis labeled `F1 Score`, ranging from 0.0 to 1.0.\n- X-axis contains rotated model labels, including `funkz-ai`, `GPT-5`, `GPT-3`, `OpenAI o3`, `DeepSeek v3.1`, `DeepSeek R1`, `GPT-4`, `Claude Sonnet 4`, `Claude 3.7 Sonnet`, `gpt-4.5`, and `BioGPT`.\n- Bars are colored individually and sorted roughly from highest to lowest F1 score.\n- Highest scores are around 0.8 for `funkz-ai`, `GPT-5`, and `GPT-3`.\n- Lowest visible score is `BioGPT`, around 0.35.\n- No biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, or transformation pipelines are shown.\n- No model architecture/interface diagram is shown; the figure only reports comparative evaluation results."]}, {"figure_index": 21, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel bar chart showing **Recall** on the y-axis, ranging from 0.0 to 1.0. The x-axis lists model names rotated diagonally, including **Yuan2-K**, **GLM3**, **GPT-3.5**, **DeepSeek-V3**, **DeepSeek-R1**, **GPT-4o**, **Claude Sonnet**, **Claude 3.7 Sonnet**, **Claude Sonnet 4**, **Gemini 2.5**, and **BioGPT**.\n\nThere are no visible biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, tissue samples, or model-interface diagrams. The figure appears to compare recall performance across language models. The highest visible recall is for **BioGPT** at roughly 0.75, while lower values include **Claude Sonnet 4** around 0.25 and **Gemini 2.5** around 0.3."]}, {"figure_index": 22, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle bar-chart panel showing **MCC** on the y-axis, scaled roughly from **0.0 to 1.0**. The x-axis lists multiple models or methods, with rotated labels including **Xunzi-R**, **Q7**, **GPT-5**, **DeepSeek-V3**, **DeepSeek-R1**, **GPT-4.1**, **Claude 3.5 Haiku**, **Claude 3.5 Sonnet**, **Claude 3.5 Sonnet-R**, **Claude 4.5**, and **BioGPT**.\n\nVisible finding: the highest MCC values are for **Xunzi-R**, **Q7**, and **GPT-5**, around the mid-0.7 range. Several other models cluster around roughly **0.4-0.6**, while **BioGPT** appears below zero with a negative MCC.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, or experimental transformations are visible. The figure is a quantitative model-performance comparison using MCC as the metric."]}, {"figure_index": 23, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart comparing model performance across five classification metrics.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: `Accuracy`\n- Top middle: `Recall`\n- Top right: `Specificity`\n- Bottom left: `F1 Score`\n- Bottom middle: `MCC`\n\nX-axis labels across panels include models such as `Xunzi-R`, `o3`, `GPT-5`, `DeepSeek-R1`, `DeepSeek-V3`, `GPT-4.1`, `Claude Sonnet 4`, `Claude 3.7 Sonnet`, `Claude Sonnet 4.5`, `BigGPT`, and `GPT-4o`.\n\nBiological source objects: none visible. The figure contains no microscopy, anatomy, sequencing plots, or biological specimens.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: the figure appears to summarize model outputs as quantitative evaluation metrics, shown as colored vertical bars. No workflow diagram, data transformation pipeline, or model architecture is shown.\n\nFindings visible from the chart:\n- `Xunzi-R`, `o3`, and `GPT-5` generally rank highly across accuracy, F1 score, and MCC.\n- Specificity is near 1.0 for most models, but `BigGPT` has extremely low specificity.\n- `BigGPT` shows high recall but poor specificity and negative or near-zero MCC.\n- `GPT-4o` appears low on recall and MCC compared with most other models."]}, {"figure_index": 24, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel bar chart showing **Specificity** on the y-axis from 0.0 to 1.0. The x-axis lists model names, rotated diagonally: **XGBoost-R**, **GPT-3**, **GPT-3.5**, **DeepSeek-V3**, **DeepSeek-R1**, **GPT-4**, **Claude 3.5**, **Claude 3.7 Sonnet**, **Claude 3 Opus**, **Sonnet 4**, and **BioGPT**.\n\nMost models have high specificity near ~0.9-1.0, while **BioGPT** has much lower specificity, around ~0.1. Bars are individually colored. No biological source objects, image transformations, microscopy, anatomical structures, or model interface screenshots are visible; this is a quantitative performance comparison plot."]}, {"figure_index": 25, "page_no": 24, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 4 | Comparative evaluation of logical reasoning capabilities between XunZi-R and GPT-4o. a -x Radar plots showing reasoning performance across 24 MeSH disease categories, excluding neoplasms and nervous system diseases. Outer green bars indicate the recall of XunZi-R for each individual disease subtype within a category.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with 24 circular radar-style plots labeled **a–x**. Each panel corresponds to a medical or biological disease category:\n\na. Bacterial infections and mycoses  \nb. Psychological phenomena and processes  \nc. Digestive system diseases  \nd. Stomatognathic diseases  \ne. Mental disorders  \nf. Respiratory tract diseases  \ng. Otorhinolaryngologic diseases  \nh. Musculoskeletal diseases  \ni. Eye diseases  \nj. Urologic and male genital diseases  \nk. Cardiovascular diseases  \nl. Hemic and lymphatic diseases  \nm. Congenital abnormality  \nn. Skin diseases  \no. Metabolic diseases  \np. Endocrine system diseases  \nq. Immune system diseases  \nr. Disorders of environmental origin  \ns. Animal diseases  \nt. Pathology  \nu. Occupational diseases  \nv. Chemically induced disorders  \nw. Wounds and injuries  \nx. Behavior and behavior mechanisms\n\nEach panel contains an outer green circular histogram/ring with many radial ticks or bars and an inner radar/spider plot with overlaid colored polygon traces. The visible legend identifies two model/interface traces: **XunZi-R** in blue and **GPT-4o** in orange. The plots appear to compare model performance or scores across subcategories within each disease/biomedical class, using radial axes with visible numeric markings such as 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.\n\nBiological/medical source objects represented are disease and disorder categories rather than microscopy objects or anatomical images. The figure visually reports comparative findings across biomedical taxonomy groups: XunZi-R and GPT-4o have similar radar profiles in many categories, with category-specific differences visible in the inner polygons. No exact quantitative values can be read reliably from the extracted image alone."]}, {"figure_index": 26, "page_no": 25, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 5 | See next page for caption.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle visible panel labeled **a**. It is a bar chart showing **F1 score** on the y-axis, ranging from 0.0 to 1.0, comparing multiple language/model systems on the x-axis.\n\nVisible x-axis labels:\n- XunZi-R\n- Claude 3.7 Sonnet\n- Claude Sonnet 4\n- Claude Sonnet 4.5\n- DeepSeek-R1\n- DeepSeek-V3\n- GPT-4o\n- GPT-5\n- o3\n- GPT-4\n\nThe tallest bar is **XunZi-R**, around **0.90 F1 score**. Other models cluster around approximately **0.64-0.69**, with **GPT-4o** and **GPT-4** among the higher non-XunZi-R values.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, tissue samples, transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 27, "page_no": 27, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel stacked bar chart comparing counts of molecules across diseases and omics types.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Y-axis: “Num. of molecules”, ranging up to slightly above 50,000.\n- X-axis: disease categories, mostly cancers followed by neurodegenerative diseases.\n- Legend title: “Omics type”.\n- Color encoding:\n  - Blue: Transcriptomics\n  - Green: Proteomics\n  - Orange: Phosphoproteomics\n- Group labels beneath the x-axis:\n  - “Cancer”\n  - “Neurodegenerative diseases”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Disease entities including melanoma, colon cancer, breast cancer, rectal cancer, glioblastoma, esophageal cancer, acute myeloid leukemia, lung adenocarcinoma, thyroid cancer, ovarian cancer, pancreatic cancer, liver cancer, bladder cancer, thymoma, sarcoma, stomach cancer, testicular cancer, kidney papillary cell carcinoma, lung squamous cell carcinoma, Alzheimer’s disease, Huntington’s disease, Lewy body dementia, Parkinson’s disease, and progressive supranuclear palsy.\n- Molecular measurement categories from omics datasets: transcriptomics, proteomics, and phosphoproteomics.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- No microscopy, imaging transformation, computational model architecture, or model interface is shown.\n- The data are transformed into stacked categorical bars, where total bar height represents the combined number of molecules across omics types for each disease.\n\nFindings visible from the chart:\n- Alzheimer’s disease has the highest total molecule count, exceeding 50,000, with large transcriptomics and proteomics contributions and a smaller phosphoproteomics segment.\n- Most cancer categories have total molecule counts roughly between 20,000 and 40,000.\n- Neurodegenerative diseases vary substantially, with Alzheimer’s disease highest and Parkinson’s disease lower than Alzheimer’s and progressive supranuclear palsy.\n- Transcriptomics and proteomics are present for all shown diseases; phosphoproteomics appears as an additional orange segment for many but not all categories."]}, {"figure_index": 28, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a bar chart labeled panel “a” with the title “Pan-cancer-AUPR”. The y-axis is labeled “AUPR” and ranges from 0.0 to about 0.8+. The x-axis compares six methods/models: XunZi, XunZi-R, XunZi-M, DNN, GPT-4o, and SVM.\n\nVisible values above bars:\n- XunZi: 0.799\n- XunZi-R: 0.736\n- XunZi-M: 0.716\n- DNN: 0.599\n- GPT-4o: 0.576\n- SVM: 0.550\n\nNo biological source objects, micr