, or interpretable experimental findings."]}, {"figure_index": 23, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small cartoon-style icon: a clipboard or chart board with a red trend line, blue circular points, and a magnifying glass. It does not show scientific data panels, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, or specific findings."]}, {"figure_index": 24, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small icon or logo-like graphic, not a scientific figure. It contains stylized shapes resembling a vertical rocket or marker with red/yellow elements and gray/black outlines. There are no visible figure panels, labels, biological objects, model interfaces, plots, microscopy images, or scientific findings."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_a62125696693_e71435b6733d/adjudicator/stderr.log:39:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 1, "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of this work. Cell-o1 achieves state-of-the-art on the CellPuzzles task.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific schematic and results figure about the “CellPuzzles” task and a Cell-o1 training strategy.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: “The CellPuzzles Task”\n  - Subpanel “(1) Previous LLM-based Annotation (Cell by Cell; No reasoning)”\n  - Input example lists marker genes: “MALAT1, RPS27, RPL10, RPL13, RPL41, EEF1A1, …”\n  - Output: “This is a CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell.”\n  - Subpanel “(2) CellPuzzles (Batch-level; Reasoning required)”\n  - Input asks to jointly assign one unique cell type to each cell in a batch using expressed genes and donor context, and provide reasoning plus final answer.\n  - Output shows reasoning text and a small cell-to-cell assignment diagram with labels including:\n    - CD4-positive, alpha-beta T Cell\n    - CD8-positive, alpha-beta T Cell\n    - Capillary Endothelial Cell\n    - Plasma Cell\n    - Respiratory Basal Cell\n\n- Middle panel: “Cell-o1 Training Strategy”\n  - Subpanel “(1) Reasoning Distillation”\n  - Shows example reasoning boxes labeled “Reasoning 1” and “Reasoning 2” with corresponding “Answer 1” and “Answer 2” diagrams.\n  - Reasoning 1 states cell 9 shows strong immunoglobulin expression and is marked “Reject.”\n  - Reasoning 2 states there are 10 cells and 10 candidate types, so each type must match exactly one cell, marked “Accept.”\n  - Subpanel “(2) Reinforcement Learning”\n  - Flow diagram includes:\n    - Input Question\n    - Policy Model\n    - Reference Model\n    - Reward Function\n    - Advantage\n    - Group Computation\n    - Reward\n    - Update\n    - KL\n    - Cold Start\n\n- Right panel: “Results”\n  - Subpanel “(1) Cell-level Accuracy” shown as a radial/polar bar chart.\n  - Values visible include 0.42, 0.58, 0.65, 0.68, 0.26, 0.26, 0.14, 0.09.\n  - Cell-o1 is highlighted with “(+5.65%).”\n  - Subpanel “(2) Batch-level Accuracy” shown as another radial/polar bar chart.\n  - Values visible include 0.03, 0.14, 0.19, 0.33, and multiple 0.00/0.01 labels.\n  - Cell-o1 is highlighted with “(+73.05%).”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell gene-expression examples and cell type annotations.\n- Cell types shown include T cells, endothelial cells, plasma cells, and respiratory basal cells.\n- Marker genes shown include MALAT1, RPS27, RPL10, RPL13, RPL41, EEF1A1, VIM, ADAMDEC1, CCL, and IGHA1/JCHAIN.\n\nModel interfaces and transformations:\n- The figure contrasts prior cell-by-cell LLM annotation with batch-level reasoning over multiple cells.\n- It depicts reasoning distillation where candidate reasoning-answer pairs are accepted or rejected.\n- It depicts reinforcement learning using a policy model, reference model, reward function, KL regularization, group computation, advantages, and rewards.\n\nFindings:\n- Cell-o1 appears to outperform comparison models in both cell-level and batch-level accuracy.\n- Reported improvement labels are +5.65% for cell-level accuracy and +73.05% for batch-level accuracy.\n- Legend compares Llama3.1-8B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama3.3-70B-Instruct, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o, o3-mini, o1, and Cell-o1."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Figure 2: CellPuzzles formulates cell type annotation as a batch-level reasoning task that integrates gene expression and contextual metadata, inspired by how experts annotate cells in practice.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic comparison of two cell type annotation workflows with four main panel regions arranged in two rows.\n\nTop row: A human expert task workflow. The left “Input” panel shows clustered single-cell data with colored clusters labeled Cluster 2, Cluster 3, Cluster 4, and Cluster N, each associated with marker gene lists such as “SFTPC, SFTPA1, SFTPB, NAPSA, ...”, “C1QA, APOE, MARCO, LYZ, ...”, and “IGHM, MZB1, JCHAIN, XBP1, ...”. The center panel labeled “Human Expert Task” shows an expert assigning a cell type label to each cluster using representative marker genes, contextual metadata, biological knowledge, and reference sources. The right “Output” panel lists assigned cell types including Pulmonary Alveolar Type 2 (AT2) Cells, CD8+ Cytotoxic T Cells, Lung Pericyte, Alveolar Macrophages, and Plasma Cells.\n\nBottom row: A “CellPuzzles Task” workflow. The left “Input” panel shows individual cells from clusters, with representative sampled cells labeled [Cell 1], [Cell 2], [Cell 3], [Cell 4], and [Cell N], each paired with gene lists such as “S100A9, TMSB10, RPL37, ...”, “MALAT1, FTL, B2M, ...”, “MALAT1, FTL, AKR1B10, ...”, “B2M, MT2A, TMSB4X, ...”, and “IGLC3, IGLC2, IGHM, ...”. Arrows indicate selected representative cells from clusters. The center panel labeled “CellPuzzles Task” shows a model/interface labeled “Cell-o1” receiving N cells, contextual metadata, and N candidate cell types, then reasoning to determine the optimal label assignment and provide reasoning traces. The right “Output” panel shows colored cell-to-label assignment lines connecting cells to candidate labels including CD4-positive, alpha-beta T Cell; CD8-positive, alpha-beta T Cell; Smooth Muscle Cell; Lung Macrophage; Non-classical Monocyte; Capillary Endothelial Cell; Plasma Cell; and Respiratory Basal Cell.\n\nBiological source objects include single-cell clusters, individual cells, marker genes, and immune/lung-related cell type labels. The figure depicts a transformation from marker gene or cell-level input data to annotated biological cell type outputs, comparing expert manual annotation with an automated CellPuzzles/Cell-o1 reasoning task."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Figure 3: Data distribution of CellPuzzles . (A) Age group distribution with male-female breakdown. (B) Sample distribution across a wide range of disease conditions. (C) Number of unique cell types observed across different tissues. For clarity, only the top 20 entries are shown in (B) and (C).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific chart with three labeled panels:\n\n(A) Age Group Distribution with Sex Breakdown  \nHorizontal bar/dot plot showing sample count by age group: 0-9 through 80-89 years. Beige horizontal bars represent total age-group sample counts, with circular markers split by sex: blue for Male and pink for Female. The largest visible age group is 60-69, followed by smaller counts in other age bins.\n\n(B) Sample Distribution across Diseases  \nHorizontal bar chart showing sample counts for disease or tissue-condition categories. Visible labels include lung adenocarcinoma, chronic obstructive condition, squamous cell lung cancer, Normal, interstitial lung disease, pulmonary fibrosis, non-small cell lung cancer, Crohn disease, lung large cell carcinoma, non-specific interstitial, chronic rhinosinusitis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, lymphangioleiomyomatosis, pleomorphic carcinoma, B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, pulmonary sarcoidosis, pneumonia, common variable immunodeficiency, acute myeloid leukemia, and type 1 diabetes mellitus. Lung adenocarcinoma has the largest count.\n\n(C) Number of Unique Cell Types per Tissue  \nHorizontal bar chart showing unique cell type counts by tissue. Visible tissues include ileum, lung, blood, duodenum, colon, bone marrow, nose, sigmoid colon, vermiform appendix, descending colon, transverse colon, lymph node, brain, caecum, jejunum, kidney, pleural effusion, liver, rectum, and adrenal tissue. Ileum and lung show the highest visible unique cell type counts.\n\nBiological source objects: human tissues, diseases/clinical conditions, age groups, sex categories, and cell-type diversity counts.  \nTransformations/interfaces: categorical metadata summarized into horizontal bar plots and sex-stratified dot/bar visualization. No microscopy, molecular structure, model architecture, or computational model interface is shown.  \nMain finding shown: the dataset/sample composition is uneven across age, disease, and tissue categories, with particularly high counts for lung adenocarcinoma in disease distribution and high unique cell-type counts for ileum/lung tissues."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Figure 4: Fine-grained analysis of model performance. (A) Cell-level and batch-level accuracy across varying numbers of cells per batch. (B) Distribution of per-batch cell-level accuracy across models.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot labeled `(A)` and `(B)`.\n\nPanel `(A)` contains two radar/spider plots comparing model accuracy by the number of cells per batch. The left radar plot is titled `Cell-level Acc by Number of Cells per Batch`; the right radar plot is titled `Batch-level Acc by Number of Cells per Batch`. Axes are labeled with batch cell counts `8`, `9`, `10`, `11`, `12`, `13`, `14`, and `15`. A legend lists multiple models or methods: `Llama3.1-8B-Instruct`, `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`, `Llama3.3-70B-Instruct`, `GPT-4o-mini`, `GPT-4o`, `o3-mini`, `o1`, and `Cell-o1`.\n\nPanel `(B)` is a violin plot titled `Per-batch Cell-level Acc Distribution`. The y-axis is labeled `Per-batch Cell-level Accuracy`, ranging from approximately `0.0` to `1.0`. The x-axis lists the same model names, rotated diagonally. The plot compares distributions of per-batch cell-level accuracy across models.\n\nNo biological source objects are directly visible in the image; it shows quantitative performance summaries rather than microscopy, cells, tissues, molecular diagrams, or biological specimens. The visible transformation is aggregation of model evaluation results into radar plots and violin distributions. The model interface is a comparison among named language/model systems, including Llama, Qwen, GPT, o-series models, and `Cell-o1`.\n\nMain visible finding: `Cell-o1` and `o1` show the highest per-batch cell-level accuracy distributions in panel `(B)`, while smaller or earlier models such as `Llama3.1-8B-Instruct` appear much lower. Panel `(A)` similarly shows larger radar coverage for stronger models, especially `Cell-o1`, `o1`, and `o3-mini` on cell-level accuracy, with generally lower and more variable batch-level accuracy."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Figure 5: Comparing training and inference dynamics across different configurations.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot with four labeled conditions: **(A)**, **(B)**, **(C)**, and **(D)**. Each condition contains two stacked line charts.\n\nVisible content:\n- Top row plots **Training Reward** in red on the left y-axis and **Response Length** in blue on the right y-axis, across training steps or iterations on the x-axis.\n- Bottom row plots **Test Performance** in red on the left y-axis and **Response Length** in blue on the right y-axis.\n- Each panel includes a small legend-like text box specifying:\n  - **Base: w/ SFT**\n  - **RL: GRPO** or **RL: PPO**\n  - **Reward: Batch Reward** or **Reward: Cell & Batch Reward**\n\nPanel details:\n- **(A)**: Base with SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward increases gradually; response length fluctuates around a high range. Test performance rises gradually.\n- **(B)**: Base with no SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward remains near zero; response length increases sharply late in training. Test performance stays near zero.\n- **(C)**: Base with SFT, RL method PPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward is noisy and variable; response length decreases over time. Test performance remains relatively flat around ~0.18–0.20.\n- **(D)**: Base with SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Cell & Batch Reward. Training reward increases to the highest visible level among panels; response length stays moderately high. Test performance increases overall with fluctuations.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, cells, tissues, or molecular diagrams are visible. The figure appears to report machine learning or reinforcement learning training outcomes, comparing model/interface conditions involving SFT, GRPO, PPO, reward design, training reward, test performance, and generated response length."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 6: Analysis of model reasoning behavior. (A) Step-by-step reasoning traces generated by each model for the same task. (B) Expert evaluation of reasoning quality comparing Cell-o1 with baseline models. (C) Proportion of traces exhibiting self-reflection and curriculum reasoning behaviors.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure comparing cell type assignment reasoning across language-model or agent systems.\n\nPanel (A) shows qualitative examples of cell type analysis and final assignments. It compares outputs from `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`, `GPT-4o`, `o1`, and `Cell-o1`. Each model interface lists predicted labels for `Cell 1` through `Cell 10`, with red and blue line annotations indicating `Right Prediction` and `Wrong Prediction`. Error categories are labeled across the top: `A. Incorrect Reasoning`, `B. No Reasoning`, `C. Plausible but Incorrect`, `D. Forced Assignment Error`, `E. Correct Reasoning, Wrong Conclusion`.\n\nThe biological source objects are single cells or cell clusters represented as numbered cells, with cell-type labels such as germinal center B cell, CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell, plasma cell, intestine goblet cell, T-helper 1 cell, capillary endothelial cell, vein endothelial cell, IgA plasma cell, activated CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell, and memory B cell. Text boxes describe cell-by-cell marker-gene reasoning, including genes such as `DEFA`, `SPP1`, `COL6`, `CCL5`, `IL1`, and `S100A4`.\n\nThe figure includes model/agent interfaces labeled `Self-Reflection Reasoning` and `Curriculum Reasoning`, shown with small icon graphics next to `Cell-o1`. Text callouts describe reasoning corrections and final assignments, including examples where cells are identified as plasma cells, CD4-positive alpha-beta T cells, and activated CD4-positive alpha-beta T cells.\n\nPanel (B) is a horizontal stacked bar chart comparing performance between `o1`, `GPT-4o`, and `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`. The legend shows `Model wins`, `Tie`, and `Cell-o1 wins`. Values indicate Cell-o1 wins most often for GPT-4o and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct comparisons, while o1 has a more balanced result.\n\nPanel (C) contains two bar charts: `Self-Reflection Reasoning` and `Curriculum Reasoning`. The x-axis compares `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`, `GPT-4o`, `o1`, and `Cell-o1`; the y-axis is `Proportion (%)`. Cell-o1 has the highest proportions in both charts, with visible values around `57%` for self-reflection reasoning and `77%` for curriculum reasoning."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 17, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow for single-cell gene expression data and annotation.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left panel labeled “A Batch of Cells from a Donor,” showing a syringe/sample collection icon and multiple colored circular cells.\n- Middle panel labeled “Donor Information,” with a small clinical metadata table:\n  - Tissue: Lung\n  - Disease: Lung Adenocarcinoma\n  - Age: 68\n  - Sex: Male\n  - Smoking Status: Non-smoker\n- Middle-right panel labeled “Gene Expression Profiles,” showing a heatmap-like matrix with rows for Cell 1, Cell 2, Cell 3, … Cell N and columns Gene 1 through Gene N.\n- Right panel labeled “Contextual Metadata,” containing a natural-language prompt: “The cell is from a male at the 68-year-old stage, originating from the lung. The patient has been diagnosed with Lung adenocarcinoma.”\n- Far-right panel labeled “Top Expressed Genes,” listing top genes per cell:\n  - Cell 1: S100A9, TMSB10, RPL37, RPL18A…\n  - Cell 2: MALAT1, FTL, B2M, CD74…\n  - Cell N: IGLC3, IGLC2, IGHM, MT-CO3,…\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Donor-derived cells, apparently single cells from lung tissue.\n- Clinical context indicates lung adenocarcinoma from a 68-year-old male non-smoker.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- Raw donor cell batch plus donor metadata are paired with gene expression profiles.\n- Metadata is converted into contextual natural-language text.\n- Expression profiles are summarized as top expressed genes per cell.\n\nFinding/message:\n- The figure illustrates integration of single-cell gene expression data with donor clinical metadata to produce cell-level contextual descriptions and top expressed gene lists."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small illustrative icon or logo, resembling a stylized target with an arrow/dart. It does not contain scientific figure elements such as panels, axes, labels, biological specimens, microscopy, model diagrams, transformations, or reported findings."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small cartoon-style ladybug or insect icon. It does not contain scientific panels, labels, biological microscopy/source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nSmall decorative icon showing a shield with medical-style symbols and a red/blue motif. It does not contain scientific figure panels, labels, biological specimens, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a tiny cropped icon or decorative graphic. It does not show discernible scientific figure panels, labels, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, microscopy, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small schematic/icon-like graphic, possibly a signpost or simple UI symbol. It does not show a scientific figure, experimental panels, biological source objects, model interfaces, transformations, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small stylized microscope icon/emoji-like graphic. It does not show scientific data, figure panels, labels, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 14, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nSmall decorative icon/illustration showing stylized lab or science-themed objects, including a flask-like shape and small geometric marks. It is not a scientific figure: no data panels, labels, axes, biological source objects, transformations, model interfaces, or findings are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 15, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nSmall stylized illustration/icon of a brain with two pink hemispheres and dark outlines. No visible scientific panels, labels, axes, scale bars, biological specimen imaging, model interface, transformations, or reported findings."]}, {"figure_index": 16, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small cartoon-style fruit icon, likely a strawberry, with simple colored shapes and no scientific panels, labels, biological microscopy/assay content, model interface, transformations, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 17, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small decorative/illustrative icon or symbol. It does not show scientific figure panels, labels, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, axes, legends, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 18, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small decorative icon resembling an artist’s paint palette with colored paint spots and a brush-like detail. It does not contain scientific panels, labels, biological objects, data visualizations, model interfaces, transformations, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 19, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small cartoon-style icon/emoji, not a scientific figure. It shows a pink animal-like character with decorative elements and no scientific panels, labels, axes, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 20, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nSmall schematic/icon-like image showing connected UI/device blocks, including a monitor-like screen, small nodes, and a cube/package symbol. It does not present scientific data, biological objects, labeled panels, experimental transformations, model interfaces, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 21, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small blue stopwatch/timer-style icon or cartoon graphic. It does not show scientific figure panels, labels, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, microscopy, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 22, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small decorative icon or emoji-like graphic: a stylized document or clipboard with red/black circular elements and small tool-like shapes. It does not contain scientific panels, axes, labels, biological objects, model diagrams, transformations, or interpretable experimental findings."]}, {"figure_index": 23, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small cartoon-style icon: a clipboard or chart board with a red trend line, blue circular points, and a magnifying glass. It does not show scientific data panels, biological source objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, or specific findings."]}, {"figure_index": 24, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small icon or logo-like graphic, not a scientific figure. It contains stylized shapes resembling a vertical rocket or marker with red/yellow elements and gray/black outlines. There are no visible figure panels, labels, biological objects, model interfaces, plots, microscopy images, or scientific findings."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_4361ad5c9fbb_5b9d8ad8c72c/specificity_selector/prompt.txt:15:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 Overview of X-Atlas/Pisces and X-Cell. (A) Schematic of X-Atlas/Pisces, which contains seven genome-scale CRISPRi Perturb-seq screens in HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC MultiDifferentiation (iPSC Multi-Diff) (left). Size comparison of X-Atlas/Orion (8M cells) versus X-Atlas/Pisces (25.6M cells) (middle). UMAP of cells in X-Atlas/Pisces, colored by screen. For visualization, the dataset was downsampled to 1M total cells while preserving the relative proportions of each original screen. Within this subset, 5% of the cells are nontargeting controls and the remainder are perturbed cells. (B) X-Cell combines diffusion LM training and cross-attention to prior knowledge to predict perturbed cell states from control cell sets. Six prior knowledge sources include pre-trained embeddings from LLM [19], ESM-2 [38], STRING [40, 41], DepMap [42], JUMP-Cell Painting [43], and scGPT [8] (Section 4.1.2). The main architecture consists of stacked self-attention blocks that encode control cell sets, with interleaved crossattention to prior knowledge embeddings. X-Cell enables diffusion-style training by randomly replacing 25%, 50%, or 75% of control gene expression values with ground-truth perturbed values, and providing a binary Diff Mask to indicate the revealed positions (Section 4.1.3). (C) During inference, X-Cell refines predictions via iterative diffusion by remasking part of its output as input for subsequent generation steps. (D) X-Cell scales from 55M parameters (X-Cell) to 4.9B parameters (X-Cell-Ultra), exceeding the size of existing single-cell foundation models.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure describing the X-Cell model and datasets.\n\nPanel A: Dataset and screen overview. Shows “X-Atlas” with two sources: Orion (8M) and Pisces (25.6M). Screens listed include HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Purposes are grouped as Architecture Validation, Context Generalization, and Cell Type Generalization. A stacked bar chart compares cell counts in Orion and Pisces by screen, and a UMAP labeled “Cell x Screen” shows colored cell clusters by dataset/screen.\n\nPanel B: X-Cell architecture schematic. Input consists of X-Atlas/Pisces perturbation examples. Prior knowledge is injected through “Gene X KD” using sources/interfaces labeled LLM, ESM2, STRING, DepMap, JUMP-Cell Painting, and scGPT. The central architecture shows control and perturbed cell sets with gene tokens, expression tokens, and diffusion masks. A “Diffusion LM Training” block indicates replacing expression values with perturbed expression for t > 0 using a diffusion mask. The model combines control encoding and perturbation conditioning, with cross-attention over prior knowledge embeddings, self-attention layers, and a perturbation decoder. Translational applications shown at right are Context Generalization, Perturbation Prediction, and Mechanism of Action.\n\nPanel C: Iterative prediction/remasking workflow for “Gene X KD.” Starts from input all-control expression plus diffusion mask at t=0, predicts intermediate values, remasks with 50% predicted and 50% control values at t=0.5, then produces a final prediction at t=1.0. Color legend indicates control versus predicted values.\n\nPanel D: Model parameter comparison bar chart. Models shown include scGPT, X-Cell, Geneformer V2, STATE-SE, scFoundation, TranscriptFormer, Tahoe-x1, and X-Cell-Ultra. Bars indicate parameter counts and years. X-Cell is labeled 55M 2026, X-Cell-Ultra 4.9B 2026, and an annotation indicates “92x.”"]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 X-Atlas/Pisces enables identification of context-dependent and context-independent perturbations. (A) Distribution of median UMIs per cell (top), median genes per cell (middle), and median on-target KD % (bottom) across GEMs for each screen in X-Atlas/Pisces. Black lines indicate median. (B) Recall of annotated gene pairs from CORUM (left) and STRING (right), calculated across all possible subsets of 1-7 screens. Error bars represent standard deviation across subsets. (C) Schematic of binary classifier for classifying perturbed cells from non-targeting controls (NTCs) (left). Input is a balanced mixture of perturbed cells for a given perturbation and NTCs. Model is logistic regression with L1-penalization (LASSO). Outputs are significance of the perturbation as measured by a one-sided binomial test and the features (genes) used to make the prediction. Percentage of perturbed cells with a significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) as measured by a LASSO binary classifier (right). (D) Heatmap of F1 scores for perturbations in X-Atlas/Pisces clustered based on F1 scores across screens. Rows represent perturbations (n=6,412) that pass the following filtering criteria: (1) present in ≥ 3 screens, (2) ≥ 10 cells in each screen the perturbation was present in, (3) significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) in at least 1 screen, and (4) F1 score ≥ 0.75 in at least 1 screen. Grey represents absence of a perturbation in that screen. Columns represent screens that are hierarchically clustered by similarity in F1 scores (colorbar). (E) Mean F1 score versus mean Jaccard index for perturbations across screens. Points are colored by F1 score coefficient of variation (CV) (colorbar). (F) UMAP of significant perturbations with at least one non-self feature (n=35,016) colored by screen. Clusters are labeled with CORUM complex enrichments, colored black if ≤ 50% of perturbations are from any individual screen, or the majority screen color otherwise. Inset structures of representative protein complexes are color-coded by whether the protein is targeted by a genetic perturbation in the indicated clusters (green shades are present, gray is absent).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-F, showing analyses of biological screening data.\n\nPanel A: Violin plots comparing distributions across multiple biological sources or screen/cell types: HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Metrics shown are UMIs, Genes, and KO (%).\n\nPanel B: Bar plots of recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB. Bars are colored by number of screens, with recall increasing across screen counts.\n\nPanel C: Schematic model interface showing inputs as perturbed cells and NTCs, a LASSO model, and outputs indicating significance and features. Adjacent bar chart reports “Significant Perturbation (%)” for the same biological sources.\n\nPanel D: Clustered heatmap with F1 score scale, comparing screens/sources. Side annotations connect clusters to enriched biological process bar plots, including respiration/electron transport, oxidative phosphorylation, cellular response to hypoxia, glucose metabolism, neural crest differentiation, epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and developmental cell lineage.\n\nPanel E: Scatter plot of Jaccard Index (Mean) versus F1 Score (Mean), with points colored by F1 CV.\n\nPanel F: UMAP embedding titled “Perturbation x Screen.” Points are colored by source/screen type with legend including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, iPSC, iPSC Multi-Diff, and HDBSCAN noise. Labeled clusters include biological complexes/pathways such as Elongator complex, RNase MRP, RFC clamp loader, Integrator complex, Emerin, p97/VCP, OST complex, mRNA decapping, DNA synthesis, cytochrome ribosome, RNA exosome, SMN complex, U3 snoRNP processing, CENP-A nucleosome, 26S proteasome, spliceosome, 60S ribosome, mediator, CUL3-RBX1 E3 ligase, eIF3 complex, TFIIH, p300-SWI/SNF, H2B ubiquitin ligase, and mitoribosome.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate perturbation screening performance across cell types/screens, biological network recovery, LASSO-based perturbation significance, reproducibility/overlap metrics, and clustering of perturbations into known molecular complexes or pathways."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Fig. 3 X-Cell achieves robust generalization across cellular contexts and perturbation benchmarks through diffusion pre-training. (A) X-Cell, initialized with scGPT encoder weights, was continually pre-trained on the XAtlas/Pisces perturbation corpus across four screens (HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC), using over 10 million cells and 37K perturbation-context combinations for training and evaluation. Model performance is evaluated on the 200 heldout validation perturbations (iPSC/HepG2-200). Fine-tuning is evaluated on two external datasets: Replogle-Nadig [12, 52] and Parse-1M [53]. Replogle-Nadig includes genetic perturbations across four training cell lines and 380 HepG2 test perturbations. Parse-1M features PBMC cells with 59 ligand signaling test perturbations in CD4 memory cells. See Section 4.1.4. (B) Diffusion LM pre-training enables iterative refinement of predicted gene expression during inference. Line plot shows Pearson ∆ and MAE across four diffusion steps on the iPSC-200 validation set. (C) Benchmark comparison of X-Cell with Cell2Sentence [22], STATE [27], and scGPT [8] across iPSC/HepG2-200 validation perturbations, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Performance is evaluated using cell-eval metrics including Pearson ∆, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE (Section 4.1.8). Radar plots summarize the relative ranking of the four benchmark models. (D) Prior knowledge attribution on Replogle-Nadig test perturbations. Pie chart shows average attention weights from X-Cell's final cross-attention layer. Line plot shows the performance drop on six metrics after randomizing embeddings from each knowledge source. (E) Zero-shot benchmark of X-Cell on a Tahoe-100M subset containing singletarget inhibitor drugs at mid dosage, compared with STATE (Section 4.1.7). Each point represents the aggregated metric across 12 drugs in each of 50 cell lines. Box plots show the median and interquartile range across cell lines; diamonds denote the mean.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-E about an X-Cell model for perturbation response prediction/evaluation.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. It shows X-Cell continual pre-training across cell/source contexts labeled HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC, with perturbations shown as stylized cell icons. Text notes “37K+ unique (perturbation, context) conditions in training and evaluation” and “iPSC/HepG2-200 200 held-out validation perturbations.” Fine-tune/evaluation tasks are shown for Replogle-Nadig with HepG2, Jurkat, K562, and RPE1, and Parse-1M with CD4 Memory and PBMCs. The schematic includes boxed unknown/query perturbation representations and colored cell-state outputs.\n\nPanel B: Boxplots comparing diffusion steps at 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. Metrics are Pearson Δ for iPSC-200 and MAE. Pink line with points marks mean. Pearson Δ increases with diffusion step, while MAE decreases slightly.\n\nPanel C: Large benchmark grid comparing methods Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, X-Cell, and Zero-shot across datasets iPSC/HepG2-200, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Metrics include Pearson Δ, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE, with arrows indicating desired direction. Bar charts and radar-by-rank plots show X-Cell generally strong/high across several metrics, especially Pearson Δ and differential expression metrics, with lower MAE in some rows.\n\nPanel D: Cross-attention contribution pie chart and randomization sensitivity line plot. Knowledge/source categories include STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting. The pie chart shows STRING as the largest contribution, followed by ESM-2/GenePT/DepMap/CellPainting. The line plot shows metric performance drop under knowledge-source randomization, with curves for centroid accuracy, overlap@N, Pearson Δ, MAE, DE Direction Match, and DE Spearman LFC.\n\nPanel E: Zero-shot comparison boxplots between STATE and X-Cell. Metrics are Pearson Δ and MAE. X-Cell shows higher Pearson Δ and lower MAE than STATE in the plotted distributions.\n\nBiological source objects visible: human/biological cell contexts and datasets including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat, K562, RPE1, CD4 Memory, and PBMCs; perturbations; gene/knowledge sources STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nOverall finding shown: X-Cell is presented as a perturbation-response model using continual pre-training and knowledge/source attention, outperforming or ranking highly against Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, and zero-shot baselines across multiple perturbation prediction metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 10, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-F about perturbation prediction and an “Inactivation Index” for genes/cell-surface targets.\n\nPanel A: Distribution plot of an “Inactivation Index” with a kernel density estimate and many genes annotated along the x-axis. A schematic inset shows “Active State” to “Resting State” with cell clusters and arrows. Labeled categories include “CD3 Complex” and “Downstream Inactivators,” plus “Putative Inactivators.” Example labels visible include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, LCP2, WDR53, APPL2, BRCA1, ITK, IRAK1, and others.\n\nPanel B: Workflow schematic comparing ground truth and model predictions. “Ground Truth (Pisces)” shows experimental perturbation leading to active perturbation. “Linear Baseline” shows perturbation effect labeled inactive and predicted perturbation. “X-Cell Prediction” shows X-Cell producing predicted perturbation. Uses colored cell clusters and arrows.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot comparing “Predicted Inactivation Index (z-scored)” for “CD3 Complex” versus “Others,” split by Linear Baseline and X-Cell. Annotated p-values: Linear Baseline p-value = 0.47; X-Cell p-value ≤ 0.01. CD3 complex genes labeled around the distributions include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, and CD3G.\n\nPanel D: Bar plots for “CD3 Complex Knockdown,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell bars are higher than Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel E: Bar plots for “Putative Inactivators,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell again outperforms Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel F: Heatmaps of differential expression patterns for selected perturbations, with rows grouped by prediction/source labels “Linear,” “Pisces,” and “X-Cell.” Gene/target labels visible on the right include CD3E, WDR53, IL6RA, and APPL2. Color scale indicates log fold-change, with blue-to-red expression changes across differentially expressed genes.\n\nBiological source objects: immune-cell-like cellular states, perturbation targets, CD3 complex genes, putative inactivator genes, and differentially expressed genes.\n\nModel interfaces/transformations: experimental perturbation data compared against a linear baseline and an X-Cell model prediction; perturbation effects are transformed into predicted perturbation profiles, inactivation index scores, auROC, Pearson correlation, and log-fold-change heatmaps.\n\nMain visible finding: X-Cell better identifies CD3 complex knockdown and putative inactivator perturbations than the linear baseline, including significantly higher predicted inactivation signal for CD3 complex genes in panel C and higher performance metrics in panels D-E."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Fig. 5 X-Cell-Ultra generalizes to melanocyte progenitors and primary human T cells in a zero-shot manner. (A) Schematic shows pre-training of X-Cell-Ultra on X-Atlas/Pisces datasets, excluding the melanocyte progenitor population from the iPSC Multi-Diff screen. We evaluated X-Cell-Ultra in a zero-shot manner on the melanocyte progenitor population as well as primary human T cell datasets from Zhu et al. [57]. We used test-time adaptation to calibrate the models on these datasets using self-supervised objectives. (B) Test loss follows a power law with trainable parameters ( L ( N ) ∝ N -0 . 03 , R 2 = 0 . 97; Table A4). Five models (83M-3.1B) were trained for 20 epochs on the Replogle-Nadig dataset under identical hyperparameters. Circle size is proportional to parameter count; color indicates DE Pearson r . Line plots (right) show how X-Cell-Architecture (blue) and X-Cell-Ultra-Architecture (pink) compare with respect to collapse metrics δ -norm ratio (top) and Pearson correlation of differentially expressed genes (bottom) on test set. (C) Performance of the control mean baseline, perturbation mean baseline, STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra with respect to Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right). Histogram (top) shows the metrics within 1,341 perturbations and the barplots (bottom) show mean ± 95% C.I. (D) Boxplots show the distribution of Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right) for 291 perturbations in resting and stimulated (2 time points) primary human T cells for 2 different donors. (E) Heatmaps show the log 2 fold change in expression of APPL2 in D2 (left) and D3 (right) 48 hours post-stimulation as observed by the data and predicted by STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about **X-Cell-Ultra**, a model for genome-scale zero-shot inference in human T cells.\n\nPanel A:\n- Schematic workflow labeled **X-Cell-Ultra**, **Pre-train**, and **Genome-scale Zero-shot Inference**.\n- Biological source objects include human cell lines / cell contexts labeled **HCT116**, **HEK293T**, **HepG2**, and **iPSC**, with perturbation cartoons.\n- Pretraining section shows **Jurkat resting**, **Jurkat active**, and **iPSC multi-diff** cell states.\n- The model appears to combine cell-state representations and perturbation/context information.\n- Inference example is for **Primary human T cell**, with donors/contexts labeled **D2** and **D3**.\n- Outputs include predicted responses for perturbations such as **8hr**, **Stim 48hr**, and NTC-related conditions.\n- A **Test-time Adaptation** module is shown with frozen cross-attention and reduced learning rate, using a query/key/value-style interface.\n\nPanel B:\n- Model scaling plots labeled **Model Scaling Dynamics (Replogle-Nadig)**.\n- Main scatter/line plot shows **Test Loss** decreasing as **Trainable Parameters** increase, with model sizes labeled approximately **83M**, **216M**, **543M**, **1.6B**, and **3.1B**.\n- Pearson correlation color scale is shown.\n- Side plots compare **X-Cell (55M)** and **X-Cell-Ultra (4.9B)** over epochs.\n- Metrics include **Pearson ρ** and **DE Pearson ρ**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra improves over epochs, with annotated gains around **2.3x** and **2.7x**.\n\nPanel C:\n- Evaluation labeled **Melanocyte Progenitor: 1,341 Test Perturbations**.\n- Histograms and bar charts compare methods on **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Methods include **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra appears to achieve higher Pearson Δ and DE direction match, and lower MAE than X-Cell.\n\nPanel D:\n- Evaluation labeled **291 Test Perturbations, 2 Donors, 3 Contexts**.\n- Boxplots compare **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- Metrics shown are **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Legend includes contexts such as **D2 Rest**, **D3 Rest**, **D2 Stim8hr**, **D3 Stim8hr**, **D2 Stim48hr**, and **D3 Stim48hr**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra generally shows stronger Pearson Δ and DE direction match and lower MAE.\n\nPanel E:\n- Heatmaps labeled **APPL2 D2 Stim 48h log₂FC** and **APPL2 D3 Stim 48h log₂FC**.\n- Rows compare **STATE**, donor/context measurements such as **D2 48h Stim** or **D3 48h Stim**, **X-Cell-Ultra**, and **X-Cell**.\n- Color scale ranges from blue to red around zero, representing log₂ fold-change values.\n- Dendrograms show clustering; X-Cell-Ultra appears closer to observed stimulated donor profiles than X-Cell in these examples."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 50, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel UMAP scatter plot of clustered cells. Axes are labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. A legend on the right maps colors to annotated cell states/types:\n\n`Epithelial Ectoderm`, `Mesenchymal Fibroblast`, `Mesenchymal Progenitor`, `Neural Crest; EMT`, `Neural Crest; Melanocyte Progenitor`, `Neuroectoderm`, `Neuron`, `Cycling; G2M`, `Cycling; S-phase`, and `Cycling; Stress Response`.\n\nThe plotted biological source objects appear to be individual cells or nuclei embedded in two-dimensional UMAP space and colored by cell-type/state annotation. The visible transformation is dimensionality reduction to UMAP coordinates followed by categorical coloring. The figure shows multiple separated and partially overlapping clusters, including a large green epithelial ectoderm cluster on the left and several right-side clusters containing neuroectoderm, mesenchymal, neural crest, cycling, and neuron-associated annotations."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 51, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological data figure with panels labeled A, B, C, D, and E.\n\nPanel A shows bar plots comparing recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB across screen groups labeled HCT116 full (n=3.4M), 2 screens (n=2-3M), and 3 screens (n=3.5-4M). A second bar plot shows number of cells, with values around millions, comparing screen groups. Error bars are shown.\n\nPanel B shows three cluster-level specificity score bar charts: non-context specificity score, HepG2 specificity score, and iPSC specificity score. Bars are plotted by cluster index 0-9, with selected clusters highlighted in dark blue and others in gray.\n\nPanel C shows a dense 2D embedding or network map with many colored points and text labels for biological features/genes or gene programs. Labels include cancer/biological annotations and many color-coded clusters. The visualization appears to represent biological source objects arranged by similarity or learned representation.\n\nPanel D shows another 2D embedding/map colored by Gini coefficient, with a green-yellow-red color scale from 0.0 to 1.0. Several dense red/orange clusters and many scattered green/yellow points are visible, suggesting specificity or concentration of signal across clusters.\n\nPanel E shows a plot comparing shuffled versus observed mean STRING context size. Many gray lines connect shuffled and observed values for labeled biological contexts, with observed values generally much lower than shuffled. A statistical annotation is shown at top with a very small p-value.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate biological screening or perturbation-derived representations against known biological networks and context specificity, using bar plots, cluster specificity scores, embedding visualizations, and STRING context-size comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 52, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 3 Performance comparison between STATE models trained on different datasets and X-Cell. For a fair comparison, we trained STATE (Pisces) model on a dataset set matching that used to train the X-Cell model, and STATE replogle on all publicly available datasets. We evaluate performance using six metrics across iPSC and HepG2 cell lines.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart labeled **iPSC-200**. It compares three methods across six metrics:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**: light blue bars\n- **STATE (Pisces)**: darker blue bars\n- **X-Cell**: magenta bars\n\nVisible panels/metrics:\n\n1. **Overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.281\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.306\n   - X-Cell: 0.306\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.823\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.796\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.859\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.884\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.099\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.104\n   - X-Cell: 0.510\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.157\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.059\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.517\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.506\n   - X-Cell: 0.553\n\nBiological source object indicated: **iPSC-200**, likely an induced pluripotent stem cell benchmark/dataset context, though no additional biological entities are shown.\n\nModel/interface comparison: performance of **STATE** trained or evaluated with **Replogle+VCC** and **Pisces**, compared against **X-Cell**.\n\nMain visible finding: **X-Cell** performs best or tied-best on all shown metrics, with especially large improvements in **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 52, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart titled **“HepG2-200”**. It contains six side-by-side quantitative panels comparing three methods or conditions:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**\n- **STATE (Pisces)**\n- **X-Cell**\n\nVisible panels and metrics:\n\n1. **overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.042\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.220\n   - X-Cell: 0.255\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.827\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.847\n   - X-Cell: 0.950\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.909\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.936\n   - X-Cell: 0.971\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.097\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.101\n   - X-Cell: 0.476\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.077\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.067\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.520\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.504\n   - X-Cell: 0.547\n\nThe biological source object indicated by the title is **HepG2**, likely a cell-line-specific benchmark or dataset subset. The figure compares model or method performance across differential-expression-related metrics. The visible finding is that **X-Cell performs best on all shown metrics**, with especially large improvements for **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 53, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A: Heatmap titled “Attention weights per perturbation.” Rows are modalities labeled ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, and scGPT. Columns represent “380 perturbations.” A hierarchical dendrogram above the heatmap clusters perturbations by distance. Color scale at right is labeled “Attention weight” and ranges roughly from 0.00 to 0.32, with darker red indicating higher attention. StringDB and scGPT show broad high attention across many perturbations, while CellPainting contains a large white/missing or near-zero region.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart titled “Total significant pathways per modality.” X-axis lists modalities StringDB, scGPT, GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting. Y-axis is “Total significant pathways.” Bars show counts: StringDB 94, scGPT 80, GenePT 44, ESM2 42, DepMap 38, CellPainting 13. StringDB and scGPT have the highest pathway counts.\n\nPanel C: Heatmap titled “Significant MSigDB Hallmark Pathways per Perturbation x Modality.” Rows are the same modalities: ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT. Columns are perturbations with dense rotated labels. Color scale at right indicates significance, with pale to darker red blocks marking significant pathway associations. The bottom x-axis is labeled “Perturbation.” Visible findings include broad significant regions for StringDB and scGPT across subsets of perturbations, fewer scattered signals for GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nBiological/source objects shown: perturbations, modalities derived from biological or biomedical data representations including protein language model embeddings (ESM2), protein interaction/network information (StringDB), gene-text embeddings or representations (GenePT), dependency map data (DepMap), cell painting image-derived features (CellPainting), and single-cell GPT embeddings or features (scGPT). The figure summarizes attention weights and MSigDB Hallmark pathway significance across perturbations and modalities."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 54, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A shows a schematic model architecture for perturbation conditioning. On the left, a “Control Encoding” block includes inputs such as “Gene Embedding,” “Exp. Cond,” and a “Diffusion Mask Encoder.” These feed into an attention-like module labeled “Q × KV.” A “Prior knowledge” box lists biological/source resources: “Depmap,” “StringDB,” and “ESM2.” The central orange label says “Perturbation Conditioning” and “Cross-attention Every 3 Layers of Self-attention.” Several vertical transformer-like blocks lead to a multiplicative interaction with “Balance Inference.” Additional prior-knowledge features are shown as “Knowledge-base Importance” and “TCR Pathway Enrichment for Top 1,000 Genes With the Highest Attention Scores.”\n\nPanel B is a bar chart titled “Change in Attention Scores of TCR Signaling Pathway Genes.” The y-axis is “Rel. Change in Contribution (pathway group %).” Methods on the x-axis include StringDB, scGPT, CellPainting, ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap. Bars show negative changes for StringDB, scGPT, and CellPainting, and positive changes for ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap, with GenePT appearing highest.\n\nPanel C is a bar chart titled “Over-representation of the Top 1,000 Genes in TCR Signaling Pathway.” The y-axis is “Odds Ratio.” Methods on the x-axis include GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB. A legend distinguishes p-value < 0.05 from p-value ≥ 0.05. A red dotted horizontal reference line marks odds ratio 1. GenePT, ESM2, and DepMap are above 1, while CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB are below or near/below 1.\n\nBiological objects explicitly shown include genes, TCR signaling pathway genes, pathway enrichment, and prior-knowledge sources such as DepMap, StringDB, and ESM2. The figure presents a model interface combining gene/expression/diffusion-mask encodings with cross-attention-based perturbation conditioning and reports that some knowledge sources increase attention or enrichment for TCR signaling pathway genes."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 55, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel quantitative scientific plot comparing cell line groups.\n\nLeft panel:\n- Title: “MAE (lower better ↓)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.065 to 0.090.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.005 **”\n- Finding: the Distant group shows higher MAE than the Similar group, indicating worse prediction error for distant cell lines.\n\nRight panel:\n- Title: “Pearson Δ (higher better ↑)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.20 to 0.50.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.026 *”\n- Finding: the Similar group shows higher Pearson Δ than the Distant group.\n\nVisible biological/source objects:\n- Cell line groups are referenced as the biological grouping variable.\n- No microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecules, or other biological objects are directly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- The figure appears to summarize model or prediction performance metrics by cell line similarity group.\n- Metrics shown are MAE and Pearson Δ.\n- No model architecture or interface is visible."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 56, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 7 Architecture ablations, training efficiency, and scaling analysis for X-Cell-Ultra. (A) Architecture and loss ablation on the 4.9B model (Replogle-Nadig, 6 epochs). Scatter shows δ -norm ratio ˆ ρ vs. DE Pearson r for six configurations; color = MAE. The red shaded region (ˆ ρ < 0 . 2) indicates conservative collapse (predicting small effects). Replacing scale-invariant losses with CCC and removing the hidden residual shortcut progressively recovered both magnitude (ˆ ρ : 0 . 22 → 0 . 51) and direction (DEr : 0 . 05 → 0 . 33); see Table A1. (B) Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) comparison. X-Cell-Ultra achieves 41% MFU on 64 H200 GPUs, comparable to Llama 3 (reported 38-43% [95]) and ∼ 20 × the 55M X-Cell (2%). (C) Normalized loss scaling (relative to 83M model). Both train (dashed, α = 0 . 32, R 2 = 0 . 96) and test (solid, α = 0 . 03, R 2 = 0 . 97) follow power laws; color = DE Pearson r . (D) Individual validation metrics vs. trainable parameters: centroid accuracy (left) and per-perturbation MAE (right), colored by validation loss. (E) Training dynamics over 20 epochs: test loss (left) and DE Pearson r (right) for all five model sizes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A-E, showing machine learning model scaling and evaluation metrics.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot comparing `DE Pearson r` on x-axis against `δ-norm ratio (ρ)` on y-axis. Points correspond to methods or model variants labeled `60M baseline`, `Tied + QK-Norm`, `CCC+dnp (residual)`, `MLP head (no resid)`, `Tied + per-gene CCC (no resid)`, and `add tied`. Colorbar indicates `MAE`.\n\nPanel B: Horizontal bar chart of `Model FLOPs Utilization (%)`. Rows include `scGPT`, `X-CELL`, `Llama 3`, and `X-Cell-Ultra`. Labels include parameter sizes such as `53M`, `55M`, `405B`, and `4.9B`, with utilization values around `1%`, `2%`, `40%`, and `41%`. A dashed vertical reference is labeled `LLM-grade`.\n\nPanel C: Scaling-law style plot with `Trainable Parameters` on the x-axis and `Normalized Loss (relative to 83M)` on the y-axis. Points are labeled `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Blue dashed line represents train behavior and pink line represents test behavior. Colorbar indicates `DE Pearson r`.\n\nPanel D: Two side-by-side scaling plots. Left plot shows `Control Accuracy` versus `Trainable Parameters`, increasing from `83M` through `3.1B`. Right plot shows `Per-gene MAE` versus `Trainable Parameters`, decreasing as model size increases. Colorbar indicates `Val Loss`.\n\nPanel E: Two training curves over `Epoch`. Left plot shows `Test Loss` decreasing over epochs for model sizes `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Right plot shows `DE Pearson r` increasing over epochs for the same model sizes.\n\nBiological source objects: none visibly depicted. The figure contains quantitative plots related to model performance, trainable parameters, loss, MAE, control accuracy, and differential expression Pearson correlation.\n\nMain visible finding: larger trainable parameter counts generally correspond to lower loss/MAE and higher control accuracy or DE Pearson correlation, with model performance improving across epochs and scaling comparisons shown across model sizes."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_4361ad5c9fbb_5b9d8ad8c72c/specificity_selector/stderr.log:47:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 Overview of X-Atlas/Pisces and X-Cell. (A) Schematic of X-Atlas/Pisces, which contains seven genome-scale CRISPRi Perturb-seq screens in HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC MultiDifferentiation (iPSC Multi-Diff) (left). Size comparison of X-Atlas/Orion (8M cells) versus X-Atlas/Pisces (25.6M cells) (middle). UMAP of cells in X-Atlas/Pisces, colored by screen. For visualization, the dataset was downsampled to 1M total cells while preserving the relative proportions of each original screen. Within this subset, 5% of the cells are nontargeting controls and the remainder are perturbed cells. (B) X-Cell combines diffusion LM training and cross-attention to prior knowledge to predict perturbed cell states from control cell sets. Six prior knowledge sources include pre-trained embeddings from LLM [19], ESM-2 [38], STRING [40, 41], DepMap [42], JUMP-Cell Painting [43], and scGPT [8] (Section 4.1.2). The main architecture consists of stacked self-attention blocks that encode control cell sets, with interleaved crossattention to prior knowledge embeddings. X-Cell enables diffusion-style training by randomly replacing 25%, 50%, or 75% of control gene expression values with ground-truth perturbed values, and providing a binary Diff Mask to indicate the revealed positions (Section 4.1.3). (C) During inference, X-Cell refines predictions via iterative diffusion by remasking part of its output as input for subsequent generation steps. (D) X-Cell scales from 55M parameters (X-Cell) to 4.9B parameters (X-Cell-Ultra), exceeding the size of existing single-cell foundation models.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure describing the X-Cell model and datasets.\n\nPanel A: Dataset and screen overview. Shows “X-Atlas” with two sources: Orion (8M) and Pisces (25.6M). Screens listed include HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Purposes are grouped as Architecture Validation, Context Generalization, and Cell Type Generalization. A stacked bar chart compares cell counts in Orion and Pisces by screen, and a UMAP labeled “Cell x Screen” shows colored cell clusters by dataset/screen.\n\nPanel B: X-Cell architecture schematic. Input consists of X-Atlas/Pisces perturbation examples. Prior knowledge is injected through “Gene X KD” using sources/interfaces labeled LLM, ESM2, STRING, DepMap, JUMP-Cell Painting, and scGPT. The central architecture shows control and perturbed cell sets with gene tokens, expression tokens, and diffusion masks. A “Diffusion LM Training” block indicates replacing expression values with perturbed expression for t > 0 using a diffusion mask. The model combines control encoding and perturbation conditioning, with cross-attention over prior knowledge embeddings, self-attention layers, and a perturbation decoder. Translational applications shown at right are Context Generalization, Perturbation Prediction, and Mechanism of Action.\n\nPanel C: Iterative prediction/remasking workflow for “Gene X KD.” Starts from input all-control expression plus diffusion mask at t=0, predicts intermediate values, remasks with 50% predicted and 50% control values at t=0.5, then produces a final prediction at t=1.0. Color legend indicates control versus predicted values.\n\nPanel D: Model parameter comparison bar chart. Models shown include scGPT, X-Cell, Geneformer V2, STATE-SE, scFoundation, TranscriptFormer, Tahoe-x1, and X-Cell-Ultra. Bars indicate parameter counts and years. X-Cell is labeled 55M 2026, X-Cell-Ultra 4.9B 2026, and an annotation indicates “92x.”"]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 X-Atlas/Pisces enables identification of context-dependent and context-independent perturbations. (A) Distribution of median UMIs per cell (top), median genes per cell (middle), and median on-target KD % (bottom) across GEMs for each screen in X-Atlas/Pisces. Black lines indicate median. (B) Recall of annotated gene pairs from CORUM (left) and STRING (right), calculated across all possible subsets of 1-7 screens. Error bars represent standard deviation across subsets. (C) Schematic of binary classifier for classifying perturbed cells from non-targeting controls (NTCs) (left). Input is a balanced mixture of perturbed cells for a given perturbation and NTCs. Model is logistic regression with L1-penalization (LASSO). Outputs are significance of the perturbation as measured by a one-sided binomial test and the features (genes) used to make the prediction. Percentage of perturbed cells with a significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) as measured by a LASSO binary classifier (right). (D) Heatmap of F1 scores for perturbations in X-Atlas/Pisces clustered based on F1 scores across screens. Rows represent perturbations (n=6,412) that pass the following filtering criteria: (1) present in ≥ 3 screens, (2) ≥ 10 cells in each screen the perturbation was present in, (3) significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) in at least 1 screen, and (4) F1 score ≥ 0.75 in at least 1 screen. Grey represents absence of a perturbation in that screen. Columns represent screens that are hierarchically clustered by similarity in F1 scores (colorbar). (E) Mean F1 score versus mean Jaccard index for perturbations across screens. Points are colored by F1 score coefficient of variation (CV) (colorbar). (F) UMAP of significant perturbations with at least one non-self feature (n=35,016) colored by screen. Clusters are labeled with CORUM complex enrichments, colored black if ≤ 50% of perturbations are from any individual screen, or the majority screen color otherwise. Inset structures of representative protein complexes are color-coded by whether the protein is targeted by a genetic perturbation in the indicated clusters (green shades are present, gray is absent).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-F, showing analyses of biological screening data.\n\nPanel A: Violin plots comparing distributions across multiple biological sources or screen/cell types: HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Metrics shown are UMIs, Genes, and KO (%).\n\nPanel B: Bar plots of recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB. Bars are colored by number of screens, with recall increasing across screen counts.\n\nPanel C: Schematic model interface showing inputs as perturbed cells and NTCs, a LASSO model, and outputs indicating significance and features. Adjacent bar chart reports “Significant Perturbation (%)” for the same biological sources.\n\nPanel D: Clustered heatmap with F1 score scale, comparing screens/sources. Side annotations connect clusters to enriched biological process bar plots, including respiration/electron transport, oxidative phosphorylation, cellular response to hypoxia, glucose metabolism, neural crest differentiation, epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and developmental cell lineage.\n\nPanel E: Scatter plot of Jaccard Index (Mean) versus F1 Score (Mean), with points colored by F1 CV.\n\nPanel F: UMAP embedding titled “Perturbation x Screen.” Points are colored by source/screen type with legend including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, iPSC, iPSC Multi-Diff, and HDBSCAN noise. Labeled clusters include biological complexes/pathways such as Elongator complex, RNase MRP, RFC clamp loader, Integrator complex, Emerin, p97/VCP, OST complex, mRNA decapping, DNA synthesis, cytochrome ribosome, RNA exosome, SMN complex, U3 snoRNP processing, CENP-A nucleosome, 26S proteasome, spliceosome, 60S ribosome, mediator, CUL3-RBX1 E3 ligase, eIF3 complex, TFIIH, p300-SWI/SNF, H2B ubiquitin ligase, and mitoribosome.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate perturbation screening performance across cell types/screens, biological network recovery, LASSO-based perturbation significance, reproducibility/overlap metrics, and clustering of perturbations into known molecular complexes or pathways."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Fig. 3 X-Cell achieves robust generalization across cellular contexts and perturbation benchmarks through diffusion pre-training. (A) X-Cell, initialized with scGPT encoder weights, was continually pre-trained on the XAtlas/Pisces perturbation corpus across four screens (HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC), using over 10 million cells and 37K perturbation-context combinations for training and evaluation. Model performance is evaluated on the 200 heldout validation perturbations (iPSC/HepG2-200). Fine-tuning is evaluated on two external datasets: Replogle-Nadig [12, 52] and Parse-1M [53]. Replogle-Nadig includes genetic perturbations across four training cell lines and 380 HepG2 test perturbations. Parse-1M features PBMC cells with 59 ligand signaling test perturbations in CD4 memory cells. See Section 4.1.4. (B) Diffusion LM pre-training enables iterative refinement of predicted gene expression during inference. Line plot shows Pearson ∆ and MAE across four diffusion steps on the iPSC-200 validation set. (C) Benchmark comparison of X-Cell with Cell2Sentence [22], STATE [27], and scGPT [8] across iPSC/HepG2-200 validation perturbations, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Performance is evaluated using cell-eval metrics including Pearson ∆, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE (Section 4.1.8). Radar plots summarize the relative ranking of the four benchmark models. (D) Prior knowledge attribution on Replogle-Nadig test perturbations. Pie chart shows average attention weights from X-Cell's final cross-attention layer. Line plot shows the performance drop on six metrics after randomizing embeddings from each knowledge source. (E) Zero-shot benchmark of X-Cell on a Tahoe-100M subset containing singletarget inhibitor drugs at mid dosage, compared with STATE (Section 4.1.7). Each point represents the aggregated metric across 12 drugs in each of 50 cell lines. Box plots show the median and interquartile range across cell lines; diamonds denote the mean.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-E about an X-Cell model for perturbation response prediction/evaluation.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. It shows X-Cell continual pre-training across cell/source contexts labeled HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC, with perturbations shown as stylized cell icons. Text notes “37K+ unique (perturbation, context) conditions in training and evaluation” and “iPSC/HepG2-200 200 held-out validation perturbations.” Fine-tune/evaluation tasks are shown for Replogle-Nadig with HepG2, Jurkat, K562, and RPE1, and Parse-1M with CD4 Memory and PBMCs. The schematic includes boxed unknown/query perturbation representations and colored cell-state outputs.\n\nPanel B: Boxplots comparing diffusion steps at 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. Metrics are Pearson Δ for iPSC-200 and MAE. Pink line with points marks mean. Pearson Δ increases with diffusion step, while MAE decreases slightly.\n\nPanel C: Large benchmark grid comparing methods Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, X-Cell, and Zero-shot across datasets iPSC/HepG2-200, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Metrics include Pearson Δ, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE, with arrows indicating desired direction. Bar charts and radar-by-rank plots show X-Cell generally strong/high across several metrics, especially Pearson Δ and differential expression metrics, with lower MAE in some rows.\n\nPanel D: Cross-attention contribution pie chart and randomization sensitivity line plot. Knowledge/source categories include STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting. The pie chart shows STRING as the largest contribution, followed by ESM-2/GenePT/DepMap/CellPainting. The line plot shows metric performance drop under knowledge-source randomization, with curves for centroid accuracy, overlap@N, Pearson Δ, MAE, DE Direction Match, and DE Spearman LFC.\n\nPanel E: Zero-shot comparison boxplots between STATE and X-Cell. Metrics are Pearson Δ and MAE. X-Cell shows higher Pearson Δ and lower MAE than STATE in the plotted distributions.\n\nBiological source objects visible: human/biological cell contexts and datasets including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat, K562, RPE1, CD4 Memory, and PBMCs; perturbations; gene/knowledge sources STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nOverall finding shown: X-Cell is presented as a perturbation-response model using continual pre-training and knowledge/source attention, outperforming or ranking highly against Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, and zero-shot baselines across multiple perturbation prediction metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 10, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-F about perturbation prediction and an “Inactivation Index” for genes/cell-surface targets.\n\nPanel A: Distribution plot of an “Inactivation Index” with a kernel density estimate and many genes annotated along the x-axis. A schematic inset shows “Active State” to “Resting State” with cell clusters and arrows. Labeled categories include “CD3 Complex” and “Downstream Inactivators,” plus “Putative Inactivators.” Example labels visible include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, LCP2, WDR53, APPL2, BRCA1, ITK, IRAK1, and others.\n\nPanel B: Workflow schematic comparing ground truth and model predictions. “Ground Truth (Pisces)” shows experimental perturbation leading to active perturbation. “Linear Baseline” shows perturbation effect labeled inactive and predicted perturbation. “X-Cell Prediction” shows X-Cell producing predicted perturbation. Uses colored cell clusters and arrows.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot comparing “Predicted Inactivation Index (z-scored)” for “CD3 Complex” versus “Others,” split by Linear Baseline and X-Cell. Annotated p-values: Linear Baseline p-value = 0.47; X-Cell p-value ≤ 0.01. CD3 complex genes labeled around the distributions include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, and CD3G.\n\nPanel D: Bar plots for “CD3 Complex Knockdown,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell bars are higher than Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel E: Bar plots for “Putative Inactivators,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell again outperforms Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel F: Heatmaps of differential expression patterns for selected perturbations, with rows grouped by prediction/source labels “Linear,” “Pisces,” and “X-Cell.” Gene/target labels visible on the right include CD3E, WDR53, IL6RA, and APPL2. Color scale indicates log fold-change, with blue-to-red expression changes across differentially expressed genes.\n\nBiological source objects: immune-cell-like cellular states, perturbation targets, CD3 complex genes, putative inactivator genes, and differentially expressed genes.\n\nModel interfaces/transformations: experimental perturbation data compared against a linear baseline and an X-Cell model prediction; perturbation effects are transformed into predicted perturbation profiles, inactivation index scores, auROC, Pearson correlation, and log-fold-change heatmaps.\n\nMain visible finding: X-Cell better identifies CD3 complex knockdown and putative inactivator perturbations than the linear baseline, including significantly higher predicted inactivation signal for CD3 complex genes in panel C and higher performance metrics in panels D-E."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Fig. 5 X-Cell-Ultra generalizes to melanocyte progenitors and primary human T cells in a zero-shot manner. (A) Schematic shows pre-training of X-Cell-Ultra on X-Atlas/Pisces datasets, excluding the melanocyte progenitor population from the iPSC Multi-Diff screen. We evaluated X-Cell-Ultra in a zero-shot manner on the melanocyte progenitor population as well as primary human T cell datasets from Zhu et al. [57]. We used test-time adaptation to calibrate the models on these datasets using self-supervised objectives. (B) Test loss follows a power law with trainable parameters ( L ( N ) ∝ N -0 . 03 , R 2 = 0 . 97; Table A4). Five models (83M-3.1B) were trained for 20 epochs on the Replogle-Nadig dataset under identical hyperparameters. Circle size is proportional to parameter count; color indicates DE Pearson r . Line plots (right) show how X-Cell-Architecture (blue) and X-Cell-Ultra-Architecture (pink) compare with respect to collapse metrics δ -norm ratio (top) and Pearson correlation of differentially expressed genes (bottom) on test set. (C) Performance of the control mean baseline, perturbation mean baseline, STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra with respect to Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right). Histogram (top) shows the metrics within 1,341 perturbations and the barplots (bottom) show mean ± 95% C.I. (D) Boxplots show the distribution of Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right) for 291 perturbations in resting and stimulated (2 time points) primary human T cells for 2 different donors. (E) Heatmaps show the log 2 fold change in expression of APPL2 in D2 (left) and D3 (right) 48 hours post-stimulation as observed by the data and predicted by STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about **X-Cell-Ultra**, a model for genome-scale zero-shot inference in human T cells.\n\nPanel A:\n- Schematic workflow labeled **X-Cell-Ultra**, **Pre-train**, and **Genome-scale Zero-shot Inference**.\n- Biological source objects include human cell lines / cell contexts labeled **HCT116**, **HEK293T**, **HepG2**, and **iPSC**, with perturbation cartoons.\n- Pretraining section shows **Jurkat resting**, **Jurkat active**, and **iPSC multi-diff** cell states.\n- The model appears to combine cell-state representations and perturbation/context information.\n- Inference example is for **Primary human T cell**, with donors/contexts labeled **D2** and **D3**.\n- Outputs include predicted responses for perturbations such as **8hr**, **Stim 48hr**, and NTC-related conditions.\n- A **Test-time Adaptation** module is shown with frozen cross-attention and reduced learning rate, using a query/key/value-style interface.\n\nPanel B:\n- Model scaling plots labeled **Model Scaling Dynamics (Replogle-Nadig)**.\n- Main scatter/line plot shows **Test Loss** decreasing as **Trainable Parameters** increase, with model sizes labeled approximately **83M**, **216M**, **543M**, **1.6B**, and **3.1B**.\n- Pearson correlation color scale is shown.\n- Side plots compare **X-Cell (55M)** and **X-Cell-Ultra (4.9B)** over epochs.\n- Metrics include **Pearson ρ** and **DE Pearson ρ**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra improves over epochs, with annotated gains around **2.3x** and **2.7x**.\n\nPanel C:\n- Evaluation labeled **Melanocyte Progenitor: 1,341 Test Perturbations**.\n- Histograms and bar charts compare methods on **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Methods include **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra appears to achieve higher Pearson Δ and DE direction match, and lower MAE than X-Cell.\n\nPanel D:\n- Evaluation labeled **291 Test Perturbations, 2 Donors, 3 Contexts**.\n- Boxplots compare **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- Metrics shown are **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Legend includes contexts such as **D2 Rest**, **D3 Rest**, **D2 Stim8hr**, **D3 Stim8hr**, **D2 Stim48hr**, and **D3 Stim48hr**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra generally shows stronger Pearson Δ and DE direction match and lower MAE.\n\nPanel E:\n- Heatmaps labeled **APPL2 D2 Stim 48h log₂FC** and **APPL2 D3 Stim 48h log₂FC**.\n- Rows compare **STATE**, donor/context measurements such as **D2 48h Stim** or **D3 48h Stim**, **X-Cell-Ultra**, and **X-Cell**.\n- Color scale ranges from blue to red around zero, representing log₂ fold-change values.\n- Dendrograms show clustering; X-Cell-Ultra appears closer to observed stimulated donor profiles than X-Cell in these examples."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 50, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel UMAP scatter plot of clustered cells. Axes are labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. A legend on the right maps colors to annotated cell states/types:\n\n`Epithelial Ectoderm`, `Mesenchymal Fibroblast`, `Mesenchymal Progenitor`, `Neural Crest; EMT`, `Neural Crest; Melanocyte Progenitor`, `Neuroectoderm`, `Neuron`, `Cycling; G2M`, `Cycling; S-phase`, and `Cycling; Stress Response`.\n\nThe plotted biological source objects appear to be individual cells or nuclei embedded in two-dimensional UMAP space and colored by cell-type/state annotation. The visible transformation is dimensionality reduction to UMAP coordinates followed by categorical coloring. The figure shows multiple separated and partially overlapping clusters, including a large green epithelial ectoderm cluster on the left and several right-side clusters containing neuroectoderm, mesenchymal, neural crest, cycling, and neuron-associated annotations."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 51, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological data figure with panels labeled A, B, C, D, and E.\n\nPanel A shows bar plots comparing recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB across screen groups labeled HCT116 full (n=3.4M), 2 screens (n=2-3M), and 3 screens (n=3.5-4M). A second bar plot shows number of cells, with values around millions, comparing screen groups. Error bars are shown.\n\nPanel B shows three cluster-level specificity score bar charts: non-context specificity score, HepG2 specificity score, and iPSC specificity score. Bars are plotted by cluster index 0-9, with selected clusters highlighted in dark blue and others in gray.\n\nPanel C shows a dense 2D embedding or network map with many colored points and text labels for biological features/genes or gene programs. Labels include cancer/biological annotations and many color-coded clusters. The visualization appears to represent biological source objects arranged by similarity or learned representation.\n\nPanel D shows another 2D embedding/map colored by Gini coefficient, with a green-yellow-red color scale from 0.0 to 1.0. Several dense red/orange clusters and many scattered green/yellow points are visible, suggesting specificity or concentration of signal across clusters.\n\nPanel E shows a plot comparing shuffled versus observed mean STRING context size. Many gray lines connect shuffled and observed values for labeled biological contexts, with observed values generally much lower than shuffled. A statistical annotation is shown at top with a very small p-value.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate biological screening or perturbation-derived representations against known biological networks and context specificity, using bar plots, cluster specificity scores, embedding visualizations, and STRING context-size comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 52, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 3 Performance comparison between STATE models trained on different datasets and X-Cell. For a fair comparison, we trained STATE (Pisces) model on a dataset set matching that used to train the X-Cell model, and STATE replogle on all publicly available datasets. We evaluate performance using six metrics across iPSC and HepG2 cell lines.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart labeled **iPSC-200**. It compares three methods across six metrics:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**: light blue bars\n- **STATE (Pisces)**: darker blue bars\n- **X-Cell**: magenta bars\n\nVisible panels/metrics:\n\n1. **Overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.281\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.306\n   - X-Cell: 0.306\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.823\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.796\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.859\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.884\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.099\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.104\n   - X-Cell: 0.510\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.157\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.059\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.517\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.506\n   - X-Cell: 0.553\n\nBiological source object indicated: **iPSC-200**, likely an induced pluripotent stem cell benchmark/dataset context, though no additional biological entities are shown.\n\nModel/interface comparison: performance of **STATE** trained or evaluated with **Replogle+VCC** and **Pisces**, compared against **X-Cell**.\n\nMain visible finding: **X-Cell** performs best or tied-best on all shown metrics, with especially large improvements in **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 52, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart titled **“HepG2-200”**. It contains six side-by-side quantitative panels comparing three methods or conditions:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**\n- **STATE (Pisces)**\n- **X-Cell**\n\nVisible panels and metrics:\n\n1. **overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.042\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.220\n   - X-Cell: 0.255\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.827\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.847\n   - X-Cell: 0.950\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.909\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.936\n   - X-Cell: 0.971\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.097\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.101\n   - X-Cell: 0.476\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.077\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.067\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.520\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.504\n   - X-Cell: 0.547\n\nThe biological source object indicated by the title is **HepG2**, likely a cell-line-specific benchmark or dataset subset. The figure compares model or method performance across differential-expression-related metrics. The visible finding is that **X-Cell performs best on all shown metrics**, with especially large improvements for **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 53, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A: Heatmap titled “Attention weights per perturbation.” Rows are modalities labeled ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, and scGPT. Columns represent “380 perturbations.” A hierarchical dendrogram above the heatmap clusters perturbations by distance. Color scale at right is labeled “Attention weight” and ranges roughly from 0.00 to 0.32, with darker red indicating higher attention. StringDB and scGPT show broad high attention across many perturbations, while CellPainting contains a large white/missing or near-zero region.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart titled “Total significant pathways per modality.” X-axis lists modalities StringDB, scGPT, GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting. Y-axis is “Total significant pathways.” Bars show counts: StringDB 94, scGPT 80, GenePT 44, ESM2 42, DepMap 38, CellPainting 13. StringDB and scGPT have the highest pathway counts.\n\nPanel C: Heatmap titled “Significant MSigDB Hallmark Pathways per Perturbation x Modality.” Rows are the same modalities: ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT. Columns are perturbations with dense rotated labels. Color scale at right indicates significance, with pale to darker red blocks marking significant pathway associations. The bottom x-axis is labeled “Perturbation.” Visible findings include broad significant regions for StringDB and scGPT across subsets of perturbations, fewer scattered signals for GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nBiological/source objects shown: perturbations, modalities derived from biological or biomedical data representations including protein language model embeddings (ESM2), protein interaction/network information (StringDB), gene-text embeddings or representations (GenePT), dependency map data (DepMap), cell painting image-derived features (CellPainting), and single-cell GPT embeddings or features (scGPT). The figure summarizes attention weights and MSigDB Hallmark pathway significance across perturbations and modalities."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 54, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A shows a schematic model architecture for perturbation conditioning. On the left, a “Control Encoding” block includes inputs such as “Gene Embedding,” “Exp. Cond,” and a “Diffusion Mask Encoder.” These feed into an attention-like module labeled “Q × KV.” A “Prior knowledge” box lists biological/source resources: “Depmap,” “StringDB,” and “ESM2.” The central orange label says “Perturbation Conditioning” and “Cross-attention Every 3 Layers of Self-attention.” Several vertical transformer-like blocks lead to a multiplicative interaction with “Balance Inference.” Additional prior-knowledge features are shown as “Knowledge-base Importance” and “TCR Pathway Enrichment for Top 1,000 Genes With the Highest Attention Scores.”\n\nPanel B is a bar chart titled “Change in Attention Scores of TCR Signaling Pathway Genes.” The y-axis is “Rel. Change in Contribution (pathway group %).” Methods on the x-axis include StringDB, scGPT, CellPainting, ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap. Bars show negative changes for StringDB, scGPT, and CellPainting, and positive changes for ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap, with GenePT appearing highest.\n\nPanel C is a bar chart titled “Over-representation of the Top 1,000 Genes in TCR Signaling Pathway.” The y-axis is “Odds Ratio.” Methods on the x-axis include GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB. A legend distinguishes p-value < 0.05 from p-value ≥ 0.05. A red dotted horizontal reference line marks odds ratio 1. GenePT, ESM2, and DepMap are above 1, while CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB are below or near/below 1.\n\nBiological objects explicitly shown include genes, TCR signaling pathway genes, pathway enrichment, and prior-knowledge sources such as DepMap, StringDB, and ESM2. The figure presents a model interface combining gene/expression/diffusion-mask encodings with cross-attention-based perturbation conditioning and reports that some knowledge sources increase attention or enrichment for TCR signaling pathway genes."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 55, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel quantitative scientific plot comparing cell line groups.\n\nLeft panel:\n- Title: “MAE (lower better ↓)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.065 to 0.090.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.005 **”\n- Finding: the Distant group shows higher MAE than the Similar group, indicating worse prediction error for distant cell lines.\n\nRight panel:\n- Title: “Pearson Δ (higher better ↑)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.20 to 0.50.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.026 *”\n- Finding: the Similar group shows higher Pearson Δ than the Distant group.\n\nVisible biological/source objects:\n- Cell line groups are referenced as the biological grouping variable.\n- No microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecules, or other biological objects are directly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- The figure appears to summarize model or prediction performance metrics by cell line similarity group.\n- Metrics shown are MAE and Pearson Δ.\n- No model architecture or interface is visible."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 56, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 7 Architecture ablations, training efficiency, and scaling analysis for X-Cell-Ultra. (A) Architecture and loss ablation on the 4.9B model (Replogle-Nadig, 6 epochs). Scatter shows δ -norm ratio ˆ ρ vs. DE Pearson r for six configurations; color = MAE. The red shaded region (ˆ ρ < 0 . 2) indicates conservative collapse (predicting small effects). Replacing scale-invariant losses with CCC and removing the hidden residual shortcut progressively recovered both magnitude (ˆ ρ : 0 . 22 → 0 . 51) and direction (DEr : 0 . 05 → 0 . 33); see Table A1. (B) Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) comparison. X-Cell-Ultra achieves 41% MFU on 64 H200 GPUs, comparable to Llama 3 (reported 38-43% [95]) and ∼ 20 × the 55M X-Cell (2%). (C) Normalized loss scaling (relative to 83M model). Both train (dashed, α = 0 . 32, R 2 = 0 . 96) and test (solid, α = 0 . 03, R 2 = 0 . 97) follow power laws; color = DE Pearson r . (D) Individual validation metrics vs. trainable parameters: centroid accuracy (left) and per-perturbation MAE (right), colored by validation loss. (E) Training dynamics over 20 epochs: test loss (left) and DE Pearson r (right) for all five model sizes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A-E, showing machine learning model scaling and evaluation metrics.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot comparing `DE Pearson r` on x-axis against `δ-norm ratio (ρ)` on y-axis. Points correspond to methods or model variants labeled `60M baseline`, `Tied + QK-Norm`, `CCC+dnp (residual)`, `MLP head (no resid)`, `Tied + per-gene CCC (no resid)`, and `add tied`. Colorbar indicates `MAE`.\n\nPanel B: Horizontal bar chart of `Model FLOPs Utilization (%)`. Rows include `scGPT`, `X-CELL`, `Llama 3`, and `X-Cell-Ultra`. Labels include parameter sizes such as `53M`, `55M`, `405B`, and `4.9B`, with utilization values around `1%`, `2%`, `40%`, and `41%`. A dashed vertical reference is labeled `LLM-grade`.\n\nPanel C: Scaling-law style plot with `Trainable Parameters` on the x-axis and `Normalized Loss (relative to 83M)` on the y-axis. Points are labeled `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Blue dashed line represents train behavior and pink line represents test behavior. Colorbar indicates `DE Pearson r`.\n\nPanel D: Two side-by-side scaling plots. Left plot shows `Control Accuracy` versus `Trainable Parameters`, increasing from `83M` through `3.1B`. Right plot shows `Per-gene MAE` versus `Trainable Parameters`, decreasing as model size increases. Colorbar indicates `Val Loss`.\n\nPanel E: Two training curves over `Epoch`. Left plot shows `Test Loss` decreasing over epochs for model sizes `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Right plot shows `DE Pearson r` increasing over epochs for the same model sizes.\n\nBiological source objects: none visibly depicted. The figure contains quantitative plots related to model performance, trainable parameters, loss, MAE, control accuracy, and differential expression Pearson correlation.\n\nMain visible finding: larger trainable parameter counts generally correspond to lower loss/MAE and higher control accuracy or DE Pearson correlation, with model performance improving across epochs and scaling comparisons shown across model sizes."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_4361ad5c9fbb_5b9d8ad8c72c/sufficiency_selector/prompt.txt:15:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 Overview of X-Atlas/Pisces and X-Cell. (A) Schematic of X-Atlas/Pisces, which contains seven genome-scale CRISPRi Perturb-seq screens in HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC MultiDifferentiation (iPSC Multi-Diff) (left). Size comparison of X-Atlas/Orion (8M cells) versus X-Atlas/Pisces (25.6M cells) (middle). UMAP of cells in X-Atlas/Pisces, colored by screen. For visualization, the dataset was downsampled to 1M total cells while preserving the relative proportions of each original screen. Within this subset, 5% of the cells are nontargeting controls and the remainder are perturbed cells. (B) X-Cell combines diffusion LM training and cross-attention to prior knowledge to predict perturbed cell states from control cell sets. Six prior knowledge sources include pre-trained embeddings from LLM [19], ESM-2 [38], STRING [40, 41], DepMap [42], JUMP-Cell Painting [43], and scGPT [8] (Section 4.1.2). The main architecture consists of stacked self-attention blocks that encode control cell sets, with interleaved crossattention to prior knowledge embeddings. X-Cell enables diffusion-style training by randomly replacing 25%, 50%, or 75% of control gene expression values with ground-truth perturbed values, and providing a binary Diff Mask to indicate the revealed positions (Section 4.1.3). (C) During inference, X-Cell refines predictions via iterative diffusion by remasking part of its output as input for subsequent generation steps. (D) X-Cell scales from 55M parameters (X-Cell) to 4.9B parameters (X-Cell-Ultra), exceeding the size of existing single-cell foundation models.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure describing the X-Cell model and datasets.\n\nPanel A: Dataset and screen overview. Shows “X-Atlas” with two sources: Orion (8M) and Pisces (25.6M). Screens listed include HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Purposes are grouped as Architecture Validation, Context Generalization, and Cell Type Generalization. A stacked bar chart compares cell counts in Orion and Pisces by screen, and a UMAP labeled “Cell x Screen” shows colored cell clusters by dataset/screen.\n\nPanel B: X-Cell architecture schematic. Input consists of X-Atlas/Pisces perturbation examples. Prior knowledge is injected through “Gene X KD” using sources/interfaces labeled LLM, ESM2, STRING, DepMap, JUMP-Cell Painting, and scGPT. The central architecture shows control and perturbed cell sets with gene tokens, expression tokens, and diffusion masks. A “Diffusion LM Training” block indicates replacing expression values with perturbed expression for t > 0 using a diffusion mask. The model combines control encoding and perturbation conditioning, with cross-attention over prior knowledge embeddings, self-attention layers, and a perturbation decoder. Translational applications shown at right are Context Generalization, Perturbation Prediction, and Mechanism of Action.\n\nPanel C: Iterative prediction/remasking workflow for “Gene X KD.” Starts from input all-control expression plus diffusion mask at t=0, predicts intermediate values, remasks with 50% predicted and 50% control values at t=0.5, then produces a final prediction at t=1.0. Color legend indicates control versus predicted values.\n\nPanel D: Model parameter comparison bar chart. Models shown include scGPT, X-Cell, Geneformer V2, STATE-SE, scFoundation, TranscriptFormer, Tahoe-x1, and X-Cell-Ultra. Bars indicate parameter counts and years. X-Cell is labeled 55M 2026, X-Cell-Ultra 4.9B 2026, and an annotation indicates “92x.”"]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 X-Atlas/Pisces enables identification of context-dependent and context-independent perturbations. (A) Distribution of median UMIs per cell (top), median genes per cell (middle), and median on-target KD % (bottom) across GEMs for each screen in X-Atlas/Pisces. Black lines indicate median. (B) Recall of annotated gene pairs from CORUM (left) and STRING (right), calculated across all possible subsets of 1-7 screens. Error bars represent standard deviation across subsets. (C) Schematic of binary classifier for classifying perturbed cells from non-targeting controls (NTCs) (left). Input is a balanced mixture of perturbed cells for a given perturbation and NTCs. Model is logistic regression with L1-penalization (LASSO). Outputs are significance of the perturbation as measured by a one-sided binomial test and the features (genes) used to make the prediction. Percentage of perturbed cells with a significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) as measured by a LASSO binary classifier (right). (D) Heatmap of F1 scores for perturbations in X-Atlas/Pisces clustered based on F1 scores across screens. Rows represent perturbations (n=6,412) that pass the following filtering criteria: (1) present in ≥ 3 screens, (2) ≥ 10 cells in each screen the perturbation was present in, (3) significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) in at least 1 screen, and (4) F1 score ≥ 0.75 in at least 1 screen. Grey represents absence of a perturbation in that screen. Columns represent screens that are hierarchically clustered by similarity in F1 scores (colorbar). (E) Mean F1 score versus mean Jaccard index for perturbations across screens. Points are colored by F1 score coefficient of variation (CV) (colorbar). (F) UMAP of significant perturbations with at least one non-self feature (n=35,016) colored by screen. Clusters are labeled with CORUM complex enrichments, colored black if ≤ 50% of perturbations are from any individual screen, or the majority screen color otherwise. Inset structures of representative protein complexes are color-coded by whether the protein is targeted by a genetic perturbation in the indicated clusters (green shades are present, gray is absent).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-F, showing analyses of biological screening data.\n\nPanel A: Violin plots comparing distributions across multiple biological sources or screen/cell types: HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Metrics shown are UMIs, Genes, and KO (%).\n\nPanel B: Bar plots of recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB. Bars are colored by number of screens, with recall increasing across screen counts.\n\nPanel C: Schematic model interface showing inputs as perturbed cells and NTCs, a LASSO model, and outputs indicating significance and features. Adjacent bar chart reports “Significant Perturbation (%)” for the same biological sources.\n\nPanel D: Clustered heatmap with F1 score scale, comparing screens/sources. Side annotations connect clusters to enriched biological process bar plots, including respiration/electron transport, oxidative phosphorylation, cellular response to hypoxia, glucose metabolism, neural crest differentiation, epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and developmental cell lineage.\n\nPanel E: Scatter plot of Jaccard Index (Mean) versus F1 Score (Mean), with points colored by F1 CV.\n\nPanel F: UMAP embedding titled “Perturbation x Screen.” Points are colored by source/screen type with legend including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, iPSC, iPSC Multi-Diff, and HDBSCAN noise. Labeled clusters include biological complexes/pathways such as Elongator complex, RNase MRP, RFC clamp loader, Integrator complex, Emerin, p97/VCP, OST complex, mRNA decapping, DNA synthesis, cytochrome ribosome, RNA exosome, SMN complex, U3 snoRNP processing, CENP-A nucleosome, 26S proteasome, spliceosome, 60S ribosome, mediator, CUL3-RBX1 E3 ligase, eIF3 complex, TFIIH, p300-SWI/SNF, H2B ubiquitin ligase, and mitoribosome.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate perturbation screening performance across cell types/screens, biological network recovery, LASSO-based perturbation significance, reproducibility/overlap metrics, and clustering of perturbations into known molecular complexes or pathways."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Fig. 3 X-Cell achieves robust generalization across cellular contexts and perturbation benchmarks through diffusion pre-training. (A) X-Cell, initialized with scGPT encoder weights, was continually pre-trained on the XAtlas/Pisces perturbation corpus across four screens (HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC), using over 10 million cells and 37K perturbation-context combinations for training and evaluation. Model performance is evaluated on the 200 heldout validation perturbations (iPSC/HepG2-200). Fine-tuning is evaluated on two external datasets: Replogle-Nadig [12, 52] and Parse-1M [53]. Replogle-Nadig includes genetic perturbations across four training cell lines and 380 HepG2 test perturbations. Parse-1M features PBMC cells with 59 ligand signaling test perturbations in CD4 memory cells. See Section 4.1.4. (B) Diffusion LM pre-training enables iterative refinement of predicted gene expression during inference. Line plot shows Pearson ∆ and MAE across four diffusion steps on the iPSC-200 validation set. (C) Benchmark comparison of X-Cell with Cell2Sentence [22], STATE [27], and scGPT [8] across iPSC/HepG2-200 validation perturbations, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Performance is evaluated using cell-eval metrics including Pearson ∆, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE (Section 4.1.8). Radar plots summarize the relative ranking of the four benchmark models. (D) Prior knowledge attribution on Replogle-Nadig test perturbations. Pie chart shows average attention weights from X-Cell's final cross-attention layer. Line plot shows the performance drop on six metrics after randomizing embeddings from each knowledge source. (E) Zero-shot benchmark of X-Cell on a Tahoe-100M subset containing singletarget inhibitor drugs at mid dosage, compared with STATE (Section 4.1.7). Each point represents the aggregated metric across 12 drugs in each of 50 cell lines. Box plots show the median and interquartile range across cell lines; diamonds denote the mean.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-E about an X-Cell model for perturbation response prediction/evaluation.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. It shows X-Cell continual pre-training across cell/source contexts labeled HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC, with perturbations shown as stylized cell icons. Text notes “37K+ unique (perturbation, context) conditions in training and evaluation” and “iPSC/HepG2-200 200 held-out validation perturbations.” Fine-tune/evaluation tasks are shown for Replogle-Nadig with HepG2, Jurkat, K562, and RPE1, and Parse-1M with CD4 Memory and PBMCs. The schematic includes boxed unknown/query perturbation representations and colored cell-state outputs.\n\nPanel B: Boxplots comparing diffusion steps at 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. Metrics are Pearson Δ for iPSC-200 and MAE. Pink line with points marks mean. Pearson Δ increases with diffusion step, while MAE decreases slightly.\n\nPanel C: Large benchmark grid comparing methods Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, X-Cell, and Zero-shot across datasets iPSC/HepG2-200, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Metrics include Pearson Δ, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE, with arrows indicating desired direction. Bar charts and radar-by-rank plots show X-Cell generally strong/high across several metrics, especially Pearson Δ and differential expression metrics, with lower MAE in some rows.\n\nPanel D: Cross-attention contribution pie chart and randomization sensitivity line plot. Knowledge/source categories include STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting. The pie chart shows STRING as the largest contribution, followed by ESM-2/GenePT/DepMap/CellPainting. The line plot shows metric performance drop under knowledge-source randomization, with curves for centroid accuracy, overlap@N, Pearson Δ, MAE, DE Direction Match, and DE Spearman LFC.\n\nPanel E: Zero-shot comparison boxplots between STATE and X-Cell. Metrics are Pearson Δ and MAE. X-Cell shows higher Pearson Δ and lower MAE than STATE in the plotted distributions.\n\nBiological source objects visible: human/biological cell contexts and datasets including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat, K562, RPE1, CD4 Memory, and PBMCs; perturbations; gene/knowledge sources STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nOverall finding shown: X-Cell is presented as a perturbation-response model using continual pre-training and knowledge/source attention, outperforming or ranking highly against Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, and zero-shot baselines across multiple perturbation prediction metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 10, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-F about perturbation prediction and an “Inactivation Index” for genes/cell-surface targets.\n\nPanel A: Distribution plot of an “Inactivation Index” with a kernel density estimate and many genes annotated along the x-axis. A schematic inset shows “Active State” to “Resting State” with cell clusters and arrows. Labeled categories include “CD3 Complex” and “Downstream Inactivators,” plus “Putative Inactivators.” Example labels visible include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, LCP2, WDR53, APPL2, BRCA1, ITK, IRAK1, and others.\n\nPanel B: Workflow schematic comparing ground truth and model predictions. “Ground Truth (Pisces)” shows experimental perturbation leading to active perturbation. “Linear Baseline” shows perturbation effect labeled inactive and predicted perturbation. “X-Cell Prediction” shows X-Cell producing predicted perturbation. Uses colored cell clusters and arrows.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot comparing “Predicted Inactivation Index (z-scored)” for “CD3 Complex” versus “Others,” split by Linear Baseline and X-Cell. Annotated p-values: Linear Baseline p-value = 0.47; X-Cell p-value ≤ 0.01. CD3 complex genes labeled around the distributions include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, and CD3G.\n\nPanel D: Bar plots for “CD3 Complex Knockdown,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell bars are higher than Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel E: Bar plots for “Putative Inactivators,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell again outperforms Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel F: Heatmaps of differential expression patterns for selected perturbations, with rows grouped by prediction/source labels “Linear,” “Pisces,” and “X-Cell.” Gene/target labels visible on the right include CD3E, WDR53, IL6RA, and APPL2. Color scale indicates log fold-change, with blue-to-red expression changes across differentially expressed genes.\n\nBiological source objects: immune-cell-like cellular states, perturbation targets, CD3 complex genes, putative inactivator genes, and differentially expressed genes.\n\nModel interfaces/transformations: experimental perturbation data compared against a linear baseline and an X-Cell model prediction; perturbation effects are transformed into predicted perturbation profiles, inactivation index scores, auROC, Pearson correlation, and log-fold-change heatmaps.\n\nMain visible finding: X-Cell better identifies CD3 complex knockdown and putative inactivator perturbations than the linear baseline, including significantly higher predicted inactivation signal for CD3 complex genes in panel C and higher performance metrics in panels D-E."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Fig. 5 X-Cell-Ultra generalizes to melanocyte progenitors and primary human T cells in a zero-shot manner. (A) Schematic shows pre-training of X-Cell-Ultra on X-Atlas/Pisces datasets, excluding the melanocyte progenitor population from the iPSC Multi-Diff screen. We evaluated X-Cell-Ultra in a zero-shot manner on the melanocyte progenitor population as well as primary human T cell datasets from Zhu et al. [57]. We used test-time adaptation to calibrate the models on these datasets using self-supervised objectives. (B) Test loss follows a power law with trainable parameters ( L ( N ) ∝ N -0 . 03 , R 2 = 0 . 97; Table A4). Five models (83M-3.1B) were trained for 20 epochs on the Replogle-Nadig dataset under identical hyperparameters. Circle size is proportional to parameter count; color indicates DE Pearson r . Line plots (right) show how X-Cell-Architecture (blue) and X-Cell-Ultra-Architecture (pink) compare with respect to collapse metrics δ -norm ratio (top) and Pearson correlation of differentially expressed genes (bottom) on test set. (C) Performance of the control mean baseline, perturbation mean baseline, STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra with respect to Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right). Histogram (top) shows the metrics within 1,341 perturbations and the barplots (bottom) show mean ± 95% C.I. (D) Boxplots show the distribution of Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right) for 291 perturbations in resting and stimulated (2 time points) primary human T cells for 2 different donors. (E) Heatmaps show the log 2 fold change in expression of APPL2 in D2 (left) and D3 (right) 48 hours post-stimulation as observed by the data and predicted by STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about **X-Cell-Ultra**, a model for genome-scale zero-shot inference in human T cells.\n\nPanel A:\n- Schematic workflow labeled **X-Cell-Ultra**, **Pre-train**, and **Genome-scale Zero-shot Inference**.\n- Biological source objects include human cell lines / cell contexts labeled **HCT116**, **HEK293T**, **HepG2**, and **iPSC**, with perturbation cartoons.\n- Pretraining section shows **Jurkat resting**, **Jurkat active**, and **iPSC multi-diff** cell states.\n- The model appears to combine cell-state representations and perturbation/context information.\n- Inference example is for **Primary human T cell**, with donors/contexts labeled **D2** and **D3**.\n- Outputs include predicted responses for perturbations such as **8hr**, **Stim 48hr**, and NTC-related conditions.\n- A **Test-time Adaptation** module is shown with frozen cross-attention and reduced learning rate, using a query/key/value-style interface.\n\nPanel B:\n- Model scaling plots labeled **Model Scaling Dynamics (Replogle-Nadig)**.\n- Main scatter/line plot shows **Test Loss** decreasing as **Trainable Parameters** increase, with model sizes labeled approximately **83M**, **216M**, **543M**, **1.6B**, and **3.1B**.\n- Pearson correlation color scale is shown.\n- Side plots compare **X-Cell (55M)** and **X-Cell-Ultra (4.9B)** over epochs.\n- Metrics include **Pearson ρ** and **DE Pearson ρ**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra improves over epochs, with annotated gains around **2.3x** and **2.7x**.\n\nPanel C:\n- Evaluation labeled **Melanocyte Progenitor: 1,341 Test Perturbations**.\n- Histograms and bar charts compare methods on **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Methods include **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra appears to achieve higher Pearson Δ and DE direction match, and lower MAE than X-Cell.\n\nPanel D:\n- Evaluation labeled **291 Test Perturbations, 2 Donors, 3 Contexts**.\n- Boxplots compare **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- Metrics shown are **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Legend includes contexts such as **D2 Rest**, **D3 Rest**, **D2 Stim8hr**, **D3 Stim8hr**, **D2 Stim48hr**, and **D3 Stim48hr**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra generally shows stronger Pearson Δ and DE direction match and lower MAE.\n\nPanel E:\n- Heatmaps labeled **APPL2 D2 Stim 48h log₂FC** and **APPL2 D3 Stim 48h log₂FC**.\n- Rows compare **STATE**, donor/context measurements such as **D2 48h Stim** or **D3 48h Stim**, **X-Cell-Ultra**, and **X-Cell**.\n- Color scale ranges from blue to red around zero, representing log₂ fold-change values.\n- Dendrograms show clustering; X-Cell-Ultra appears closer to observed stimulated donor profiles than X-Cell in these examples."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 50, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel UMAP scatter plot of clustered cells. Axes are labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. A legend on the right maps colors to annotated cell states/types:\n\n`Epithelial Ectoderm`, `Mesenchymal Fibroblast`, `Mesenchymal Progenitor`, `Neural Crest; EMT`, `Neural Crest; Melanocyte Progenitor`, `Neuroectoderm`, `Neuron`, `Cycling; G2M`, `Cycling; S-phase`, and `Cycling; Stress Response`.\n\nThe plotted biological source objects appear to be individual cells or nuclei embedded in two-dimensional UMAP space and colored by cell-type/state annotation. The visible transformation is dimensionality reduction to UMAP coordinates followed by categorical coloring. The figure shows multiple separated and partially overlapping clusters, including a large green epithelial ectoderm cluster on the left and several right-side clusters containing neuroectoderm, mesenchymal, neural crest, cycling, and neuron-associated annotations."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 51, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological data figure with panels labeled A, B, C, D, and E.\n\nPanel A shows bar plots comparing recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB across screen groups labeled HCT116 full (n=3.4M), 2 screens (n=2-3M), and 3 screens (n=3.5-4M). A second bar plot shows number of cells, with values around millions, comparing screen groups. Error bars are shown.\n\nPanel B shows three cluster-level specificity score bar charts: non-context specificity score, HepG2 specificity score, and iPSC specificity score. Bars are plotted by cluster index 0-9, with selected clusters highlighted in dark blue and others in gray.\n\nPanel C shows a dense 2D embedding or network map with many colored points and text labels for biological features/genes or gene programs. Labels include cancer/biological annotations and many color-coded clusters. The visualization appears to represent biological source objects arranged by similarity or learned representation.\n\nPanel D shows another 2D embedding/map colored by Gini coefficient, with a green-yellow-red color scale from 0.0 to 1.0. Several dense red/orange clusters and many scattered green/yellow points are visible, suggesting specificity or concentration of signal across clusters.\n\nPanel E shows a plot comparing shuffled versus observed mean STRING context size. Many gray lines connect shuffled and observed values for labeled biological contexts, with observed values generally much lower than shuffled. A statistical annotation is shown at top with a very small p-value.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate biological screening or perturbation-derived representations against known biological networks and context specificity, using bar plots, cluster specificity scores, embedding visualizations, and STRING context-size comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 52, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 3 Performance comparison between STATE models trained on different datasets and X-Cell. For a fair comparison, we trained STATE (Pisces) model on a dataset set matching that used to train the X-Cell model, and STATE replogle on all publicly available datasets. We evaluate performance using six metrics across iPSC and HepG2 cell lines.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart labeled **iPSC-200**. It compares three methods across six metrics:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**: light blue bars\n- **STATE (Pisces)**: darker blue bars\n- **X-Cell**: magenta bars\n\nVisible panels/metrics:\n\n1. **Overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.281\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.306\n   - X-Cell: 0.306\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.823\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.796\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.859\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.884\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.099\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.104\n   - X-Cell: 0.510\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.157\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.059\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.517\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.506\n   - X-Cell: 0.553\n\nBiological source object indicated: **iPSC-200**, likely an induced pluripotent stem cell benchmark/dataset context, though no additional biological entities are shown.\n\nModel/interface comparison: performance of **STATE** trained or evaluated with **Replogle+VCC** and **Pisces**, compared against **X-Cell**.\n\nMain visible finding: **X-Cell** performs best or tied-best on all shown metrics, with especially large improvements in **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 52, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart titled **“HepG2-200”**. It contains six side-by-side quantitative panels comparing three methods or conditions:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**\n- **STATE (Pisces)**\n- **X-Cell**\n\nVisible panels and metrics:\n\n1. **overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.042\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.220\n   - X-Cell: 0.255\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.827\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.847\n   - X-Cell: 0.950\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.909\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.936\n   - X-Cell: 0.971\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.097\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.101\n   - X-Cell: 0.476\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.077\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.067\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.520\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.504\n   - X-Cell: 0.547\n\nThe biological source object indicated by the title is **HepG2**, likely a cell-line-specific benchmark or dataset subset. The figure compares model or method performance across differential-expression-related metrics. The visible finding is that **X-Cell performs best on all shown metrics**, with especially large improvements for **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 53, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A: Heatmap titled “Attention weights per perturbation.” Rows are modalities labeled ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, and scGPT. Columns represent “380 perturbations.” A hierarchical dendrogram above the heatmap clusters perturbations by distance. Color scale at right is labeled “Attention weight” and ranges roughly from 0.00 to 0.32, with darker red indicating higher attention. StringDB and scGPT show broad high attention across many perturbations, while CellPainting contains a large white/missing or near-zero region.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart titled “Total significant pathways per modality.” X-axis lists modalities StringDB, scGPT, GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting. Y-axis is “Total significant pathways.” Bars show counts: StringDB 94, scGPT 80, GenePT 44, ESM2 42, DepMap 38, CellPainting 13. StringDB and scGPT have the highest pathway counts.\n\nPanel C: Heatmap titled “Significant MSigDB Hallmark Pathways per Perturbation x Modality.” Rows are the same modalities: ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT. Columns are perturbations with dense rotated labels. Color scale at right indicates significance, with pale to darker red blocks marking significant pathway associations. The bottom x-axis is labeled “Perturbation.” Visible findings include broad significant regions for StringDB and scGPT across subsets of perturbations, fewer scattered signals for GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nBiological/source objects shown: perturbations, modalities derived from biological or biomedical data representations including protein language model embeddings (ESM2), protein interaction/network information (StringDB), gene-text embeddings or representations (GenePT), dependency map data (DepMap), cell painting image-derived features (CellPainting), and single-cell GPT embeddings or features (scGPT). The figure summarizes attention weights and MSigDB Hallmark pathway significance across perturbations and modalities."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 54, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A shows a schematic model architecture for perturbation conditioning. On the left, a “Control Encoding” block includes inputs such as “Gene Embedding,” “Exp. Cond,” and a “Diffusion Mask Encoder.” These feed into an attention-like module labeled “Q × KV.” A “Prior knowledge” box lists biological/source resources: “Depmap,” “StringDB,” and “ESM2.” The central orange label says “Perturbation Conditioning” and “Cross-attention Every 3 Layers of Self-attention.” Several vertical transformer-like blocks lead to a multiplicative interaction with “Balance Inference.” Additional prior-knowledge features are shown as “Knowledge-base Importance” and “TCR Pathway Enrichment for Top 1,000 Genes With the Highest Attention Scores.”\n\nPanel B is a bar chart titled “Change in Attention Scores of TCR Signaling Pathway Genes.” The y-axis is “Rel. Change in Contribution (pathway group %).” Methods on the x-axis include StringDB, scGPT, CellPainting, ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap. Bars show negative changes for StringDB, scGPT, and CellPainting, and positive changes for ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap, with GenePT appearing highest.\n\nPanel C is a bar chart titled “Over-representation of the Top 1,000 Genes in TCR Signaling Pathway.” The y-axis is “Odds Ratio.” Methods on the x-axis include GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB. A legend distinguishes p-value < 0.05 from p-value ≥ 0.05. A red dotted horizontal reference line marks odds ratio 1. GenePT, ESM2, and DepMap are above 1, while CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB are below or near/below 1.\n\nBiological objects explicitly shown include genes, TCR signaling pathway genes, pathway enrichment, and prior-knowledge sources such as DepMap, StringDB, and ESM2. The figure presents a model interface combining gene/expression/diffusion-mask encodings with cross-attention-based perturbation conditioning and reports that some knowledge sources increase attention or enrichment for TCR signaling pathway genes."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 55, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel quantitative scientific plot comparing cell line groups.\n\nLeft panel:\n- Title: “MAE (lower better ↓)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.065 to 0.090.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.005 **”\n- Finding: the Distant group shows higher MAE than the Similar group, indicating worse prediction error for distant cell lines.\n\nRight panel:\n- Title: “Pearson Δ (higher better ↑)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.20 to 0.50.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.026 *”\n- Finding: the Similar group shows higher Pearson Δ than the Distant group.\n\nVisible biological/source objects:\n- Cell line groups are referenced as the biological grouping variable.\n- No microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecules, or other biological objects are directly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- The figure appears to summarize model or prediction performance metrics by cell line similarity group.\n- Metrics shown are MAE and Pearson Δ.\n- No model architecture or interface is visible."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 56, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 7 Architecture ablations, training efficiency, and scaling analysis for X-Cell-Ultra. (A) Architecture and loss ablation on the 4.9B model (Replogle-Nadig, 6 epochs). Scatter shows δ -norm ratio ˆ ρ vs. DE Pearson r for six configurations; color = MAE. The red shaded region (ˆ ρ < 0 . 2) indicates conservative collapse (predicting small effects). Replacing scale-invariant losses with CCC and removing the hidden residual shortcut progressively recovered both magnitude (ˆ ρ : 0 . 22 → 0 . 51) and direction (DEr : 0 . 05 → 0 . 33); see Table A1. (B) Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) comparison. X-Cell-Ultra achieves 41% MFU on 64 H200 GPUs, comparable to Llama 3 (reported 38-43% [95]) and ∼ 20 × the 55M X-Cell (2%). (C) Normalized loss scaling (relative to 83M model). Both train (dashed, α = 0 . 32, R 2 = 0 . 96) and test (solid, α = 0 . 03, R 2 = 0 . 97) follow power laws; color = DE Pearson r . (D) Individual validation metrics vs. trainable parameters: centroid accuracy (left) and per-perturbation MAE (right), colored by validation loss. (E) Training dynamics over 20 epochs: test loss (left) and DE Pearson r (right) for all five model sizes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A-E, showing machine learning model scaling and evaluation metrics.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot comparing `DE Pearson r` on x-axis against `δ-norm ratio (ρ)` on y-axis. Points correspond to methods or model variants labeled `60M baseline`, `Tied + QK-Norm`, `CCC+dnp (residual)`, `MLP head (no resid)`, `Tied + per-gene CCC (no resid)`, and `add tied`. Colorbar indicates `MAE`.\n\nPanel B: Horizontal bar chart of `Model FLOPs Utilization (%)`. Rows include `scGPT`, `X-CELL`, `Llama 3`, and `X-Cell-Ultra`. Labels include parameter sizes such as `53M`, `55M`, `405B`, and `4.9B`, with utilization values around `1%`, `2%`, `40%`, and `41%`. A dashed vertical reference is labeled `LLM-grade`.\n\nPanel C: Scaling-law style plot with `Trainable Parameters` on the x-axis and `Normalized Loss (relative to 83M)` on the y-axis. Points are labeled `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Blue dashed line represents train behavior and pink line represents test behavior. Colorbar indicates `DE Pearson r`.\n\nPanel D: Two side-by-side scaling plots. Left plot shows `Control Accuracy` versus `Trainable Parameters`, increasing from `83M` through `3.1B`. Right plot shows `Per-gene MAE` versus `Trainable Parameters`, decreasing as model size increases. Colorbar indicates `Val Loss`.\n\nPanel E: Two training curves over `Epoch`. Left plot shows `Test Loss` decreasing over epochs for model sizes `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Right plot shows `DE Pearson r` increasing over epochs for the same model sizes.\n\nBiological source objects: none visibly depicted. The figure contains quantitative plots related to model performance, trainable parameters, loss, MAE, control accuracy, and differential expression Pearson correlation.\n\nMain visible finding: larger trainable parameter counts generally correspond to lower loss/MAE and higher control accuracy or DE Pearson correlation, with model performance improving across epochs and scaling comparisons shown across model sizes."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_4361ad5c9fbb_5b9d8ad8c72c/sufficiency_selector/stderr.log:45:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 Overview of X-Atlas/Pisces and X-Cell. (A) Schematic of X-Atlas/Pisces, which contains seven genome-scale CRISPRi Perturb-seq screens in HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC MultiDifferentiation (iPSC Multi-Diff) (left). Size comparison of X-Atlas/Orion (8M cells) versus X-Atlas/Pisces (25.6M cells) (middle). UMAP of cells in X-Atlas/Pisces, colored by screen. For visualization, the dataset was downsampled to 1M total cells while preserving the relative proportions of each original screen. Within this subset, 5% of the cells are nontargeting controls and the remainder are perturbed cells. (B) X-Cell combines diffusion LM training and cross-attention to prior knowledge to predict perturbed cell states from control cell sets. Six prior knowledge sources include pre-trained embeddings from LLM [19], ESM-2 [38], STRING [40, 41], DepMap [42], JUMP-Cell Painting [43], and scGPT [8] (Section 4.1.2). The main architecture consists of stacked self-attention blocks that encode control cell sets, with interleaved crossattention to prior knowledge embeddings. X-Cell enables diffusion-style training by randomly replacing 25%, 50%, or 75% of control gene expression values with ground-truth perturbed values, and providing a binary Diff Mask to indicate the revealed positions (Section 4.1.3). (C) During inference, X-Cell refines predictions via iterative diffusion by remasking part of its output as input for subsequent generation steps. (D) X-Cell scales from 55M parameters (X-Cell) to 4.9B parameters (X-Cell-Ultra), exceeding the size of existing single-cell foundation models.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure describing the X-Cell model and datasets.\n\nPanel A: Dataset and screen overview. Shows “X-Atlas” with two sources: Orion (8M) and Pisces (25.6M). Screens listed include HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Purposes are grouped as Architecture Validation, Context Generalization, and Cell Type Generalization. A stacked bar chart compares cell counts in Orion and Pisces by screen, and a UMAP labeled “Cell x Screen” shows colored cell clusters by dataset/screen.\n\nPanel B: X-Cell architecture schematic. Input consists of X-Atlas/Pisces perturbation examples. Prior knowledge is injected through “Gene X KD” using sources/interfaces labeled LLM, ESM2, STRING, DepMap, JUMP-Cell Painting, and scGPT. The central architecture shows control and perturbed cell sets with gene tokens, expression tokens, and diffusion masks. A “Diffusion LM Training” block indicates replacing expression values with perturbed expression for t > 0 using a diffusion mask. The model combines control encoding and perturbation conditioning, with cross-attention over prior knowledge embeddings, self-attention layers, and a perturbation decoder. Translational applications shown at right are Context Generalization, Perturbation Prediction, and Mechanism of Action.\n\nPanel C: Iterative prediction/remasking workflow for “Gene X KD.” Starts from input all-control expression plus diffusion mask at t=0, predicts intermediate values, remasks with 50% predicted and 50% control values at t=0.5, then produces a final prediction at t=1.0. Color legend indicates control versus predicted values.\n\nPanel D: Model parameter comparison bar chart. Models shown include scGPT, X-Cell, Geneformer V2, STATE-SE, scFoundation, TranscriptFormer, Tahoe-x1, and X-Cell-Ultra. Bars indicate parameter counts and years. X-Cell is labeled 55M 2026, X-Cell-Ultra 4.9B 2026, and an annotation indicates “92x.”"]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 X-Atlas/Pisces enables identification of context-dependent and context-independent perturbations. (A) Distribution of median UMIs per cell (top), median genes per cell (middle), and median on-target KD % (bottom) across GEMs for each screen in X-Atlas/Pisces. Black lines indicate median. (B) Recall of annotated gene pairs from CORUM (left) and STRING (right), calculated across all possible subsets of 1-7 screens. Error bars represent standard deviation across subsets. (C) Schematic of binary classifier for classifying perturbed cells from non-targeting controls (NTCs) (left). Input is a balanced mixture of perturbed cells for a given perturbation and NTCs. Model is logistic regression with L1-penalization (LASSO). Outputs are significance of the perturbation as measured by a one-sided binomial test and the features (genes) used to make the prediction. Percentage of perturbed cells with a significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) as measured by a LASSO binary classifier (right). (D) Heatmap of F1 scores for perturbations in X-Atlas/Pisces clustered based on F1 scores across screens. Rows represent perturbations (n=6,412) that pass the following filtering criteria: (1) present in ≥ 3 screens, (2) ≥ 10 cells in each screen the perturbation was present in, (3) significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) in at least 1 screen, and (4) F1 score ≥ 0.75 in at least 1 screen. Grey represents absence of a perturbation in that screen. Columns represent screens that are hierarchically clustered by similarity in F1 scores (colorbar). (E) Mean F1 score versus mean Jaccard index for perturbations across screens. Points are colored by F1 score coefficient of variation (CV) (colorbar). (F) UMAP of significant perturbations with at least one non-self feature (n=35,016) colored by screen. Clusters are labeled with CORUM complex enrichments, colored black if ≤ 50% of perturbations are from any individual screen, or the majority screen color otherwise. Inset structures of representative protein complexes are color-coded by whether the protein is targeted by a genetic perturbation in the indicated clusters (green shades are present, gray is absent).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-F, showing analyses of biological screening data.\n\nPanel A: Violin plots comparing distributions across multiple biological sources or screen/cell types: HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Metrics shown are UMIs, Genes, and KO (%).\n\nPanel B: Bar plots of recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB. Bars are colored by number of screens, with recall increasing across screen counts.\n\nPanel C: Schematic model interface showing inputs as perturbed cells and NTCs, a LASSO model, and outputs indicating significance and features. Adjacent bar chart reports “Significant Perturbation (%)” for the same biological sources.\n\nPanel D: Clustered heatmap with F1 score scale, comparing screens/sources. Side annotations connect clusters to enriched biological process bar plots, including respiration/electron transport, oxidative phosphorylation, cellular response to hypoxia, glucose metabolism, neural crest differentiation, epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and developmental cell lineage.\n\nPanel E: Scatter plot of Jaccard Index (Mean) versus F1 Score (Mean), with points colored by F1 CV.\n\nPanel F: UMAP embedding titled “Perturbation x Screen.” Points are colored by source/screen type with legend including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, iPSC, iPSC Multi-Diff, and HDBSCAN noise. Labeled clusters include biological complexes/pathways such as Elongator complex, RNase MRP, RFC clamp loader, Integrator complex, Emerin, p97/VCP, OST complex, mRNA decapping, DNA synthesis, cytochrome ribosome, RNA exosome, SMN complex, U3 snoRNP processing, CENP-A nucleosome, 26S proteasome, spliceosome, 60S ribosome, mediator, CUL3-RBX1 E3 ligase, eIF3 complex, TFIIH, p300-SWI/SNF, H2B ubiquitin ligase, and mitoribosome.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate perturbation screening performance across cell types/screens, biological network recovery, LASSO-based perturbation significance, reproducibility/overlap metrics, and clustering of perturbations into known molecular complexes or pathways."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Fig. 3 X-Cell achieves robust generalization across cellular contexts and perturbation benchmarks through diffusion pre-training. (A) X-Cell, initialized with scGPT encoder weights, was continually pre-trained on the XAtlas/Pisces perturbation corpus across four screens (HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC), using over 10 million cells and 37K perturbation-context combinations for training and evaluation. Model performance is evaluated on the 200 heldout validation perturbations (iPSC/HepG2-200). Fine-tuning is evaluated on two external datasets: Replogle-Nadig [12, 52] and Parse-1M [53]. Replogle-Nadig includes genetic perturbations across four training cell lines and 380 HepG2 test perturbations. Parse-1M features PBMC cells with 59 ligand signaling test perturbations in CD4 memory cells. See Section 4.1.4. (B) Diffusion LM pre-training enables iterative refinement of predicted gene expression during inference. Line plot shows Pearson ∆ and MAE across four diffusion steps on the iPSC-200 validation set. (C) Benchmark comparison of X-Cell with Cell2Sentence [22], STATE [27], and scGPT [8] across iPSC/HepG2-200 validation perturbations, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Performance is evaluated using cell-eval metrics including Pearson ∆, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE (Section 4.1.8). Radar plots summarize the relative ranking of the four benchmark models. (D) Prior knowledge attribution on Replogle-Nadig test perturbations. Pie chart shows average attention weights from X-Cell's final cross-attention layer. Line plot shows the performance drop on six metrics after randomizing embeddings from each knowledge source. (E) Zero-shot benchmark of X-Cell on a Tahoe-100M subset containing singletarget inhibitor drugs at mid dosage, compared with STATE (Section 4.1.7). Each point represents the aggregated metric across 12 drugs in each of 50 cell lines. Box plots show the median and interquartile range across cell lines; diamonds denote the mean.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-E about an X-Cell model for perturbation response prediction/evaluation.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. It shows X-Cell continual pre-training across cell/source contexts labeled HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC, with perturbations shown as stylized cell icons. Text notes “37K+ unique (perturbation, context) conditions in training and evaluation” and “iPSC/HepG2-200 200 held-out validation perturbations.” Fine-tune/evaluation tasks are shown for Replogle-Nadig with HepG2, Jurkat, K562, and RPE1, and Parse-1M with CD4 Memory and PBMCs. The schematic includes boxed unknown/query perturbation representations and colored cell-state outputs.\n\nPanel B: Boxplots comparing diffusion steps at 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. Metrics are Pearson Δ for iPSC-200 and MAE. Pink line with points marks mean. Pearson Δ increases with diffusion step, while MAE decreases slightly.\n\nPanel C: Large benchmark grid comparing methods Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, X-Cell, and Zero-shot across datasets iPSC/HepG2-200, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Metrics include Pearson Δ, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE, with arrows indicating desired direction. Bar charts and radar-by-rank plots show X-Cell generally strong/high across several metrics, especially Pearson Δ and differential expression metrics, with lower MAE in some rows.\n\nPanel D: Cross-attention contribution pie chart and randomization sensitivity line plot. Knowledge/source categories include STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting. The pie chart shows STRING as the largest contribution, followed by ESM-2/GenePT/DepMap/CellPainting. The line plot shows metric performance drop under knowledge-source randomization, with curves for centroid accuracy, overlap@N, Pearson Δ, MAE, DE Direction Match, and DE Spearman LFC.\n\nPanel E: Zero-shot comparison boxplots between STATE and X-Cell. Metrics are Pearson Δ and MAE. X-Cell shows higher Pearson Δ and lower MAE than STATE in the plotted distributions.\n\nBiological source objects visible: human/biological cell contexts and datasets including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat, K562, RPE1, CD4 Memory, and PBMCs; perturbations; gene/knowledge sources STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nOverall finding shown: X-Cell is presented as a perturbation-response model using continual pre-training and knowledge/source attention, outperforming or ranking highly against Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, and zero-shot baselines across multiple perturbation prediction metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 10, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-F about perturbation prediction and an “Inactivation Index” for genes/cell-surface targets.\n\nPanel A: Distribution plot of an “Inactivation Index” with a kernel density estimate and many genes annotated along the x-axis. A schematic inset shows “Active State” to “Resting State” with cell clusters and arrows. Labeled categories include “CD3 Complex” and “Downstream Inactivators,” plus “Putative Inactivators.” Example labels visible include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, LCP2, WDR53, APPL2, BRCA1, ITK, IRAK1, and others.\n\nPanel B: Workflow schematic comparing ground truth and model predictions. “Ground Truth (Pisces)” shows experimental perturbation leading to active perturbation. “Linear Baseline” shows perturbation effect labeled inactive and predicted perturbation. “X-Cell Prediction” shows X-Cell producing predicted perturbation. Uses colored cell clusters and arrows.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot comparing “Predicted Inactivation Index (z-scored)” for “CD3 Complex” versus “Others,” split by Linear Baseline and X-Cell. Annotated p-values: Linear Baseline p-value = 0.47; X-Cell p-value ≤ 0.01. CD3 complex genes labeled around the distributions include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, and CD3G.\n\nPanel D: Bar plots for “CD3 Complex Knockdown,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell bars are higher than Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel E: Bar plots for “Putative Inactivators,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell again outperforms Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel F: Heatmaps of differential expression patterns for selected perturbations, with rows grouped by prediction/source labels “Linear,” “Pisces,” and “X-Cell.” Gene/target labels visible on the right include CD3E, WDR53, IL6RA, and APPL2. Color scale indicates log fold-change, with blue-to-red expression changes across differentially expressed genes.\n\nBiological source objects: immune-cell-like cellular states, perturbation targets, CD3 complex genes, putative inactivator genes, and differentially expressed genes.\n\nModel interfaces/transformations: experimental perturbation data compared against a linear baseline and an X-Cell model prediction; perturbation effects are transformed into predicted perturbation profiles, inactivation index scores, auROC, Pearson correlation, and log-fold-change heatmaps.\n\nMain visible finding: X-Cell better identifies CD3 complex knockdown and putative inactivator perturbations than the linear baseline, including significantly higher predicted inactivation signal for CD3 complex genes in panel C and higher performance metrics in panels D-E."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Fig. 5 X-Cell-Ultra generalizes to melanocyte progenitors and primary human T cells in a zero-shot manner. (A) Schematic shows pre-training of X-Cell-Ultra on X-Atlas/Pisces datasets, excluding the melanocyte progenitor population from the iPSC Multi-Diff screen. We evaluated X-Cell-Ultra in a zero-shot manner on the melanocyte progenitor population as well as primary human T cell datasets from Zhu et al. [57]. We used test-time adaptation to calibrate the models on these datasets using self-supervised objectives. (B) Test loss follows a power law with trainable parameters ( L ( N ) ∝ N -0 . 03 , R 2 = 0 . 97; Table A4). Five models (83M-3.1B) were trained for 20 epochs on the Replogle-Nadig dataset under identical hyperparameters. Circle size is proportional to parameter count; color indicates DE Pearson r . Line plots (right) show how X-Cell-Architecture (blue) and X-Cell-Ultra-Architecture (pink) compare with respect to collapse metrics δ -norm ratio (top) and Pearson correlation of differentially expressed genes (bottom) on test set. (C) Performance of the control mean baseline, perturbation mean baseline, STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra with respect to Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right). Histogram (top) shows the metrics within 1,341 perturbations and the barplots (bottom) show mean ± 95% C.I. (D) Boxplots show the distribution of Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right) for 291 perturbations in resting and stimulated (2 time points) primary human T cells for 2 different donors. (E) Heatmaps show the log 2 fold change in expression of APPL2 in D2 (left) and D3 (right) 48 hours post-stimulation as observed by the data and predicted by STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about **X-Cell-Ultra**, a model for genome-scale zero-shot inference in human T cells.\n\nPanel A:\n- Schematic workflow labeled **X-Cell-Ultra**, **Pre-train**, and **Genome-scale Zero-shot Inference**.\n- Biological source objects include human cell lines / cell contexts labeled **HCT116**, **HEK293T**, **HepG2**, and **iPSC**, with perturbation cartoons.\n- Pretraining section shows **Jurkat resting**, **Jurkat active**, and **iPSC multi-diff** cell states.\n- The model appears to combine cell-state representations and perturbation/context information.\n- Inference example is for **Primary human T cell**, with donors/contexts labeled **D2** and **D3**.\n- Outputs include predicted responses for perturbations such as **8hr**, **Stim 48hr**, and NTC-related conditions.\n- A **Test-time Adaptation** module is shown with frozen cross-attention and reduced learning rate, using a query/key/value-style interface.\n\nPanel B:\n- Model scaling plots labeled **Model Scaling Dynamics (Replogle-Nadig)**.\n- Main scatter/line plot shows **Test Loss** decreasing as **Trainable Parameters** increase, with model sizes labeled approximately **83M**, **216M**, **543M**, **1.6B**, and **3.1B**.\n- Pearson correlation color scale is shown.\n- Side plots compare **X-Cell (55M)** and **X-Cell-Ultra (4.9B)** over epochs.\n- Metrics include **Pearson ρ** and **DE Pearson ρ**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra improves over epochs, with annotated gains around **2.3x** and **2.7x**.\n\nPanel C:\n- Evaluation labeled **Melanocyte Progenitor: 1,341 Test Perturbations**.\n- Histograms and bar charts compare methods on **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Methods include **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra appears to achieve higher Pearson Δ and DE direction match, and lower MAE than X-Cell.\n\nPanel D:\n- Evaluation labeled **291 Test Perturbations, 2 Donors, 3 Contexts**.\n- Boxplots compare **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- Metrics shown are **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Legend includes contexts such as **D2 Rest**, **D3 Rest**, **D2 Stim8hr**, **D3 Stim8hr**, **D2 Stim48hr**, and **D3 Stim48hr**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra generally shows stronger Pearson Δ and DE direction match and lower MAE.\n\nPanel E:\n- Heatmaps labeled **APPL2 D2 Stim 48h log₂FC** and **APPL2 D3 Stim 48h log₂FC**.\n- Rows compare **STATE**, donor/context measurements such as **D2 48h Stim** or **D3 48h Stim**, **X-Cell-Ultra**, and **X-Cell**.\n- Color scale ranges from blue to red around zero, representing log₂ fold-change values.\n- Dendrograms show clustering; X-Cell-Ultra appears closer to observed stimulated donor profiles than X-Cell in these examples."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 50, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel UMAP scatter plot of clustered cells. Axes are labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. A legend on the right maps colors to annotated cell states/types:\n\n`Epithelial Ectoderm`, `Mesenchymal Fibroblast`, `Mesenchymal Progenitor`, `Neural Crest; EMT`, `Neural Crest; Melanocyte Progenitor`, `Neuroectoderm`, `Neuron`, `Cycling; G2M`, `Cycling; S-phase`, and `Cycling; Stress Response`.\n\nThe plotted biological source objects appear to be individual cells or nuclei embedded in two-dimensional UMAP space and colored by cell-type/state annotation. The visible transformation is dimensionality reduction to UMAP coordinates followed by categorical coloring. The figure shows multiple separated and partially overlapping clusters, including a large green epithelial ectoderm cluster on the left and several right-side clusters containing neuroectoderm, mesenchymal, neural crest, cycling, and neuron-associated annotations."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 51, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological data figure with panels labeled A, B, C, D, and E.\n\nPanel A shows bar plots comparing recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB across screen groups labeled HCT116 full (n=3.4M), 2 screens (n=2-3M), and 3 screens (n=3.5-4M). A second bar plot shows number of cells, with values around millions, comparing screen groups. Error bars are shown.\n\nPanel B shows three cluster-level specificity score bar charts: non-context specificity score, HepG2 specificity score, and iPSC specificity score. Bars are plotted by cluster index 0-9, with selected clusters highlighted in dark blue and others in gray.\n\nPanel C shows a dense 2D embedding or network map with many colored points and text labels for biological features/genes or gene programs. Labels include cancer/biological annotations and many color-coded clusters. The visualization appears to represent biological source objects arranged by similarity or learned representation.\n\nPanel D shows another 2D embedding/map colored by Gini coefficient, with a green-yellow-red color scale from 0.0 to 1.0. Several dense red/orange clusters and many scattered green/yellow points are visible, suggesting specificity or concentration of signal across clusters.\n\nPanel E shows a plot comparing shuffled versus observed mean STRING context size. Many gray lines connect shuffled and observed values for labeled biological contexts, with observed values generally much lower than shuffled. A statistical annotation is shown at top with a very small p-value.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate biological screening or perturbation-derived representations against known biological networks and context specificity, using bar plots, cluster specificity scores, embedding visualizations, and STRING context-size comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 52, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 3 Performance comparison between STATE models trained on different datasets and X-Cell. For a fair comparison, we trained STATE (Pisces) model on a dataset set matching that used to train the X-Cell model, and STATE replogle on all publicly available datasets. We evaluate performance using six metrics across iPSC and HepG2 cell lines.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart labeled **iPSC-200**. It compares three methods across six metrics:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**: light blue bars\n- **STATE (Pisces)**: darker blue bars\n- **X-Cell**: magenta bars\n\nVisible panels/metrics:\n\n1. **Overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.281\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.306\n   - X-Cell: 0.306\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.823\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.796\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.859\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.884\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.099\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.104\n   - X-Cell: 0.510\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.157\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.059\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.517\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.506\n   - X-Cell: 0.553\n\nBiological source object indicated: **iPSC-200**, likely an induced pluripotent stem cell benchmark/dataset context, though no additional biological entities are shown.\n\nModel/interface comparison: performance of **STATE** trained or evaluated with **Replogle+VCC** and **Pisces**, compared against **X-Cell**.\n\nMain visible finding: **X-Cell** performs best or tied-best on all shown metrics, with especially large improvements in **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 52, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart titled **“HepG2-200”**. It contains six side-by-side quantitative panels comparing three methods or conditions:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**\n- **STATE (Pisces)**\n- **X-Cell**\n\nVisible panels and metrics:\n\n1. **overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.042\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.220\n   - X-Cell: 0.255\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.827\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.847\n   - X-Cell: 0.950\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.909\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.936\n   - X-Cell: 0.971\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.097\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.101\n   - X-Cell: 0.476\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.077\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.067\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.520\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.504\n   - X-Cell: 0.547\n\nThe biological source object indicated by the title is **HepG2**, likely a cell-line-specific benchmark or dataset subset. The figure compares model or method performance across differential-expression-related metrics. The visible finding is that **X-Cell performs best on all shown metrics**, with especially large improvements for **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 53, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A: Heatmap titled “Attention weights per perturbation.” Rows are modalities labeled ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, and scGPT. Columns represent “380 perturbations.” A hierarchical dendrogram above the heatmap clusters perturbations by distance. Color scale at right is labeled “Attention weight” and ranges roughly from 0.00 to 0.32, with darker red indicating higher attention. StringDB and scGPT show broad high attention across many perturbations, while CellPainting contains a large white/missing or near-zero region.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart titled “Total significant pathways per modality.” X-axis lists modalities StringDB, scGPT, GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting. Y-axis is “Total significant pathways.” Bars show counts: StringDB 94, scGPT 80, GenePT 44, ESM2 42, DepMap 38, CellPainting 13. StringDB and scGPT have the highest pathway counts.\n\nPanel C: Heatmap titled “Significant MSigDB Hallmark Pathways per Perturbation x Modality.” Rows are the same modalities: ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT. Columns are perturbations with dense rotated labels. Color scale at right indicates significance, with pale to darker red blocks marking significant pathway associations. The bottom x-axis is labeled “Perturbation.” Visible findings include broad significant regions for StringDB and scGPT across subsets of perturbations, fewer scattered signals for GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nBiological/source objects shown: perturbations, modalities derived from biological or biomedical data representations including protein language model embeddings (ESM2), protein interaction/network information (StringDB), gene-text embeddings or representations (GenePT), dependency map data (DepMap), cell painting image-derived features (CellPainting), and single-cell GPT embeddings or features (scGPT). The figure summarizes attention weights and MSigDB Hallmark pathway significance across perturbations and modalities."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 54, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A shows a schematic model architecture for perturbation conditioning. On the left, a “Control Encoding” block includes inputs such as “Gene Embedding,” “Exp. Cond,” and a “Diffusion Mask Encoder.” These feed into an attention-like module labeled “Q × KV.” A “Prior knowledge” box lists biological/source resources: “Depmap,” “StringDB,” and “ESM2.” The central orange label says “Perturbation Conditioning” and “Cross-attention Every 3 Layers of Self-attention.” Several vertical transformer-like blocks lead to a multiplicative interaction with “Balance Inference.” Additional prior-knowledge features are shown as “Knowledge-base Importance” and “TCR Pathway Enrichment for Top 1,000 Genes With the Highest Attention Scores.”\n\nPanel B is a bar chart titled “Change in Attention Scores of TCR Signaling Pathway Genes.” The y-axis is “Rel. Change in Contribution (pathway group %).” Methods on the x-axis include StringDB, scGPT, CellPainting, ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap. Bars show negative changes for StringDB, scGPT, and CellPainting, and positive changes for ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap, with GenePT appearing highest.\n\nPanel C is a bar chart titled “Over-representation of the Top 1,000 Genes in TCR Signaling Pathway.” The y-axis is “Odds Ratio.” Methods on the x-axis include GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB. A legend distinguishes p-value < 0.05 from p-value ≥ 0.05. A red dotted horizontal reference line marks odds ratio 1. GenePT, ESM2, and DepMap are above 1, while CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB are below or near/below 1.\n\nBiological objects explicitly shown include genes, TCR signaling pathway genes, pathway enrichment, and prior-knowledge sources such as DepMap, StringDB, and ESM2. The figure presents a model interface combining gene/expression/diffusion-mask encodings with cross-attention-based perturbation conditioning and reports that some knowledge sources increase attention or enrichment for TCR signaling pathway genes."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 55, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel quantitative scientific plot comparing cell line groups.\n\nLeft panel:\n- Title: “MAE (lower better ↓)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.065 to 0.090.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.005 **”\n- Finding: the Distant group shows higher MAE than the Similar group, indicating worse prediction error for distant cell lines.\n\nRight panel:\n- Title: “Pearson Δ (higher better ↑)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.20 to 0.50.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.026 *”\n- Finding: the Similar group shows higher Pearson Δ than the Distant group.\n\nVisible biological/source objects:\n- Cell line groups are referenced as the biological grouping variable.\n- No microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecules, or other biological objects are directly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- The figure appears to summarize model or prediction performance metrics by cell line similarity group.\n- Metrics shown are MAE and Pearson Δ.\n- No model architecture or interface is visible."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 56, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 7 Architecture ablations, training efficiency, and scaling analysis for X-Cell-Ultra. (A) Architecture and loss ablation on the 4.9B model (Replogle-Nadig, 6 epochs). Scatter shows δ -norm ratio ˆ ρ vs. DE Pearson r for six configurations; color = MAE. The red shaded region (ˆ ρ < 0 . 2) indicates conservative collapse (predicting small effects). Replacing scale-invariant losses with CCC and removing the hidden residual shortcut progressively recovered both magnitude (ˆ ρ : 0 . 22 → 0 . 51) and direction (DEr : 0 . 05 → 0 . 33); see Table A1. (B) Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) comparison. X-Cell-Ultra achieves 41% MFU on 64 H200 GPUs, comparable to Llama 3 (reported 38-43% [95]) and ∼ 20 × the 55M X-Cell (2%). (C) Normalized loss scaling (relative to 83M model). Both train (dashed, α = 0 . 32, R 2 = 0 . 96) and test (solid, α = 0 . 03, R 2 = 0 . 97) follow power laws; color = DE Pearson r . (D) Individual validation metrics vs. trainable parameters: centroid accuracy (left) and per-perturbation MAE (right), colored by validation loss. (E) Training dynamics over 20 epochs: test loss (left) and DE Pearson r (right) for all five model sizes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A-E, showing machine learning model scaling and evaluation metrics.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot comparing `DE Pearson r` on x-axis against `δ-norm ratio (ρ)` on y-axis. Points correspond to methods or model variants labeled `60M baseline`, `Tied + QK-Norm`, `CCC+dnp (residual)`, `MLP head (no resid)`, `Tied + per-gene CCC (no resid)`, and `add tied`. Colorbar indicates `MAE`.\n\nPanel B: Horizontal bar chart of `Model FLOPs Utilization (%)`. Rows include `scGPT`, `X-CELL`, `Llama 3`, and `X-Cell-Ultra`. Labels include parameter sizes such as `53M`, `55M`, `405B`, and `4.9B`, with utilization values around `1%`, `2%`, `40%`, and `41%`. A dashed vertical reference is labeled `LLM-grade`.\n\nPanel C: Scaling-law style plot with `Trainable Parameters` on the x-axis and `Normalized Loss (relative to 83M)` on the y-axis. Points are labeled `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Blue dashed line represents train behavior and pink line represents test behavior. Colorbar indicates `DE Pearson r`.\n\nPanel D: Two side-by-side scaling plots. Left plot shows `Control Accuracy` versus `Trainable Parameters`, increasing from `83M` through `3.1B`. Right plot shows `Per-gene MAE` versus `Trainable Parameters`, decreasing as model size increases. Colorbar indicates `Val Loss`.\n\nPanel E: Two training curves over `Epoch`. Left plot shows `Test Loss` decreasing over epochs for model sizes `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Right plot shows `DE Pearson r` increasing over epochs for the same model sizes.\n\nBiological source objects: none visibly depicted. The figure contains quantitative plots related to model performance, trainable parameters, loss, MAE, control accuracy, and differential expression Pearson correlation.\n\nMain visible finding: larger trainable parameter counts generally correspond to lower loss/MAE and higher control accuracy or DE Pearson correlation, with model performance improving across epochs and scaling comparisons shown across model sizes."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_4361ad5c9fbb_5b9d8ad8c72c/adjudicator/prompt.txt:12:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 Overview of X-Atlas/Pisces and X-Cell. (A) Schematic of X-Atlas/Pisces, which contains seven genome-scale CRISPRi Perturb-seq screens in HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC MultiDifferentiation (iPSC Multi-Diff) (left). Size comparison of X-Atlas/Orion (8M cells) versus X-Atlas/Pisces (25.6M cells) (middle). UMAP of cells in X-Atlas/Pisces, colored by screen. For visualization, the dataset was downsampled to 1M total cells while preserving the relative proportions of each original screen. Within this subset, 5% of the cells are nontargeting controls and the remainder are perturbed cells. (B) X-Cell combines diffusion LM training and cross-attention to prior knowledge to predict perturbed cell states from control cell sets. Six prior knowledge sources include pre-trained embeddings from LLM [19], ESM-2 [38], STRING [40, 41], DepMap [42], JUMP-Cell Painting [43], and scGPT [8] (Section 4.1.2). The main architecture consists of stacked self-attention blocks that encode control cell sets, with interleaved crossattention to prior knowledge embeddings. X-Cell enables diffusion-style training by randomly replacing 25%, 50%, or 75% of control gene expression values with ground-truth perturbed values, and providing a binary Diff Mask to indicate the revealed positions (Section 4.1.3). (C) During inference, X-Cell refines predictions via iterative diffusion by remasking part of its output as input for subsequent generation steps. (D) X-Cell scales from 55M parameters (X-Cell) to 4.9B parameters (X-Cell-Ultra), exceeding the size of existing single-cell foundation models.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure describing the X-Cell model and datasets.\n\nPanel A: Dataset and screen overview. Shows “X-Atlas” with two sources: Orion (8M) and Pisces (25.6M). Screens listed include HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Purposes are grouped as Architecture Validation, Context Generalization, and Cell Type Generalization. A stacked bar chart compares cell counts in Orion and Pisces by screen, and a UMAP labeled “Cell x Screen” shows colored cell clusters by dataset/screen.\n\nPanel B: X-Cell architecture schematic. Input consists of X-Atlas/Pisces perturbation examples. Prior knowledge is injected through “Gene X KD” using sources/interfaces labeled LLM, ESM2, STRING, DepMap, JUMP-Cell Painting, and scGPT. The central architecture shows control and perturbed cell sets with gene tokens, expression tokens, and diffusion masks. A “Diffusion LM Training” block indicates replacing expression values with perturbed expression for t > 0 using a diffusion mask. The model combines control encoding and perturbation conditioning, with cross-attention over prior knowledge embeddings, self-attention layers, and a perturbation decoder. Translational applications shown at right are Context Generalization, Perturbation Prediction, and Mechanism of Action.\n\nPanel C: Iterative prediction/remasking workflow for “Gene X KD.” Starts from input all-control expression plus diffusion mask at t=0, predicts intermediate values, remasks with 50% predicted and 50% control values at t=0.5, then produces a final prediction at t=1.0. Color legend indicates control versus predicted values.\n\nPanel D: Model parameter comparison bar chart. Models shown include scGPT, X-Cell, Geneformer V2, STATE-SE, scFoundation, TranscriptFormer, Tahoe-x1, and X-Cell-Ultra. Bars indicate parameter counts and years. X-Cell is labeled 55M 2026, X-Cell-Ultra 4.9B 2026, and an annotation indicates “92x.”"]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 X-Atlas/Pisces enables identification of context-dependent and context-independent perturbations. (A) Distribution of median UMIs per cell (top), median genes per cell (middle), and median on-target KD % (bottom) across GEMs for each screen in X-Atlas/Pisces. Black lines indicate median. (B) Recall of annotated gene pairs from CORUM (left) and STRING (right), calculated across all possible subsets of 1-7 screens. Error bars represent standard deviation across subsets. (C) Schematic of binary classifier for classifying perturbed cells from non-targeting controls (NTCs) (left). Input is a balanced mixture of perturbed cells for a given perturbation and NTCs. Model is logistic regression with L1-penalization (LASSO). Outputs are significance of the perturbation as measured by a one-sided binomial test and the features (genes) used to make the prediction. Percentage of perturbed cells with a significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) as measured by a LASSO binary classifier (right). (D) Heatmap of F1 scores for perturbations in X-Atlas/Pisces clustered based on F1 scores across screens. Rows represent perturbations (n=6,412) that pass the following filtering criteria: (1) present in ≥ 3 screens, (2) ≥ 10 cells in each screen the perturbation was present in, (3) significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) in at least 1 screen, and (4) F1 score ≥ 0.75 in at least 1 screen. Grey represents absence of a perturbation in that screen. Columns represent screens that are hierarchically clustered by similarity in F1 scores (colorbar). (E) Mean F1 score versus mean Jaccard index for perturbations across screens. Points are colored by F1 score coefficient of variation (CV) (colorbar). (F) UMAP of significant perturbations with at least one non-self feature (n=35,016) colored by screen. Clusters are labeled with CORUM complex enrichments, colored black if ≤ 50% of perturbations are from any individual screen, or the majority screen color otherwise. Inset structures of representative protein complexes are color-coded by whether the protein is targeted by a genetic perturbation in the indicated clusters (green shades are present, gray is absent).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-F, showing analyses of biological screening data.\n\nPanel A: Violin plots comparing distributions across multiple biological sources or screen/cell types: HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Metrics shown are UMIs, Genes, and KO (%).\n\nPanel B: Bar plots of recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB. Bars are colored by number of screens, with recall increasing across screen counts.\n\nPanel C: Schematic model interface showing inputs as perturbed cells and NTCs, a LASSO model, and outputs indicating significance and features. Adjacent bar chart reports “Significant Perturbation (%)” for the same biological sources.\n\nPanel D: Clustered heatmap with F1 score scale, comparing screens/sources. Side annotations connect clusters to enriched biological process bar plots, including respiration/electron transport, oxidative phosphorylation, cellular response to hypoxia, glucose metabolism, neural crest differentiation, epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and developmental cell lineage.\n\nPanel E: Scatter plot of Jaccard Index (Mean) versus F1 Score (Mean), with points colored by F1 CV.\n\nPanel F: UMAP embedding titled “Perturbation x Screen.” Points are colored by source/screen type with legend including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, iPSC, iPSC Multi-Diff, and HDBSCAN noise. Labeled clusters include biological complexes/pathways such as Elongator complex, RNase MRP, RFC clamp loader, Integrator complex, Emerin, p97/VCP, OST complex, mRNA decapping, DNA synthesis, cytochrome ribosome, RNA exosome, SMN complex, U3 snoRNP processing, CENP-A nucleosome, 26S proteasome, spliceosome, 60S ribosome, mediator, CUL3-RBX1 E3 ligase, eIF3 complex, TFIIH, p300-SWI/SNF, H2B ubiquitin ligase, and mitoribosome.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate perturbation screening performance across cell types/screens, biological network recovery, LASSO-based perturbation significance, reproducibility/overlap metrics, and clustering of perturbations into known molecular complexes or pathways."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Fig. 3 X-Cell achieves robust generalization across cellular contexts and perturbation benchmarks through diffusion pre-training. (A) X-Cell, initialized with scGPT encoder weights, was continually pre-trained on the XAtlas/Pisces perturbation corpus across four screens (HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC), using over 10 million cells and 37K perturbation-context combinations for training and evaluation. Model performance is evaluated on the 200 heldout validation perturbations (iPSC/HepG2-200). Fine-tuning is evaluated on two external datasets: Replogle-Nadig [12, 52] and Parse-1M [53]. Replogle-Nadig includes genetic perturbations across four training cell lines and 380 HepG2 test perturbations. Parse-1M features PBMC cells with 59 ligand signaling test perturbations in CD4 memory cells. See Section 4.1.4. (B) Diffusion LM pre-training enables iterative refinement of predicted gene expression during inference. Line plot shows Pearson ∆ and MAE across four diffusion steps on the iPSC-200 validation set. (C) Benchmark comparison of X-Cell with Cell2Sentence [22], STATE [27], and scGPT [8] across iPSC/HepG2-200 validation perturbations, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Performance is evaluated using cell-eval metrics including Pearson ∆, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE (Section 4.1.8). Radar plots summarize the relative ranking of the four benchmark models. (D) Prior knowledge attribution on Replogle-Nadig test perturbations. Pie chart shows average attention weights from X-Cell's final cross-attention layer. Line plot shows the performance drop on six metrics after randomizing embeddings from each knowledge source. (E) Zero-shot benchmark of X-Cell on a Tahoe-100M subset containing singletarget inhibitor drugs at mid dosage, compared with STATE (Section 4.1.7). Each point represents the aggregated metric across 12 drugs in each of 50 cell lines. Box plots show the median and interquartile range across cell lines; diamonds denote the mean.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-E about an X-Cell model for perturbation response prediction/evaluation.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. It shows X-Cell continual pre-training across cell/source contexts labeled HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC, with perturbations shown as stylized cell icons. Text notes “37K+ unique (perturbation, context) conditions in training and evaluation” and “iPSC/HepG2-200 200 held-out validation perturbations.” Fine-tune/evaluation tasks are shown for Replogle-Nadig with HepG2, Jurkat, K562, and RPE1, and Parse-1M with CD4 Memory and PBMCs. The schematic includes boxed unknown/query perturbation representations and colored cell-state outputs.\n\nPanel B: Boxplots comparing diffusion steps at 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. Metrics are Pearson Δ for iPSC-200 and MAE. Pink line with points marks mean. Pearson Δ increases with diffusion step, while MAE decreases slightly.\n\nPanel C: Large benchmark grid comparing methods Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, X-Cell, and Zero-shot across datasets iPSC/HepG2-200, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Metrics include Pearson Δ, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE, with arrows indicating desired direction. Bar charts and radar-by-rank plots show X-Cell generally strong/high across several metrics, especially Pearson Δ and differential expression metrics, with lower MAE in some rows.\n\nPanel D: Cross-attention contribution pie chart and randomization sensitivity line plot. Knowledge/source categories include STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting. The pie chart shows STRING as the largest contribution, followed by ESM-2/GenePT/DepMap/CellPainting. The line plot shows metric performance drop under knowledge-source randomization, with curves for centroid accuracy, overlap@N, Pearson Δ, MAE, DE Direction Match, and DE Spearman LFC.\n\nPanel E: Zero-shot comparison boxplots between STATE and X-Cell. Metrics are Pearson Δ and MAE. X-Cell shows higher Pearson Δ and lower MAE than STATE in the plotted distributions.\n\nBiological source objects visible: human/biological cell contexts and datasets including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat, K562, RPE1, CD4 Memory, and PBMCs; perturbations; gene/knowledge sources STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nOverall finding shown: X-Cell is presented as a perturbation-response model using continual pre-training and knowledge/source attention, outperforming or ranking highly against Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, and zero-shot baselines across multiple perturbation prediction metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 10, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-F about perturbation prediction and an “Inactivation Index” for genes/cell-surface targets.\n\nPanel A: Distribution plot of an “Inactivation Index” with a kernel density estimate and many genes annotated along the x-axis. A schematic inset shows “Active State” to “Resting State” with cell clusters and arrows. Labeled categories include “CD3 Complex” and “Downstream Inactivators,” plus “Putative Inactivators.” Example labels visible include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, LCP2, WDR53, APPL2, BRCA1, ITK, IRAK1, and others.\n\nPanel B: Workflow schematic comparing ground truth and model predictions. “Ground Truth (Pisces)” shows experimental perturbation leading to active perturbation. “Linear Baseline” shows perturbation effect labeled inactive and predicted perturbation. “X-Cell Prediction” shows X-Cell producing predicted perturbation. Uses colored cell clusters and arrows.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot comparing “Predicted Inactivation Index (z-scored)” for “CD3 Complex” versus “Others,” split by Linear Baseline and X-Cell. Annotated p-values: Linear Baseline p-value = 0.47; X-Cell p-value ≤ 0.01. CD3 complex genes labeled around the distributions include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, and CD3G.\n\nPanel D: Bar plots for “CD3 Complex Knockdown,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell bars are higher than Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel E: Bar plots for “Putative Inactivators,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell again outperforms Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel F: Heatmaps of differential expression patterns for selected perturbations, with rows grouped by prediction/source labels “Linear,” “Pisces,” and “X-Cell.” Gene/target labels visible on the right include CD3E, WDR53, IL6RA, and APPL2. Color scale indicates log fold-change, with blue-to-red expression changes across differentially expressed genes.\n\nBiological source objects: immune-cell-like cellular states, perturbation targets, CD3 complex genes, putative inactivator genes, and differentially expressed genes.\n\nModel interfaces/transformations: experimental perturbation data compared against a linear baseline and an X-Cell model prediction; perturbation effects are transformed into predicted perturbation profiles, inactivation index scores, auROC, Pearson correlation, and log-fold-change heatmaps.\n\nMain visible finding: X-Cell better identifies CD3 complex knockdown and putative inactivator perturbations than the linear baseline, including significantly higher predicted inactivation signal for CD3 complex genes in panel C and higher performance metrics in panels D-E."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Fig. 5 X-Cell-Ultra generalizes to melanocyte progenitors and primary human T cells in a zero-shot manner. (A) Schematic shows pre-training of X-Cell-Ultra on X-Atlas/Pisces datasets, excluding the melanocyte progenitor population from the iPSC Multi-Diff screen. We evaluated X-Cell-Ultra in a zero-shot manner on the melanocyte progenitor population as well as primary human T cell datasets from Zhu et al. [57]. We used test-time adaptation to calibrate the models on these datasets using self-supervised objectives. (B) Test loss follows a power law with trainable parameters ( L ( N ) ∝ N -0 . 03 , R 2 = 0 . 97; Table A4). Five models (83M-3.1B) were trained for 20 epochs on the Replogle-Nadig dataset under identical hyperparameters. Circle size is proportional to parameter count; color indicates DE Pearson r . Line plots (right) show how X-Cell-Architecture (blue) and X-Cell-Ultra-Architecture (pink) compare with respect to collapse metrics δ -norm ratio (top) and Pearson correlation of differentially expressed genes (bottom) on test set. (C) Performance of the control mean baseline, perturbation mean baseline, STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra with respect to Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right). Histogram (top) shows the metrics within 1,341 perturbations and the barplots (bottom) show mean ± 95% C.I. (D) Boxplots show the distribution of Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right) for 291 perturbations in resting and stimulated (2 time points) primary human T cells for 2 different donors. (E) Heatmaps show the log 2 fold change in expression of APPL2 in D2 (left) and D3 (right) 48 hours post-stimulation as observed by the data and predicted by STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about **X-Cell-Ultra**, a model for genome-scale zero-shot inference in human T cells.\n\nPanel A:\n- Schematic workflow labeled **X-Cell-Ultra**, **Pre-train**, and **Genome-scale Zero-shot Inference**.\n- Biological source objects include human cell lines / cell contexts labeled **HCT116**, **HEK293T**, **HepG2**, and **iPSC**, with perturbation cartoons.\n- Pretraining section shows **Jurkat resting**, **Jurkat active**, and **iPSC multi-diff** cell states.\n- The model appears to combine cell-state representations and perturbation/context information.\n- Inference example is for **Primary human T cell**, with donors/contexts labeled **D2** and **D3**.\n- Outputs include predicted responses for perturbations such as **8hr**, **Stim 48hr**, and NTC-related conditions.\n- A **Test-time Adaptation** module is shown with frozen cross-attention and reduced learning rate, using a query/key/value-style interface.\n\nPanel B:\n- Model scaling plots labeled **Model Scaling Dynamics (Replogle-Nadig)**.\n- Main scatter/line plot shows **Test Loss** decreasing as **Trainable Parameters** increase, with model sizes labeled approximately **83M**, **216M**, **543M**, **1.6B**, and **3.1B**.\n- Pearson correlation color scale is shown.\n- Side plots compare **X-Cell (55M)** and **X-Cell-Ultra (4.9B)** over epochs.\n- Metrics include **Pearson ρ** and **DE Pearson ρ**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra improves over epochs, with annotated gains around **2.3x** and **2.7x**.\n\nPanel C:\n- Evaluation labeled **Melanocyte Progenitor: 1,341 Test Perturbations**.\n- Histograms and bar charts compare methods on **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Methods include **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra appears to achieve higher Pearson Δ and DE direction match, and lower MAE than X-Cell.\n\nPanel D:\n- Evaluation labeled **291 Test Perturbations, 2 Donors, 3 Contexts**.\n- Boxplots compare **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- Metrics shown are **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Legend includes contexts such as **D2 Rest**, **D3 Rest**, **D2 Stim8hr**, **D3 Stim8hr**, **D2 Stim48hr**, and **D3 Stim48hr**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra generally shows stronger Pearson Δ and DE direction match and lower MAE.\n\nPanel E:\n- Heatmaps labeled **APPL2 D2 Stim 48h log₂FC** and **APPL2 D3 Stim 48h log₂FC**.\n- Rows compare **STATE**, donor/context measurements such as **D2 48h Stim** or **D3 48h Stim**, **X-Cell-Ultra**, and **X-Cell**.\n- Color scale ranges from blue to red around zero, representing log₂ fold-change values.\n- Dendrograms show clustering; X-Cell-Ultra appears closer to observed stimulated donor profiles than X-Cell in these examples."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 50, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel UMAP scatter plot of clustered cells. Axes are labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. A legend on the right maps colors to annotated cell states/types:\n\n`Epithelial Ectoderm`, `Mesenchymal Fibroblast`, `Mesenchymal Progenitor`, `Neural Crest; EMT`, `Neural Crest; Melanocyte Progenitor`, `Neuroectoderm`, `Neuron`, `Cycling; G2M`, `Cycling; S-phase`, and `Cycling; Stress Response`.\n\nThe plotted biological source objects appear to be individual cells or nuclei embedded in two-dimensional UMAP space and colored by cell-type/state annotation. The visible transformation is dimensionality reduction to UMAP coordinates followed by categorical coloring. The figure shows multiple separated and partially overlapping clusters, including a large green epithelial ectoderm cluster on the left and several right-side clusters containing neuroectoderm, mesenchymal, neural crest, cycling, and neuron-associated annotations."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 51, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological data figure with panels labeled A, B, C, D, and E.\n\nPanel A shows bar plots comparing recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB across screen groups labeled HCT116 full (n=3.4M), 2 screens (n=2-3M), and 3 screens (n=3.5-4M). A second bar plot shows number of cells, with values around millions, comparing screen groups. Error bars are shown.\n\nPanel B shows three cluster-level specificity score bar charts: non-context specificity score, HepG2 specificity score, and iPSC specificity score. Bars are plotted by cluster index 0-9, with selected clusters highlighted in dark blue and others in gray.\n\nPanel C shows a dense 2D embedding or network map with many colored points and text labels for biological features/genes or gene programs. Labels include cancer/biological annotations and many color-coded clusters. The visualization appears to represent biological source objects arranged by similarity or learned representation.\n\nPanel D shows another 2D embedding/map colored by Gini coefficient, with a green-yellow-red color scale from 0.0 to 1.0. Several dense red/orange clusters and many scattered green/yellow points are visible, suggesting specificity or concentration of signal across clusters.\n\nPanel E shows a plot comparing shuffled versus observed mean STRING context size. Many gray lines connect shuffled and observed values for labeled biological contexts, with observed values generally much lower than shuffled. A statistical annotation is shown at top with a very small p-value.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate biological screening or perturbation-derived representations against known biological networks and context specificity, using bar plots, cluster specificity scores, embedding visualizations, and STRING context-size comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 52, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 3 Performance comparison between STATE models trained on different datasets and X-Cell. For a fair comparison, we trained STATE (Pisces) model on a dataset set matching that used to train the X-Cell model, and STATE replogle on all publicly available datasets. We evaluate performance using six metrics across iPSC and HepG2 cell lines.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart labeled **iPSC-200**. It compares three methods across six metrics:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**: light blue bars\n- **STATE (Pisces)**: darker blue bars\n- **X-Cell**: magenta bars\n\nVisible panels/metrics:\n\n1. **Overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.281\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.306\n   - X-Cell: 0.306\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.823\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.796\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.859\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.884\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.099\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.104\n   - X-Cell: 0.510\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.157\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.059\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.517\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.506\n   - X-Cell: 0.553\n\nBiological source object indicated: **iPSC-200**, likely an induced pluripotent stem cell benchmark/dataset context, though no additional biological entities are shown.\n\nModel/interface comparison: performance of **STATE** trained or evaluated with **Replogle+VCC** and **Pisces**, compared against **X-Cell**.\n\nMain visible finding: **X-Cell** performs best or tied-best on all shown metrics, with especially large improvements in **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 52, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart titled **“HepG2-200”**. It contains six side-by-side quantitative panels comparing three methods or conditions:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**\n- **STATE (Pisces)**\n- **X-Cell**\n\nVisible panels and metrics:\n\n1. **overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.042\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.220\n   - X-Cell: 0.255\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.827\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.847\n   - X-Cell: 0.950\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.909\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.936\n   - X-Cell: 0.971\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.097\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.101\n   - X-Cell: 0.476\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.077\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.067\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.520\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.504\n   - X-Cell: 0.547\n\nThe biological source object indicated by the title is **HepG2**, likely a cell-line-specific benchmark or dataset subset. The figure compares model or method performance across differential-expression-related metrics. The visible finding is that **X-Cell performs best on all shown metrics**, with especially large improvements for **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 53, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A: Heatmap titled “Attention weights per perturbation.” Rows are modalities labeled ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, and scGPT. Columns represent “380 perturbations.” A hierarchical dendrogram above the heatmap clusters perturbations by distance. Color scale at right is labeled “Attention weight” and ranges roughly from 0.00 to 0.32, with darker red indicating higher attention. StringDB and scGPT show broad high attention across many perturbations, while CellPainting contains a large white/missing or near-zero region.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart titled “Total significant pathways per modality.” X-axis lists modalities StringDB, scGPT, GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting. Y-axis is “Total significant pathways.” Bars show counts: StringDB 94, scGPT 80, GenePT 44, ESM2 42, DepMap 38, CellPainting 13. StringDB and scGPT have the highest pathway counts.\n\nPanel C: Heatmap titled “Significant MSigDB Hallmark Pathways per Perturbation x Modality.” Rows are the same modalities: ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT. Columns are perturbations with dense rotated labels. Color scale at right indicates significance, with pale to darker red blocks marking significant pathway associations. The bottom x-axis is labeled “Perturbation.” Visible findings include broad significant regions for StringDB and scGPT across subsets of perturbations, fewer scattered signals for GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nBiological/source objects shown: perturbations, modalities derived from biological or biomedical data representations including protein language model embeddings (ESM2), protein interaction/network information (StringDB), gene-text embeddings or representations (GenePT), dependency map data (DepMap), cell painting image-derived features (CellPainting), and single-cell GPT embeddings or features (scGPT). The figure summarizes attention weights and MSigDB Hallmark pathway significance across perturbations and modalities."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 54, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A shows a schematic model architecture for perturbation conditioning. On the left, a “Control Encoding” block includes inputs such as “Gene Embedding,” “Exp. Cond,” and a “Diffusion Mask Encoder.” These feed into an attention-like module labeled “Q × KV.” A “Prior knowledge” box lists biological/source resources: “Depmap,” “StringDB,” and “ESM2.” The central orange label says “Perturbation Conditioning” and “Cross-attention Every 3 Layers of Self-attention.” Several vertical transformer-like blocks lead to a multiplicative interaction with “Balance Inference.” Additional prior-knowledge features are shown as “Knowledge-base Importance” and “TCR Pathway Enrichment for Top 1,000 Genes With the Highest Attention Scores.”\n\nPanel B is a bar chart titled “Change in Attention Scores of TCR Signaling Pathway Genes.” The y-axis is “Rel. Change in Contribution (pathway group %).” Methods on the x-axis include StringDB, scGPT, CellPainting, ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap. Bars show negative changes for StringDB, scGPT, and CellPainting, and positive changes for ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap, with GenePT appearing highest.\n\nPanel C is a bar chart titled “Over-representation of the Top 1,000 Genes in TCR Signaling Pathway.” The y-axis is “Odds Ratio.” Methods on the x-axis include GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB. A legend distinguishes p-value < 0.05 from p-value ≥ 0.05. A red dotted horizontal reference line marks odds ratio 1. GenePT, ESM2, and DepMap are above 1, while CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB are below or near/below 1.\n\nBiological objects explicitly shown include genes, TCR signaling pathway genes, pathway enrichment, and prior-knowledge sources such as DepMap, StringDB, and ESM2. The figure presents a model interface combining gene/expression/diffusion-mask encodings with cross-attention-based perturbation conditioning and reports that some knowledge sources increase attention or enrichment for TCR signaling pathway genes."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 55, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel quantitative scientific plot comparing cell line groups.\n\nLeft panel:\n- Title: “MAE (lower better ↓)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.065 to 0.090.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.005 **”\n- Finding: the Distant group shows higher MAE than the Similar group, indicating worse prediction error for distant cell lines.\n\nRight panel:\n- Title: “Pearson Δ (higher better ↑)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.20 to 0.50.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.026 *”\n- Finding: the Similar group shows higher Pearson Δ than the Distant group.\n\nVisible biological/source objects:\n- Cell line groups are referenced as the biological grouping variable.\n- No microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecules, or other biological objects are directly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- The figure appears to summarize model or prediction performance metrics by cell line similarity group.\n- Metrics shown are MAE and Pearson Δ.\n- No model architecture or interface is visible."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 56, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 7 Architecture ablations, training efficiency, and scaling analysis for X-Cell-Ultra. (A) Architecture and loss ablation on the 4.9B model (Replogle-Nadig, 6 epochs). Scatter shows δ -norm ratio ˆ ρ vs. DE Pearson r for six configurations; color = MAE. The red shaded region (ˆ ρ < 0 . 2) indicates conservative collapse (predicting small effects). Replacing scale-invariant losses with CCC and removing the hidden residual shortcut progressively recovered both magnitude (ˆ ρ : 0 . 22 → 0 . 51) and direction (DEr : 0 . 05 → 0 . 33); see Table A1. (B) Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) comparison. X-Cell-Ultra achieves 41% MFU on 64 H200 GPUs, comparable to Llama 3 (reported 38-43% [95]) and ∼ 20 × the 55M X-Cell (2%). (C) Normalized loss scaling (relative to 83M model). Both train (dashed, α = 0 . 32, R 2 = 0 . 96) and test (solid, α = 0 . 03, R 2 = 0 . 97) follow power laws; color = DE Pearson r . (D) Individual validation metrics vs. trainable parameters: centroid accuracy (left) and per-perturbation MAE (right), colored by validation loss. (E) Training dynamics over 20 epochs: test loss (left) and DE Pearson r (right) for all five model sizes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A-E, showing machine learning model scaling and evaluation metrics.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot comparing `DE Pearson r` on x-axis against `δ-norm ratio (ρ)` on y-axis. Points correspond to methods or model variants labeled `60M baseline`, `Tied + QK-Norm`, `CCC+dnp (residual)`, `MLP head (no resid)`, `Tied + per-gene CCC (no resid)`, and `add tied`. Colorbar indicates `MAE`.\n\nPanel B: Horizontal bar chart of `Model FLOPs Utilization (%)`. Rows include `scGPT`, `X-CELL`, `Llama 3`, and `X-Cell-Ultra`. Labels include parameter sizes such as `53M`, `55M`, `405B`, and `4.9B`, with utilization values around `1%`, `2%`, `40%`, and `41%`. A dashed vertical reference is labeled `LLM-grade`.\n\nPanel C: Scaling-law style plot with `Trainable Parameters` on the x-axis and `Normalized Loss (relative to 83M)` on the y-axis. Points are labeled `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Blue dashed line represents train behavior and pink line represents test behavior. Colorbar indicates `DE Pearson r`.\n\nPanel D: Two side-by-side scaling plots. Left plot shows `Control Accuracy` versus `Trainable Parameters`, increasing from `83M` through `3.1B`. Right plot shows `Per-gene MAE` versus `Trainable Parameters`, decreasing as model size increases. Colorbar indicates `Val Loss`.\n\nPanel E: Two training curves over `Epoch`. Left plot shows `Test Loss` decreasing over epochs for model sizes `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Right plot shows `DE Pearson r` increasing over epochs for the same model sizes.\n\nBiological source objects: none visibly depicted. The figure contains quantitative plots related to model performance, trainable parameters, loss, MAE, control accuracy, and differential expression Pearson correlation.\n\nMain visible finding: larger trainable parameter counts generally correspond to lower loss/MAE and higher control accuracy or DE Pearson correlation, with model performance improving across epochs and scaling comparisons shown across model sizes."]}]
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/crops/models/model_4361ad5c9fbb_5b9d8ad8c72c/adjudicator/stderr.log:39:[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 Overview of X-Atlas/Pisces and X-Cell. (A) Schematic of X-Atlas/Pisces, which contains seven genome-scale CRISPRi Perturb-seq screens in HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC MultiDifferentiation (iPSC Multi-Diff) (left). Size comparison of X-Atlas/Orion (8M cells) versus X-Atlas/Pisces (25.6M cells) (middle). UMAP of cells in X-Atlas/Pisces, colored by screen. For visualization, the dataset was downsampled to 1M total cells while preserving the relative proportions of each original screen. Within this subset, 5% of the cells are nontargeting controls and the remainder are perturbed cells. (B) X-Cell combines diffusion LM training and cross-attention to prior knowledge to predict perturbed cell states from control cell sets. Six prior knowledge sources include pre-trained embeddings from LLM [19], ESM-2 [38], STRING [40, 41], DepMap [42], JUMP-Cell Painting [43], and scGPT [8] (Section 4.1.2). The main architecture consists of stacked self-attention blocks that encode control cell sets, with interleaved crossattention to prior knowledge embeddings. X-Cell enables diffusion-style training by randomly replacing 25%, 50%, or 75% of control gene expression values with ground-truth perturbed values, and providing a binary Diff Mask to indicate the revealed positions (Section 4.1.3). (C) During inference, X-Cell refines predictions via iterative diffusion by remasking part of its output as input for subsequent generation steps. (D) X-Cell scales from 55M parameters (X-Cell) to 4.9B parameters (X-Cell-Ultra), exceeding the size of existing single-cell foundation models.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure describing the X-Cell model and datasets.\n\nPanel A: Dataset and screen overview. Shows “X-Atlas” with two sources: Orion (8M) and Pisces (25.6M). Screens listed include HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Purposes are grouped as Architecture Validation, Context Generalization, and Cell Type Generalization. A stacked bar chart compares cell counts in Orion and Pisces by screen, and a UMAP labeled “Cell x Screen” shows colored cell clusters by dataset/screen.\n\nPanel B: X-Cell architecture schematic. Input consists of X-Atlas/Pisces perturbation examples. Prior knowledge is injected through “Gene X KD” using sources/interfaces labeled LLM, ESM2, STRING, DepMap, JUMP-Cell Painting, and scGPT. The central architecture shows control and perturbed cell sets with gene tokens, expression tokens, and diffusion masks. A “Diffusion LM Training” block indicates replacing expression values with perturbed expression for t > 0 using a diffusion mask. The model combines control encoding and perturbation conditioning, with cross-attention over prior knowledge embeddings, self-attention layers, and a perturbation decoder. Translational applications shown at right are Context Generalization, Perturbation Prediction, and Mechanism of Action.\n\nPanel C: Iterative prediction/remasking workflow for “Gene X KD.” Starts from input all-control expression plus diffusion mask at t=0, predicts intermediate values, remasks with 50% predicted and 50% control values at t=0.5, then produces a final prediction at t=1.0. Color legend indicates control versus predicted values.\n\nPanel D: Model parameter comparison bar chart. Models shown include scGPT, X-Cell, Geneformer V2, STATE-SE, scFoundation, TranscriptFormer, Tahoe-x1, and X-Cell-Ultra. Bars indicate parameter counts and years. X-Cell is labeled 55M 2026, X-Cell-Ultra 4.9B 2026, and an annotation indicates “92x.”"]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 X-Atlas/Pisces enables identification of context-dependent and context-independent perturbations. (A) Distribution of median UMIs per cell (top), median genes per cell (middle), and median on-target KD % (bottom) across GEMs for each screen in X-Atlas/Pisces. Black lines indicate median. (B) Recall of annotated gene pairs from CORUM (left) and STRING (right), calculated across all possible subsets of 1-7 screens. Error bars represent standard deviation across subsets. (C) Schematic of binary classifier for classifying perturbed cells from non-targeting controls (NTCs) (left). Input is a balanced mixture of perturbed cells for a given perturbation and NTCs. Model is logistic regression with L1-penalization (LASSO). Outputs are significance of the perturbation as measured by a one-sided binomial test and the features (genes) used to make the prediction. Percentage of perturbed cells with a significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) as measured by a LASSO binary classifier (right). (D) Heatmap of F1 scores for perturbations in X-Atlas/Pisces clustered based on F1 scores across screens. Rows represent perturbations (n=6,412) that pass the following filtering criteria: (1) present in ≥ 3 screens, (2) ≥ 10 cells in each screen the perturbation was present in, (3) significant phenotype (FDR < 0.05) in at least 1 screen, and (4) F1 score ≥ 0.75 in at least 1 screen. Grey represents absence of a perturbation in that screen. Columns represent screens that are hierarchically clustered by similarity in F1 scores (colorbar). (E) Mean F1 score versus mean Jaccard index for perturbations across screens. Points are colored by F1 score coefficient of variation (CV) (colorbar). (F) UMAP of significant perturbations with at least one non-self feature (n=35,016) colored by screen. Clusters are labeled with CORUM complex enrichments, colored black if ≤ 50% of perturbations are from any individual screen, or the majority screen color otherwise. Inset structures of representative protein complexes are color-coded by whether the protein is targeted by a genetic perturbation in the indicated clusters (green shades are present, gray is absent).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-F, showing analyses of biological screening data.\n\nPanel A: Violin plots comparing distributions across multiple biological sources or screen/cell types: HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, and iPSC Multi-Diff. Metrics shown are UMIs, Genes, and KO (%).\n\nPanel B: Bar plots of recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB. Bars are colored by number of screens, with recall increasing across screen counts.\n\nPanel C: Schematic model interface showing inputs as perturbed cells and NTCs, a LASSO model, and outputs indicating significance and features. Adjacent bar chart reports “Significant Perturbation (%)” for the same biological sources.\n\nPanel D: Clustered heatmap with F1 score scale, comparing screens/sources. Side annotations connect clusters to enriched biological process bar plots, including respiration/electron transport, oxidative phosphorylation, cellular response to hypoxia, glucose metabolism, neural crest differentiation, epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and developmental cell lineage.\n\nPanel E: Scatter plot of Jaccard Index (Mean) versus F1 Score (Mean), with points colored by F1 CV.\n\nPanel F: UMAP embedding titled “Perturbation x Screen.” Points are colored by source/screen type with legend including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, Jurkat Resting, Jurkat Active, iPSC, iPSC Multi-Diff, and HDBSCAN noise. Labeled clusters include biological complexes/pathways such as Elongator complex, RNase MRP, RFC clamp loader, Integrator complex, Emerin, p97/VCP, OST complex, mRNA decapping, DNA synthesis, cytochrome ribosome, RNA exosome, SMN complex, U3 snoRNP processing, CENP-A nucleosome, 26S proteasome, spliceosome, 60S ribosome, mediator, CUL3-RBX1 E3 ligase, eIF3 complex, TFIIH, p300-SWI/SNF, H2B ubiquitin ligase, and mitoribosome.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate perturbation screening performance across cell types/screens, biological network recovery, LASSO-based perturbation significance, reproducibility/overlap metrics, and clustering of perturbations into known molecular complexes or pathways."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Fig. 3 X-Cell achieves robust generalization across cellular contexts and perturbation benchmarks through diffusion pre-training. (A) X-Cell, initialized with scGPT encoder weights, was continually pre-trained on the XAtlas/Pisces perturbation corpus across four screens (HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC), using over 10 million cells and 37K perturbation-context combinations for training and evaluation. Model performance is evaluated on the 200 heldout validation perturbations (iPSC/HepG2-200). Fine-tuning is evaluated on two external datasets: Replogle-Nadig [12, 52] and Parse-1M [53]. Replogle-Nadig includes genetic perturbations across four training cell lines and 380 HepG2 test perturbations. Parse-1M features PBMC cells with 59 ligand signaling test perturbations in CD4 memory cells. See Section 4.1.4. (B) Diffusion LM pre-training enables iterative refinement of predicted gene expression during inference. Line plot shows Pearson ∆ and MAE across four diffusion steps on the iPSC-200 validation set. (C) Benchmark comparison of X-Cell with Cell2Sentence [22], STATE [27], and scGPT [8] across iPSC/HepG2-200 validation perturbations, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Performance is evaluated using cell-eval metrics including Pearson ∆, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE (Section 4.1.8). Radar plots summarize the relative ranking of the four benchmark models. (D) Prior knowledge attribution on Replogle-Nadig test perturbations. Pie chart shows average attention weights from X-Cell's final cross-attention layer. Line plot shows the performance drop on six metrics after randomizing embeddings from each knowledge source. (E) Zero-shot benchmark of X-Cell on a Tahoe-100M subset containing singletarget inhibitor drugs at mid dosage, compared with STATE (Section 4.1.7). Each point represents the aggregated metric across 12 drugs in each of 50 cell lines. Box plots show the median and interquartile range across cell lines; diamonds denote the mean.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A-E about an X-Cell model for perturbation response prediction/evaluation.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. It shows X-Cell continual pre-training across cell/source contexts labeled HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, and iPSC, with perturbations shown as stylized cell icons. Text notes “37K+ unique (perturbation, context) conditions in training and evaluation” and “iPSC/HepG2-200 200 held-out validation perturbations.” Fine-tune/evaluation tasks are shown for Replogle-Nadig with HepG2, Jurkat, K562, and RPE1, and Parse-1M with CD4 Memory and PBMCs. The schematic includes boxed unknown/query perturbation representations and colored cell-state outputs.\n\nPanel B: Boxplots comparing diffusion steps at 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. Metrics are Pearson Δ for iPSC-200 and MAE. Pink line with points marks mean. Pearson Δ increases with diffusion step, while MAE decreases slightly.\n\nPanel C: Large benchmark grid comparing methods Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, X-Cell, and Zero-shot across datasets iPSC/HepG2-200, Replogle-Nadig, and Parse-1M. Metrics include Pearson Δ, Overlap@N, DE Direction Match, DE Spearman LFC, Centroid Accuracy, and MAE, with arrows indicating desired direction. Bar charts and radar-by-rank plots show X-Cell generally strong/high across several metrics, especially Pearson Δ and differential expression metrics, with lower MAE in some rows.\n\nPanel D: Cross-attention contribution pie chart and randomization sensitivity line plot. Knowledge/source categories include STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting. The pie chart shows STRING as the largest contribution, followed by ESM-2/GenePT/DepMap/CellPainting. The line plot shows metric performance drop under knowledge-source randomization, with curves for centroid accuracy, overlap@N, Pearson Δ, MAE, DE Direction Match, and DE Spearman LFC.\n\nPanel E: Zero-shot comparison boxplots between STATE and X-Cell. Metrics are Pearson Δ and MAE. X-Cell shows higher Pearson Δ and lower MAE than STATE in the plotted distributions.\n\nBiological source objects visible: human/biological cell contexts and datasets including HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat, K562, RPE1, CD4 Memory, and PBMCs; perturbations; gene/knowledge sources STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nOverall finding shown: X-Cell is presented as a perturbation-response model using continual pre-training and knowledge/source attention, outperforming or ranking highly against Cell2Sentence, STATE, scGPT, and zero-shot baselines across multiple perturbation prediction metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 10, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-F about perturbation prediction and an “Inactivation Index” for genes/cell-surface targets.\n\nPanel A: Distribution plot of an “Inactivation Index” with a kernel density estimate and many genes annotated along the x-axis. A schematic inset shows “Active State” to “Resting State” with cell clusters and arrows. Labeled categories include “CD3 Complex” and “Downstream Inactivators,” plus “Putative Inactivators.” Example labels visible include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, LCP2, WDR53, APPL2, BRCA1, ITK, IRAK1, and others.\n\nPanel B: Workflow schematic comparing ground truth and model predictions. “Ground Truth (Pisces)” shows experimental perturbation leading to active perturbation. “Linear Baseline” shows perturbation effect labeled inactive and predicted perturbation. “X-Cell Prediction” shows X-Cell producing predicted perturbation. Uses colored cell clusters and arrows.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot comparing “Predicted Inactivation Index (z-scored)” for “CD3 Complex” versus “Others,” split by Linear Baseline and X-Cell. Annotated p-values: Linear Baseline p-value = 0.47; X-Cell p-value ≤ 0.01. CD3 complex genes labeled around the distributions include CD247, CD3D, CD3E, and CD3G.\n\nPanel D: Bar plots for “CD3 Complex Knockdown,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell bars are higher than Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel E: Bar plots for “Putative Inactivators,” comparing Linear Baseline versus X-Cell. Metrics shown: “DE auROC” and “Pearson Δ.” X-Cell again outperforms Linear Baseline.\n\nPanel F: Heatmaps of differential expression patterns for selected perturbations, with rows grouped by prediction/source labels “Linear,” “Pisces,” and “X-Cell.” Gene/target labels visible on the right include CD3E, WDR53, IL6RA, and APPL2. Color scale indicates log fold-change, with blue-to-red expression changes across differentially expressed genes.\n\nBiological source objects: immune-cell-like cellular states, perturbation targets, CD3 complex genes, putative inactivator genes, and differentially expressed genes.\n\nModel interfaces/transformations: experimental perturbation data compared against a linear baseline and an X-Cell model prediction; perturbation effects are transformed into predicted perturbation profiles, inactivation index scores, auROC, Pearson correlation, and log-fold-change heatmaps.\n\nMain visible finding: X-Cell better identifies CD3 complex knockdown and putative inactivator perturbations than the linear baseline, including significantly higher predicted inactivation signal for CD3 complex genes in panel C and higher performance metrics in panels D-E."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Fig. 5 X-Cell-Ultra generalizes to melanocyte progenitors and primary human T cells in a zero-shot manner. (A) Schematic shows pre-training of X-Cell-Ultra on X-Atlas/Pisces datasets, excluding the melanocyte progenitor population from the iPSC Multi-Diff screen. We evaluated X-Cell-Ultra in a zero-shot manner on the melanocyte progenitor population as well as primary human T cell datasets from Zhu et al. [57]. We used test-time adaptation to calibrate the models on these datasets using self-supervised objectives. (B) Test loss follows a power law with trainable parameters ( L ( N ) ∝ N -0 . 03 , R 2 = 0 . 97; Table A4). Five models (83M-3.1B) were trained for 20 epochs on the Replogle-Nadig dataset under identical hyperparameters. Circle size is proportional to parameter count; color indicates DE Pearson r . Line plots (right) show how X-Cell-Architecture (blue) and X-Cell-Ultra-Architecture (pink) compare with respect to collapse metrics δ -norm ratio (top) and Pearson correlation of differentially expressed genes (bottom) on test set. (C) Performance of the control mean baseline, perturbation mean baseline, STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra with respect to Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right). Histogram (top) shows the metrics within 1,341 perturbations and the barplots (bottom) show mean ± 95% C.I. (D) Boxplots show the distribution of Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right) for 291 perturbations in resting and stimulated (2 time points) primary human T cells for 2 different donors. (E) Heatmaps show the log 2 fold change in expression of APPL2 in D2 (left) and D3 (right) 48 hours post-stimulation as observed by the data and predicted by STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about **X-Cell-Ultra**, a model for genome-scale zero-shot inference in human T cells.\n\nPanel A:\n- Schematic workflow labeled **X-Cell-Ultra**, **Pre-train**, and **Genome-scale Zero-shot Inference**.\n- Biological source objects include human cell lines / cell contexts labeled **HCT116**, **HEK293T**, **HepG2**, and **iPSC**, with perturbation cartoons.\n- Pretraining section shows **Jurkat resting**, **Jurkat active**, and **iPSC multi-diff** cell states.\n- The model appears to combine cell-state representations and perturbation/context information.\n- Inference example is for **Primary human T cell**, with donors/contexts labeled **D2** and **D3**.\n- Outputs include predicted responses for perturbations such as **8hr**, **Stim 48hr**, and NTC-related conditions.\n- A **Test-time Adaptation** module is shown with frozen cross-attention and reduced learning rate, using a query/key/value-style interface.\n\nPanel B:\n- Model scaling plots labeled **Model Scaling Dynamics (Replogle-Nadig)**.\n- Main scatter/line plot shows **Test Loss** decreasing as **Trainable Parameters** increase, with model sizes labeled approximately **83M**, **216M**, **543M**, **1.6B**, and **3.1B**.\n- Pearson correlation color scale is shown.\n- Side plots compare **X-Cell (55M)** and **X-Cell-Ultra (4.9B)** over epochs.\n- Metrics include **Pearson ρ** and **DE Pearson ρ**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra improves over epochs, with annotated gains around **2.3x** and **2.7x**.\n\nPanel C:\n- Evaluation labeled **Melanocyte Progenitor: 1,341 Test Perturbations**.\n- Histograms and bar charts compare methods on **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Methods include **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra appears to achieve higher Pearson Δ and DE direction match, and lower MAE than X-Cell.\n\nPanel D:\n- Evaluation labeled **291 Test Perturbations, 2 Donors, 3 Contexts**.\n- Boxplots compare **Control mean**, **Pert. mean**, **STATE**, **X-Cell**, and **X-Cell-Ultra**.\n- Metrics shown are **Pearson Δ**, **DE Direction Match**, and **MAE**.\n- Legend includes contexts such as **D2 Rest**, **D3 Rest**, **D2 Stim8hr**, **D3 Stim8hr**, **D2 Stim48hr**, and **D3 Stim48hr**.\n- X-Cell-Ultra generally shows stronger Pearson Δ and DE direction match and lower MAE.\n\nPanel E:\n- Heatmaps labeled **APPL2 D2 Stim 48h log₂FC** and **APPL2 D3 Stim 48h log₂FC**.\n- Rows compare **STATE**, donor/context measurements such as **D2 48h Stim** or **D3 48h Stim**, **X-Cell-Ultra**, and **X-Cell**.\n- Color scale ranges from blue to red around zero, representing log₂ fold-change values.\n- Dendrograms show clustering; X-Cell-Ultra appears closer to observed stimulated donor profiles than X-Cell in these examples."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 50, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel UMAP scatter plot of clustered cells. Axes are labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. A legend on the right maps colors to annotated cell states/types:\n\n`Epithelial Ectoderm`, `Mesenchymal Fibroblast`, `Mesenchymal Progenitor`, `Neural Crest; EMT`, `Neural Crest; Melanocyte Progenitor`, `Neuroectoderm`, `Neuron`, `Cycling; G2M`, `Cycling; S-phase`, and `Cycling; Stress Response`.\n\nThe plotted biological source objects appear to be individual cells or nuclei embedded in two-dimensional UMAP space and colored by cell-type/state annotation. The visible transformation is dimensionality reduction to UMAP coordinates followed by categorical coloring. The figure shows multiple separated and partially overlapping clusters, including a large green epithelial ectoderm cluster on the left and several right-side clusters containing neuroectoderm, mesenchymal, neural crest, cycling, and neuron-associated annotations."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 51, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological data figure with panels labeled A, B, C, D, and E.\n\nPanel A shows bar plots comparing recall of biological networks for CORUM and StringDB across screen groups labeled HCT116 full (n=3.4M), 2 screens (n=2-3M), and 3 screens (n=3.5-4M). A second bar plot shows number of cells, with values around millions, comparing screen groups. Error bars are shown.\n\nPanel B shows three cluster-level specificity score bar charts: non-context specificity score, HepG2 specificity score, and iPSC specificity score. Bars are plotted by cluster index 0-9, with selected clusters highlighted in dark blue and others in gray.\n\nPanel C shows a dense 2D embedding or network map with many colored points and text labels for biological features/genes or gene programs. Labels include cancer/biological annotations and many color-coded clusters. The visualization appears to represent biological source objects arranged by similarity or learned representation.\n\nPanel D shows another 2D embedding/map colored by Gini coefficient, with a green-yellow-red color scale from 0.0 to 1.0. Several dense red/orange clusters and many scattered green/yellow points are visible, suggesting specificity or concentration of signal across clusters.\n\nPanel E shows a plot comparing shuffled versus observed mean STRING context size. Many gray lines connect shuffled and observed values for labeled biological contexts, with observed values generally much lower than shuffled. A statistical annotation is shown at top with a very small p-value.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate biological screening or perturbation-derived representations against known biological networks and context specificity, using bar plots, cluster specificity scores, embedding visualizations, and STRING context-size comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 52, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 3 Performance comparison between STATE models trained on different datasets and X-Cell. For a fair comparison, we trained STATE (Pisces) model on a dataset set matching that used to train the X-Cell model, and STATE replogle on all publicly available datasets. We evaluate performance using six metrics across iPSC and HepG2 cell lines.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart labeled **iPSC-200**. It compares three methods across six metrics:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**: light blue bars\n- **STATE (Pisces)**: darker blue bars\n- **X-Cell**: magenta bars\n\nVisible panels/metrics:\n\n1. **Overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.281\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.306\n   - X-Cell: 0.306\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.823\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.796\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.859\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.884\n   - X-Cell: 0.915\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.099\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.104\n   - X-Cell: 0.510\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.157\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.059\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.517\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.506\n   - X-Cell: 0.553\n\nBiological source object indicated: **iPSC-200**, likely an induced pluripotent stem cell benchmark/dataset context, though no additional biological entities are shown.\n\nModel/interface comparison: performance of **STATE** trained or evaluated with **Replogle+VCC** and **Pisces**, compared against **X-Cell**.\n\nMain visible finding: **X-Cell** performs best or tied-best on all shown metrics, with especially large improvements in **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 52, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart titled **“HepG2-200”**. It contains six side-by-side quantitative panels comparing three methods or conditions:\n\n- **STATE (Replogle+VCC)**\n- **STATE (Pisces)**\n- **X-Cell**\n\nVisible panels and metrics:\n\n1. **overlap@N ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.042\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.220\n   - X-Cell: 0.255\n\n2. **DE Direction Match ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.827\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.847\n   - X-Cell: 0.950\n\n3. **DE Spearman LFC ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.909\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.936\n   - X-Cell: 0.971\n\n4. **Pearson Δ ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.097\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.101\n   - X-Cell: 0.476\n\n5. **MAE ↓**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.077\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.067\n   - X-Cell: 0.026\n\n6. **Discrimination L2 ↑**\n   - STATE (Replogle+VCC): 0.520\n   - STATE (Pisces): 0.504\n   - X-Cell: 0.547\n\nThe biological source object indicated by the title is **HepG2**, likely a cell-line-specific benchmark or dataset subset. The figure compares model or method performance across differential-expression-related metrics. The visible finding is that **X-Cell performs best on all shown metrics**, with especially large improvements for **Pearson Δ** and **MAE**."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 53, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A: Heatmap titled “Attention weights per perturbation.” Rows are modalities labeled ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, and scGPT. Columns represent “380 perturbations.” A hierarchical dendrogram above the heatmap clusters perturbations by distance. Color scale at right is labeled “Attention weight” and ranges roughly from 0.00 to 0.32, with darker red indicating higher attention. StringDB and scGPT show broad high attention across many perturbations, while CellPainting contains a large white/missing or near-zero region.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart titled “Total significant pathways per modality.” X-axis lists modalities StringDB, scGPT, GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting. Y-axis is “Total significant pathways.” Bars show counts: StringDB 94, scGPT 80, GenePT 44, ESM2 42, DepMap 38, CellPainting 13. StringDB and scGPT have the highest pathway counts.\n\nPanel C: Heatmap titled “Significant MSigDB Hallmark Pathways per Perturbation x Modality.” Rows are the same modalities: ESM2, StringDB, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT. Columns are perturbations with dense rotated labels. Color scale at right indicates significance, with pale to darker red blocks marking significant pathway associations. The bottom x-axis is labeled “Perturbation.” Visible findings include broad significant regions for StringDB and scGPT across subsets of perturbations, fewer scattered signals for GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, and CellPainting.\n\nBiological/source objects shown: perturbations, modalities derived from biological or biomedical data representations including protein language model embeddings (ESM2), protein interaction/network information (StringDB), gene-text embeddings or representations (GenePT), dependency map data (DepMap), cell painting image-derived features (CellPainting), and single-cell GPT embeddings or features (scGPT). The figure summarizes attention weights and MSigDB Hallmark pathway significance across perturbations and modalities."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 54, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A shows a schematic model architecture for perturbation conditioning. On the left, a “Control Encoding” block includes inputs such as “Gene Embedding,” “Exp. Cond,” and a “Diffusion Mask Encoder.” These feed into an attention-like module labeled “Q × KV.” A “Prior knowledge” box lists biological/source resources: “Depmap,” “StringDB,” and “ESM2.” The central orange label says “Perturbation Conditioning” and “Cross-attention Every 3 Layers of Self-attention.” Several vertical transformer-like blocks lead to a multiplicative interaction with “Balance Inference.” Additional prior-knowledge features are shown as “Knowledge-base Importance” and “TCR Pathway Enrichment for Top 1,000 Genes With the Highest Attention Scores.”\n\nPanel B is a bar chart titled “Change in Attention Scores of TCR Signaling Pathway Genes.” The y-axis is “Rel. Change in Contribution (pathway group %).” Methods on the x-axis include StringDB, scGPT, CellPainting, ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap. Bars show negative changes for StringDB, scGPT, and CellPainting, and positive changes for ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap, with GenePT appearing highest.\n\nPanel C is a bar chart titled “Over-representation of the Top 1,000 Genes in TCR Signaling Pathway.” The y-axis is “Odds Ratio.” Methods on the x-axis include GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB. A legend distinguishes p-value < 0.05 from p-value ≥ 0.05. A red dotted horizontal reference line marks odds ratio 1. GenePT, ESM2, and DepMap are above 1, while CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB are below or near/below 1.\n\nBiological objects explicitly shown include genes, TCR signaling pathway genes, pathway enrichment, and prior-knowledge sources such as DepMap, StringDB, and ESM2. The figure presents a model interface combining gene/expression/diffusion-mask encodings with cross-attention-based perturbation conditioning and reports that some knowledge sources increase attention or enrichment for TCR signaling pathway genes."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 55, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel quantitative scientific plot comparing cell line groups.\n\nLeft panel:\n- Title: “MAE (lower better ↓)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.065 to 0.090.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.005 **”\n- Finding: the Distant group shows higher MAE than the Similar group, indicating worse prediction error for distant cell lines.\n\nRight panel:\n- Title: “Pearson Δ (higher better ↑)”\n- Y-axis values roughly 0.20 to 0.50.\n- X-axis: “Cell Line Group”\n- Groups: “Similar (Training-Like)” and “Distant”\n- Boxplots with overlaid individual sample points.\n- Pink diamond markers indicate group summary values.\n- Statistical annotation above groups: “p = 0.026 *”\n- Finding: the Similar group shows higher Pearson Δ than the Distant group.\n\nVisible biological/source objects:\n- Cell line groups are referenced as the biological grouping variable.\n- No microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecules, or other biological objects are directly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- The figure appears to summarize model or prediction performance metrics by cell line similarity group.\n- Metrics shown are MAE and Pearson Δ.\n- No model architecture or interface is visible."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 56, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 7 Architecture ablations, training efficiency, and scaling analysis for X-Cell-Ultra. (A) Architecture and loss ablation on the 4.9B model (Replogle-Nadig, 6 epochs). Scatter shows δ -norm ratio ˆ ρ vs. DE Pearson r for six configurations; color = MAE. The red shaded region (ˆ ρ < 0 . 2) indicates conservative collapse (predicting small effects). Replacing scale-invariant losses with CCC and removing the hidden residual shortcut progressively recovered both magnitude (ˆ ρ : 0 . 22 → 0 . 51) and direction (DEr : 0 . 05 → 0 . 33); see Table A1. (B) Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) comparison. X-Cell-Ultra achieves 41% MFU on 64 H200 GPUs, comparable to Llama 3 (reported 38-43% [95]) and ∼ 20 × the 55M X-Cell (2%). (C) Normalized loss scaling (relative to 83M model). Both train (dashed, α = 0 . 32, R 2 = 0 . 96) and test (solid, α = 0 . 03, R 2 = 0 . 97) follow power laws; color = DE Pearson r . (D) Individual validation metrics vs. trainable parameters: centroid accuracy (left) and per-perturbation MAE (right), colored by validation loss. (E) Training dynamics over 20 epochs: test loss (left) and DE Pearson r (right) for all five model sizes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A-E, showing machine learning model scaling and evaluation metrics.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot comparing `DE Pearson r` on x-axis against `δ-norm ratio (ρ)` on y-axis. Points correspond to methods or model variants labeled `60M baseline`, `Tied + QK-Norm`, `CCC+dnp (residual)`, `MLP head (no resid)`, `Tied + per-gene CCC (no resid)`, and `add tied`. Colorbar indicates `MAE`.\n\nPanel B: Horizontal bar chart of `Model FLOPs Utilization (%)`. Rows include `scGPT`, `X-CELL`, `Llama 3`, and `X-Cell-Ultra`. Labels include parameter sizes such as `53M`, `55M`, `405B`, and `4.9B`, with utilization values around `1%`, `2%`, `40%`, and `41%`. A dashed vertical reference is labeled `LLM-grade`.\n\nPanel C: Scaling-law style plot with `Trainable Parameters` on the x-axis and `Normalized Loss (relative to 83M)` on the y-axis. Points are labeled `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Blue dashed line represents train behavior and pink line represents test behavior. Colorbar indicates `DE Pearson r`.\n\nPanel D: Two side-by-side scaling plots. Left plot shows `Control Accuracy` versus `Trainable Parameters`, increasing from `83M` through `3.1B`. Right plot shows `Per-gene MAE` versus `Trainable Parameters`, decreasing as model size increases. Colorbar indicates `Val Loss`.\n\nPanel E: Two training curves over `Epoch`. Left plot shows `Test Loss` decreasing over epochs for model sizes `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Right plot shows `DE Pearson r` increasing over epochs for the same model sizes.\n\nBiological source objects: none visibly depicted. The figure contains quantitative plots related to model performance, trainable parameters, loss, MAE, control accuracy, and differential expression Pearson correlation.\n\nMain visible finding: larger trainable parameter counts generally correspond to lower loss/MAE and higher control accuracy or DE Pearson correlation, with model performance improving across epochs and scaling comparisons shown across model sizes."]}]
